˜F	]À                                                                  9‚-And if he knocks out the tooth of his male servant or female servant, he must let the servant go free because of <n>[the loss of]</n> the tooth.(8‚K<pb/>"If a man hits the eye of his male servant or female servant and it is destroyed, he must let the servant go free because of <n>[the loss of]</n> the eye.:7qburn for burn, wound for wound, bruise for bruise.O6<f>[4]</f> eye for eye, tooth for tooth, hand for hand, foot for foot,o5YBut if there is <i>any further</i> injury, then you shall require <n>[as a penalty]</n> life for life,‚%4„E<pb/>"If men fight with each other and injure a pregnant woman so that she gives birth prematurely <n>[and the baby lives]</n>, yet there is no <i>further</i> injury, the one who hurt her must be punished with a fine <n>[paid]</n> to the woman's husband, as much as the judges decide.3‚'If, however, the servant survives for a day or two, the offender shall not be punished, for the <n>[injured]</n> servant is his own property.2<pb/>"If a man strikes his male or his female servant with a staff and the servant dies at his hand, he must be punished.‚1„if he gets up and walks around leaning on his cane, then the one who struck him shall be left <n>[physically]</n> unpunished; he must only pay for his loss of time <n>[at work]</n>, and the costs <n>[of treatment and recuperation]</n> until he is thoroughly healed. 0{<pb/>"If men quarrel and one strikes another with a stone or with his fist, and he does not die but is confined to bed,{/q<pb/>"Whoever<f>[3]</f> curses his father or his mother <i>or</i> treats them contemptuously must be put to death.x.k<pb/>"Whoever kidnaps a man, whether he sells him or is found with him in his possession, must be put to death.M-<pb/>"Whoever strikes his father or his mother must be put to death.a,ƒ=But if a man acts intentionally against another and kills him by <n>[design through]</n> treachery, you are to take him from My altar <n>[to which he may have fled for protection]</n>, so that he may be put to death.c+ƒA
However,<f>[1]</f> if he did not lie in wait <n>[for him]</n>, but God allowed him to fall into his hand, then I will establish for you a<f>[2]</f> place to which he may escape <n>[for protection until duly tried]</n>.E*"Whoever strikes a man so that he dies must be put to death.r)_If he does not do these three things for her, then shall she leave free, without <i>payment of</i> money.r(_
If her master marries another wife, he may not reduce her food, her clothing, or her privilege as a wife.'y	If her master chooses her <n>[as a wife]</n> for his son, he shall act toward her as if she were legally his daughter.u&ƒeIf she does not please her master who has chosen her for himself <n>[as a wife]</n>, he shall let her be redeemed <n>[by her family]</n>. He does not have the authority to sell her to a foreign people, because he has been unfair to her.%‚<pb/>"If a man sells his daughter to be a female servant, she shall not go free <n>[after six years]</n> as male servants do.y$ƒmthen his master shall bring him to God <n>[that is, to the judges who act in God's name]</n>, then he shall bring him to the door or doorpost. And his master shall pierce his ear with an awl (strong needle); and he shall serve him for life.v#gBut if the servant plainly says, 'I love my master, my wife and my children; I will not leave as a free man,'8"‚kIf his master gives him a wife, and she gives birth to sons or daughters, the wife and her children shall belong to her master, and he shall leave <n>[your service]</n> alone.x!kIf he came <n>[to you]</n> alone, he shall leave alone; if he came married, then his wife shall leave with him.A ‚}<pb/>"If you purchase a Hebrew servant <n>[because of his debt or poverty]</n>, he shall serve six years, and in the seventh <n>[year]</n> he shall leave as a free man, paying nothing.\	5<pb/>"Now these are the ordinances (laws) which you shall set before the Israelites:oYNor shall you go up to My altar on steps, so that your nakedness will not be<f>[5]</f> exposed on it.'‚If you make an altar of stone for Me, you shall not build it of cut stones, for if you use a chisel on it, you will<f>[4]</f> profane it.‚*„OYou shall make an altar of earth for Me, and sacrifice on it your burnt offerings and your peace offerings, your sheep and your oxen. In every place where I cause My Name to be recorded <i>and</i> remembered <n>[through revelation of My divine nature]</n> I will come to you and bless you..‚WYou shall not make <i>other gods</i> <n>[to worship]</n> besides Me; gods of silver or gods of gold, you shall not make <n>[these lifeless idols]</n> for yourselves.‚'<pb/>Then the LORD said to Moses, "Thus you shall say to the Israelites, 'You have seen for yourselves that I have spoken to you from heaven.mUSo the people stood at a <n>[safe]</n> distance, but Moses approached the thick cloud where God was.\ƒ3Moses said to the people, "Do not be afraid; for God has come in order to test you, and in order that the fear of Him <n>[that is, a profound reverence for Him]</n> will remain with you, so that you do not sin."vgThen they said to Moses, "You speak to us and we will listen, but do not let God speak to us or we will die."‚„<pb/>Now all the people witnessed the thunder and the flashes of lightning and the sound of the trumpet and the smoking mountain; and as they looked, the people were afraid, and they trembled <n>[and moved backward]</n> and stood at a <n>[safe]</n> distance.‚„<pb/>"You shall not covet <n>[that is, selfishly desire and attempt to acquire]</n> your neighbor's house; you shall not covet your neighbor's wife, or his male servant, or his female servant, or his ox, or his donkey, or anything that belongs to your neighbor."	‚
<pb/>"You shall not testify falsely <n>[that is, lie, withhold, or manipulate the truth]</n> against your neighbor (any person).dC<pb/>"You shall not steal <n>[secretly, openly, fraudulently, or through carelessness]</n>.6i<pb/>"You shall not commit<f>[3]</f> adultery.N
<pb/>"You shall not commit murder (unjustified, deliberate homicide).‚)<pb/>"Honor (respect, obey, care for) your father and your mother, so that your days may be prolonged in the land the LORD your God gives you.‚„For in six days the LORD made the heavens and the earth, the sea and everything that is in them, and He rested (ceased) on the seventh day. That is why the LORD blessed the Sabbath day and made it holy <n>[that is, set it apart for His purposes]</n>.‚;„q
but the seventh day is a Sabbath <n>[a day of rest dedicated]</n> to the LORD your God; on that day you shall not do any work, you or your son, or your daughter, or your male servant, or your female servant, or your livestock or the temporary resident (foreigner) who stays within your <n>[city]</n> gates.6
i	Six days you shall labor and do all your work,`;<pb/>"Remember the Sabbath (seventh) day to keep it holy (set apart, dedicated to God).‚K…<pb/>"You shall not take the name of the LORD your God in vain <n>[that is, irreverently, in false affirmations or in ways that impugn the character of God]</n>; for the LORD will not hold guiltless <i>nor</i> leave unpunished the one who<f>[2]</f> takes His name in vain <n>[disregarding its reverence and its power]</n>.
‚-but showing graciousness <i>and</i> steadfast lovingkindness to thousands <n>[of generations]</n> of those who love Me and keep My commandments.ƒ	†You shall not worship them nor serve them; for I, the LORD your God, am a jealous (impassioned) God <n>[<f>[1]</f> demanding what is rightfully and uniquely mine]</n>, visiting (avenging) the iniquity (sin, guilt) of the fathers on the children <n>[that is, calling the children to account for the sins of their fathers]</n>, to the third and fourth generations of those who hate Me,Sƒ!<pb/>"You shall not make for yourself any idol, or any likeness (form, manifestation) of what is in heaven above or on the earth beneath or in the water under the earth <n>[as an object to worship]</n>.5g<pb/>"You shall have no other gods before Me.q]<pb/>"I am the LORD your God, who has brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of slavery.+	U<pb/>Then God spoke all these words:[1So Moses went down to the people and told them <n>[again about God's warning]</n>.‚„Then the LORD said to him, "Go down and come up again, you and Aaron with you; but do not let the priests and the people break through <n>[the barriers]</n> to come up to the LORD, or He will break forth <n>[in judgment]</n> against them <n>[and destroy them]</n>."!‚=Moses said to the LORD, "The people cannot come up to Mount Sinai, because You warned us, saying, 'Set barriers around the mountain and consecrate it.'"`ƒ;Also have the priests who approach the LORD consecrate (sanctify, set apart) themselves <n>[for My sacred purpose]</n>, or else the LORD will break forth <n>[in judgment]</n> against them <n>[and destroy them]</n>."[ ƒ1Then the LORD spoke to Moses, "Go down, warn the people, so that they do not break through <n>[the barriers around the mountain]</n> to the LORD to see <n>[Me]</n>, and many of them perish <n>[as a result]</n>.
‚The LORD came down on Mount Sinai, to the top of the mountain; and the LORD called Moses to the top of the mountain, and he went up.~‚!And it happened, as the blast of the ram's horn grew louder and louder, Moses spoke and God answered him with <n>[a voice of]</n> thunder..}‚WMount Sinai was wrapped in smoke because the LORD descended upon it in fire; its smoke ascended like the smoke of a furnace, and the whole mountain quaked violently.|‚Then Moses brought the people out of the camp to meet God, and they stood <i>and</i> presented themselves at the foot of the mountain.‚{„<pb/>So it happened on the third day, when it was morning, that there were thunder and flashes of lightning, and a thick cloud was on the mountain, and a very loud blast was sounded on a ram's horn, so that all the people who were in the camp trembled.kzQHe said to the people, "Be prepared for the third day; do not<f>[1]</f> be intimate with a woman."y‚So Moses went down from the mountain to the people and sanctified them <n>[for God's sacred purpose]</n>, and they washed their clothes.‚?x„y
No hand shall touch him <n>[that is, no one shall try to save the guilty party]</n>, but the offender must be stoned or shot through <n>[with arrows]</n>; whether man or animal <n>[that touches the mountain]</n>, he shall not live.' When the ram's horn sounds a long blast, they shall come up to the mountain."NwƒYou shall set barriers for the people all around <n>[the mountain]</n>, saying, 'Beware that you do not go up on the mountain or touch its border; whoever touches the mountain must be put to death.v‚5and be ready by the third day, because on the third day the LORD will come down on Mount Sinai <n>[in the cloud]</n> in the sight of all the people.:u‚o
<pb/>The LORD also said to Moses, "Go to the people and consecrate them today and tomorrow <n>[that is, prepare them for My sacred purpose]</n>, and have them wash their clothesktƒQ	The LORD said to Moses, "Behold, I will come to you in a thick cloud, so that the people may hear when I speak with you and may believe <i>and</i> trust in you forever." Then Moses repeated the words of the people to the LORD.s‚5All the people answered together and said, "We will do everything that the LORD has spoken." And Moses reported the words of the people to the LORD.wri<pb/>So Moses called for the elders of the people, and told them all these words which the LORD commanded him.'q‚Iand you shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation <n>[set apart for My purpose]</n>.' These are the words that you shall speak to the Israelites."dpƒCNow therefore, if you will in fact obey My voice and keep My covenant (agreement), then you shall be My own special possession <i>and</i> treasure from among all peoples <n>[of the world]</n>, for all the earth is Mine;woi'You have seen what I did to the Egyptians, and how I carried you on eagles' wings, and brought you to Myself.&n‚GMoses went up to God <n>[on the mountain]</n>, and the LORD called to him from the mountain, saying, "Say this to the house of Jacob and tell the Israelites:$m‚CWhen they moved out from Rephidim, they came to the Wilderness of Sinai and they camped there; Israel camped at the base of the mountain <n>[of Sinai]</n>.l	‚#<pb/>In the third month after the children of Israel had left the land of Egypt, the very same day, they came into the Wilderness of Sinai.okYThen Moses<f>[4]</f> said goodbye to his father-in-law, and Jethro went back to his own land (Midian).#j‚AAnd they judged the people at all times; they would bring the difficult cases to Moses, but every minor dispute they judged <i>and</i> decided themselves.EiƒMoses chose able men from all Israel and made them heads over the people, leaders of thousands, of hundreds, of fifties and of tens <n>[from the highest to the lowest judicial levels]</n>.Yh-<pb/>So Moses listened to his father-in-law and did everything that he had said.Bg‚If you will do this thing and God so commands you, then you will be able to endure <n>[the responsibility]</n>, and all these people will also go <n>[back]</n> to their tents in peace."^fƒ7They shall judge the people at all times; have them bring every major dispute to you, but let them judge every minor dispute themselves. So it will be easier for you, and they will bear <i>the burden</i> with you.{eƒqFurthermore, you shall select from all the people competent men who <n>[reverently]</n> fear God, men of truth, those who hate dishonest gain; you shall place these over the people as leaders of thousands, of hundreds, of fifties and of tens.}duYou shall teach them the decrees and laws. You shall show them the way they are to live and the work they are to do.\cƒ3Now listen to<f>[2]</f> me;<f>[3]</f> I will advise you, and may God be with you <n>[to confirm my advice]</n>. You shall represent the people before God. You shall bring their disputes <i>and</i> causes to Him.%b‚EYou will certainly wear out both yourself and these people who are with you, because the task is too heavy for you <n>[to bear]</n>; you cannot do it alone.Ua%Moses' father-in-law said to him, "The thing that you are doing is not good.`‚When they have a dispute they come to me, and I judge between a man and his neighbor and I make known the statutes of God and His laws."o_YMoses said to his father-in-law, "Because the people come to me to ask <n>[about the will]</n> of God.w^ƒiWhen Moses' father-in-law saw everything that he was doing for the people, he said, "What is this that you are doing for the people? Why are you sitting alone <n>[as a judge]</n> with all the people standing around you from dawn to dusk?"&]‚G
<pb/>Now the next day Moses sat to judge <n>[the disputes]</n> the people <n>[had with one another]</n>, and the people stood around Moses from dawn to dusk.Z\ƒ/Then Jethro, Moses' father-in-law, took a burnt offering and <n>[other]</n> sacrifices <n>[to offer]</n> to God, and Aaron came with all the elders of Israel to eat a meal with Moses' father-in-law before God.Q[ƒNow I know that the LORD is<f>[1]</f> greater than all gods; indeed, it was proven when they acted insolently toward Israel <n>[and the LORD showed Himself infinitely superior to all their gods]</n>."AZ‚}
Jethro said, "Blessed be the LORD, who has rescued you from the hand of the Egyptians and from the hand of Pharaoh, and who has rescued the people from under the hand of the Egyptians.
Y‚	Jethro rejoiced over all the good things the LORD had done to Israel, in that He had rescued them from the hand of the Egyptians.ZXƒ/Moses told his father-in-law about all that the LORD had done to Pharaoh and the Egyptians for Israel's sake, and about all the hardship that had happened during the journey, and how the LORD had rescued them.0W‚[So Moses went out to meet his father-in-law, and he bowed down <n>[in respect]</n> and kissed him. They asked each other about their well-being and went into the tent.V‚He sent a message to Moses, "I, your father-in-law Jethro, am coming to you with your wife and her two sons <n>[who are]</n> with her."KUƒ<pb/>Then Jethro, his father-in-law, came with Moses' sons and his wife to <n>[join]</n> Moses in the wilderness where he was camped, at the mountain of God <n>[that is, Mt. Sinai in Horeb]</n>.+T‚QThe other <n>[son]</n> was named Eliezer (my God is help), for <i>Moses said</i>, "The God of my father was my help, and He rescued me from the sword of Pharaoh."S‚	along with her two sons, of whom one was named Gershom (stranger), for Moses said, "I have been a stranger in a foreign land."vRgThen Jethro, Moses' father-in-law, took Moses' wife Zipporah, after he had sent her away <n>[from Egypt]</n>,CQ	ƒ<pb/>Now Jethro (Reuel), the priest of Midian, Moses' father-in-law, heard of all that God had done for Moses and for Israel His people, and that the LORD had brought Israel out of Egypt.P‚!saying, "The LORD has sworn <n>[an oath]</n>; the LORD will have war against <n>[the people of]</n> Amalek from generation to generation."OOAnd Moses built an altar and named it<f>[8]</f> The LORD Is My Banner;QNƒ<pb/>Then the LORD said to Moses, "Write this in the book as a memorial and<f>[7]</f> recite it to Joshua, that I will utterly wipe out the memory of Amalek <n>[and his people]</n> from under heaven."nMW
So Joshua overwhelmed <i>and</i> defeated<f>[6]</f> Amalek and his people with the edge of the sword.‚L„But Moses' hands were heavy <i>and</i> he grew tired. So they took a stone and put it under him, and he sat on it. Then Aaron and Hur held up his hands, one on one side and one on the other side; so it was that his hands were steady until the sun set.K‚Now when Moses held up his hand, Israel prevailed, and when he lowered his hand <n>[due to fatigue]</n>, Amalek prevailed.yJm
So Joshua did as Moses said, and fought with Amalek; and Moses, Aaron, and<f>[5]</f> Hur went up to the hilltop.HIƒ	So Moses said to<f>[4]</f> Joshua, "Choose men for us and go out, fight against Amalek <n>[and his people]</n>. Tomorrow I will stand on the top of the hill with the staff of God in my hand."_H9Then<f>[3]</f> Amalek <n>[and his people]</n> came and fought with Israel at Rephidim.‚G„He named the place <n>[where this miracle occurred]</n> Massah (test) and<f>[2]</f> Meribah (contention) because of the quarreling of the sons of Israel, and because they tested the <n>[patience of the]</n> LORD, saying, "Is the LORD among us, or not?"FƒyBehold, I will stand before you there on the rock at<f>[1]</f> Horeb; there you shall strike the rock, and water will come out of it, so that the people may <n>[have something to]</n> drink." And Moses did so in the sight of the elders of Israel.5E‚eThen the LORD said to Moses, "Pass before the people and take with you some of the elders of Israel; and take in your hand the staff with which you struck the Nile, and go. D{So Moses cried out to the LORD for help, saying, "What shall I do with this people? They are almost ready to stone me."?C‚yBut the people were thirsty for water; and the people murmured against Moses and said, "Why did you bring us up from Egypt to kill us and our children and our livestock with thirst?"eBƒETherefore the people quarreled with Moses and said, "Give us water so we may <n>[have something to]</n> drink." And Moses said to them, "Why do you quarrel with me? Why do you tempt the LORD <i>and</i> try His patience?"aA	ƒ?<pb/>Then all the congregation of the children of Israel moved on from the Wilderness of Sin by stages, according to the commandment of the LORD, and camped at Rephidim, but there was no water for the people to drink.D@$(Now an<f>[6]</f> omer is the tenth of an<f>[7]</f> ephah.)?‚3#The Israelites ate manna forty years, until they reached an inhabited land; they ate the manna until they came to the border of the land of Canaan.>‚"As the LORD commanded Moses, so Aaron <n>[eventually]</n> placed it in the presence of the<f>[5]</f> Testimony, to be kept.=‚!So Moses said to Aaron, "Take a pot and put an omer of manna in it, and place it before the LORD to be kept throughout your generations."o<ƒY Then Moses said, "This is the word which the LORD commands, 'Let an omer of it be kept throughout your generations, that they may see the bread with which I fed you in the wilderness, when I brought you out of the land of Egypt.'";‚%<pb/>The house of Israel called the bread manna; it was like coriander seed, white, and it tasted like flat pastry (wafers) made with honey.0:]So the people rested on the seventh day.D9ƒSee, the LORD has given you the Sabbath; therefore He gives you the bread for two days on the sixth day. Let every man stay in his place; no man is to leave his place on the seventh day."8}Then the LORD said to Moses, "How long do you <n>[people]</n> refuse to keep My commandments and My instructions (laws)?`7;<pb/>Now on the seventh day some of the people went out to gather, but they found none.v6gSix days you shall gather it, but on the seventh day, the Sabbath, there will be none <n>[in the field]</n>."w5iThen Moses said, "Eat that today, for today is a Sabbath to the LORD; today you will not find it in the field.j4OThey put it aside until morning, as Moses told them, and it did not become foul nor was it wormy.}3ƒuHe said to them, "This is what the LORD has said: 'Tomorrow is a solemn rest, a holy Sabbath to the LORD'; bake and boil what you will bake and boil <n>[today]</n>, and all that remains left over put aside for yourselves to keep until morning."2‚+Now on the sixth day they gathered twice as much bread, two omers for each person; and all the leaders of the congregation came and told Moses.n1WSo they gathered it every morning, each as much as he needed, because when the sun was hot it melted.*0‚OBut they did not listen to Moses, and some left a supply of it until morning, and it bred worms and became foul <i>and</i> rotten; and Moses was angry with them.a/=Moses said, "Let none of it be left <n>[overnight]</n> until <n>[the next]</n> morning."B.‚When they measured it with an omer, he who had gathered a large amount had no excess, and he who had gathered little had no lack; every man gathered according to his need (family size).f-GThe Israelites did so, and some gathered much <n>[of it]</n> and some <n>[only a]</n> little.G,ƒ	This is what the LORD has commanded: 'Let every man gather as much of it as he needs. Take an<f>[4]</f> omer for each person, according to the number of people each of you has in his tent.'"J+ƒWhen the Israelites saw it, they said to one another, "<f>[3]</f> What is it?" For they did not know what it was. And Moses said to them, "This is the bread which the LORD has given you to eat.*‚When the layer of dew evaporated, on the surface of the wilderness there was a fine, flake-like thing, as fine as frost on the ground.)‚	
<pb/>So in the evening the quails came up and covered the camp, and in the morning there was a blanket of dew around the camp.U(ƒ%"I have heard the murmurings of the Israelites; speak to them, saying, 'At twilight you shall eat meat, and in the morning you shall be filled with bread; and you shall know that I am the LORD your God.'"-'WThen the LORD spoke to Moses, saying,B&‚
So it happened that as Aaron spoke to the whole congregation of Israel, they looked toward the wilderness, and behold, the glory <i>and</i> brilliance of the LORD appeared in the cloud!%‚	<pb/>Then Moses said to Aaron, "Say to all the congregation of Israel, 'Approach the LORD, because He has heard your murmurings.'"‚%$„EMoses said, "<i>This will happen</i> when the LORD gives you meat to eat in the evening, and in the morning <n>[enough]</n> bread to be fully satisfied, because the LORD has heard your murmurings against Him; for what are we? Your murmurings are not against us, but against the LORD.")#‚Mand in the morning you will see the glory of the LORD, for He hears your murmurings against the LORD. What are we, that you murmur <i>and</i> rebel against us?""So Moses and Aaron said to all Israel, "At evening you shall know that the LORD has brought you out of the land of Egypt,4!‚cAnd it shall be that on the sixth day, they shall prepare to bring in twice as much as they gather daily <n>[so that they will not need to gather on the seventh day]</n>."‚ „/<pb/>Then the LORD said to Moses, "Behold, I will cause bread to rain from heaven for you; the people shall go out and gather a day's portion every day, so that I may test them <n>[to determine]</n> whether or not they will walk <n>[obediently]</n> in My instruction (law).‚„+and the Israelites said to them, "<f>[2]</f> Would that we had died by the hand of the LORD in the land of Egypt, when we sat by the pots of meat and ate bread until we were full; for you have brought us out into this wilderness to kill this entire assembly with hunger."‚+The whole congregation of the Israelites <n>[grew discontented and]</n> murmured <i>and</i> rebelled against Moses and Aaron in the wilderness,]	ƒ7<pb/>They set out from Elim, and all the congregation of Israel came to the Wilderness of<f>[1]</f> Sin, which is between Elim and Sinai, on the fifteenth day of the second month after they left the land of Egypt. ‚;<pb/>Then the children of Israel came to Elim where there were twelve springs of water and seventy date palms, and they camped there beside the waters.ƒ†saying, "If you will diligently listen <i>and</i> pay attention to the voice of the LORD your God, and do what is right in His sight, and listen to His commandments, and keep <n>[foremost in your thoughts and actively obey]</n> all His precepts <i>and</i> statutes, then I will not put on you any of the diseases which I have put on the Egyptians; for I am the<f>[6]</f> LORD who heals you."‚„<t>Then he cried to the LORD <n>[for help]</n>, and the LORD showed him a tree, <n>[a branch of]</n> which he threw into the waters, and the waters became sweet. </t><br/>There the LORD made a statute and an ordinance for them, and there<f>[5]</f> He tested them,kQThe people <n>[grew discontented and]</n> grumbled at Moses, saying, "What are we going to drink?"‚Then they came to Marah, but they could not drink its waters because they were<f>[4]</f> bitter; therefore it was named Marah (bitter).HƒThen Moses led Israel from the Red Sea, and they went into the Wilderness of Shur; they went <n>[a distance of]</n> three days (about thirty-three miles) in the wilderness and found no water.Cƒ<t>Miriam answered them, </t><br/><t>"Sing to the LORD, for He has triumphed gloriously <i>and</i> is highly exalted; </t><t>The horse and its rider He has hurled into the sea."</t><br/>'‚I<pb/>Then Miriam the prophetess, the sister of Aaron <n>[and Moses]</n>, took a timbrel in her hand, and all the women followed her with timbrels and dancing.aƒ=<pb/>For the horses of Pharaoh went with his war-chariots and his charioteers into the sea, and the LORD brought back the waters of the sea on them, but the sons of Israel walked on dry land in the middle of the sea.=w<t>"The LORD shall reign to eternity and beyond."</t>‚)„M<t>"You will bring them <n>[into the land of promise]</n> and plant them on the mountain (Mt. Moriah in Jerusalem) of Your inheritance, </t><t>The place, O LORD, You have made for Your dwelling <n>[among them]</n>, </t><t>The sanctuary, O Lord, which Your hands have established.</t><br/>‚ ƒ{<t>Terror and dread fall on them; </t><t>Because of the greatness of Your arm they are as still as a stone; </t><t>Until Your people pass by <i>and</i> <n>[into Canaan]</n>, O LORD, </t><t>Until the people pass by whom You have purchased.</t><br/>pƒ[<t>"Then the <n>[tribal]</n> chiefs of Edom were dismayed <i>and</i> horrified; </t><t>The <n>[mighty]</n> leaders of Moab, trembling grips them; </t><t>All the inhabitants of Canaan have melted away <n>[in despair]</n>â€”</t><br/>‚)<t>"The peoples have heard <n>[about You]</n>, they tremble; </t><t>Anguish <i>and</i> fear has gripped the inhabitants of Philistia.</t><br/>@‚{
