`ñoŽ£         g’ƒIYou shall put the two stones on the <n>[two]</n> shoulder pieces of the ephod <n>[of the high priest]</n>, as memorial stones for Israel; and Aaron shall bear their names on his two shoulders as a memorial before the LORD.]’ƒ5With the work of a jeweler, like the engravings of a signet, you shall engrave the two stones according to the names of the sons of Israel. You shall have them set in<f>[1]</f> filigree <n>[settings]</n> of gold.’ y
six of their names on one stone and the remaining six names on the other stone, arranged in the order of their births.l‘S	You shall take two onyx stones and engrave on them the names of the <n>[twelve]</n> sons of Israel,&‘~‚GAnd the skillfully woven sash, which is on the ephod shall be made of the same material: of gold, of blue, purple, and scarlet fabric and fine twisted linen.}‘}uIt is to have two shoulder pieces joined to its two <n>[back and front]</n> ends, so that it may be joined together.)‘|‚M<pb/>and they shall make the ephod of gold and blue, purple, and scarlet fabric and fine twisted linen, skillfully woven <i>and</i> <n>[beautifully]</n> worked.‘{‚They are to use the gold and the blue and the purple and the scarlet fabric and fine twisted linen <n>[from the people]</n>,‚‘z„1These are the garments which they shall make: a breastpiece and an ephod <n>[for the breastpiece]</n> and a robe and a tunic of checkered work, a turban, and a sash. They shall make sacred garments for Aaron your brother and his sons, so that he may serve as a priest to Me.R‘yƒTell all the skilled <i>and</i> talented people whom I have endowed with a spirit of wisdom, that they are to make Aaron's garments to sanctify him <i>and</i> set him apart to serve as a priest for Me. ‘x‚;You are to make sacred garments <n>[official clothing reserved for holy services]</n> for Aaron your brother, for honor and for beauty (ornamentation).G‘w	ƒ<pb/>"Now bring your brother Aaron near, and his sons with him from among the sons of Israel, so that he may serve as priest to Meâ€”Aaron, Nadab and Abihu, Eleazar and Ithamar, Aaron's sons.ƒ‘v†	In the Tent of Meeting <n>[of God with His people]</n>, outside the veil which is in front of the <n>[ark of the]</n> Testimony <n>[and sets it apart]</n>, Aaron <n>[the high priest]</n> and his sons shall keep the lamp burning from evening to morning before the LORD. It shall be a perpetual statute <n>[to be observed]</n> throughout their generations on behalf of the Israelites.#‘u‚A<pb/>"You shall command the Israelites to provide you with clear oil of beaten olives for the light, to make a lamp burn continually <n>[every night]</n>. ‘t‚;All the tabernacle's utensils <i>and</i> instruments used in all its service, and all its stakes, and all the stakes for the court, shall be of bronze.:‘s‚oThe length of the court shall be a hundred cubits, and the width fifty <n>[cubits]</n> throughout, and the height five cubits of fine twisted linen, and their sockets of bronze. ‘r‚;All the pillars (support poles) around the court shall be joined together with silver rods; their hooks shall be of silver and their sockets of bronze.‚
‘q„For the gate of the court there shall be a screen <n>[to provide a covering]</n> of twenty cubits, of blue, purple, and scarlet fabric and finely woven <n>[embroidered]</n> linen, the work of an embroiderer, with four pillars and four <n>[base]</n> sockets.z‘poOn the other side <n>[of the gate]</n> the curtains shall be fifteen cubits with three pillars and three sockets.u‘oeThe curtains for one side <n>[of the gate]</n> shall be fifteen cubits with three pillars and three sockets.^‘n7
The width of the court <n>[to the front]</n>, on the east side shall be fifty cubits.‘m‚For the width of the court on the west side there shall be curtains of fifty cubits, with ten pillars (support poles) and ten sockets.O‘lƒlikewise for the north side there shall be curtains, a hundred <i>cubits</i> long, and its twenty pillars and twenty bronze sockets; but the hooks of the pillars and their fasteners shall be silver.‘k
it shall have twenty pillars and twenty bronze sockets; but the hooks of the pillars and their fasteners shall be silver; ‘j‚;	"You shall make the court of the tabernacle. The south side of the court is to have curtains of fine twisted linen, a hundred cubits long for one side; ‘i{You are to make the altar hollow with planks; as you were shown on the mountain <n>[of Sinai]</n>, so shall it be made.o‘hYThe poles shall be inserted through the rings on the two sides of the altar so that it may be carried.j‘gOYou shall make <n>[carrying]</n> poles for the altar, poles of acacia wood, overlaid with bronze.n‘fWAnd you shall put it under the ledge of the altar, so that the grid will extend halfway up the altar.~‘ewAlso make a grate for it, a network of bronze; and on the grid you are to make four bronze rings at its four corners.]‘dƒ5You shall make pots to remove its ashes, and shovels, basins <n>[to catch the blood of the sacrificed animal]</n>, meat-forks, and firepans <n>[to store live coals]</n>. You shall make all its utensils of bronze.‘c‚+Make horns (horn-shaped projections) for it on its four corners; the horns shall be of one piece with it, and you shall overlay it with bronze.?‘b	‚{<pb/>"And you shall make the altar <n>[for burnt offerings]</n> of acacia wood, five cubits long and five cubits wide; the altar shall be square, and its height shall be three cubits.^‘aƒ7%You shall make five pillars (support poles) of acacia wood to support the hanging curtain and overlay them with gold. Their hooks shall be of gold, and you shall cast five <n>[base]</n> sockets of bronze for them.N‘`ƒ$<pb/>"You shall make a screen <n>[to provide a covering]</n> for the doorway of the tent of blue, purple, and scarlet fabric and finely woven <n>[embroidered]</n> linen, the work of an embroiderer.@‘_‚{#You shall set the table <n>[for the bread]</n> outside the veil <n>[in the Holy Place]</n> on the north side, and the lampstand opposite the table on the south side of the tabernacle.b‘^?"You shall put the<f>[3]</f> mercy seat on the ark of the Testimony in the Holy of Holies.g‘]ƒI!You shall hang the veil from the hooks <n>[that connect the curtains together]</n>, and you shall bring the ark of the Testimony there within the veil. The veil shall separate for you the Holy Place and the Holy of Holies.‘\‚ You shall hang it on four pillars (support poles) of acacia wood overlaid with gold, with gold hooks, on four silver sockets.#‘[‚A"You shall make a veil <n>[to divide the two rooms]</n> of blue, purple, and scarlet fabric and fine twisted linen, skillfully worked with cherubim on it.*‘Z‚OYou shall erect the tabernacle according to its plan <n>[the direction corresponding to its meaning and purpose]</n> which has been shown to you on the mountain.‘Y‚You shall overlay the boards with gold and make their rings of gold to hold the bars. You shall overlay the bars with gold.q‘X]And the middle bar in the center of the boards shall pass through <n>[horizontally]</n> from end to end.-‘W‚Uand five bars for the boards of the other side of the tabernacle, and five bars for the boards of the rear end of the tabernacle, for the back wall toward the west.{‘Vq<pb/>"Then you shall make <n>[fifteen]</n> bars of acacia wood: five for the boards of one side of the tabernacle,^‘U7There shall be eight boards and sixteen silver sockets; two sockets under each board.,‘T‚SThey shall be joined together underneath, and joined together on top with one ring. So shall it be for both of them; they shall form the two <n>[rear]</n> corners.^‘S7Make two boards for the corners of the tabernacle at the rear <n>[on both sides]</n>.O‘RFor the back or west side of the tabernacle you shall make six boards.F‘Qand their forty silver sockets, two sockets under each board.K‘Pfor the north side of the tabernacle there shall be twenty boards,4‘O‚cYou shall make forty silver sockets under the twenty boards, two sockets under each board for its two dovetails, and two sockets under another board for its two dovetails;}‘NuYou shall make the boards for the tabernacle <n>[in the following quantities]</n>: twenty boards for the south side.‘MMake two dovetails in each board for fitting <n>[them]</n> together; you shall do the same for all the tabernacle boards.q‘L]The length of each board shall be ten cubits and the width of each board shall be one and a half cubits.~‘Kw"Then you shall make the boards for the tabernacle of acacia wood, standing upright <n>[as a trellis-like frame]</n>.$‘J‚CYou shall make a <i>third</i> covering for the tent of rams'<f>[1]</f> skins dyed red, and a <i>fourth</i> covering above that of<f>[2]</f> porpoise skins.Q‘Iƒ
The cubit on one side and the cubit on the other, of what is left over in the length of the curtains of the tent shall lap over the sides of the tabernacle on one side and the other side, to cover it.‘H‚'The overlapping part that is left over from the tent curtains, the half curtain that is left over, shall lap over the back of the tabernacle.‘G‚<pb/>"You shall make fifty bronze hooks and put the hooks into the loops and join the tent together so that it may be one unit.&‘F‚G
Make fifty loops on the edge of the outermost curtain in the <i>first</i> set, and fifty loops on the edge of the <i>outermost</i> curtain in the second set.I‘Eƒ
	You shall join five curtains by themselves and the <i>other</i> six curtains by themselves, and you shall double over the sixth curtain at the front of the tent <n>[to make a closed door]</n>.w‘DiEach curtain shall be thirty cubits long and four cubits wide. The eleven curtains shall all measure the same.‘C‚#"Then you shall make <n>[exterior]</n> curtains of goats' <i>hair</i> as a tent over the tabernacle. You shall make eleven curtains in all. ‘B{You shall make fifty gold hooks, and fasten the curtains together with the hooks; and the tabernacle shall be one unit.H‘AƒYou shall make fifty loops on the one curtain, and fifty loops on the edge of the <i>last</i> curtain that is in the second set. The loops on one curtain correspond to the loops on the other.‘@‚You shall make loops of blue on the <i>outer</i> edge of the last curtain in the <i>first</i> set, and likewise in the second set.}‘?uThe five curtains shall be joined to one another, and <i>the other</i> five curtains shall be joined to one another.‘>‚+The length of each curtain shall be twenty-eight cubits, and the width of each curtain four cubits; all of the curtains shall measure the same.‚
‘=	„<pb/>"Moreover, you shall make the tabernacle (sacred tent of worship) with ten <n>[interior]</n> curtains of fine twisted linen, and blue, purple, and scarlet fabric; you shall make them with <n>[embroidered]</n> cherubim, the handwork of a skillful craftsman.l‘<S(See that you make them <n>[exactly]</n> after their pattern which was shown to you on the mountain.`‘;;'It shall be made from a talent (50-80 lbs.) of pure gold, including all these utensils.;‘:s&Its snuffers and their trays shall be of pure gold.t‘9ƒc%Then you shall make the lamps <n>[of the lampstand]</n><f>[11]</f> seven <i>in number</i> <n>[with one lamp at the top of the shaft]</n>. The priests shall set up its <i>seven</i> lamps so that they will light the space in front of it.‘8‚$Their calyxes and their branches <i>shall be of one piece</i> with it; all of it shall be one piece of hammered work of pure gold.‚‘7„#A calyx shall be under the <i>first</i> pair of branches coming out of it, and a calyx under the <i>second</i> pair of branches coming out of it, and a calyx under the <i>third</i> pair of branches coming out of it, for the six branches coming out of the lampstand.&‘6‚G"and in the <n>[center shaft of the]</n> lampstand <n>[you shall make]</n> four cups shaped like almond <i>blossoms</i>, with their calyxes and their flowers.‚‘5ƒ}!Three cups shall be made like almond <i>blossoms</i>, each with a calyx and a flower on one branch, and three cups made like almond <i>blossoms</i> on the other branch with a calyx and a flowerâ€”so for the six branches coming out of the lampstand;M‘4ƒ Six branches shall come out of its sides; three branches of the lampstand out of the one side and three branches of the lampstand out of its other side <n>[the shaft being the seventh branch]</n>.`‘3ƒ;"You shall make a lampstand of pure gold. The<f>[9]</f> lampstand and its base and its shaft shall be made of hammered work; its cups, its<f>[10]</f> calyxes and its flowers shall be <i>all of one piece</i> with it.k‘2QYou shall set the bread of the<f>[8]</f> Presence (showbread) on the table before Me at all times.=‘1‚uYou shall make its plates <n>[for the showbread]</n> and its cups <n>[for incense]</n> and its pitchers and bowls for sacrificial drink offerings; you shall make them of pure gold.x‘0kYou shall make the poles of acacia wood and overlay them with gold, so that the table may be carried with them.^‘/7The rings shall be close against the rim as holders for the poles to carry the table.u‘.eYou shall make four gold rings for it and fasten them at the four corners that are on the table's four legs.l‘-SYou shall make a rim of a hand width around it; you shall make a gold border for the rim around it.\‘,3You shall overlay it with pure gold and make a border of gold around the top of it.q‘+]"You shall make a table of acacia wood, two cubits long, one cubit wide, and one and a half cubits high.s‘*ƒaThere I will meet with you; from above the mercy seat, from between the two cherubim which are on the ark of the Testimony, I will speak <n>[intimately]</n> with you regarding every commandment that I will give you for the Israelites.~‘)wYou shall put the mercy seat on the top of the ark, and in the ark you shall put the Testimony which I will give you.N‘(ƒThe cherubim shall have their wings spread upward, covering the mercy seat with their wings and facing each other. The faces of the cherubim are to be <i>looking downward</i> toward the mercy seat.r‘'_Make one cherub at each end, making the cherubim <i>of one piece</i> with the mercy seat at its two ends. ‘&{You shall make two cherubim (winged angelic figures) of <n>[solid]</n> hammered gold at the two ends of the mercy seat.‘%‚<pb/>"You shall make a<f>[7]</f> mercy seat (cover) of pure gold, two and a half cubits long and one and a half cubits wide.[‘$1You shall put into the ark the Testimony (Ten Commandments) which I will give you.‘#‚The poles shall remain in the rings of the ark; they shall not be removed from it <n>[so that the ark itself need not be touched]</n>.[‘"1and put the poles through the rings on the sides of the ark, by which to carry it.Z‘!/
You shall make <n>[carrying]</n> poles of acacia wood and overlay them with gold,j‘ OYou shall cast four gold rings for it and attach them to the four feet, two rings on either side.‘‚You shall overlay the ark with pure gold, overlay it inside and out, and you shall make a gold border (frame) around its top.‘‚
"They shall make an ark of acacia wood two and a half<f>[6]</f> cubits long, one and a half cubits wide, and one and a half cubits high.!‘‚=	You shall construct it in accordance with everything that I am going to show you, as the pattern of the tabernacle and the pattern of all its furniture.L‘Have them build a sanctuary for Me, so that I may dwell among them.k‘Q<f>[5]</f> onyx stones and setting stones for the <n>[priest's]</n> ephod and for the breastpiece.n‘W<n>[olive]</n> oil for lighting,<f>[4]</f> balsam for the anointing oil and for the fragrant incense,Y‘-rams'<f>[1]</f> skins dyed red,<f>[2]</f> porpoise skins,<f>[3]</f> acacia wood,R‘blue, purple, and scarlet fabric, fine twisted linen, goats' <i>hair</i>,U‘%This is the offering you are to receive from them: gold, silver, and bronze,‘‚5"Tell the children of Israel to take an offering for Me. From every man whose heart moves him <n>[to give willingly]</n> you shall take My offering.1‘	a<pb/>Then the LORD spoke to Moses, saying,‘yMoses entered the midst of the cloud and went up the mountain; and he was on the mountain forty days and forty nights.‘‚-In the sight of the Israelites the appearance of the glory <i>and</i> brilliance of the LORD was like consuming fire on the top of the mountain.6‘‚gThe glory <i>and</i> brilliance of the LORD rested on Mount Sinai, and the cloud covered it for six days. On the seventh day God called to Moses from the midst of the cloud.P‘Then Moses went up to the mountain, and the cloud covered the mountain.+‘‚QAnd he said to the elders, "Wait here for us until we come back to you. Remember that Aaron and Hur are with you; whoever has a legal matter, let him go to them."c‘A
So Moses<f>[1]</f> arose with Joshua his attendant, and he went up to the mountain of God.M‘ƒ<pb/>Now the LORD said to Moses, "Come up to Me on the mountain and stay there, and I will give you the stone tablets with the law and the commandments which I have written for their instruction."%‘
‚EYet He did not stretch out His hand against the nobles of the Israelites; and they saw <n>[the manifestation of the presence of]</n> God, and ate and drank.%‘‚E
and they saw <n>[a manifestation of]</n> the God of Israel; and under His feet there appeared to be a pavement of sapphire, just as clear as the sky itself.y‘m	<pb/>Then Moses, Aaron, Nadab, and Abihu, and seventy of the elders of Israel went up <n>[the mountainside]</n>,f‘
ƒGSo Moses took the blood <n>[which had been placed in the large basins]</n> and sprinkled it on the people, and said, "Behold the blood of the covenant, which the LORD has made with you in accordance with all these words."'‘	‚IThen he took the Book of the Covenant and read it aloud to the people; and they said, "Everything that the LORD has said we will do, and we will be obedient."‘‚Moses took half of the blood and put it in large basins, and <n>[the other]</n> half of the blood he sprinkled on the altar.‘‚Then he sent young Israelite men, and they offered burnt offerings and sacrificed young bulls as peace offerings to the LORD.m‘ƒUMoses wrote down all the words of the LORD. Then he got up early in the morning, and built an altar <n>[for worship]</n> at the foot of the mountain with twelve pillars (memorial stones) representing the twelve tribes of Israel.B‘‚<pb/>Then Moses came and told the people everything that the LORD had said and all the ordinances. And all the people answered with one voice, "Everything the LORD has said we will do."y‘mMoses alone shall approach the LORD, but the others shall not come near, nor shall the people come up with him."B‘	ƒ<pb/>Then God said to Moses, "Come up to the LORD, you and Aaron, Nadab and Abihu (Aaron's older sons), and seventy of Israel's elders, and you shall worship at a <n>[safe]</n> distance.6‘‚g!They shall not live in your land, because they will make you sin against Me; for if you serve their gods, it is certain to be a trap for you <n>[resulting in judgment]</n>."D‘ You shall not make a covenant with them or with their gods.‚‘ ƒ}I will establish your borders from the Red Sea to the Sea of the Philistines (the Mediterranean), and from the wilderness to the River <i>Euphrates</i>; for I will hand over the residents of the land to you, and you shall drive them out before you.
‚I will drive them out before you little by little, until you have increased and are strong enough to take possession of the land.Z~ƒ/I will not drive them out before you in a single year, so that the land does not become desolate <n>[due to lack of attention]</n> and the <n>[wild]</n> animals of the field do not become too numerous for you.v}gI will send hornets ahead of you which shall drive out the Hivite, the Canaanite, and the Hittite before you.=|‚uI will send My terror ahead of you, and I will throw into confusion all the people among whom you come, and I will make all your enemies turn their backs to you <n>[in flight]</n>.u{eNo one shall suffer miscarriage or be barren in your land;<f>[5]</f> I will fulfill the number of your days.z‚You shall serve <n>[only]</n> the LORD your God, and He shall bless your bread and water. I will also remove sickness from among you.‚yƒ}You shall not bow down to worship their gods, nor serve them, nor do <n>[anything]</n> in accordance with their practices. You shall completely overthrow them and break down their <n>[sacred]</n> pillars <i>and</i> images <n>[of pagan worship]</n>.Zxƒ/When My Angel goes before you and brings you to <n>[the land of]</n> the Amorite, the Hittite, the Perizzite, the Canaanite, the Hivite, and the Jebusite, I will reject them <i>and</i> completely destroy them.5w‚eBut if you will indeed listen to <i>and</i> truly obey His voice and do everything that I say, then I will be an enemy to your enemies and an adversary to your adversaries.QvƒBe on your guard before Him, listen to <i>and</i> obey His voice; do not be rebellious toward Him <i>or</i> provoke Him, for He will not pardon your transgression, since My Name (authority) is in Him.u‚-"Behold, I am going to send an<f>[4]</f> Angel before you to keep <i>and</i> guard you on the way and to bring you to the place I have prepared.7t‚i<pb/><t>"You shall bring the choice first fruits of your ground into the house of the LORD your God. </t><br/>"You shall not<f>[3]</f> boil a young goat in its mother's milk.s‚7<pb/>"You shall not offer the blood of My sacrifice with<f>[2]</f> leavened bread; and the fat of My feast is not to be left overnight until morning.Vr'Three times a year all your males shall appear before the<f>[1]</f> Lord GOD.‚Nq…Also <n>[you shall observe]</n> the Feast of Harvest (Weeks, Pentecost, or First Fruits), acknowledging the first fruits of your labor, of what you sow in the field. And <n>[third]</n> the Feast of Ingathering (Booths or Tabernacles) at the end of the year when you gather in <n>[the fruit of]</n> your labors from the field.‚(p„KYou shall observe the Feast of Unleavened Bread; for seven days you shall eat unleavened bread, as I commanded you, at the appointed time in the month of Abib, for in it you came out of Egypt. No one shall appear before Me empty-handed <n>[but you shall bring sacrificial offerings]</n>.Ro"Three times a year you shall celebrate a feast <n>[dedicated]</n> to Me.bnƒ?
Now concerning everything which I have said to you, be on your guard; do not mention the name of other gods <n>[either in a blessing or in a curse]</n>; do not let such speech be heard <n>[coming]</n> from your mouth.‚
m„<pb/>"Six days <n>[each week]</n> you shall do your work, but on the seventh day you shall stop <n>[working]</n> so that your ox and your donkey may settle down <i>and</i> rest, and the son of your female servant, as well as your stranger, may be refreshed.‚l„but the seventh year you shall let it rest and lie uncultivated, so that the poor among your people may eat <n>[what the land grows naturally]</n>; whatever they leave the animals of the field may eat. You shall do the same with your vineyard and olive grove.Ak
"You shall sow your land six years and harvest its yield,$j‚C	<pb/>"You shall not oppress a stranger, for you know the soul <n>[the feelings, thoughts, and concerns]</n> of a stranger, for you were strangers in Egypt.i‚!<pb/>"You shall not accept a bribe, for a bribe blinds the clear-sighted and subverts the testimony <i>and</i> the cause of the righteous.,h‚SKeep far away from a false charge <i>or</i> action, and do not condemn to death the innocent or the righteous, for I will not justify <i>and</i> acquit the guilty.Xg+<pb/>"You shall not pervert (bend) the justice due to your poor in his dispute.MfƒIf you see the donkey of one who hates you lying helpless under its load, you shall not leave the man to deal with it <n>[alone]</n>; you must help him release the animal <n>[from its burden]</n>.feG<pb/>"If you meet your enemy's ox or his donkey wandering off, you must bring it back to him.rd_nor shall you favor <i>or</i> be partial to a poor man in his dispute <n>[simply because he is poor]</n>.;c‚qYou shall not follow a crowd to do <n>[something]</n> evil, nor shall you testify at a trial <i>or</i> in a dispute so as to side with a crowd in order to pervert <i>justice</i>;b	‚1<pb/>"You shall not give a false report; you shall not join hands with the wicked to be a malicious witness <n>[promoting wrong and violence]</n>.Paƒ<pb/>"You shall be<f>[1]</f> holy men to Me; therefore you shall not eat meat <n>[from any animal]</n> that has been torn to pieces <n>[by predators]</n> in the field; you shall throw it to the dogs.8`‚kYou shall do the same with your oxen <i>and</i> with your sheep. It shall be with its mother for seven days; on the eighth day you shall give it <n>[as an offering]</n> to Me."_‚?<pb/>"You shall not delay <i>the offering from</i> your harvest and your vintage. You shall give (consecrate, dedicate) to Me the firstborn of your sons.s^a<pb/>"You shall not curse God, nor curse the ruler of your people <n>[since he administers God's law]</n>.H]ƒfor that is his only covering; it is his clothing for his body. In what shall he sleep? And when he cries to Me <n>[for help]</n>, I will hear him, for I am compassionate <i>and</i> gracious.p\[If you ever take your <n>[poor]</n> neighbor's robe in pledge, you must return it to him before sunset,4[‚c<pb/>"If you lend money to any one of My people with you who is poor, you shall not act as a creditor (professional moneylender) to him; you shall not charge him interest.Z‚+and My wrath shall be kindled <i>and</i> burn; I will kill you with the sword, and your wives shall become widows and your children fatherless.
Y‚If you harm <i>or</i> oppress them in any way, and they cry at all to Me <n>[for help]</n>, I will most certainly hear their cry;LXYou shall not harm <i>or</i> oppress any widow or fatherless child.jWO<pb/>"You shall not wrong a stranger or oppress him, for you were strangers in the land of Egypt.
V‚<pb/>"He who sacrifices to any god, other than to the LORD alone, shall be put under a ban (designated) for destruction (execution).?U{<pb/>"Whoever lies with an animal must be put to death.IT
<pb/>"You shall not allow a woman who practices sorcery to live.SIf her father absolutely refuses to give her to him, he must <n>[still]</n> pay money equivalent to the dowry of virgins.	R‚
"If a man seduces a virgin who is not betrothed, and lies with her, he must pay a dowry (marriage price) for her to be his wife.9Q‚mBut if the owner is with it <n>[when the damage is done]</n>, the borrower shall not make restitution. If it was hired, the damage is included in <n>[the price of]</n> its fee.&P‚G<pb/>"And if a man borrows an animal from his neighbor, and it gets injured or dies while its owner is not with it, the borrower shall make full restitution.4O‚c
If it is torn to pieces <n>[by some predator or by accident]</n>, let him bring the mangled carcass as evidence; he shall not make restitution for what was torn to pieces.qN]But if it is actually stolen from him <n>[when in his care]</n>, he shall make restitution to its owner.HMƒthen an oath before the LORD shall be made by the two of them that he has not taken his neighbor's property; and the owner of it shall accept his word and not require him to make restitution.?L‚y
<pb/>"If a man gives his neighbor a donkey or an ox or a sheep or any <n>[other]</n> animal to keep <n>[for him]</n>, and it dies or is injured or taken away while no one is looking,‚<K„s	For every offense involving property, whether it concerns ox, donkey, sheep, clothing, or any piece of lost property, which another identifies as his, the case of both parties shall come before the judges <n>[who act in God's name]</n>. Whomever the judges pronounce guilty shall pay double to his neighbor.:J‚oIf the thief is not caught, the owner of the house shall appear before the judges <n>[who act in God's name]</n>, to determine whether or not he had stolen his neighbor's goods.aIƒ=<pb/>"If a man gives his neighbor money or <n>[other]</n> goods to keep <n>[for him while he is away]</n> and it is stolen from the neighbor's house, then, if the thief is caught, he shall pay double <n>[for it]</n>.8H‚k<pb/>"If fire breaks out and spreads to thorn bushes so that the stacked grain or standing grain or the field is consumed, he who started the fire shall make full restitution.eGƒE<pb/>"If a man causes a field or vineyard to be grazed bare or lets his livestock loose so that it grazes in another man's field, he shall make restitution from the best of his own field and the best of his own vineyard.F‚If the animal that he stole is found alive in his possession, whether it is ox or donkey or sheep, he shall pay double <n>[for it]</n>.hEƒKBut if the sun has risen, there will be bloodguilt for him. The thief <n>[if he lives]</n> must make <n>[full]</n> restitution; if he has nothing, then he shall be sold <n>[as a slave to make restitution]</n> for his theft.D‚7<pb/>"If a thief is caught breaking in <n>[after dark]</n> and is struck <n>[by the owner]</n> so that he dies, there shall be no bloodguilt for him.C	‚<pb/>"If a man steals an ox or sheep and kills or sells it, he shall make restitution of five oxen for an ox or four sheep for a sheep.GBƒ	$Or if it is known that the ox was previously in the habit of goring, and its owner has not kept it confined, he must make restitution of ox for ox, and the dead <n>[animal]</n> shall be his.=A‚u#<pb/>"If one man's ox injures another's so that it dies, then they shall sell the live ox and divide the proceeds equally; they shall also divide the dead ox <n>[between them]</n>.@‚"the owner of the pit shall make restitution; he shall give money to the animal's owner, but the dead <n>[animal]</n> shall be his.t?c!<pb/>"If a man leaves a pit open, or digs a pit and does not cover it, and an ox or a donkey falls into it,?>‚y If the ox gores a male or a female servant, the owner shall give to the servant's master thirty shekels of silver <n>[the purchase price for a slave]</n>, and the ox shall be stoned.k=QIf the ox has gored another's son or daughter, he shall be dealt with according to this same rule.<‚If a ransom is demanded of him <n>[in return for his life]</n>, then he shall give whatever is demanded for the redemption of his life._;ƒ9But if the ox has tried to gore on a previous occasion, and its owner has been warned, but has not kept it confined and it kills a man or a woman, the ox shall be stoned and its owner shall be put to death as well.4:‚c<pb/>"If an ox gores a man or a woman to death, the ox must be stoned and its meat shall not be eaten; but the owner of the ox shall be cleared <n>[of responsibility]</n>.
