R	ãó*ñö¢7œ8TÈ2Xæ                                                                                      o“FY"Then the LORD descended in the cloud and stood there with Moses as he proclaimed the Name of the LORD.V“Eƒ'"So Moses cut two tablets of stone like the first ones, and he got up early in the morning and went up on Mount Sinai, as the LORD had commanded him, and took<f>[1]</f> the two tablets of stone in his hand.“D‚"No man is to come up with you, nor let any man be seen anywhere on the mountain; nor let flocks or herds feed in front of that mountain."“C‚"So be ready by morning, and come up in the morning to Mount Sinai, and present yourself there to Me on the top of the mountain.`“B	ƒ="<pb/>Then the LORD said to Moses, "Cut two tablets of stone like the first, and I will write on these tablets the words that were on the first tablets which you smashed <n>[when you learned of Israel's idolatry]</n>.a“A=!Then I will take away My hand and you shall see My back; but My face shall not be seen."“@‚)!and while My glory is passing by, I will put you in a cleft of the rock and <i>protectively</i> cover you with My hand until I have passed by.h“?K!Then the LORD said, "Behold, there is a place beside Me, and you shall stand there on the rock;Q“>!But He said, "You cannot see My face, for no man shall see Me and live!"w“=ƒi!And God said, "I will make all My goodness pass before you, and I will proclaim the Name of the LORD before you; for I will be gracious to whom I will be gracious, and will show compassion (lovingkindness) on whom I will show compassion."6“<i!Then Moses said, "Please, show me Your glory!"E“;ƒ!<pb/>The LORD said to Moses, "I will also do this thing that you have asked; for you have found favor (lovingkindness, mercy) in My sight and I have known you <n>[personally]</n> by name."l“:ƒS!For how then can it be known that Your people and I have found favor in Your sight? Is it not by Your going with us, so that we are distinguished, Your people and I, from all the <n>[other]</n> people on the face of the earth?"l“9S!And Moses said to Him, "If Your presence does not go <n>[with me]</n>, do not lead us up from here.“8‚3!And the LORD said, "My presence shall go <i>with you</i>, and I will give you rest <n>[by bringing you and the people into the promised land]</n>."‚[“7…1!
Now therefore, I pray you, if I have found favor in Your sight, let me know Your ways so that I may know You <n>[becoming more deeply and intimately acquainted with You, recognizing and understanding Your ways more clearly]</n> and that I may find grace <i>and</i> favor in Your sight. And consider also, that this nation is Your people."Z“6ƒ/!Moses said to the LORD, "See, You say to me, 'Bring up this people,' but You have not let me know whom You will send with me. Yet You have said, 'I know you by name, and you have also found favor in My sight.'U“5ƒ%!And so the LORD used to speak to Moses face to face, just as a man speaks to his friend. When Moses returned to the camp, his attendant Joshua, the son of Nun, a young man, would not depart from the tent.
“4‚!
When all the people saw the pillar of cloud standing at the tent door, all the people would rise and worship, each at his tent door.“3‚'!	Whenever Moses entered the tent, the pillar of cloud would descend and stand at the doorway of the tent; and the LORD would speak with Moses.“2‚'!Whenever Moses went out to the tent, all the people would rise and stand, each at his tent door, and look at Moses until he entered the tent.‚&“1„G!<pb/>Now Moses used to take his own tent and pitch it outside the camp, far away from the camp, and he called it the tent of meeting <n>[of God with His own people]</n>. And everyone who sought the LORD would go out to the <n>[temporary]</n> tent of meeting which was outside the camp.w“0i!So the Israelites left off all their ornaments <n>[in repentance]</n>, from Mount Horeb (Sinai) <i>onward</i>.‚!“/„=!For the LORD had said to Moses, "Say to the sons of Israel, 'You are a stiff-necked (stubborn, rebellious) people! If I should come among you for one moment, I would destroy you. Now therefore, <n>[penitently]</n> take off your ornaments, so that I may know what to do with you.'"g“.I!<pb/>When the people heard this sad word, they mourned, and none of them put on his ornaments.\“-ƒ3!Go up to a land <n>[of abundance]</n><f>[1]</f> flowing with milk and honey; for I will not go up in your midst, because you are a stiff-necked (stubborn, rebellious) people, and I might destroy you on the way."“,‚!!I will send an Angel before you and I will drive out the Canaanite, the Amorite, the Hittite, the Perizzite, the Hivite, and the Jebusite.w“+	ƒk!<pb/>The LORD spoke to Moses, saying, "Depart, go up from here, you and the people whom you have brought from the land of Egypt, to the land which I swore to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob (Israel), saying, 'To your descendants I will give it.'	“*‚
 #So the LORD struck the people with a plague, because of what they had done with the calf which Aaron had made <n>[for them]</n>.L“)ƒ "But now go, lead the people <n>[to the place]</n> where I have told you. Behold, My<f>[3]</f> Angel shall go before you; nevertheless, in the day when I punish, I will punish them for their sin!"u“(e !But the LORD said to Moses, "Whoever has sinned against Me, I will blot him out of My book <n>[not you]</n>.“'}  Yet now, if You will, forgive their sinâ€”and if not, please blot me out of Your book which You have written (kill me)!"“&‚3 So Moses returned to the LORD, and said, "Oh, these people have committed a great sin <n>[against You]</n>, and have made themselves a god of gold.$“%‚C <pb/>Then the next day Moses said to the people, "You have committed a great sin. Now I will go up to the LORD; perhaps I can make atonement for your sin."‚“$„ Then Moses said <n>[to the Levites]</n>, "Dedicate yourselves today to the LORDâ€”for each man has been against his own son and his own brother <n>[in his attempt to escape execution]</n>â€”so that He may restore <i>and</i> bestow His blessing on you this day."	“#‚
 So the sons of Levi did as Moses instructed, and about three thousand men of the people <n>[of Israel]</n> were killed that day.‚!“"„= He said to them, "Thus says the LORD God of Israel, 'Every man strap his sword on his thigh and go back and forth from gate to gate throughout the camp, and every man kill his brother, and every man his friend, and every man his neighbor <n>[all who continue pagan worship]</n>.'"?“!‚y then Moses stood in the gate of the camp, and said, "Whoever is on the <e>Lord's</e> side, come to me!" And all the sons of Levi <n>[the priestly tribe]</n> gathered together to him.2“ ‚_ <pb/>Now when Moses saw that the people were out of controlâ€”for Aaron had let them get out of control to the point of being an object of mockery among their enemiesâ€”“‚5 I said to them, 'Let whoever has gold <n>[jewelry]</n>, take it off.' So they gave it to me; then I threw it into the fire, and out came this calf."/“‚Y For they said to me, 'Make us a god who will go before us; as for this Moses, the man who brought us out of the land of Egypt, we do not know what has become of him.'v“g Aaron said, "Do not let the anger of my lord burn; you know the people yourself, that they are prone to evil.w“i <pb/>Then Moses said to Aaron, "What did this people do to you, that you have brought so great a sin on them?"2“‚_ Then Moses took the calf they had made and burned it in the fire, and ground it to powder, and scattered it on the surface of the water and made the Israelites drink it.>“‚w And as soon as he approached the camp and he saw the calf and the dancing, Moses' anger burned; and he threw the tablets from his hands and smashed them at the foot of the mountain.6“‚g <t>But Moses said, </t><br/><t>"It is not the sound of the cry of victory, </t><t>Nor is it the sound of the cry of defeat; </t><t>But I hear the sound of singing."</t><br/>“‚ Now when Joshua heard the noise of the people as they shouted, he said to Moses, "There is a sound of battle in the camp."f“G The tablets were the work of God; the writing was the writing of God engraved on the tablets.L“ƒ <pb/>Then Moses turned and went down from the mountain with the two tablets of the Testimony in his hand; tablets that were written on both sidesâ€”they were written on one side and on the other.a“= So the LORD changed His mind about the harm which He had said He would do to His people.‚3“„a 
Remember Abraham, Isaac, and Israel (Jacob), Your servants to whom You swore <n>[an oath]</n> by Yourself, and said to them, 'I will multiply your descendants as the stars of the heavens, and all this land of which I have spoken I will give to your descendants, and they shall inherit it forever.'"z“ƒo Why should the Egyptians say, 'With evil <n>[intent]</n> their God brought them out to kill them in the mountains and destroy them from the face of the earth'? Turn away from Your burning anger and change Your mind about harming Your people.S“ƒ! But Moses appeased <i>and</i> entreated the LORD his God, and said, "LORD, why does Your anger burn against Your people whom You have brought out of the land of Egypt with great power and a mighty hand?@“‚{ 
Now therefore, let Me alone <i>and</i> do not interfere, so that My anger may burn against them and that I may destroy them; and I will make of you (your descendants) a great nation."}“u 	The LORD said to Moses, "I have seen this people, and behold, they are a stiff-necked (stubborn, rebellious) people.u“ƒe They have quickly turned aside from the way which I commanded them. They have made themselves a molten calf, and have worshiped it and sacrificed to it, and said, 'This is your god, O Israel, who brought you up from the land of Egypt!'"“‚# <pb/>Then the LORD said to Moses, "Go down at once, for your people, whom you brought up from the land of Egypt, have corrupted themselves.Q“
ƒ So they got up early the next day and offered burnt offerings, and brought peace offerings; then the people sat down to eat and drink, and got up to play <n>[shamefullyâ€”without moral restraint]</n>.“‚1 Now when Aaron saw the molten calf, he built an altar before it; and Aaron made a proclamation, and said, "Tomorrow shall be a feast to the LORD!"`“ƒ; And he took <i>the gold</i> from their hands, and fashioned it with an engraving tool and made it into a molten<f>[2]</f> calf; and they said, "This is your god, O Israel, who brought you up from the land of Egypt."e“
E So all the people took off the gold rings that were in their ears and brought them to Aaron.“	‚ So Aaron replied to them, "Take off the gold rings that are in the ears of your wives, your sons and daughters, and bring them to me."‚.“	„Y <pb/>Now when the people saw that Moses delayed coming down from the mountain, they gathered together before Aaron and said to him, "Come, make us a<f>[1]</f> god who will go before us; as for this Moses, the man who brought us up from the land of Egypt, we do not know what has become of him."&“‚G<pb/>When He had finished speaking with him on Mount Sinai, He gave Moses the two tablets of the Testimony, tablets of stone, written with the finger of God.*“‚OIt is a sign between Me and the Israelites forever; for in six days the LORD made the heavens and the earth, and on the seventh day He ceased and was refreshed."“‚So the Israelites shall observe the Sabbath, to celebrate the Sabbath throughout their generations as a perpetual covenant.''“‚IFor six days work may be done, but the seventh is the Sabbath of complete rest, sacred to the LORD; whoever does work on the Sabbath day must be put to death.‚“„Therefore, you shall keep the Sabbath, for it is holy to you. Everyone who profanes it must be put to death; for whoever does work on the Sabbath, that person (soul) shall be cut off from among his people <n>[excluding him from the atonement made for them]</n>.‚<“„s
"But as for you, say to the Israelites, 'You shall most certainly observe My Sabbaths, for it is a sign between Me and you throughout your generations, so that you may know <n>[without any doubt]</n> <i>and</i> acknowledge that I am the LORD who sanctifies you <i>and</i> sets you apart <n>[for Myself]</n>.#“CAnd the LORD said to Moses,&“ ‚Gand the anointing oil and the sweet <i>and</i> fragrant incense for the Holy Place. They are to make <i>them</i> according to all that I have commanded you."’‚
the finely worked garments, the holy garments for Aaron the <n>[high]</n> priest and the garments for his sons to minister as priests,e’~E	the <n>[bronze]</n> altar of burnt offering with all its utensils, the basin and its baseâ€”’}‚)the table <n>[for the bread]</n> and its utensils, the pure <i>gold</i> lampstand with all its utensils, the <n>[golden]</n> altar of incense,z’|othe Tent of Meeting, the ark of the Testimony, the mercy seat that is upon it, all the furnishings of the tentâ€”Y’{ƒ-And behold, I Myself have appointed with him Oholiab, son of Ahisamach, of the tribe of Dan; to all who are wise-hearted I have given the skill <i>and</i> ability to make everything that I have commanded you:v’zgand in the cutting of stones for settings, and in the carving of wood, to work in all kinds of craftsmanship.M’yto make artistic designs for work in gold, in silver, and in bronze,(’x‚KI have filled him with the Spirit of God in wisdom <i>and</i> skill, in understanding <i>and</i> intelligence, in knowledge, and in all kinds of craftsmanship,`’w;"See, I have called by name Bezalel, son of Uri, the son of Hur, of the tribe of Judah.'’v	M<pb/>Now the LORD said to Moses,’u‚&Whoever makes any like it, to use as perfume shall be cut off from his people <n>[excluding him from the atonement made for them]</n>."’t‚%The incense which you shall make, you shall not make in the same proportions for yourselves; it shall be holy to you for the LORD.H’sƒ$You shall crush some of it <n>[into a]</n> very fine <n>[powder]</n>, and put some of it before the Testimony in the Tent of Meeting, where I will meet with you; it shall be most holy to you.^’r7#and make incense with it, a perfume, the work of a perfumer, salted, pure and sacred.O’qƒ"Then the LORD said to Moses, "Take sweet <i>and</i> fragrant spicesâ€”stacte, onycha, and galbanum, sweet <i>and</i> fragrant spices with pure frankincense; there shall be an equal amount of eachâ€”’p‚9!Whoever prepares any like it or puts any of it on a layman shall be cut off from his people <n>[excluding him from the atonement made for them]</n>.'"’o‚# It shall not be poured on anyone's body, nor shall you make any like it in the same composition. It is holy, and it shall be sacred to you.’n‚You shall say to the Israelites, 'This shall be a holy <i>and</i> sacred anointing oil, to Me <n>[alone]</n> throughout your generations.h’mKYou shall anoint Aaron and his sons, and consecrate them, that they may serve as priests to Me.w’liYou shall consecrate them, that they may be most holy; whatever touches them must be holy (set apart for God).r’k_and the altar of burnt offering with all its utensils, and the basin <n>[for cleansing]</n> and its base.’j}and the table <n>[for the bread]</n> and all its utensils, and the lampstand and its utensils, and the altar of incense,T’i#You shall anoint the Tent of Meeting with it, and the ark of the Testimony,’h‚You shall make of these a holy anointing oil, a perfume mixture, the work of a perfumer; it shall be a sacred anointing oil.w’giand five hundred <i>shekels</i> of cinnamon blossom according to the sanctuary shekel, and a hin of olive oil.G’fƒ	"Take for yourself the best spices: five hundred <i>shekels</i> of liquid myrrh, half as muchâ€”two hundred and fiftyâ€”of sweet-scented cinnamon, and two hundred and fifty of fragrant cane,)’eOMoreover, the LORD said to Moses,6’d‚gThey shall wash their hands and their feet, so that they will not die; it shall be a perpetual statute for them, for Aaron and his descendants throughout their generations."a’cƒ=When they enter the Tent of Meeting, they shall wash with water, so that they will not die. Also, when they approach the altar to minister, to burn an offering in the fire to the LORD <n>[they shall do the same]</n>.A’bAaron and his sons shall wash their hands and their feet.h’aƒK"You shall also make a basin of bronze, with a base of bronze, for washing. You shall put it <n>[outside in the court]</n> between the Tent of Meeting and the altar <n>[of burnt offering]</n>, and you shall put water in it.$’`EThen the LORD said to Moses,k’_ƒQYou shall take the atonement money from the Israelites and use it <n>[exclusively]</n> for the service of the Tent of Meeting, so that it may be a memorial for the Israelites before the LORD, to make atonement for yourselves."(’^‚KThe rich shall not give more and the poor shall not give less than half a shekel, when you give this contribution to the LORD to make atonement for yourselves.’]‚)Everyone who is counted, from twenty years old and over, <n>[as he joins those already counted]</n>, shall give this contribution to the LORD.a’\ƒ=
This is what everyone who is counted shall give <n>[as he joins those already counted]</n>: a half shekel, according to the sanctuary shekel (the shekel is twenty gerahs); a half shekel as a contribution to the LORD.9’[‚m"When you take the census of the Israelites, each one shall give a ransom for himself to the LORD when you count them, so that no plague will come on them when you number them.$’ZEThen the LORD said to Moses,c’YƒA
Once a year Aaron shall make atonement <n>[for sin]</n> on its horns. He shall make atonement on it with the blood of the sin offering of atonement once a year throughout your generations. It is most holy to the LORD."’X‚	You shall not offer any strange incense on this altar, or burnt offering or meal offering; you shall not pour out a drink offering on it.
’W‚When Aaron sets up the lamps at twilight, he shall burn incense, a perpetual incense before the LORD throughout your generations.’V‚Aaron shall burn sweet <i>and</i> fragrant incense on it; he shall burn it every morning when he trims <i>and</i> tends the lamps.m’UƒUYou shall put the altar of incense <n>[in the Holy Place]</n> in front <i>and</i> outside of the veil that screens the ark of the<f>[1]</f> Testimony, before the mercy seat that is over the Testimony, where I will meet with you.D’TYou shall make the poles of acacia wood overlaid with gold.+’S‚QYou shall make two gold rings under its molding, make them on the two side wallsâ€”on opposite sidesâ€”they shall be holders for the poles with which to carry it.’R‚You shall overlay it with pure gold, its top and its sides all around, and its horns; and you shall make a gold molding all around it.’Q‚	It shall be a cubit long and a cubit wide. It shall be square and it shall be two cubits high. Its horns of one piece with it.c’P	C<pb/>"You shall make an altar upon which to burn incense; you shall make it of acacia wood.S’Oƒ!.They shall know <n>[from personal experience]</n> <i>and</i> acknowledge that I am the LORD their God who brought them out of the land of Egypt so that I might dwell among them; I am the LORD their God.?’N{-I will dwell among the sons of Israel and be their God.’M‚-,I will sanctify the Tent of Meeting and the altar <n>[of burnt offering]</n>; also I will sanctify Aaron and his sons to serve as priests to Me.+’L‚Q+There I will meet with the Israelites, and the Tent of Meeting shall be<f>[5]</f> sanctified by My glory <n>[the<f>[6]</f> Shekinah, God's dwelling presence]</n>.6’K‚g*This will be a continual burnt offering throughout your generations at the doorway of the Tent of Meeting before the LORD, where I will meet with you, to speak to you there.n’JƒW)And the other lamb you shall offer at twilight, and do with it as with the grain offering of the morning and with the drink offering, for a sweet <i>and</i> soothing aroma <n>[to appease God]</n>, an offering by fire to the LORD.f’IƒG(and with the one lamb there shall be one-tenth of <i>a measure</i> of fine flour mixed with one-fourth of a hin of beaten <n>[olive]</n> oil, and one-fourth of a hin of wine for a drink offering <n>[to be poured out]</n>.Z’H/'One lamb you shall offer in the morning and the other lamb<f>[4]</f> at twilight;}’Gu&<pb/>"Now this is what you shall offer on the altar: two one year old lambs shall be offered each day, continuously.V’Fƒ'%For seven days you shall make atonement for the altar <n>[of burnt offering]</n> and consecrate it; then the altar shall be most holy. Whatever touches the altar must be holy (set apart for God's service).b’Eƒ?$You shall offer a bull every day as a sin offering for atonement. You shall cleanse the altar <i>from sin</i> when you make atonement for it, and you shall anoint it to consecrate it <n>[for God's sacred purpose]</n>.’D‚#<pb/>"So you shall do to Aaron and to his sons in accordance with all I have commanded you; during seven days you are to ordain them.’C‚1"And if any of the meat of ordination or the bread remains until morning, you shall burn it in the fire; it shall not be eaten, because it is holy.P’Bƒ!They shall eat those things by which atonement was made at their ordination and consecration; but a layman shall not eat them, because they are holy <n>[that is, set apart to the worship of God]</n>.}’Au Aaron and his sons shall eat the meat of the ram and the bread in the basket, at the doorway of the Tent of Meeting.U’@%"You shall take the ram of the ordination and boil its meat in a holy place.3’?‚aThat son who is <n>[high]</n> priest in his place shall put them on <n>[each day for]</n> seven days when he comes into the Tent of Meeting to minister in the Holy Place.o’>Y<pb/>"The holy garments of Aaron shall be for his sons after him, to be anointed and ordained in them.n’=ƒWIt shall be for Aaron and his sons as their due portion from the Israelites forever, for it is a<f>[3]</f> heave offering. It shall be a heave offering to the LORD from the Israelites from the sacrifices of their peace offerings.X’<ƒ+You shall consecrate the waved breast offering <n>[of the ram]</n> used in the ordination and the waved thigh offering of the priests' portion, since it is <n>[a contribution]</n> for Aaron and for his sons.%’;‚E<pb/>"Then you shall take the breast of the ram of Aaron's ordination, and wave it as a wave offering before the LORD; and it shall be your (Moses) portion.L’:ƒThen you shall take them from their hands, add them to the burnt offering, and burn them on the altar for a sweet <i>and</i> soothing aroma before the LORD; it is an offering by fire to the LORD.’9‚and you shall put all these in the hands of Aaron and his sons, and wave them as a<f>[2]</f> wave offering before the LORD.’8‚and one loaf of bread and one cake of oiled bread and one wafer out of the basket of the unleavened bread that is before the LORD;c’7ƒA<pb/>"You shall also take the fat of the ram, the fat tail, the fat that covers the intestines, the lobe of the liver, the two kidneys with the fat that is on them, and the right thigh; (for it is a ram of ordination),‚;’6„qThen you shall take some of the blood that is on the altar and some of the anointing oil, and sprinkle it on Aaron and his garments and on his sons and their garments. Now Aaron and his garments and his sons and their garments shall be consecrated (dedicated, made holy, declared sacred for God's purpose).‚*’5„OThen you shall kill the ram, and take some of its blood and put it on the tip of the right ears of Aaron and his sons and on the thumbs of their right hands and on the big toes of their right feet, and sprinkle the <n>[rest of the]</n> blood around on the altar <n>[of burnt offering]</n>.v’4g<pb/>"Then you shall take the other ram, and Aaron and his sons shall lay their hands on the head of the ram.%’3‚Eand you shall burn the whole ram on the altar. It is a burnt offering to the LORD: it is a sweet <i>and</i> soothing aroma, an offering by fire to the LORD.’2yThen you shall cut the ram into pieces, and wash its intestines and legs, and place them with its pieces and its head,~’1wthen you shall kill the ram and you shall take its blood and sprinkle it around the altar <n>[of burnt offering]</n>.w’0i<pb/>"And you shall take one of the rams, and Aaron and his sons shall lay their hands on the head of the ram;’/‚But the meat of the bull, its hide, and the contents of its intestines you shall burn in the fire outside the camp; it is a sin offering.3’.‚a
You shall take all the fat that covers the intestines and the lobe of the liver, and the two kidneys and the fat that is on them, and offer them up in smoke on the altar.W’-ƒ)And you shall take some of the blood of the bull and with your finger put it on the horns of the altar <n>[of burnt offering]</n>, and you shall pour out the remainder of the blood at the base of the altar.\’,3Then you shall kill the bull before the LORD by the doorway of the Tent of Meeting.’+‚
"Then you shall bring the bull before the Tent of Meeting, and Aaron and his sons shall<f>[1]</f> lay their hands on the bull's head.W’*ƒ)	And you shall wrap them with sashes, Aaron and his sons, and put the <n>[ornamental]</n> caps on them; and the priest's office shall be theirs by a perpetual statute. So you shall ordain Aaron and his sons.8’)mYou shall bring his sons and put tunics on them.V’('Then you shall take the anointing oil and pour it on his head and anoint him.W’')and you shall put the turban on his head and put the holy crown on the turban.<’&‚sThen you shall take the garments, and put on Aaron the tunic and the robe of the ephod and the ephod and the breastpiece, and wrap him with the skillfully woven sash of the ephod;’%‚Then bring Aaron and his sons to the doorway of the Tent of Meeting <n>[out where the basin is]</n> and wash them with water.o’$YYou shall put them in one basket, and present them in the basket along with the bull and the two rams.’#‚and unleavened bread and unleavened cakes mixed with oil, and unleavened wafers spread with oil; you shall make them of fine wheat flour.!’"	‚?<pb/>"This is what you shall do to consecrate Aaron and his sons so that they may serve Me as priests: take one young bull and two rams, without blemish,‚5’!„e+The various articles of clothing shall be on Aaron and on his sons when they enter the Tent of Meeting, or when they approach the altar <n>[of incense]</n> to minister in the Holy Place, so that they do not incur guilt and die. It shall be a statute forever to Aaron and to his descendants after him.’ ‚*You shall make for them <n>[white]</n> linen undergarments to cover their bare flesh, reaching from the waist to the thighs.A’‚})You shall put the various articles of clothing on Aaron your brother and on his sons with him, and shall anoint them and ordain and sanctify them, so that they may serve Me as priests.’‚(<pb/>"For Aaron's sons you shall make tunics and sashes and <n>[ornamental]</n> caps, for glory <i>and</i> honor and beauty.’‚5'<pb/>"You shall weave the tunic of checkered work of fine linen, and make a turban of fine linen. You shall make a sash, the work of an embroiderer.|’ƒs&It shall be on Aaron's forehead, and Aaron shall take away the guilt from the holy things which the sons of Israel dedicate, with regard to all their holy gifts. It shall always be on his forehead, so that they may be accepted before the LORD.I’
