ƒ'!They brought the tabernacle to Moses <n>[for him to inspect]</n>: the tent and all its furnishings, its hooks, its <n>[frame]</n> boards, its bars, its support poles, its sockets <i>or</i> bases;8•	‚k' <pb/>Thus all the work of the tabernacle of the Tent of Meeting was finished; and the Israelites did according to all that the LORD had commanded Moses; that is what they did.i•M'They tied a blue cord to it, to fasten it on the turban above, just as the LORD commanded Moses.•‚''<pb/>They made the plate of the holy crown of pure gold, and wrote on it an inscription, like the engravings of a signet, "Holy to the LORD."•‚'and the sash of fine twisted linen, and blue, purple, and scarlet fabric, the work of an embroiderer, just as the LORD commanded Moses. •{'and the turban of fine linen, and the ornamental caps of fine linen, and the linen undergarments of fine twisted linen,P•'<pb/>And they made tunics of finely woven linen for Aaron and his sons,3•‚a'a bell and a pomegranate, a bell and a pomegranate, all <n>[the way]</n> around the hem of the robe, for service <i>and</i> ministering, just as the LORD commanded Moses.r•_'They also made bells of pure gold, and put the bells between the pomegranates around the hem of the robe;s•a'On the hem of the robe they made pomegranates of blue, purple, and scarlet fabric, and fine twisted linen.8• ‚k'there was an opening <n>[for the head]</n> in the middle of the robe, like the opening in a coat of armor, with a hem around it, so that it would not be frayed <i>or</i> torn.Q”'<pb/>Then Bezalel made the robe of the ephod of woven work, all of blue;t”~ƒc'They bound the breastpiece by its rings to the rings of the ephod with a blue cord, so that it would lie on the woven sash of the ephod, and so that the breastpiece would not come loose from the ephod, just as the LORD commanded Moses.d”}ƒC'Furthermore, they made two <n>[other]</n> gold rings and attached them to the bottom of the two shoulder pieces of the ephod, at the front of it, close to the place where it is joined, above the woven sash of the ephod.”|‚'They made two rings of gold and put them on the two ends of the breastpiece, on its inner edge which was next to the ephod.!”{‚='They put the <n>[other]</n> two ends of the two cords on the two filigree settings, and put them on the shoulder pieces of the ephod at the front of it.e”zE'Then they put the two twisted cords of gold in the two rings on the ends of the breastpiece.~”yw'They made two settings of gold filigree and two gold rings, and put the two rings on the two ends of the breastpiece.Q”x'And they made on the breastpiece twisted chains like cords of pure gold.G”wƒ	'The stones corresponded to the names of the sons of Israel; they were twelve <n>[in all]</n>, corresponding to their names, engraved like a signet, each with its name, for the twelve tribes.l”vS'
and the fourth row, a beryl, an onyx, and a jasper; they were mounted in settings of gold filigree.@”u}'and the third row, a jacinth, an agate, and an amethyst;D”t'and the second row, a turquoise, a sapphire, and a diamond;i”sM'
And they mounted four rows of stones on it. The first row was a row of ruby, topaz, and emerald;”r‚'	It was square; they made the breastpiece folded double, a <n>[hand's]</n> span long and a <n>[hand's]</n> span wide when folded double.9”q‚m'<pb/>Bezalel made the breastpiece, the work of a skillful craftsman, like the workmanship of the ephod: of gold and of blue, purple, and scarlet fabric, and fine twisted linen.(”p‚K'And he put them on the shoulder pieces of the ephod to be memorial stones (a remembrance) for the sons of Israel (Jacob), just as the LORD had commanded Moses.:”o‚o'<pb/>They made the onyx stones, set in settings of gold filigree; they were engraved as signets are engraved, with the names of <n>[the twelve tribes of]</n> the sons of Israel.‚
”n„'The skillfully woven sash with which to bind it, which was on the ephod <n>[to hold it in place]</n>, was like its workmanship, of the same material: of gold and of blue, purple, and scarlet fabric, and fine twisted linen, just as the LORD had commanded Moses.l”mS'They made attaching shoulder pieces for the ephod; it was attached at its two <n>[upper]</n> edges.R”lƒ'Then Bezalel and Oholiab hammered the gold <n>[into thin sheets]</n> and cut it into threads to work into the blue, purple, and scarlet fabric, and into the fine linen, the work of a skilled craftsman.n”kW'<pb/>Bezalel made the ephod of gold, and of blue, purple, and scarlet fabric, and fine twisted linen.f”j	ƒI'<pb/>Moreover, from the blue and purple and scarlet fabric, they made finely woven garments for serving <i>and</i> ministering in the Holy Place; they made the holy garments for Aaron, just as the LORD had commanded Moses.”i‚-&and the sockets of the court all around and the sockets of the court gate, and all the pegs of the tabernacle and all the pegs around the court.!”h‚=&With it Bezalel made the sockets for the doorway of the Tent of Meeting, and the bronze altar and its bronze grating, and all the utensils of the altar,O”g&The bronze of the wave offering was seventy talents and 2,400 shekels.”f‚&Of the 1,775 <i>shekels</i>, he made hooks for the support poles and overlaid their tops and made connecting rings for them.G”eƒ	&The hundred talents of silver were for casting the sockets of the sanctuary and the sockets of the veil (partition curtain); a hundred sockets for the hundred talents, a talent for a socket./”d‚Y&a beka for each man (that is, half a shekel according to the shekel of the sanctuary) for everyone who was counted, from twenty years old and upward, for 603,550 men.%”c‚E&The silver from those of the congregation who were assembled <i>and</i> counted was 100 talents and 1,775 shekels, according to the shekel of the sanctuary;{”bƒq&<pb/>All the gold that was used for the work, in all the building <i>and</i> furnishing of the sanctuary, the gold from the wave offering, was twenty-nine talents and seven hundred and thirty shekels, according to the shekel of the sanctuary.@”a‚{&With him was Oholiab the son of Ahisamach, of the tribe of Dan, an engraver and a skillful craftsman and an embroiderer in blue and in purple and in scarlet fabric, and in fine linen.z”`o&Now Bezalel the son of Uri, the son of Hur, of the tribe of Judah, made everything that the LORD commanded Moses.b”_ƒ?&This is the sum of the things for the tabernacle, the tabernacle of the Testimony, as counted according to the command of Moses, for the work of the Levites, under the direction of Ithamar the son of Aaron the priest.C”^&All the pegs for the tabernacle and the court were bronze.”]‚-&Their four support poles and their four sockets were bronze; their hooks were silver, and silver overlaid their tops and their connecting rings.‚”\„&The screen (curtain) for the gate of the courtyard <n>[on the east side]</n> was the work of an embroiderer, in blue, purple, and scarlet fabric, and fine twisted linen; it was twenty cubits long and five cubits high, corresponding to the curtains of the court.|”[ƒs&The sockets for the support poles were <i>made of</i> bronze, the hooks of the support poles and their connecting rings were <i>made of</i> silver; and silver overlaid their tops. All the support poles of the court had silver connecting rings.F”Z&All the curtains around the court were of fine twisted linen.K”Yƒ&and <i>the same</i> for the other side <n>[of the court gate]</n>. Left and right of the court gate there were curtains of fifteen cubits; with their three support poles and their three sockets.”X‚&The curtains for one side <i>of the court gate were</i> fifteen cubits, with their three support poles and their three sockets;d”WC&
For the east side <n>[the front of the courtyard, there were curtains of]</n> fifty cubits.O”Vƒ&For the west side <n>[of the court]</n> there were curtains of fifty cubits with their ten support poles and their ten sockets; the hooks of the support poles and their connecting rings were silver.a”Uƒ=&And for the north side <n>[of the court the curtains were also]</n> a hundred cubits; their twenty support poles and their twenty bronze sockets; the hooks of the support poles and their connecting rings were silver.”T‚&
their twenty support poles, and their twenty bronze sockets; the hooks of the support poles and their connecting rings were silver. ”S{&	<pb/>Then he made the court: for the south side the curtains of the court were of fine twisted linen, a hundred cubits;+”R‚Q&<pb/>Bezalel made the basin and its base of bronze from the mirrors of the attending women who served <i>and</i> ministered at the doorway of the Tent of Meeting.}”Qu&He put the poles through the rings on the sides of the altar, with which to carry it; he made it hollow with planks.U”P%&And he made the carrying poles of acacia wood and overlaid them with bronze.i”OM&He cast four rings for the four corners of the bronze grating as holders for the carrying poles._”N9&He made for the altar a grating of bronze mesh under its rim, extending halfway up it.‚”M„&He made all the utensils <i>and</i> vessels of the altar <n>[of burnt offering]</n>, the pots, shovels, basins <n>[to catch the blood of the sacrificed animal]</n>, meat hooks and the firepans <n>[to store live coals]</n>. He made all its utensils of bronze.”L‚9&And he made its horns (horn-shaped projections) on the four corners of it; the horns were <i>of one piece</i> with it, and he overlaid it with bronze.”K	‚5&<pb/>Then Bezalel made the altar of burnt offering of acacia wood; its top was square, five cubits long and five cubits wide, and three cubits high.n”JW%He also made the holy anointing oil and the pure, fragrant incense of spices, the work of a perfumer.F”I%He made the poles of acacia wood and overlaid them with gold.”H‚%He made two rings of gold for it under its rim, on its two opposite sides, as holders for the poles <n>[to pass through]</n> to carry it.z”Go%He overlaid it with pure gold, its top, its sides all around and its horns; he also made a rim of gold around it.$”F‚C%<pb/>Then Bezalel made the incense altar of acacia wood; its top was a cubit square and it was two cubits high; the horns were <i>of one piece</i> with it.O”E%He made the lampstand and all its utensils from a talent of pure gold.N”D%He made its seven lamps with its snuffers and its trays of pure gold.~”Cw%Their calyxes and their branches were <i>of one piece</i> with it; all of it was a single hammered work of pure gold.‚'”B„I%and a calyx was under the <i>first</i> pair of branches <i>coming</i> out of it, and a calyx under the <i>second</i> pair of branches, <i>coming</i> out of it, and a calyx under the <i>third</i> pair of branches <i>coming</i> out of it, for the six branches coming out of the lampstand.”A‚9%On <i>the center shaft of</i> the lampstand there were four cups shaped like almond <i>blossoms</i>, with calyxes and flowers <n>[one at the top]</n>;‚”@„%three cups shaped like almond <i>blossoms</i>, a calyx and a flower in one branch, and three cups shaped like almond <i>blossoms</i>, a calyx and a flower in the opposite branchâ€”so for the six branches coming out of <i>the center shaft of</i> the lampstand.0”?‚[%There were six branches coming out of the sides of the lampstand, three branches from one side of <i>the center shaft</i> and three branches from the other side of it;g”>ƒI%<pb/>Then he made the lampstand (menorah) of pure gold. He made the lampstand of hammered work, its base and its <n>[center]</n> shaft; its cups, its<f>[1]</f> calyxes, and its flowers were <i>all of one piece</i> with it.*”=‚O%He made the utensils which were to be on the table, its dishes and its pans <n>[for bread]</n>, its bowls and its jars for pouring drink offerings, of pure gold.g”<I%Bezalel made the carrying poles of acacia wood to carry the table and overlaid them with gold.p”;[%Close by the rim were the rings, the holders for the poles <n>[to pass through]</n> to carry the table.q”:]%
He cast four rings of gold for it and fastened the rings to the four corners that were at its four legs.|”9s%He made a rim for it <n>[just under the top]</n> a hand width wide all around, and a border of gold around its rim.e”8E%He overlaid it with pure gold, and made a border of gold for it all around <n>[its top]</n>.”7‚%
<pb/>Bezalel made the table <n>[for the bread]</n> of acacia wood; it was two cubits long, a cubit wide, and one and a half cubits high.h”6ƒK%	The cherubim spread out their wings upward, covering <i>and</i> protecting the mercy seat with their wings, with their faces toward each other; the faces of the cherubim were <n>[looking downward]</n> toward the mercy seat.”5‚%one cherub at one end and one cherub at the other end; he made the cherubim <n>[of one piece]</n> with the mercy seat at the two ends._”49%He made two cherubim of hammered gold; he made them at the two ends of the mercy seat,u”3e%Bezalel made the mercy seat of pure gold; it was two and a half cubits long, and one and a half cubits wide.Z”2/%He put the carrying poles through the rings at the sides of the ark, to carry it.K”1%He made carrying poles of acacia wood and overlaid them with gold.s”0a%He cast four rings of gold for it on its four feet, two rings on one side and two rings on the other side.”/‚	%And he overlaid it with pure gold inside and out, and made a molding (border) of gold to go all around <n>[the top of it]</n>..”.	‚Y%<pb/>Bezalel made the ark <n>[of the covenant]</n> of acacia woodâ€”it was two and a half cubits long, and one and a half cubits wide, and one and a half cubits high.6”-‚g$&and <n>[he made]</n> the five support poles with their hooks, and overlaid their <n>[ornamental]</n> tops and connecting rings with gold; but their five sockets were bronze.”,‚-$%He made a screen (curtain) for the doorway of the tent, of blue, purple, and scarlet fabric, and fine twisted linen, the work of an embroiderer;1”+‚]$$For the veil (partition curtain) he made four support poles of acacia wood and overlaid them with gold; their hooks were gold, and he cast for them four silver sockets.!”*‚=$#<pb/>Further, Bezalel made the veil of blue and purple and scarlet fabric, and fine twisted linen; he made it with cherubim, the work of an embroiderer.l”)S$"He overlaid the boards and the bars with gold and made their rings of gold as holders for the bars.w”(i$!And he made the middle bar pass through <n>[horizontally]</n> halfway up the boards from one end to the other. ”'{$ and five bars for the boards of the tabernacle's other side, and five bars for the boards at the rear side to the west.u”&e$<pb/>Bezalel made bars of acacia wood, five for the <n>[frame]</n> boards of the one side of the tabernacle,s”%a$There were eight boards with sixteen silver sockets, and under <n>[the end of]</n> each board two sockets.{”$q$They were separate below, but linked together at the top with one ring; thus he made both of them in both corners.N”#$And he made two boards for each corner of the tabernacle in the rear.\”"3$And for the rear of the tabernacle, to the west, he made six <n>[frame]</n> boards.Z”!/$and their forty silver sockets; two sockets under <n>[the end of]</n> each board.U” %$For the other side of the tabernacle, the north side, he made twenty boards,6”‚g$and he made under the twenty boards forty silver sockets; two sockets under one board for its two tenons (dovetails), and two sockets under another board for its two tenons.~”w$And <n>[this is how]</n> he made the boards <n>[for frames]</n> for the tabernacle: twenty boards for the south side;x”k$Each board had two tenons (dovetails), fitted to one another; he did this for all the boards of the tabernacle.G”	$Each board was ten cubits long and one and a half cubits wide.]”5$<pb/>Bezalel made boards of acacia wood for the upright framework of the tabernacle.”‚9$He made a <n>[third]</n> covering for the tent of rams'<f>[1]</f> skins dyed red, and above it a <n>[fourth]</n> covering of<f>[2]</f> porpoise skins.J”$He made fifty bronze hooks to join the tent together into a unit.1”‚]$He made fifty loops on the edge of the outermost curtain in the <i>first</i> set, and he made fifty loops on the edge of the <i>outermost</i> curtain of the second set.f”G$Bezalel joined five curtains by themselves and <n>[the other]</n> six curtains by themselves.j”O$Each curtain was thirty cubits long and four cubits wide; the eleven curtains were of equal size.y”m$<pb/>Then he made curtains of goats' <i>hair</i> for a tent over the tabernacle; he made eleven curtains in all.x”k$
He made fifty gold hooks and joined the curtains together with the hooks, so that the tabernacle became a unit.6”‚g$He made fifty loops in the one curtain <n>[of the first set]</n> and fifty loops on the edge of the curtain which was in the second set; the loops were opposite one another.'”‚I$He made loops of blue on the edge of the outermost curtain in the first set; he also did this on the edge of the curtain that was outermost in the second set.t”c$
Bezalel joined five curtains one to another, and <n>[the other]</n> five curtains he joined one to another.h”K$	Each curtain was twenty-eight cubits long and four cubits wide; all the curtains were one size.w”ƒi$All the skilled men among them who were doing the work on the tabernacle made ten curtains of fine twisted linen and blue, purple, and scarlet fabric, with cherubim <n>[worked into them]</n>, the work of an embroiderer, Bezalel made them.Z”/$for the material they had was sufficient and more than enough to do all the work.Y”
ƒ-$So Moses issued a command, and it was proclaimed throughout the camp, "Let neither man nor woman do any more work for the sanctuary offering." So the people were restrained from bringing <i>anything more</i>;
