Every grain offering, mixed with <n>[olive]</n> oil or dry, all the sons of Aaron may have, one as well as another.&–I‚G	Likewise, every grain offering that is baked in the oven and everything that is prepared in a pan or on a griddle shall belong to the priest who presents it.–H‚	The priest who presents any man's burnt offering shall have for himself the hide of the burnt offering which he has presented.#–G‚AThe guilt offering is like the sin offering, there is one law for <n>[both of]</n> them: the priest who makes atonement with it shall have it for himself.e–FEEvery male among the priests may eat it; it shall be eaten in a holy place; it is most holy.y–EmThe priest shall offer them up in smoke on the altar as an offering by fire to the LORD; it is a guilt offering.
–D‚and the two kidneys with the fat that is on them at the loins, and the lobe of the liver, which he shall remove with the kidneys.\–C3Then he shall offer all its fat, the fat tail and the fat that covers the entrails,–B‚In the place where they kill the burnt offering they are to kill the guilt offering, and he shall sprinkle its blood around on the altar.E–A	<pb/>'This is the law of the guilt offering; it is most holy.D–@ƒBut no sin offering from which any of the blood is brought into the Tent of Meeting to make atonement in the Holy Place shall be eaten; it shall be <n>[completely]</n> burned in the fire.M–?Every male among the priests may eat this offering; it is most holy.,–>‚SAlso the earthenware vessel in which it was boiled shall be broken; and if it was boiled in a bronze vessel, then that vessel shall be scoured and rinsed in water.=–=‚u<f>[5]</f> Whatever touches its meat will become consecrated (ceremonially clean). When any of its blood splashes on a garment, you shall wash what was splashed on in a holy place.–<‚The priest who offers it for sin shall eat it. It shall be eaten in a holy place, in the courtyard of the Tent of Meeting.U–;ƒ%"Speak to Aaron and his sons, saying, 'This is the law of the sin offering: the sin offering shall be killed before the LORD in the <n>[same]</n> place where the burnt offering is killed; it is most holy.2–:a<pb/>Then the LORD spoke to Moses, saying,`–9;So every grain offering of the priest shall be burned entirely. It shall not be eaten."*–8‚OThe priest from among the sons of Aaron who is anointed in his place shall offer it. By a permanent statute it shall be entirely offered up in smoke to the LORD.H–7ƒIt shall be prepared with oil on a griddle. When it is well stirred, you shall bring it. You shall present the grain offering in baked pieces as a sweet <i>and</i> soothing aroma to the LORD.l–6ƒS"This is the offering which Aaron and his sons are to present to the LORD on the day when he is anointed: the tenth of an ephah of fine flour as a regular grain offering, half of it in the morning and half of it in the evening.2–5a<pb/>Then the LORD spoke to Moses, saying,z–4ƒoEvery male among the sons of Aaron may eat it <n>[as his share]</n>; it is a permanent ordinance throughout your generations, from offerings by fire to the LORD.<f>[4]</f> Whatever touches them will become consecrated (ceremonially clean).'"X–3ƒ+It shall not be baked with leaven <n>[which represents corruption or sin]</n>. I have given it as their share of My offerings by fire; it is<f>[3]</f> most holy, like the sin offering and the guilt offering.-–2‚UWhat is left of it Aaron and his sons are to eat. It shall be eaten as unleavened bread in a holy place; they are to eat it in the courtyard of the Tent of Meeting.‚–1„%One of them shall take up from it a handful of the fine flour of the grain offering with its oil and all the incense that is on the grain offering, and he shall offer it up in smoke on the altar, a sweet <i>and</i> soothing aroma, as the memorial offering to the LORD.–0‚<pb/>'Now this is the law of the grain offering: the sons of Aaron shall present it before the LORD in front of the altar.h–/K
The fire shall be burning continually on the altar; it shall not <n>[be allowed to]</n> go out.‚–.„The fire on the altar shall be kept burning; it shall not <n>[be allowed to]</n> go out. The priest shall burn wood on it every morning, and he shall arrange the burnt offering on it and offer the fat portions of the peace offerings up in smoke on it.–-‚Then he shall take off his garments and put on something else, and take the ashes outside the camp to a (ceremonially) clean place.]–,ƒ5
The priest is to put on his linen robe, with his linen undergarments next to his body. Then he shall take up the ashes of the burnt offering which the fire has consumed on the altar and put them beside the altar.o–+ƒY	"Command Aaron and his sons, saying, 'This is the law of the burnt offering: the burnt offering <i>shall remain</i> on the hearth <i>that is</i> on the altar all night until morning and the fire is to be kept burning on the altar.8–*m<f>[2]</f> Then the LORD spoke to Moses, saying,–)‚1The priest shall make atonement for him before the LORD, and he will be forgiven for any one of the things which he may have done to incur guilt."–(‚)Then he shall bring to the priest his guilt offering to the LORD, a ram without blemish from the flock, as valued by you, as a guilt offering.Q–'ƒor anything about which he has sworn falsely; he shall not only restore it in full, but shall add to it one-fifth more. He shall give it to the one to whom it belongs on the day of his guilt offering.A–&‚}then if he has sinned and is guilty, he shall restore what he took by robbery, or what he got by extortion, or the deposit which was entrusted to him, or the lost thing which he found,–%‚or has found what was lost and lied about it and sworn falsely, so that he sins in regard to any one of the things a man may doâ€”m–$ƒU"When anyone sins and acts unfaithfully against the LORD by deceiving his neighbor (companion, associate) in regard to a deposit or a security entrusted <i>to him</i>, or through robbery, or if he has extorted from his neighbor,<–#	w<pb/><f>[1]</f> Then the LORD spoke to Moses, saying,J–"It is a guilt offering; he was certainly guilty before the LORD."‚–!„5He is then to bring to the priest a ram without blemish from the flock, according to your valuation, for a guilt offering. In this way the priest shall make atonement for him regarding the error which he committed unintentionally and did not know it, and he shall be forgiven.+– ‚Q<pb/>"Now if anyone sins and does any of the things which the LORD has forbidden, though he was not aware of it, still he is guilty and shall bear his punishment.‚–„3He shall make restitution for the sin which he has committed against the holy thing, and shall add a fifth <n>[of the ram's value]</n> to it, and give it to the priest. The priest shall then make atonement for him with the ram of the guilt offering, and he shall be forgiven.‚&–„G"If a person commits a breach of faith and sins unintentionally against the holy things of the LORD, then he shall bring his guilt offering to the LORD, a ram without blemish from the flock, valued by you in shekels of silver, that is, the shekel of the sanctuary, as a guilt offering.2–a<pb/>Then the LORD spoke to Moses, saying,]–ƒ5
In this way the priest shall make atonement for him for the sin which he has committed in one of these things, and it will be forgiven him; then <i>the rest</i> shall be for the priest, like the grain offering.'"C–ƒHe shall bring it to the priest, who shall take a handful of it as a memorial portion and offer it up in smoke on the altar, with the offerings by fire to the LORD; it is a sin offering.‚–„1<pb/>'But if he cannot afford to bring two turtledoves or two young pigeons, then he shall bring as his offering for his sin the tenth part of an<f>[4]</f> ephah of fine flour as a sin offering; he shall not put <n>[olive]</n> oil or incense on it, for it is a sin offering.U–ƒ%
The second <n>[bird]</n> he shall prepare as a burnt offering, according to the ordinance. So the priest shall make atonement on his behalf for the sin which he has committed, and it will be forgiven him.>–‚w	He shall also sprinkle some of the blood of the sin offering on the side of the altar, and the rest of the blood shall be drained out at the base of the altar; it is a sin offering.<–‚sHe shall bring them to the priest, who shall offer first the one for the sin offering, and shall nip its head at the front of its neck, but shall not sever it <n>[completely]</n>.Y–ƒ-<pb/>'But if he<f>[3]</f> cannot afford a lamb, then he shall bring two turtledoves or two young pigeons as his guilt offering for his sin to the LORD, one as a sin offering and the other as a burnt offering.U–ƒ%He shall bring his guilt offering to the LORD for the sin which he has committed, a female from the flock, a lamb or a goat as a sin offering. So the priest shall make atonement on his behalf for his sin.p–[So it shall be when a person is guilty in one of these, that he shall confess the sin he has committed.‚1–„]Or if anyone swears <n>[an oath]</n> thoughtlessly <i>or</i> impulsively<f>[2]</f> aloud that he will do either evil or good, in whatever manner a person may speak thoughtlessly <i>or</i> impulsively with an oath, but he is unaware of it, when he recognizes it, he will be guilty in one of these."–‚?Or if he touches human uncleannessâ€”whatever kind it may beâ€”and he becomes unclean, but he is unaware of it, when he recognizes it, he will be guilty.‚–„/Or if someone touches any <n>[ceremonially]</n> unclean thingâ€”whether the carcass of an unclean wild animal or the carcass of an unclean domestic animal or the carcass of unclean creeping thingsâ€”even if he is unaware of it, he has become unclean, and he will be guilty.‚–	„)<pb/>'If anyone sins after he hears a<f>[1]</f> public adjuration (solemn command to testify) when he is a witness, whether he has seen or <n>[otherwise]</n> known <n>[something]</n>â€”if he fails to report it, then he will bear his guilt <i>and</i> be held responsible.‚T–…##Then he shall remove all its fat, just as the fat of the lamb is removed from the sacrifice of the peace offerings, and the priest shall offer it up in smoke on the altar, on the offerings by fire to the LORD. In this way the priest shall make atonement for him in regard to the sin which he has committed, and he will be forgiven.h–ƒK"The priest is to take some of the blood of the sin offering with his finger and put it on the horns of the altar of burnt offering and all <i>the rest of</i> the blood of the lamb he shall pour out at the base of the altar.L–
ƒ!He shall lay his hand on the head of the sin offering <n>[transferring symbolically his guilt to the sacrifice]</n>, and kill it as a sin offering in the place where they kill the burnt offering.o–Y <pb/>'If he brings a lamb as his offering for a sin offering, he shall bring a female without blemish.‚$–„CThen he shall remove all its fat, just as the fat was removed from the sacrifice of peace offerings; and the priest shall offer it up in smoke on the altar as a sweet <i>and</i> soothing aroma to the LORD. In this way the priest shall make atonement for him, and he will be forgiven.E–
