Egypt to be your God; therefore you shall be holy, for I am holy.'"J—bƒ,For I am the LORD your God; so consecrate yourselves and be holy, for I am holy. You shall not make yourselves unclean with any of the swarming things that swarm <i>or</i> crawls on the ground..—a‚W+Do not make yourselves loathsome (impure, repulsive) by <n>[eating]</n> any swarming thing; you shall not make yourselves unclean by them so as to defile yourselves.9—`‚m*Whatever crawls on its belly, and whatever walks on all fours, and whatever has many feet among all things that swarm on the ground, you shall not eat; for they are detestable.]—_5)<pb/>'Now everything that swarms on the ground is detestable; it is not to be eaten.<—^‚s(And whoever eats some of its meat shall wash his clothes, and be unclean until evening; also whoever picks up its carcass shall wash his clothes, and be unclean until the evening.—]‚%'<pb/>'If one of the animals that you may eat dies <n>[of natural causes]</n>, whoever touches its carcass becomes unclean until the evening.g—\I&but if water is put on the seed and a part of their carcass falls on it, it is unclean to you.b—[?%If a part of their carcass falls on any seed for sowing which is to be sown, it is clean;—Z‚%$Nevertheless a spring or a cistern (reservoir) collecting water shall be clean; but whoever touches one of these carcasses shall be unclean.#—Y‚A#Everything that part of their carcass falls on becomes unclean; an oven, or a small stove shall be smashed; they are unclean, and shall be unclean to you.7—X‚i"Any of the food which may be eaten, but on which <n>[unclean]</n> water falls, shall become unclean, and any liquid that may be drunk in every container shall become unclean.'—W‚I!As for any earthenware container into which any of these <n>[crawling things]</n> falls, whatever is in it becomes unclean, and you shall break the container.‚—V„ Also anything on which one of them falls after dying becomes unclean, whether it is an article of wood or clothing, or a skin, or a sackâ€”any article that is usedâ€”it must be put in water, and will be unclean until the evening; then it becomes clean.—U‚These <n>[creatures]</n> are unclean to you among all that swarm; whoever touches them when they are dead becomes unclean until evening.S—T!the gecko, the crocodile, the lizard, the sand reptile, and the chameleon.:—S‚o<pb/>'These also are unclean to you among the swarming things that crawl around on the ground <n>[and multiply profusely]</n>: the mole, the mouse, and any kind of great lizard,—R‚and the one who picks up their carcasses shall wash his clothes and be unclean until the evening; they are unclean to you.Q—QƒAlso all <i>animals</i> that walk on their paws, among all <i>kinds of</i> animals that walk on four <i>legs</i>, are unclean to you; whoever touches their carcasses becomes unclean until the evening,5—P‚eConcerning all the animals which divide the hoof, but do not have a split hoof, or which do not chew the cud, they are unclean to you; whoever touches them becomes unclean.m—OUand whoever picks up any of their carcasses shall wash his clothes and be unclean until the evening.—N‚<pb/>'By <n>[contact with]</n> these you will become unclean; whoever touches their carcasses becomes unclean until the evening (dusk),R—MBut all other winged insects which are four footed are detestable to you.u—LeOf these you may eat: the whole species of migratory locust, of bald locust, of cricket, and of grasshopper.—K‚yet of all winged insects that walk on all fours you may eat those which have legs above their feet with which to leap on the ground.O—J<pb/>'All winged insects that walk on all fours are detestable to you;?—I{the stork, all kinds of heron, the hoopoe, and the bat.8—Hmthe white owl, the pelican, the carrion vulture,;—Gsthe little owl and the cormorant and the great owl,I—F
