m twenty years old and upward, all who <i>were able to</i> go to war:4œ*	g(those of the tribe of Reuben numbered 46,500.‚œ)	„(<pb/>The sons of Reuben, Israel's (Jacob's) firstborn, their generations, by their families (clans), by their fathers' households, according to the number of names, head by head, every male from twenty years old and upward, all who <i>were able to</i> go to war:]œ(	7(just as the LORD had commanded Moses. So he numbered them in the Wilderness of Sinai.zœ'	ƒq(and assembled all the congregation on the first day of the second month, and they registered by ancestry in their families (clans), by their fathers' households, according to the number of names from twenty years old and upward, head by head,Kœ&	(<pb/>So Moses and Aaron took these men who were designated by name,Cœ%	ƒ(These men were the ones called from the congregation, the leaders of their fathers' (ancestors') tribes; they were the heads of thousands <n>[the highest ranking officers]</n> in Israel."Cœ$	(from <n>[the tribe of]</n> Naphtali, Ahira the son of Enan.Bœ#	(from <n>[the tribe of]</n> Gad, Eliasaph the son of Deuel;Cœ"	(
from <n>[the tribe of]</n> Asher, Pagiel the son of Ochran;Gœ!	(from <n>[the tribe of]</n> Dan, Ahiezer the son of Ammishaddai;Gœ 	(from <n>[the tribe of]</n> Benjamin, Abidan the son of Gideoni;<œ	‚u(
from the sons (descendants) of Joseph: from <n>[the tribe of]</n><f>[3]</f> Ephraim, Elishama the son of Ammihud; from <n>[the tribe of]</n> Manasseh, Gamaliel the son of Pedahzur;Cœ	(	from <n>[the tribe of]</n> Zebulun, Eliab the son of Helon;Fœ		(from <n>[the tribe of]</n> Issachar, Nethanel the son of Zuar;Gœ	(from <n>[the tribe of]</n> Judah, Nahshon the son of Amminadab;Lœ	(from <n>[the tribe of]</n> Simeon, Shelumiel the son of Zurishaddai;œ	‚(These then are the names of the men who shall stand with you: from <n>[the tribe of]</n> Reuben, Elizur the son of Shedeur; œ	}(And with you there shall be a man <n>[to assist you]</n> from each tribe, each being the head of his father's household.œ	‚(from twenty years old and upward, all in Israel who <i>are able to</i> go out to war. You and Aaron shall number them, army by army.Cœ	ƒ("Take a census of all the congregation of the sons of Israel, by their families (clans), by their fathers' households, according to the number of names, every male,<f>[2]</f> head by headœ		ƒ}(<pb/>The LORD spoke <n>[by special revelation]</n> to Moses in the Wilderness of Sinai in the Tent of Meeting (tabernacle) on the first day of the second month in the second year after the<f>[1]</f> Israelites came out of the land of Egypt, saying,rœ_"<pb/>These are the commandments which the LORD commanded Moses on Mount Sinai for the children of Israel.Vœƒ'!The man is not to be concerned whether <i>the animal is</i> good or bad, nor shall he exchange it. But if he does exchange it, then both it and its substitute shall become holy; it shall not be redeemed.'"œ‚' For every tithe of the herd or flock, whatever<f>[3]</f> passes under the <n>[shepherd's]</n> staff, the tenth one shall be holy to the LORD.Wœ)If a man wishes to redeem any part of his tithe, he shall add one-fifth to it.œ‚9<pb/>'And all the tithe (tenth part) of the land, whether the seed of the land or the fruit of the tree, is the <e>Lord's</e>; it is holy to the LORD.œ‚No one who may have been set apart among men shall be ransomed <n>[from death]</n>, he shall most certainly be put to death.‚'œ„I<pb/>'But nothing that a man<f>[2]</f> sets apart <n>[that is, devotes as an offering]</n> to the LORD out of all that he has, of man or of animal or of the fields of his own property, shall be sold or redeemed. Anything devoted to destruction (banned, cursed) is most holy to the LORD.Sœƒ!If it is among the unclean animals, the owner may redeem it in accordance with your valuation, and add one-fifth to it; or if it is not redeemed, then it shall be sold in accordance with your valuation.9œ
‚m<pb/>'However, the firstborn among animals, which as a firstborn belongs to the LORD, no man may consecrate, whether an ox or a sheep. It is <n>[already]</n> the <e>Lord's</e>.vœgEvery valuation of yours shall be in accordance with the sanctuary shekel; twenty gerahs shall make a shekel.œ‚7In the Year of Jubilee the field shall return to the one from whom it was purchased, to whom the land belonged <n>[as his ancestral inheritance]</n>.=œ
‚uthen the priest shall calculate for him the amount of your valuation up to the Year of Jubilee; and the man shall give that <n>[amount]</n> on that day as a holy thing to the LORD.œ	‚Or if a man consecrates to the LORD a field which he has bought, which is not part of the field of his <n>[ancestral]</n> property,#œ‚AWhen the field reverts in the Jubilee, the field shall be holy to the LORD, like a field set apart (devoted); the priest shall possess it as his property.gœIIf he does not redeem the field, but has sold it to another man, it may no longer be redeemed.$œ‚CIf the one who consecrates the field should ever wish to redeem it, then he shall add one-fifth of the appraisal price to it, so that it may return to him.]œƒ5But if he consecrates his field after the Jubilee, then the priest shall calculate the price for him in proportion to the years that remain until the Year of Jubilee; and it shall be deducted from your valuation.lœSIf he consecrates his field during the Year of Jubilee, it shall stand according to your valuation.aœƒ=<pb/>'And if a man consecrates to the LORD part of a field of his own property, then your valuation shall be proportionate to the seed needed for it; a homer of barley seed shall be valued at fifty shekels of silver. œ‚;If the one who consecrates his house should <i>wish to</i> redeem it, then he shall add one-fifth of your valuation price to it, so that it may be his.'œ‚I<pb/>'If a man consecrates his house as sacred to the LORD, the priest shall appraise it as either good or bad; as the priest appraises it, so shall it stand.hœ K
But if he ever <i>wishes to</i> redeem it, then he shall add one-fifth of it to your valuation.g›Iand the priest shall value it as either good or bad; it shall be as you, the priest, value it.›~‚)If it is any unclean animal of the kind which men do not present as an offering to the LORD, then he shall bring the animal before the priest,J›}ƒ
He shall not replace it or exchange it, a good for a bad, or a bad for a good; but if he does exchange an animal for an animal, then both the original offering and its substitute shall be holy.›|‚'	<pb/>'Now if it is an animal of the kind which men can present as an offering to the LORD, any such that one gives to the LORD shall be holy.S›{ƒ!But if the person is too poor to pay your valuation, then he shall be placed before the priest, and the priest shall value him; according to the ability of the one who vowed, the priest shall value him.
›z‚If the person is sixty years old and above, your valuation shall be fifteen shekels for the male, and ten shekels for the female.(›y‚KBut if the child is between one month and five years of age, then your valuation shall be five shekels of silver for the male and three shekels for the female.›x‚7If the person is between five years and twenty years of age, then your valuation for the male shall be twenty shekels and for the female ten shekels.S›w!Or if the person is a female, then your valuation shall be thirty shekels..›v‚WIf your valuation is of a male between twenty and sixty years of age, then your valuation shall be fifty shekels of silver, according to the shekel of the sanctuary.ƒ&›u†G"Speak to the children of Israel and say to them, 'When a man makes a<f>[1]</f> special vow <n>[consecrating himself or a member of his family]</n>, he <i>shall be valued</i> according to your <n>[established system of]</n> valuation of people belonging to the LORD <n>[that is, the priest accepts from the man making the vow a specified amount of money for the temple treasury in place of the actual person]</n>.3›t	e<pb/>Again, the LORD spoke to Moses, saying,›s‚'.<pb/>These are the statutes, ordinances, and laws which the LORD established between Himself and the Israelites through Moses at Mount Sinai.[›rƒ1-But I will, for their sake, <n>[earnestly]</n> remember the covenant with their forefathers, whom I have brought out of the land of Egypt in the sight of the nations, that I might be their God. I am the LORD.'"N›qƒ,Yet in spite of this, when they are in the land of their enemies, I will not reject them, nor will I so despise them as to destroy them, breaking My covenant with them; for I am the LORD their God.‚	›p„
+But the land will be abandoned by them and will enjoy its Sabbaths while it lies desolate without them; and they will accept the punishment for their wickedness <i>and</i> make amends because they rejected My ordinances and their soul rejected My statutes.›o‚*then I will remember My covenant with Jacob, and also My covenant with Isaac, and also My covenant with Abraham, and remember the land.X›nƒ+)I also was acting with hostility toward them and brought them into the land of their enemiesâ€”then if their uncircumcised (sin-filled) hearts are humbled and they accept the punishment for their wickedness,L›mƒ(<pb/>'If they confess their wickedness and the wickedness of their forefathers, in their unfaithfulness which they have committed against Meâ€”and also in their acting with hostility toward Meâ€”<›l‚s'Those of you who are left will rot away because of their wickedness in the lands of your enemies; also because of the wickedness of their forefathers they will rot away like them.V›k'&You will perish among the nations; the land of your enemies will consume you.&›j‚G%They shall stumble over one another as if <i>to escape</i> from a sword when no one is chasing them; and you will have no power to stand before your enemies.‚-›i„U$As for those who are left of you, I will bring despair (lack of courage, weakness) into their hearts in the lands of their enemies; the sound of a scattered leaf will put them to flight, and they will flee as if <n>[running]</n> from the sword, and will fall even when no one is chasing them. ›h{#As long as it lies desolate, it will have rest, the rest it did not have on your Sabbaths, while you were living on it.9›g‚m"<pb/>'Then the land <n>[of Israel]</n> will enjoy its Sabbaths as long as it lies desolate, while you are in your enemies' land; then the land will rest and enjoy its Sabbaths.*›f‚O!I will scatter you among the nations and draw out the sword <n>[of your enemies]</n> after you; your land will become desolate and your cities will become ruins.a›e= I will make the land desolate, and your enemies who settle in it will be appalled at it.0›d‚[I will lay waste your cities as well and will make your sanctuaries desolate, and I will not smell your sweet <i>and</i> soothing aromas <n>[of offerings by fire]</n>.‚›c„I will destroy your high places <n>[devoted to idolatrous worship]</n>, and cut down your incense altars, and heap your dead bodies upon the <n>[crushed]</n> bodies of your idols, and My soul will detest you <n>[with deep and unutterable loathing]</n>.M›bYou will eat the flesh of your sons and the flesh of your daughters.s›aathen I will act with hostility against you in wrath, and I also will punish you seven times for your sins.x›`k<pb/>'Yet if in spite of this you will not <n>[attentively]</n> listen to Me but act with hostility against me,L›_ƒWhen I break your staff of bread <n>[that is, cut off your supply of food]</n>, ten women will bake your bread in one oven, and they will ration your bread; and you will eat and not be satisfied.q›^ƒ]I will bring a sword on you that will execute vengeance for <n>[breaking]</n> the covenant; and when you gather together in your cities, I will send pestilence (virulent disease) among you, and you shall be handed over to the enemy.j›]Othen I also will act with hostility against you, and I will strike you seven times for your sins.l›\S<pb/>'And if by these things you are not turned to Me, but act<f>[3]</f> with hostility against Me,c›[ƒAI will let loose the <n>[wild]</n> animals of the field among you, which will bereave you of your children and destroy your livestock and make you so few in number that your roads will lie deserted <i>and</i> desolate."›Z‚?<pb/>'If then, you act with hostility toward Me and are unwilling to obey Me, I will increase the plague on you seven times in accordance with your sins.›Y‚Your strength will be spent uselessly, for your land will not yield its produce and the trees of the land will not yield their fruit.N›XƒI will break your pride in your power, and I will make your sky like iron <n>[giving no rain and blocking all prayers]</n> and your ground like bronze <n>[hard to plow and yielding no produce]</n>.›W‚If in spite of all this you still will not listen to Me <i>and</i> be obedient, then I will punish you seven times more for your sins.<›V‚sI will set My face against you so that<f>[2]</f> you will be struck down before your enemies; those who hate you will rule over you, and you will flee when no one is pursuing you.z›UƒoI, in turn, will do this to you: I will appoint over you sudden terror, consumption, and fever that will waste away the eyes and cause the soul to languish also. And you will sow your seed uselessly, for your enemies will eat what you plant.9›T‚mif, instead, you reject My statutes, and if your soul rejects My ordinances, so that you will not <n>[obediently]</n> do all My commandments, and in this way break My covenant,]›S5'But if you do not obey Me and do not <n>[obediently]</n> do all these commandments,W›Rƒ)
I am the LORD your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt so that you would not be their slaves; and I broke the bars of your yoke and made you walk upright <n>[with heads held high as free men]</n>.K›QI will walk among you and be your God, and you shall be My people.l›PSI will make My dwelling among you, and My soul will not reject <i>nor</i> separate itself from you.{›Oq
You will eat the old supply of <n>[abundant]</n> produce, and clear out the old <n>[to make room]</n> for the new.(›N‚K	For I will turn toward you <n>[with favor and regard]</n> and make you fruitful and multiply you, and I will establish <i>and</i> confirm My covenant with you.›M‚Five of you will chase a hundred, and a hundred of you will put ten thousand to flight; your enemies will fall before you by the sword.U›L%And you will chase your enemies, and they will fall before you by the sword.R›KƒI will also grant peace in the land, so that you may lie down and there will be no one to make you afraid. I will also eliminate harmful animals from the land, and no sword will pass through your land.L›JƒAnd your threshing season will last until grape gathering and the grape gathering <n>[time]</n> will last until planting, and you will eat your bread and be filled and live securely in your land.›Ithen I will give you rain in its season, and the land will yield her produce and the trees of the field bear their fruit.]›H5If you walk in My statutes and keep My commandments and <n>[obediently]</n> do them,W›G)You shall keep My Sabbaths and have reverence for My sanctuary. I am the LORD.s›F	ƒc<pb/><f>[1]</f>'You shall not make idols for yourselves, nor shall you erect an image, a sacred pillar <i>or</i> an obelisk, nor shall you place any figured stone in your land so that you may bow down to it; for I am the LORD your God.›E7For the children of Israel are My servants; My servants, whom I brought out of the land of Egypt. I am the LORD your God.%›D‚E6Even if he is not redeemed during these years <i>and</i> under these provisions, then he shall go free in the Year of Jubilee, he and his children with him.w›Ci5Like a man hired year by year he shall deal with him; he shall not rule over him with harshness in your sight.&›B‚G4And if <i>only</i> a few years remain until the Year of Jubilee, he shall so calculate it with him. He is to refund the proportionate amount for his release.O›Aƒ3If there are still many years <n>[before the Year of Jubilee]</n>, in proportion to them he must refund <n>[to the purchaser]</n> part of the price of his sale for his redemption <i>and</i> release.‚C›@…2Then he <n>[or his redeemer]</n> shall calculate with his purchaser from the year when he sold himself to the purchaser to the Year of Jubilee, and the <n>[original]</n> price of his sale shall be adjusted according to the number of years. The time he was with his owner shall be considered as that of a hired man.$›?‚C1either his uncle or his uncle's son may redeem him, or one of his blood relatives from his family may redeem him; or if he prospers, he may redeem himself.j›>O0then after he is sold he shall have the right of redemption. One of his relatives may redeem him:‚›=„!/'Now if the financial means of a stranger or temporary resident among you become sufficient, and your fellow countryman becomes poor <i>in comparison</i> to him and sells himself to the stranger who is living among you or to the descendants of the stranger's family,‚ ›<„;.You may even bequeath them as an inheritance to your children after you, to receive as a possession; you can use them as permanent slaves. But in respect to your fellow countrymen, the children of Israel, you shall not rule over one another with harshness (severity, oppression).h›;ƒK-Moreover, from the children of the strangers who live as aliens among you, from them you may buy <i>slaves</i> and from their families who are with you, whom they have produced in your land; they may become your possession.›:‚,As for your male and female slaves whom you may haveâ€”you may acquire male and female slaves from the pagan nations that are around you.
›9‚+You shall not rule over him with harshness (severity, oppression), but you are to fear your God <n>[with profound reverence]</n>.|›8s*For the Israelites are My servants whom I brought out of the land of Egypt; they shall not be sold in a slave sale.›7‚)and then he shall leave you, he and his children with him, and shall go back to his own family and return to the property of his fathers.›6‚(but he is to be with you as a hired man, as if he were a temporary resident; he shall serve with you until the Year of Jubilee,s›5ƒa'<pb/>'And if your fellow countryman becomes so poor <n>[in his dealings]</n> with you that he sells himself to you <n>[as payment for a debt]</n>, you shall not let him do the work of a slave <n>[who is ineligible for redemption]</n>,|›4s&I am the LORD your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt to give you the land of Canaan and to be your God.R›3%You shall not give him your money at interest, nor your food at a profit.›2‚5$Do not charge him usurious interest, but fear your God <n>[with profound reverence]</n>, so your countryman may <n>[continue to]</n> live among you.‚Z›1…/#'Now if your fellow countryman becomes poor and his hand falters with you <n>[that is, he has trouble repaying you for something]</n>, then you are to help <i>and</i> sustain him, <n>[with courtesy and consideration]</n> like <n>[you would]</n> a stranger or a temporary resident <n>[without property]</n>, so that he may live among you.g›0I"But the pasture lands of their cities may not be sold, for that is their permanent possession.‚›/„/!Therefore, what is <n>[purchased]</n> from the Levites may be redeemed <n>[by a Levite]</n>, and the house that was sold in the city they possess reverts in the Year of Jubilee, for the houses in the Levite cities are their <n>[ancestral]</n> property among the Israelites.›.‚ As for the cities of the Levites, the Levites have a permanent right of redemption for the houses in the cities which they possess.)›-‚MThe houses of the villages that have no surrounding walls, however, shall be considered as open fields. They may be redeemed, and revert in the Year of Jubilee.o›,ƒYBut if it is not redeemed for him within a full year, then the house that is in the walled city passes permanently <i>and</i> irrevocably to the purchaser throughout his generations. It does not revert back in the Year of Jubilee.-›+‚U<pb/>'If a man sells a house in a walled city, then his right of redemption remains valid for a full year after its sale; his right of redemption lasts a full year.W›*ƒ)But if<f>[3]</f> he is unable to redeem it, then what he has sold shall remain in the hands of the purchaser until the Year of Jubilee; but at the Jubilee it shall revert, and he may return to his property.!›)‚=then he shall calculate the years since its sale and refund the balance to the man to whom he sold it, and so return to his <n>[ancestral]</n> property.›(‚Or in case a man has no relative <n>[to redeem his property]</n>, but he has become more prosperous <i>and</i> has enough to buy it back,:›'‚o<pb/>'If a fellow countryman of yours becomes so poor he has to sell some of his property, then his nearest relative is to come and buy back (redeem) what his relative has sold.›&‚So in all the country that you possess, you are to provide for the redemption of the land <n>[in the Year of Jubilee]</n>.	›%‚
'The land shall not be sold permanently, for the land is Mine; you are <n>[only]</n> foreigners and temporary residents with Me.›$‚'When you are sowing the eighth year, you can still eat old things from the crops, eating the old until the ninth year when its crop comes in.1›#‚]then <n>[this is My answer:]</n> I will order My <n>[special]</n> blessing for you in the sixth year, so that it will produce <n>[sufficient]</n> crops for three years.~›"wAnd if you say, "What are we going to eat in the seventh year if we do not sow <n>[seed]</n> or gather in our crops?"e›!EThen the land will yield its produce, so that you can eat your fill and live securely on it.› ‚	<pb/>'Therefore you shall carry out My statutes and keep My ordinances and do them, so that you may live securely on the land.›‚You shall not wrong one another, but you shall fear your God <n>[with profound reverence]</n>; for I am the LORD your God.Z›ƒ/If the years <n>[until the next Jubilee]</n> are many, you shall increase the price, but if the years remaining are few, you shall reduce the price, because it is the number of crops that he is selling to you.v›ƒgAccording to the number of years after the Jubilee, you shall<f>[2]</f> buy from your friend. And he is to sell to you according to the number of years of crops <n>[which may be harvested before you must restore the property to him]</n>.f›GIf you sell anything to your friend or buy from your friend, you shall not wrong one another.g›I
<pb/>'In this Year of Jubilee each of you shall return to his own <n>[ancestral]</n> property.b›?For it is the Jubilee; it shall be holy to you; you shall eat its crops out of the field.'›‚IThat fiftieth year shall be a Jubilee for you; you shall not sow <n>[seed]</n>, nor reap what reseeds itself, nor gather the grapes of the uncultivated vines.ƒ›†7
And you shall consecrate the fiftieth year and<f>[1]</f> proclaim freedom <n>[for the slaves]</n> throughout the land to all its inhabitants. It shall be a Jubilee (year of remission) for you, and each of you shall return to his own <n>[ancestral]</n> property <n>[that was sold to another because of poverty]</n>, and each of you shall return to his family <n>[from whom he was separated by bondage]</n>.
