But when the cloud had withdrawn from over the tent, behold, Miriam was leprous, as <i>white as</i> snow. And Aaron turned <i>and</i> looked at Miriam, and, behold, she was leprous.^Ÿe7(	<pb/>And the anger of the LORD was kindled against Miriam and Aaron, and He departed.lŸdƒS(<t>"With him I speak mouth to mouth <n>[directly]</n>, </t><t>Clearly <i>and</i> openly and not in riddles; </t><t>And he beholds the form of the LORD. </t><t>Why then were you not afraid to speak against My servant Moses?"</t>yŸcm(<t>"But it is not so with My servant Moses; </t><t>He is entrusted <i>and</i> faithful in all My house.</t><br/>PŸbƒ(<t>And He said, </t><br/><t>"Hear now My words: </t><t>If there is a prophet among you, </t><t>I the LORD will make Myself known to him in a vision </t><t>And I will speak to him in a dream.</t><br/>Ÿa‚!(The LORD came down in a pillar of cloud and stood at the doorway of the tabernacle, and He called Aaron and Miriam, and they came forward.Ÿ`‚+(Suddenly the LORD said to Moses, Aaron, and Miriam, "Come out, you three, to the Tent of Meeting (tabernacle)." And the three of them came out.Ÿ_‚-((<f>[3]</f> Now the man Moses was very humble (gentle, kind, devoid of self-righteousness), more than any man who was on the face of the earth.)Ÿ^(and they said, "Has the LORD really spoken only through Moses? Has He not spoken also through us?" And the LORD heard it. Ÿ]	‚=(<pb/>Now<f>[1]</f> Miriam and Aaron spoke against Moses because of the<f>[2]</f> Cushite woman whom he had married (for he had married a Cushite woman);_Ÿ\9(#From Kibroth-hattaavah the people set out for Hazeroth, and they remained at Hazeroth.@Ÿ[‚{("So that place was named Kibroth-hattaavah (the graves of greediness), because there they buried the people who had been greedy <n>[for more than the manna that God provided them]</n>.6ŸZ‚g(!While the meat was still between their teeth, before it was chewed, the anger of the LORD was kindled against the people, and the LORD struck them with a very severe plague.|ŸYƒs( The people spent all that day and all night and all the next day and caught <i>and</i> gathered the quail (the one who gathered least gathered ten homers) and they spread them out for themselves around the camp <n>[to cure them by drying]</n>.‚1ŸX„](Now there went forth a wind from the LORD and it brought quails from the sea, and let <i>them</i> fall <n>[so they flew low]</n> beside the camp, about a day's journey on this side and on the other side, all around the camp, about two cubits (three feet) <i>deep</i> on the surface of the ground.IŸW
(Then Moses went back into the camp, he and the elders of Israel.&ŸV‚G(But Moses said to him, "Are you jealous for my sake? Would that all the <e>Lord's</e> people were prophets and that the LORD would put His Spirit upon them!"mŸUU(Then Joshua the son of Nun, the attendant of Moses from his youth, said, "My lord Moses, stop them!" ŸT‚;(So a young man ran and told Moses and said, "Eldad and Medad are prophesying <n>[extolling the praises of God and declaring His will]</n> in the camp."rŸSƒ_(<pb/>But two men had remained in the camp; one named Eldad and the other named Medad. The Spirit rested upon them (now they were among those who had been registered, but had not gone out to the Tent), and they prophesied in the camp.‚ŸR„1(Then the LORD came down in the cloud and spoke to him; and He took some of the Spirit who was upon Moses and put <i>Him</i> upon the seventy elders. When the Spirit rested upon them, they prophesied <n>[praising God and declaring His will]</n>, but they did not do it again.?ŸQ‚y(<pb/>So Moses went out and spoke to the people the words of the LORD, and he gathered seventy men from among the elders of the people and stationed them around the Tent (tabernacle)..ŸP‚W(The LORD said to Moses, "Is the <e>Lord's</e> hand (ability, power) limited (short, inadequate)? You shall see now whether My word will come to pass for you or not."(ŸO‚K(Should flocks and herds be slaughtered for them, to be sufficient for them? Or should all the fish of the sea be collected for them to be sufficient for them?"dŸNƒC(But Moses said, "The people, among whom I am, are 600,000 <n>[fighting men]</n> on foot <n>[besides all the women and children]</n>; yet You have said, 'I will give them meat, so that they may eat it for a whole month!'~ŸMƒw(but a whole monthâ€”until it comes out of your nostrils and is disgusting to youâ€”because you have rejected <i>and</i> despised the LORD who is among you, and have wept <n>[in self-pity]</n> before Him, saying, "Why did we come out of Egypt?"'"`ŸL;(You shall eat, not one day, nor two days, nor five days, nor ten days, nor twenty days,‚-ŸK„U(Say to the people, 'Consecrate (separate as holy) yourselves for tomorrow, and you shall eat meat; for you have wept <n>[in self-pity]</n> in the ears of the LORD, saying, "Who will give us meat to eat? For we were well-off in Egypt." Therefore the LORD will give you meat, and you shall eat.{ŸJƒq(Then I will come down and speak with you there, and I will take away some of the Spirit who is upon you, and will put <i>Him</i> upon them; and they shall bear the burden of the people with you, so that you will not have to bear it all alone.‚ŸI„(Accordingly, the LORD said to Moses, "Gather for Me<f>[7]</f> seventy men from among the elders of Israel whom you know to be the elders of the people and their officers; bring them to the Tent of Meeting (tabernacle) and let them stand there with you."ŸH‚?(So if this is the way You are going to deal with me, please kill me at once, if I have found favor in your sight, and do not let me see my wretchedness."_ŸG9(I am not able to carry all these people alone, because the burden is too heavy for me.ŸF}(
