and spare my father and my mother and my brothers and my sisters, along with everyone who belongs to them, and let us all live."^­zƒ7<And now, please swear <n>[an oath]</n> to me by the LORD, since I have shown you kindness, that you also will show kindness to my father's household (family), and give me a pledge of truth <i>and</i> faithfulness,U­yƒ%<When we heard it, our hearts melted <n>[in despair]</n>, and a <n>[fighting]</n> spirit no longer remained in any man because of you; for the LORD your God, He is God in heaven above and on earth beneath.‚­x„<
For we have heard how the LORD dried up the water of the<f>[4]</f> Red Sea for you when you came out of Egypt, and what you did to the two kings of the Amorites who were beyond the Jordan <n>[on the east]</n>, to Sihon and Og, whom you<f>[5]</f> utterly destroyed.f­wƒG<	and she said to the men, "I know that the LORD has given you the land, and that the terror <i>and</i> dread of you has fallen on us, and that all the inhabitants of the land have melted <n>[in despair]</n> because of you.c­vA<<pb/>Now before the two men lay down <n>[to sleep]</n>, Rahab came up to them on the roof,[­uƒ1<So the <n>[king's]</n> men pursued them on the road to the Jordan as far as the<f>[3]</f> fords <n>[east of Jericho]</n>; as soon as the pursuers had gone out after them, the gate <n>[of the city]</n> was shut.7­t‚i<But <n>[in fact]</n> she had brought the scouts up to the roof and had hidden them under the<f>[2]</f> stalks of flax which she had laid in order on the roof <n>[to dry]</n>.;­s‚q<When <i>it was time</i> to close the <n>[city]</n> gate at dark, the men left; I do not know where they went. Pursue them quickly, for <n>[if you do]</n> you will overtake them."
