³Q„F	She said, "I will certainly go with you; nevertheless,<f>[2]</f> the journey that you are about to take will not be for your honor <i>and</i> glory, because the LORD will sell Sisera into the hand of a woman." Then Deborah got up and went with Barak to Kedesh.y³PmFThen Barak said to her, "If you will go with me, then I will go; but if you will not go with me, I will not go."&³O‚GFI will draw out Sisera, the commander of Jabin's army, with his chariots and his infantry to meet you at the river Kishon, and I will hand him over to you.'"‚³N„5FNow she sent <i>word</i> and summoned Barak the son of Abinoam from Kedesh-naphtali, and said to him, "Behold, the LORD, the God of Israel, has commanded, 'Go and march to Mount Tabor, and take with you ten thousand men <n>[of war]</n> from the tribes of Naphtali and Zebulun.E³MƒFShe used to sit <n>[to hear and decide disputes]</n> under the palm tree of Deborah between Ramah and Bethel in the hill country of Ephraim; and the Israelites came up to her for judgment.`³L;F<pb/>Now Deborah, a prophetess, the wife of Lappidoth, was judging Israel at that time.C³KƒFThen the Israelites cried out to the LORD <n>[for help]</n>, for Jabin had nine hundred iron chariots and had oppressed <i>and</i> tormented the sons of Israel severely for twenty years.+³J‚QFSo the LORD sold them into the hand of Jabin king of Canaan, who reigned in Hazor. The commander of his army was Sisera, who lived in<f>[1]</f> Harosheth-hagoyim.Y³I	/F<pb/>But the Israelites again did evil in the sight of the LORD, after Ehud died.
³H‚FAfter Ehud came Shamgar the son of Anath, who struck down six hundred Philistine men with an<f>[13]</f> oxgoad. He too saved Israel.}³GuFSo Moab was subdued <i>and</i> humbled that day under the hand of Israel, and the land was at rest for eighty years.u³FeFThey struck down at that time about ten thousand Moabite men, all strong, courageous men; not a man escaped.m³EƒUFAnd he said to them, "Pursue <i>them</i>, for the LORD has handed over your enemies the Moabites to you." So they went down after him and seized the<f>[12]</f> fords of the Jordan opposite Moab and did not allow anyone to cross.-³D‚UFWhen he had arrived, he blew a trumpet in the hill country of Ephraim; and the sons of Israel went down with him from the hill country, and he was in front of them.u³CeF<pb/>Now Ehud escaped while they lingered, and he passed beyond the sculptured stones and escaped to Seirah.‚³B„FThey waited <n>[a very long time]</n> until they became embarrassed <i>and</i> uneasy, but he still did not open the doors of the upper room. So <n>[finally]</n> they took the key and opened them, and behold, their master had fallen to the floor, dead.?³A‚yF<pb/>When Ehud departed, Eglon's servants came. And when they saw that the doors of the upper room were locked, they said, "He is only<f>[11]</f> relieving himself in the cool room."z³@oFThen Ehud went out into the vestibule and shut the doors of the upper chamber behind him, and locked <i>them</i>.%³?‚EFAnd the hilt also went in after the blade, and the fat closed over the blade, because Ehud did not draw the sword out of his belly; and the refuse came out.}³>uFThen Ehud reached out with his left hand and took the sword from his right thigh, and plunged it into Eglon's belly.4³=‚cFEhud came to him as he was sitting alone in his <n>[private]</n> cool upper chamber, and Ehud said, "I have a message from God for you." And the king got up from his seat.{³<ƒqFBut Ehud himself turned back from the<f>[10]</f> sculptured stones at Gilgal, <n>[and he returned to Eglon]</n> and said <n>[to him]</n>, "I have a secret message for you, O king." Eglon said "Keep silence." And all who attended him left him.q³;]FAnd when Ehud had finished presenting the<f>[9]</f> tribute, he sent away the people who had carried it.X³:+FAnd he brought the tribute to Eglon king of Moab. Now Eglon was a very fat man.³9‚#FNow Ehud made for himself a sword a<f>[7]</f> cubit long, which had two edges, and he bound it on his<f>[8]</f> right thigh under his robe.‚³8„FBut when the Israelites cried out to the LORD <n>[for help]</n>, the LORD raised up a<f>[5]</f> man to rescue them, Ehud the son of Gera, a Benjamite,<f>[6]</f> a left-handed man. And the Israelites sent <i>a gift</i> of tribute by him to Eglon king of Moab.E³7FAnd the Israelites served Eglon king of Moab eighteen years.2³6‚_F
