The Ammonites' king replied to the messengers of Jephthah, "It is because Israel took away<f>[3]</f> my land when they came up from Egypt, from the <n>[river]</n> Arnon as far as the Jabbok and <n>[east of]</n> the Jordan; so now, return those lands peaceably."6µ:‚gF<pb/>Now Jephthah sent messengers to the king of the Ammonites, saying, "What is <n>[the problem]</n> between you and me, that you have come against me to fight in my land?"Bµ9‚FSo Jephthah went with the elders of Gilead, and the people made him head and leader over them. And Jephthah<f>[2]</f> repeated everything that he had promised before the LORD at Mizpah.µ8‚F
The elders of Gilead said to Jephthah, "The LORD is<f>[1]</f> the witness between us; be assured that we will do as you have said."Bµ7‚F	So Jephthah said to the elders of Gilead, "If you take me back <n>[home]</n> to fight against the Ammonites and the LORD gives them over to me, will I <n>[really]</n> become your head?"<µ6‚sFThe elders of Gilead said to Jephthah, "This is why we have turned to you now: that you may go with us and fight the Ammonites and become head over all the inhabitants of Gilead."(µ5‚KFBut Jephthah said to the elders of Gilead, "Did you not hate me and drive me from the house of my father? Why have you come to me now when you are in trouble?"iµ4MFand they said to Jephthah, "Come and be our leader, so that we may fight against the Ammonites."rµ3_FWhen the Ammonites fought against Israel, the elders of Gilead went to get Jephthah from the land of Tob;Uµ2%F<pb/>Now it happened after a while that the Ammonites fought against Israel.8µ1‚kFThen Jephthah fled from his brothers and lived in the land of Tob; and worthless <i>and</i> unprincipled men gathered around Jephthah, and went out <n>[on raids]</n> with him.Uµ0ƒ%FGilead's wife bore him sons, and when his wife's sons grew up, they drove Jephthah out and said to him, "You shall not have an inheritance in our father's house, because you are the son of another woman."µ/	‚F<pb/>Now Jephthah the Gileadite was a brave warrior, but he was the son of a prostitute. Gilead was the father of Jephthah.Aµ.‚}F
