<pb/>After Abimelech died, Tola the son of Puah, the son of Dodo, a man of Issachar, arose to save Israel; and he lived in Shamir, in the hill country of Ephraim.µ‚)F	9Also God repaid all the wickedness of the men of Shechem on their heads, and the curse of Jotham the son of Jerubbaal (Gideon) came upon them.µ‚F	8In this way God repaid the wickedness of Abimelech, which he had done to his father <n>[Jerubbaal]</n> by killing his seventy brothers.Wµ)F	7When the men of Israel saw that Abimelech was dead, each departed to his home.fµƒGF	6Then he called quickly to the young man who was his armor bearer, and said to him, "Draw your sword and kill me, so that it will not be said of me, 'A woman killed him.'" So the young man pierced him through, and he died.nµWF	5But a certain woman threw an upper millstone <n>[down]</n> on Abimelech's head and crushed his skull.µF	4So Abimelech came to the tower and fought against it, and approached the entrance of the tower to burn it down with fire.Uµƒ%F	3But there was a strong (fortified) tower in the center of the city, and all the men and women with all the leaders of the city fled to it and shut themselves in; and they went up on the roof of the tower.Sµ!F	2<pb/>Then Abimelech went to Thebez, and camped against Thebez and took it.‚µ„)F	1So everyone of the people also cut down his branch and followed Abimelech, and they put <i>the branches</i> on top of the inner chamber and set it on fire over those <i>inside</i>, so that all the people in the Tower of Shechem also died, about a thousand men and women.‚'µ„IF	0So Abimelech went up to Mount Zalmon, he and all the people with him; and Abimelech took an axe in his hand and cut down a branch from the trees, picked it up, and laid it on his shoulder. And he said to the people with him, "What you have seen me do, hurry and do just as I have done."aµ=F	/Abimelech was told that all the leaders of the Tower of Shechem were assembled together.4µ‚cF	.<pb/>When all the leaders of the Tower of Shechem heard about it, they entered the inner chamber (stronghold) of the<f>[6]</f> temple of El-berith (the god of a covenant).+µ‚QF	-Abimelech fought against the city that entire day. He took the city and killed the people who were in it; he demolished the city and<f>[5]</f> sowed it with salt.9µ‚mF	,Then Abimelech and the company with him advanced forward and stood in the entrance of the city gate; the two other companies attacked all who were in the field and killed them.PµƒF	+So he took his people and divided them into three companies, and set an ambush in the field; and he looked and saw the people coming out of the city. And he rose up against them and struck them down.^µ
7F	*<pb/>The next day the people went out to the field, and it was reported to Abimelech.~µwF	)Then Abimelech stayed at Arumah, and Zebul drove out Gaal and his relatives so that they could not remain in Shechem.pµ[F	(Abimelech chased him, and he fled before him; and many fell wounded as far as the entrance of the gate.Tµ
#F	'So Gaal went out ahead of the leaders of Shechem and fought with Abimelech.Sµ	ƒ!F	&Then Zebul said to Gaal, "Where is your <n>[boasting]</n> mouth now, you who said, 'Who is Abimelech that we should serve him?' Is this not the people whom you despised? Go out now and fight with them!""µ‚?F	%Gaal spoke again and said, "Look! People are coming down from the highest part of the land, and one company is coming by way of the sorcerers' oak tree."Vµƒ'F	$When Gaal saw the people, he said to Zebul, "Look, people are coming down from the mountaintops." But Zebul said to him, "You are <i>only</i> seeing the shadow of the mountains as <i>if they were</i> men."µ‚7F	#Now Gaal the son of Ebed came out and stood in the entrance of the city gate; then Abimelech and the people who were with him got up from the ambush.µ‚F	"<pb/>So Abimelech and all the people who were with him got up during the night, and set up an ambush against Shechem, in four companies._µƒ9F	!Then in the morning, at sunrise, you will get up early and rush upon <i>and</i> attack the city; and when Gaal and the people who are with him come out against you, you shall do to them<f>[4]</f> whatever you can."sµaF	 Now then, get up during the night, you and the people who are with you, and set up an ambush in the field./µ‚YF	He sent messengers to Abimelech secretly, saying, "Behold, Gaal the son of Ebed and his relatives have come to Shechem; and they are stirring up the city against you.iµMF	<pb/>When Zebul the ruler of the city heard the words of Gaal the son of Ebed, his anger burned..µ ‚WF	If only this people were under my authority! Then I would remove Abimelech and say to him, 'Increase <n>[the size of]</n> your army and come out <n>[to fight]</n>.'"‚´„F	Gaal the son of Ebed said, "Who is Abimelech, and who is Shechem, that we should serve him? Is he not <n>[merely]</n> the son of Jerubbaal and is Zebul not his lieutenant? Serve the men of Hamor the father (founder) of Shechem. Why then should we serve Abimelech?G´~ƒ	F	They went out into the field, gathered the grapes of their vineyard and trod them, and held a festival; and they entered the house of their god, and they ate and drank, and cursed Abimelech.´}yF	<pb/>Now Gaal the son of Ebed came with his relatives, and moved into Shechem; and the leaders of Shechem trusted him.1´|‚]F	The leaders of Shechem set men in ambush against Abimelech on the mountaintops, and they robbed all who passed by them along the road; and it was reported to Abimelech.‚6´{„gF	so that the violence done to the seventy sons of Jerubbaal (Gideon) might come <n>[on the guilty]</n>, and that their <n>[innocent]</n> blood might be laid on Abimelech their brother, who had killed them, and on the leaders of Shechem, who strengthened his hands (encouraged him) to kill his brothers.´z‚9F	Then God sent an<f>[3]</f> evil spirit between Abimelech and the leaders of Shechem; and the leaders of Shechem acted treacherously against Abimelech,4´yeF	Abimelech ruled over Israel for three years.i´xMF	Then Jotham escaped and fled, and went to Beer and lived there because of Abimelech his brother.>´w‚wF	But if not, may fire come out from Abimelech and devour the people of Shechem and Beth-millo; and may fire come out from the people of Shechem and Beth-millo, and devour Abimelech."´v‚-F	if then you have acted in truth and integrity with Jerubbaal and his house this day, then rejoice in Abimelech, and let him also rejoice in you.c´uƒAF	but you have risen against my father's house today and have murdered his sons, seventy men, on one stone, and have made Abimelech, son of his maidservant, king over the people of Shechem, because he is your relativeâ€”b´t?F	for my father fought for you and risked his life and rescued you from the hand of Midian;=´s‚uF	<pb/>"Now then, if you acted in truth and integrity when you made Abimelech king, and if you have dealt well with Jerubbaal and his house, and have dealt with him as he deservedâ€”R´rƒF	So the bramble said to the trees, 'If in truth you are anointing me king over you, then come and take refuge in my shade; but if not, let fire come out of the bramble and devour the cedars of Lebanon.'N´qF	Then all the trees said to the bramble, 'You come and reign over us.'|´psF	
And the vine replied, 'Should I give up my new wine, which makes God and men happy, and go to wave over the trees?'G´o	F	Then the trees said to the vine, 'You come and reign over us.'y´nmF	But the fig tree said to them, 'Should I give up my sweetness and my good fruit, and go to wave over the trees?'K´mF	
Then the trees said to the fig tree, 'You come and reign over us!'´l‚F		But the olive tree said to them, 'Should I give up my fatness by which God and men are honored, and go to wave over the trees?'{´kqF	<f>[2]</f> Once the trees went forth to anoint a king over them, and they said to the olive tree, 'Reign over us!'´j‚5F	<pb/>When they told Jotham, he went and stood at the top of Mount Gerizim and shouted to them, "Hear me, O men of Shechem, so that God may hear you.<´i‚sF	All the men of Shechem and all of<f>[1]</f> Beth-millo assembled together, and they went and made Abimelech king, by the oak (terebinth) of the pillar (memorial stone) at Shechem.´hƒyF	Then he went to his father's house at Ophrah and murdered his brothers the sons of Jerubbaal, seventy men, <n>[in a public execution]</n> on one stone. But Jotham the youngest son of Jerubbaal was left <i>alive</i>, because he had hidden himself.8´g‚kF	And they gave him seventy <i>pieces</i> of silver from the house of Baal-berith, with which Abimelech hired worthless and undisciplined men, and they followed (supported) him.O´fƒF	So his mother's relatives spoke all these words concerning him so that all the leaders of Shechem could hear; and their hearts were inclined to follow Abimelech, for they said, "He is our relative."s´eƒaF	"Speak now in the hearing of all the leaders of Shechem, 'Which is better for you, that seventy men, all of the sons of Jerubbaal rule over you, or that one man rule over you?' Also, remember that I am your <i>own</i> bone and flesh."3´d	‚cF	<pb/>Now Abimelech the son of Jerubbaal (Gideon) went to Shechem to his mother's relatives, and said to them and to the whole clan of the household of his mother's father,´c‚F#nor did they show kindness to the family of Jerubbaal (<i>that is</i>, Gideon) in return for all the good that he had done for Israel.´b‚	F"And the Israelites did not remember the LORD their God, who had rescued them from the hand of all their enemies on every side;´a‚5F!<pb/>Then it came about, as soon as Gideon was dead, that the Israelites again played the prostitute with the Baals, and made Baal-berith their god.´`‚F Gideon the son of Joash died at a good advanced age and was buried in the tomb of Joash his father in Ophrah of the Abiezrites.e´_EFAnd his<f>[6]</f> concubine who was in Shechem also bore him a son, whom he named Abimelech.L´^FNow Gideon had seventy sons born to him, because he had many wives.R´]F<pb/>Jerubbaal (Gideon) the son of Joash went and lived in his own house.H´\ƒFSo Midian was subdued <i>and</i> humbled before the sons of Israel, and they no longer lifted up their heads <n>[in pride]</n>. And the land was at rest for forty years in the days of Gideon.‚´[„FGideon made <n>[all the golden earrings into]</n> an ephod <n>[a sacred, high priest's garment]</n>, and put it in his city of Ophrah, and all Israel<f>[4]</f> worshiped it as an idol there, and<f>[5]</f> it became a trap for Gideon and his household.‚!´Z„=FAnd the weight of the golden earrings that he requested was<f>[3]</f> seventeen hundred <i>shekels</i> of gold, apart from the crescent amulets and pendants and the purple garments which were worn by the kings of Midian, and apart from the chains that were on their camels' necks. ´Y‚;FThey answered, "We will certainly give them <i>to you</i>." And they spread out a garment, and every one of them threw an earring there from his spoil.l´XƒSFAnd Gideon said to them, "I would make a request of you, that each one of you give me an earring from his spoil." For the Midianites had gold earrings, because they were<f>[2]</f> Ishmaelites <n>[who customarily wore them]</n>.~´WwFBut Gideon said to them, "I will not rule over you, and my son will not rule over you; the LORD shall rule over you."2´V‚_F<pb/>Then the men of Israel said to Gideon, "Rule <n>[as king]</n> over us, both you and your son, also your son's son, for you have rescued us from the hand of Midian."^´Uƒ7FThen Zebah and Zalmunna said, "Rise up yourself and strike us; for as the man is, so is his strength." So Gideon arose and killed Zebah and Zalmunna, and took the crescent amulets that were on their camels' necks.I´Tƒ
FSo <n>[to humiliate them]</n> Gideon said to Jether his firstborn, "Stand up, and kill them!" But the youth did not draw his sword, because he was afraid, for he was still <n>[just]</n> a boy.	´S‚
FHe said, "They were my brothers, the sons of my mother. As the LORD lives, if only you had let them live, I would not kill you."B´R‚F<pb/>Then Gideon said to Zebah and Zalmunna, "What kind of men were they whom you killed at Tabor?" And they replied, "They were like you, each one of them resembled the son of a king."I´Q
FHe tore down the tower of Penuel and killed the men of the city.	´P‚
FHe took the elders of the city, and thorns of the wilderness and briars, and with them he<f>[1]</f> punished the men of Succoth.X´Oƒ+FHe came to the men of Succoth and said, "Look here, Zebah and Zalmunna, about whom you taunted me, saying, 'Are Zebah and Zalmunna now in your hand, that we should give bread to your men who are exhausted?'"7´N‚iFHe captured a young man of Succoth and questioned him. And <i>the youth</i> wrote down for him <n>[the names of]</n> the leaders of Succoth and its elders, seventy-seven men.[´M1F
<pb/>Then Gideon the son of Joash returned from the battle by the ascent of Heres.´L‚FWhen Zebah and Zalmunna fled, he pursued them and captured the two kings of Midian, Zebah and Zalmunna, and terrified the entire army.´K‚7FGideon went up by the route of those who lived in tents to the east of Nobah and Jogbehah, and he attacked their camp when the camp was unsuspecting.j´JƒOF
<pb/>Now Zebah and Zalmunna were in Karkor with their armies, about fifteen thousand <n>[fighting]</n> men, all who were left of the entire army of the sons of the east; for a hundred and twenty thousand swordsmen had fallen.m´IUF	So Gideon said also to the men of Penuel, "When I come again in peace, I will tear down this tower."´H‚FHe went from there up to Penuel and spoke similarly to them; and the men of Penuel answered him just as the men of Succoth had answered.3´G‚aFGideon said, "For that <n>[response]</n>, when the LORD has handed over Zebah and Zalmunna to me, I will thrash your bodies with the thorns and briars of the wilderness."´F}FBut the leaders of Succoth said, "Are Zebah and Zalmunna already in your hands, that we should give bread to your army?"9´E‚mFHe said to the men of Succoth, "Please give loaves of bread to the people who are following me since they are exhausted, and I am pursuing Zebah and Zalmunna, kings of Midian.";´D‚qF<pb/>So Gideon came to the Jordan and crossed over <n>[the river]</n>, he and the three hundred men who were with himâ€”exhausted, yet <n>[still]</n> pursuing <n>[the enemy]</n>.A´C‚}FGod has given the leaders of Midian, Oreb and Zeeb into your hands; and what was I able to do in comparison with you?" Then their anger toward him subsided when he made this statement.‚´B„FBut he said to them, "What have I done now <n>[that is so significant]</n> in comparison with you? Is not the gleaning (leftovers) <i>of the grapes</i> of <n>[your tribe of]</n> Ephraim better than the vintage (entire harvest) of <n>[my clan of]</n> Abiezer?S´A	ƒ#F<pb/>And the men of <n>[the tribe of]</n> Ephraim said to Gideon, "What is this thing that you have done to us, not calling us when you went to fight with Midian?" And they quarreled with him vehemently.‚´@„FThen the men of Ephraim took the two leaders of Midian, Oreb and Zeeb, and they killed Oreb at the rock of Oreb, and they killed Zeeb at the wine press of Zeeb, and pursued Midian; and they brought the heads of Oreb and Zeeb to Gideon from across the Jordan.ƒ*´?†OF<pb/>Then Gideon sent messengers throughout the hill country of <n>[the tribe of]</n> Ephraim, saying, "Come down against the Midianites and take <n>[control of]</n> the waters before them <n>[thereby cutting off the Midianites]</n>, as far as Beth-barah and the Jordan <n>[River]</n>." So all the men of Ephraim were assembled together and they took <i>control of</i> the waters, as far as Beth-barah and the Jordan.´>‚FThe men of Israel were summoned together from <n>[the tribes of]</n> Naphtali and Asher and all Manasseh, and they pursued Midian.‚´=„	FWhen Gideon's men blew the three hundred trumpets, the LORD set the sword of one <n>[Midianite]</n> against another even throughout the whole army; and the army fled as far as Beth-shittah toward Zererah, as far as the border of Abel-meholah, by Tabbath.{´<qFThen each stood in his place around the camp; and the entire <n>[Midianite]</n> army ran, crying out as they fled.Z´;ƒ/FWhen three companies blew the trumpets and broke the pitchers, they held the torches in their left hands, and the trumpets in their right hands to blow, and they shouted, "A sword for the LORD and for Gideon!"z´:ƒoFSo Gideon and the hundred men who were with him came to the edge of the camp at the beginning of the<f>[6]</f> middle watch, when the guards had just been changed, and they blew the trumpets and smashed the pitchers that were in their hands.%´9‚EFWhen I and all who are with me blow the trumpet (ram's horn), then all around the camp you also blow the trumpets and shout, 'For the LORD and for Gideon!'"r´8_FAnd he said to them, "Look at me, then do likewise. When I come to the edge of the camp, do just as I do.'´7‚IFHe divided the three hundred men into three companies, and he put trumpets and empty pitchers into the hands of all of them, with torches inside the pitchers.^´6ƒ7F<pb/>When Gideon heard the account of the dream and its interpretation, he bowed down in worship. Then he returned to the camp of Israel and said, "Arise, for the LORD has given the camp of Midian into your hand.":´5‚oFAnd his friend replied, "This <n>[dream]</n> is nothing less than the sword of Gideon the son of Joash, a man of Israel. God has given Midian and the entire camp into his hand."‚?´4„yF
When Gideon arrived,<f>[4]</f> there was a man telling a dream to his friend. And he said, "Listen carefully, I had a dream: there was a loaf of<f>[5]</f> barley bread tumbling into the camp of Midian, and it came to the tent and struck it so that it fell, and turned it upside down so that the tent lay flat."]´3ƒ5FNow the Midianites and the Amalekites and all the sons of the east were lying <n>[camped]</n> in the valley, as countless as locusts; and their camels were without number, as numerous as the sand on the seashore.^´2ƒ7Fand you will hear what they say; and afterward<f>[2]</f> you will have the courage to go down against the camp." Then he went down with Purah his servant to the<f>[3]</f> outposts of the army that was in the camp.l´1SF
But if you are afraid to go down <n>[by yourself]</n>, go with Purah your servant down to the camp,´0‚F	<pb/>Now on that same night the LORD said to Gideon, "Arise, go down against their camp, for I have given it into your hand.‚;´/„qFSo the three hundred men took people's provisions <n>[for the journey]</n> and their trumpets <n>[made of rams' horns]</n> in their hands. And Gideon sent <n>[away]</n> all the <i>other</i> men of Israel, each to his tent, but kept the three hundred men. And the camp of Midian was below him in the valley.A´.‚}FAnd the LORD told Gideon, "With the three hundred men who lapped I will rescue you, and will hand over the Midianites to you. Let all the <i>other</i> people go, each man to his home."5´-‚eFNow the number of those who lapped <n>[the water]</n>, putting their hand to their mouth, was three hundred men, but all the rest of the people kneeled down to drink water.O´,ƒFSo he brought the people down to the water, and the LORD said to Gideon, "You shall separate everyone who laps the water with his tongue as a dog laps, as well as everyone who kneels down to drink."‚M´+…F<pb/>Then the LORD said to Gideon, "There are still too many people; bring them down to the water and I will test them for you there. Therefore it shall be that he of whom I say to you, 'This one shall go with you,' he shall go with you; but everyone of whom I say to you, 'This one shall not go with you,' he shall not go."S´*ƒ!FSo now, proclaim in the hearing of the people, 'Whoever is afraid and trembling, let him turn back and leave Mount Gilead.'" So twenty-two thousand men returned <n>[home]</n>, but ten thousand remained.f´)ƒGF<pb/>Then the LORD said to Gideon, "There are too many people with you for Me to hand over Midian to them, otherwise Israel will boast <n>[about themselves]</n> against Me, saying, 'My own<f>[1]</f> power has rescued me.'S´(	ƒ#F<pb/>Then Jerubbaal (that is, Gideon) and all the people who were with him got up early and camped beside the spring of Harod; and the camp of Midian was north of them by the hill of Moreh in the valley.z´'oF(God did so that night; for it was dry only on the fleece, and there was dew on all the ground <n>[around it]</n>.f´&ƒGF'Then Gideon said to God, "Do not let your anger burn against me, so that I may speak once more. Please let me make a test once more with the fleece; now let only the fleece be dry, and let there be dew on all the ground."´%‚F&And it was so. When he got up early the next morning and squeezed the dew out of the fleece, he wrung from it a bowl full of water.
