·t‚PThe servant in charge of the reapers answered, "She is the young Moabite woman who came back with Naomi from the country of Moab.e·sEPThen Boaz said to his servant who was in charge of the reapers, "Whose young woman is this?"·r‚%PIt was then that Boaz came back from Bethlehem and said to the reapers, "The LORD be with you!" And they answered him, "The LORD bless you!"8·q‚kPSo Ruth went and picked up the leftover grain in a field after the reapers; and she happened to stop at the plot of land belonging to Boaz, who was of the family of Elimelech.c·pƒAPAnd Ruth the Moabitess said to Naomi, "Please let me go to the field and<f>[1]</f> glean among the ears of grain after one <n>[of the reapers]</n> in whose sight I may find favor." Naomi said to her, "Go, my daughter."·o	‚%P<pb/>Now Naomi had a relative of her husband, a man of great wealth <i>and</i> influence, from the family of Elimelech, whose name was Boaz.4·n	‚eP<pb/>So Naomi returned from the country of Moab, and with her Ruth the Moabitess, her daughter-in-law. And they arrived in Bethlehem at the beginning of the barley harvest.@·m	‚}PI left full <n>[with a husband and two sons]</n>, but the LORD has brought me back empty. Why call me Naomi, since the LORD has testified against me and the Almighty has afflicted me?"·l	‚'PShe said to them, "Do not call me Naomi (sweetness); call me Mara (bitter), for the Almighty has caused me great grief <i>and</i> bitterness.E·k	ƒP<pb/>So the two of them went on until they came to Bethlehem. And when they arrived in Bethlehem, the whole city was<f>[3]</f> stirred because of them, and the women asked, "Is this Naomi?"V·j	)PWhen Naomi saw that Ruth was determined to go with her, she said nothing more.:·i	‚qPWhere you die, I will die, and there I will be buried. May the LORD do the same to me <n>[as He has done to you]</n>, and more also, if anything but death separates me from you."L·h	ƒPBut Ruth said, "Do not urge me to leave you or to turn back from following you; for where you go, I will go, and where you lodge, I will lodge. Your people will be my people, and your God, my God.·g	‚'P<pb/>Then Naomi said, "Look, your sister-in-law has gone back to her people and to her gods; turn back <i>and</i> follow your sister-in-law."o·f	[PThen they wept aloud again; and Orpah kissed her mother-in-law <n>[goodbye]</n>, but Ruth clung to her.P·e	ƒP
would you wait until they were grown? Would you go without marrying? No, my daughters; for it is much more<f>[2]</f> difficult for me than for you, because the <e>Lord's</e> hand has gone against me.""·d	‚APGo back, my daughters, go, for I am too old to have a husband. If I said I have hope, and if I actually had a husband tonight and even gave birth to sons,	·c	‚PBut Naomi said, "Go back, my daughters, why should you go with me? Do I still have sons in my womb that may become your husbands?Y·b	/P
