	‚AZ<pb/>Samuel said to Saul, "The Lord sent me to anoint you king over His people Israel. Now listen <i>and</i> pay close attention to the words of the Lord.;»	‚qZ4<pb/>Now the war against the Philistines was severe (brutal, relentless) all the days of Saul; and whenever Saul saw any mighty or courageous man, he recruited him for his staff.W»)Z3Kish was the father of Saul, and Ner the father of Abner was the son of Abiel.»‚Z2The name of Saul's wife was Ahinoam the daughter of Ahimaaz. The commander of his army was named Abner, the son of Ner, Saul's uncle.@»‚{Z1<pb/>Now Saul's sons were Jonathan, Ishvi, and Malchi-shua. The names of his two daughters <i>were these</i>: the name of the firstborn was Merab, and the name of the younger, Michal.{»qZ0He acted valiantly and defeated the Amalekites, and rescued Israel from the hands of those who had plundered them.p»ƒ[Z/When Saul assumed control of the kingdom of Israel, he fought against all his enemies on every side: Moab, the sons (descendants) of Ammon, Edom, the kings of Zobah, and the Philistines. Wherever he turned, he inflicted punishment.a»=Z.Then Saul stopped pursuing the Philistines, and the Philistines went to their own place.‚/»„YZ-But the people said to Saul, "Must Jonathan, who has brought about this great victory in Israel, be put to death? Far from it! As the LORD lives, not one hair of his head shall fall to the ground, for he has worked with God this day." So the people rescued Jonathan and he was not put to death.»‚#Z,Saul answered, "May God do so <n>[to me]</n>, and more also <n>[if I do not keep my word]</n>, for you shall most certainly die, Jonathan."8» ‚kZ+<pb/>Saul said to Jonathan, "Tell me what you have done." So Jonathan told him, "I tasted a little honey with the end of the staff that was in my hand. Here I am, I must die!"cºAZ*Saul said, "Cast <n>[lots]</n> between me and my son Jonathan." And Jonathan was selected.Lº~ƒZ)Therefore, Saul said to the LORD, the God of Israel, "Give a perfect lot <n>[identifying the transgressor]</n>." Then Saul and Jonathan were selected <n>[by lot]</n>, but the other men went free.:º}‚oZ(Then he said to all the Israelites, "<f>[4]</f> You shall be on one side; I and my son Jonathan will be on the other side." The people said to Saul, "Do what seems good to you."6º|‚gZ'For as the LORD lives, who saves Israel, for <i>even</i> if <i>the guilt</i> is in my son Jonathan, he shall most certainly die." But not one of all the people answered him.º{‚1Z&Then Saul said, "Come here, all you who are leaders of the people, and let us find out how this sin <n>[causing God's silence]</n> happened today.ºz‚3Z%Saul asked <n>[counsel]</n> of God, "Shall I go down after the Philistines? Will You hand them over to Israel?" But He did not answer him that day.‚ºy„Z$<pb/>Then Saul said, "Let us go down after the Philistines by night and plunder them until the morning light, and let us not leave a man of them <n>[alive]</n>." They said, "Do whatever seems good to you." Then the priest said, "Let us approach God here."_ºx9Z#And Saul built an altar to the LORD; it was the first altar that he built to the LORD.‚2ºw„_Z"Saul said, "Spread out among the people and tell them, 'Each one of you bring me his ox or his sheep, and butcher it <n>[properly]</n> here and eat; and do not sin against the LORD by eating <n>[the meat with]</n> the blood.'" So that night each one brought his ox with him and butchered it there.jºvƒOZ!Then Saul was told, "Look, the people are sinning against the LORD by eating <n>[the meat]</n> with the blood." And he said, "You have violated <n>[the Law]</n> <i>and</i> acted treacherously; roll a large stone to me today."lºuƒSZ <n>[When night came and the oath ended]</n> the people rushed greedily upon the spoil. They took sheep, oxen, and calves, and slaughtered them on the ground; and they ate them <n>[raw]</n> with the blood <n>[still in them]</n>.lºtSZ<pb/>They struck the Philistines that day from Michmash to Aijalon. And the people were very tired.QºsƒZHow much better <n>[it would have been]</n> if only the people had eaten freely today from the spoil of their enemies which they found! For now the slaughter among the Philistines has not been great."/ºr‚YZThen Jonathan said, "My father has troubled the land <n>[with his foolish curse]</n>. See how my<f>[3]</f> energy is restored because I tasted a little of this honey.Aºq‚}ZBut one of the people told him, "Your father strictly put the people under an oath, saying, 'Cursed be the man who eats food today.'" And the people were exhausted <n>[and hungry]</n>.xºpƒkZBut Jonathan had not heard when his father put the people under the oath. So he put out the end of the staff that was in his hand and dipped it into a honeycomb, and then he put his hand to his mouth, and his<f>[2]</f> energy was restored.4ºo‚cZWhen the people entered the forest, the honey was dripping, but no man put his hand to his mouth <n>[to taste it]</n>, because the people feared the oath <n>[of Saul]</n>._ºn9ZAll <i>the people of</i> the land came to a forest, and there was honey on the ground.xºmƒkZBut the men of Israel were hard-pressed that day, because Saul had put the people under a curse, saying, "Cursed be the man who eats food before evening, and before I have taken vengeance on my enemies." So none of the people ate any food.Sºl!ZSo the LORD saved Israel that day, and the battle spread beyond Beth-aven..ºk‚WZWhen all the men of Israel who had hidden themselves in the hill country of Ephraim heard that the Philistines had fled, they too pursued them closely in the battle.CºjƒZNow the Hebrews who were with the Philistines previously, who went up with them all around in the camp, they also <i>turned</i> to be with the Israelites who were with Saul and