Now it came about on the next day that an evil spirit from God came forcefully on Saul, and he raved <n>[madly]</n> inside his house, while David was playing <i>the harp</i> with his hand, as usual; and there was a spear in Saul's hand.Z¼/Z	Saul looked at David with suspicion <n>[and jealously]</n> from that day forward.o¼ƒYZ<pb/>Then Saul became very angry, for this saying<f>[3]</f> displeased him; and he said, "They have ascribed to David ten thousands, but to me they have ascribed <n>[only]</n> thousands. Now what more can he have but the kingdom?"¼‚+Z<t>The women sang as they played <i>and</i> danced, saying, </t><br/><t>"Saul has slain his thousands, </t><t>And David his ten thousands."</t>‚¼„Z<pb/>As they were coming <n>[home]</n>, when David returned from killing the Philistine, the women came out of all the cities of Israel, singing and dancing, to meet King Saul with tambourines, <n>[songs of]</n> joy, and<f>[2]</f> musical instruments.@¼‚{ZSo David went out wherever Saul sent him, and he acted wisely <i>and</i> prospered; and Saul appointed him over the men of war. And it pleased all the people and also Saul's servants.¼‚/ZJonathan stripped himself of the outer robe that he was wearing and gave it to David, with his armor, including his sword, his bow, and his belt.R¼ ZThen Jonathan made a covenant with David because he loved him as himself.S»!ZSaul took David that day and did not let him return to his father's house.»~	‚/Z<pb/>When David had finished speaking to Saul, the soul of Jonathan was bonded to the soul of David, and<f>[1]</f> Jonathan loved him as himself.»}‚Z:Saul asked him, "Whose son are you, young man?" And David answered, "<i>I am</i> the son of your servant Jesse of Bethlehem.""»|‚?Z9When David returned from killing <n>[Goliath]</n> the Philistine, Abner took him and brought him before Saul with the head of the Philistine in his hand.8»{mZ8The king said, "Ask whose son the young man is."N»zƒZ7<pb/>When Saul saw David going out against the Philistine, he said to Abner the captain of the army, "Abner, whose son is this young man?" And Abner answered, "By your life, O king, I do not know."t»ycZ6Then David took the head of the Philistine and brought it to Jerusalem, but he put his weapons in his tent.d»xCZ5The sons of Israel returned from their pursuit of the Philistines and plundered their camp.|»wƒsZ4The men of Israel and Judah stood with a shout and pursued the Philistines as far as the entrance to the valley and the gates of Ekron. And the <n>[fatally]</n> wounded Philistines fell along the way to Shaaraim, even as far as Gath and Ekron.a»vƒ=Z3So he ran and stood over the Philistine, grasped his sword and drew it out of its sheath and killed him, and cut off his head with it. When the Philistines saw that their <n>[mighty]</n> champion was dead, they fled.(»u‚KZ2<pb/>So David triumphed over the Philistine with a sling and a stone, and he struck down the Philistine and killed him; but there was no sword in David's hand.A»t‚}Z1David put his hand into his bag and took out a stone and slung it, and it struck the Philistine on his forehead. The stone penetrated his forehead, and he fell face down on the ground.»s‚	Z0<pb/>When the Philistine rose and came forward to meet David, David ran quickly toward the battle line to meet the Philistine.3»r‚aZ/and that this entire assembly may know that the LORD does not save with the sword or with the spear; for the battle is the <e>Lord's</e> and He will hand you over to us."‚"»q„?Z.This day the LORD will hand you over to me, and I will strike you down and cut off your head. And I will give the corpses of the army of the Philistines this day to the birds of the sky and the wild beasts of the earth, so that all the earth may know that there is a God in Israel,X»pƒ+Z-Then David said to the Philistine, "You come to me with a sword, a spear, and a<f>[14]</f> javelin, but I come to you in the name of the LORD of hosts, the God of the armies of Israel, whom you have taunted.	»o‚
Z,The Philistine also said to David, "Come to me, and I will give your flesh to the birds of the sky and the beasts of the field."»n‚'Z+The Philistine said to David, "Am I a dog, that you come to me with <n>[shepherd's]</n> staffs?" And the Philistine cursed David by his gods.C»mƒZ*When the Philistine looked around and saw David, he derided <i>and</i> disparaged him because he was <n>[just]</n> a young man, with a ruddy <i>complexion</i>, and a handsome appearance._»l9Z)<pb/>The Philistine came and approached David, with his shield-bearer in front of him.‚»k„)Z(Then he took his <n>[shepherd's]</n> staff in his hand and chose for himself five<f>[13]</f> smooth stones out of the stream bed, and put them in his shepherd's bag which he had, that is, in his shepherd's pouch. With his sling in his hand, he approached the Philistine.m»jƒUZ'Then David fastened his sword over his armor and tried to walk, <n>[but he could not,]</n> because he was not used to them. And David said to Saul, "I cannot go with these, because I am not used to them." So David took them off.|»isZ&Then Saul dressed David in his garments and put a bronze helmet on his head, and put a coat of mail (armor) on him.U»hƒ%Z%David said, "The LORD who rescued me from the paw of the lion and from the paw of the bear, He will rescue me from the hand of this Philistine." And Saul said to David, "Go, and may the LORD be with you.">»g‚wZ$Your servant has killed both the lion and the bear; and this uncircumcised Philistine will be like one of them, since he has taunted <i>and</i> defied the armies of the living God."'»f‚IZ#I went out after it and attacked it and rescued the lamb from its mouth; and when it rose up against me, I seized it by its whiskers and struck and killed it.»e‚Z"But David said to Saul, "Your servant was tending his father's sheep. When a lion or a bear came and took a lamb out of the flock,1»d‚]Z!Then Saul said to David, "You are not able to go against this Philistine to fight him. For you are <n>[only]</n> a young man and he has been a warrior since his youth."»c‚'Z David said to Saul, "Let no man's<f>[12]</f> courage fail because of him (Goliath). Your servant will go out and fight with this Philistine."h»bKZWhen the words that David spoke were heard, the men reported them to Saul, and he sent for him.»a‚ZThen David turned away from Eliab to someone else and asked the same question; and the people gave him the same answer as the first time.^»`7ZBut David said, "What have I done now? Was it not just a <n>[harmless]</n> question?"‚Z»_…/Z<pb/>Now Eliab his oldest brother heard what he said to the men; and Eliab's<f>[11]</f> anger burned against David and he said, "Why have you come down here? With whom did you leave those few sheep in the wilderness? I know your presumption (overconfidence) and the evil of your heart; for you have come down in order to see the battle."\»^3ZThe men<f>[10]</f> told him, "That is what will be done for the man who kills him."‚)»]„MZ<pb/>Then David spoke to the men who were standing by him, "What will be done for the man who kills this Philistine and removes the disgrace <n>[of his taunting]</n> from Israel? For who is this uncircumcised Philistine that he has taunted <i>and</i> defied the armies of the living God?"‚9»\„mZThe men of Israel said, "Have you seen this man who is coming up? Surely he is coming up to defy Israel. The king will reward the man who kills him with great riches, and will give him his daughter <n>[in marriage]</n> and make his father's house (family) free <n>[from taxes and service]</n> in Israel."c»[AZ<pb/>When the men of Israel all saw the man, they fled from him, and were very frightened.Y»Zƒ-ZAs he was talking with them, behold, the champion, the Philistine of Gath named Goliath, was coming up from the army of the Philistines, and he spoke these same words <i>again</i>; and David heard <i>him</i>.»Y}ZThen David left his provisions in the care of the supply keeper, and ran to the ranks and came and greeted his brothers.S»X!ZIsrael and the Philistines drew up in battle formation, army against army.z»WƒoZSo David got up early in the morning, left the flock with a keeper, picked up <i>the provisions</i> and went just as Jesse had directed him. And he came to the encampment as the army was going out in battle formation shouting the battle cry.p»V[ZNow they are with Saul and all the men of Israel in the Valley of Elah, fighting with the Philistines."»U‚ZAlso take these ten cuts of cheese to the commander of the<f>[9]</f> unit. See how your brothers are doing and bring back news of them.2»T‚_Z<pb/>Then Jesse said to David his son, "Take for your brothers an ephah of this roasted grain and these ten loaves of bread and run quickly to the camp to your brothers.i»SMZThe Philistine <n>[Goliath]</n> came out morning and evening, and took his stand for forty days.Y»R-Zbut David went back and forth from Saul to tend his father's flock at Bethlehem.D»QZDavid was the youngest. Now the three oldest followed Saul,*»P‚OZ
His three older sons had followed Saul into battle. The names of his three sons who went to battle were Eliab, the firstborn; next, Abinadab; and third, Shammah.>»O‚wZ<pb/>Now David was the son of the<f>[8]</f> Ephrathite of Bethlehem in Judah, named Jesse, who had eight sons. Jesse was old in the days of Saul, advanced <i>in years</i> among men.m»NUZWhen Saul and all Israel heard these words of the Philistine, they were dismayed and greatly afraid.~»MwZ
Again the Philistine said, "I defy the battle lines of Israel this day; give me a man so that we may fight together."4»L‚cZ	If he is able to fight with me and kill me, then we will become your servants; but if I prevail against him and kill him, then you shall become our servants and serve us."s»KƒaZGoliath stood and shouted to the battle lines of Israel, saying to them, "Why have you come out to draw up for battle? Am I not the Philistine and are you not servants of Saul? Choose a man for yourselves and have him come down to me.[»Jƒ1ZThe <n>[wooden]</n> shaft of his spear was like a<f>[5]</f> weaver's beam; the blade-head of his spear <i>weighed</i><f>[6]</f> six hundred shekels of iron. And a<f>[7]</f> shield-bearer walked in front of him.t»IcZ<i>He had</i> bronze shin protectors on his legs and a bronze<f>[4]</f> javelin hung between his shoulders.»H‚5Z<i>He had</i> a bronze helmet on his head, and wore a coat of scale-armor (overlapping metal plates) which weighed<f>[3]</f>5,000 shekels of bronze.»G‚%ZThen a<f>[1]</f> champion came out from the camp of the Philistines named Goliath of Gath, whose height was<f>[2]</f> six cubits and a span.»F‚1ZThe Philistines were standing on the mountain on one side and Israel was standing on the mountain on the other side, with the valley between them.»E‚3ZSaul and the men of Israel were gathered together and they camped in the Valley of Elah, and assembled in battle formation to meet the Philistines.1»D	‚_Z<pb/>Now the Philistines gathered their armies for battle and were assembled at Socoh, which belongs to Judah; and they camped between Socoh and Azekah, in Ephes-dammim.R»CƒZSo it came about that whenever the <n>[evil]</n> spirit from God was on Saul, David took a harp and played it with his hand; so Saul would be refreshed and be well, and the evil spirit would leave him.»BZSaul sent <i>word</i> to Jesse, saying, "Please let David<f>[3]</f> be my attendant, for he has found favor in my sight."{»AqZThen David came to Saul and attended him. Saul loved him greatly and <n>[later]</n> David became his armor bearer.»@‚ZJesse took a donkey <n>[loaded with]</n> bread and a jug of wine and a young goat, and sent them to Saul with David his son.d»?CZSo Saul sent messengers to Jesse and said, "Send me David your son, who is with the flock."z»>ƒoZOne of the young men said, "Behold, I have seen a son of Jesse the Bethlehemite who is a skillful musician, a brave <i>and</i> competent man, a warrior, discerning (prudent, eloquent) in speech, and a handsome man; and the LORD is with him."W»=)ZSo Saul told his servants, "Find me a man who plays well and bring him to me."i»<ƒMZ"Let our lord now command your servants who are here before you to find a man who plays skillfully on the harp; and when the evil spirit from God is on you, he shall play <i>the harp</i> with his hand, and you will be well."Y»;-ZSaul's servants said to him, "Behold, an evil spirit from God is tormenting you.»:}Z<pb/>Now the Spirit of the LORD departed from Saul, and an evil spirit from the Lord tormented <i>and</i> terrified him.L»9ƒZ
Then Samuel took the horn of oil and anointed David in the presence of his brothers; and the Spirit of the LORD came mightily upon David from that day forward. And Samuel arose and went to Ramah.K»8ƒZSo Jesse sent <i>word</i> and brought him in. Now he had a ruddy complexion, with beautiful eyes and a handsome appearance. The LORD said <n>[to Samuel]</n>, "Arise, anoint him; for this is he."‚»7„5ZThen Samuel said to Jesse, "Are all your sons here?" Jesse replied, "There is still one left, the youngest; he is tending the sheep." Samuel said to Jesse, "Send <i>word</i> and bring him; because we will not sit down <n>[to eat the sacrificial meal]</n> until he comes here."»6‚Z
Jesse had seven of his sons pass before Samuel. But Samuel said to Jesse, "The LORD has not chosen <n>[any of]</n> these."_»59Z	Next Jesse had Shammah pass by. And Samuel said, "The LORD has not chosen him either."»4yZThen Jesse called Abinadab and had him pass before Samuel. But Samuel said, "The LORD has not chosen this one either."z»3ƒoZBut the LORD said to Samuel, "Do not look at his appearance or at the height of his stature, because I have rejected him. For the LORD sees not as man sees; for man looks<f>[2]</f> at the outward appearance, but the LORD looks at the heart."»2‚3Z<pb/>So it happened, when they had come, he looked at Eliab <n>[the eldest son]</n> and thought, "Surely the <e>Lord's</e> anointed is before Him."J»1ƒZAnd he said, "In peace; I have come to sacrifice to the LORD. Consecrate yourselves and come with me to the sacrifice." He also consecrated Jesse and his sons and invited them to the sacrifice.»0‚-ZSo Samuel did what the LORD said, and came to Bethlehem. And the elders of the city came trembling to meet him and said, "Do you come in peace?"#»/‚AZYou shall invite Jesse to the sacrifice, and I will show you what you shall do <n>[after that]</n>; and you shall anoint for Me the one whom I designate."E».ƒZBut Samuel said, "How can I go? When Saul hears <i>about it</i>, he will kill me." And the LORD said, "Take a heifer from the herd with you and say, 'I have come to sacrifice to the Lord.'‚»-	ƒZ<pb/>The LORD said to Samuel, "How long will you grieve for Saul, when I have rejected him as king over Israel?<f>[1]</f> Fill your horn with oil and go; I will send you to Jesse the Bethlehemite, for I have chosen a king for Myself among his sons."»,‚9Z#Samuel did not see Saul again until the day of his death, for Samuel grieved over Saul. And the LORD regretted that He had made Saul king over Israel.Y»+-Z"<pb/>Then Samuel went to Ramah, but Saul went up to his house in Gibeah of Saul.'»*‚IZ!Samuel said, "As your sword has made women childless, so shall your mother be childless among women." And Samuel cut Agag in pieces before the LORD in Gilgal.;»)‚qZ <pb/>Then Samuel said, "Bring me Agag, the king of the Amalekites." And Agag came to him<f>[5]</f> cheerfully. And Agag said, "Surely the bitterness of death has come to an end."I»(
ZSo Samuel went back following Saul, and Saul worshiped the LORD.,»'‚SZSaul said, "I have sinned; but please honor me now before the elders of my people and before Israel, and go back with me, so that I may worship the LORD your God."»&‚9ZAlso the Splendor <i>and</i> Glory <i>and</i> Eminence of Israel will not lie or change His mind; for He is not a man that He should change His mind."»%‚ZSo Samuel said to him, "The LORD has torn the kingdom of Israel from you today and has given it to your neighbor, who is better than you.y»$mZAs Samuel turned to go <n>[away]</n>, <i>Saul</i> grabbed the hem of his robe <n>[to stop him]</n>, and it tore.&»#‚GZBut Samuel said to Saul, "I will not return with you; for you have rejected the word of the LORD, and the LORD has rejected you from being king over Israel."X»"+ZNow, please, pardon my sin and return with me, so that I may worship the LORD."+»!‚QZ<pb/>Then Saul said to Samuel, "I have sinned; for I have transgressed the command of the LORD and your words, because I feared the people and obeyed their voice.‚» „1Z<t>"For rebellion is as <n>[serious as]</n> the sin of divination (fortune-telling), </t><t>And disobedience is as <n>[serious as]</n> false religion and idolatry. </t><t>Because you have rejected the word of the LORD, </t><t>He also has rejected<f>[4]</f> you as king."</t>‚»„Z<t>Samuel said, </t><br/><t>"Has the LORD as great a delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices </t><t>As in obedience to the voice of the LORD? </t><t>Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice, </t><t>And to heed <n>[is better]</n> than the fat of rams.</t><br/>1»‚]Z"But the people took some of the spoil, sheep and oxen, the best of the things <n>[that were]</n> to be totally destroyed, to sacrifice to the LORD your God at Gilgal."]»ƒ5Z<pb/>Saul said to Samuel, "I have obeyed the voice of the LORD, and have gone on the mission on which the LORD sent me, and have brought back Agag the king of Amalek, and have completely destroyed the Amalekites.-»‚UZWhy did you not obey the voice of the LORD, but <n>[instead]</n> swooped down on the plunder <n>[with shouts of victory]</n> and did evil in the sight of the LORD?"»‚1Zand the LORD sent you on a mission, and said, 'Go, totally destroy the sinners, the Amalekites, and fight against them until they are eliminated.'O»ƒZ<pb/>Samuel said, "Is it not true that even though you were small (insignificant) in your own eyes, you were <i>made</i> the head of the tribes of Israel? And the LORD anointed you king over Israel,~»wZThen Samuel said to Saul, "Stop, and let me tell you what the LORD said to me last night." Saul said to him, "Speak."N»ƒZSaul said, "They have brought them from the Amalekites, for the people spared the best of the sheep and oxen<f>[3]</f> to sacrifice to the LORD your God; but the rest we have destroyed completely."x»kZBut Samuel said, "What then is this bleating of the sheep in my ears, and the lowing of the oxen which I hear?"»}Z
So Samuel came to Saul, and Saul said to him, "Blessed are you of the LORD. I have carried out the command of the LORD."q»ƒ]ZWhen Samuel got up early in the morning to meet Saul, he was told, "Saul came to<f>[2]</f> Carmel, and behold, he set up for himself a monument <n>[commemorating his victory]</n>, then he turned and went on and went down to Gilgal."N»ƒZ"I regret that I made Saul king, for he has turned away from following Me and has not carried out My commands." Samuel was angry <n>[over Saul's failure]</n> and he cried out to the LORD all night.9»oZ
Then the word of the LORD came to Samuel, saying,‚»„Z	Saul and the people spared Agag and the best of the sheep, the oxen, the fatlings, the lambs, and everything that was good, and they were not willing to destroy them entirely; but everything that was undesirable or worthless they destroyed completely.»‚ZHe captured Agag the king of the Amalekites alive, though he totally destroyed all <n>[the rest of]</n> the people with the sword.[»1ZSaul defeated the Amalekites, from Havilah as far as Shur, which is east of Egypt.
