Then David said, "O LORD, the God of Israel, Your servant has heard for certain that Saul intends to come to Keilah, to destroy the city on my account.|½sZ	But David knew that Saul was plotting evil against him; and he said to Abiathar the priest, "Bring the ephod here."o½YZSo Saul summoned all the people (soldiers) for war, to go down to Keilah to besiege David and his men.8½
‚kZNow when Saul was informed that David had come to Keilah, Saul said, "God has handed him over to me, for he shut himself in by entering a city that has double gates and bars."q½	]Z<pb/>When Abiathar the son of Ahimelech fled to David at Keilah, he came down with an ephod in his hand.7½‚iZSo David and his men went to Keilah and fought the Philistines; he drove away their cattle and struck them with a great slaughter. So David rescued the inhabitants of Keilah.½‚!ZThen David inquired of the LORD again. And the LORD answered him, "Arise, go down to Keilah, for I will hand over the Philistines to you."½‚9ZBut David's men said to him, "Listen, we are afraid here in Judah. How much more then if we go to Keilah against the battle lines of the Philistines?"'½‚IZSo David inquired of the LORD, saying, "Shall I go and attack these Philistines?" And the Lord said to David, "Go and attack the Philistines and save Keilah.")½	‚OZ<pb/>Then they told David, saying, "Behold, the Philistines are fighting against Keilah and are plundering (robbing) the threshing floors <n>[of the grain]</n>."m½UZStay with me; do not be afraid, for he who seeks my life seeks your life, but you are safe with me."[½ƒ1ZThen David said to Abiathar, "I knew that day, when Doeg the Edomite was there, that he would certainly tell Saul. I have brought about <i>the death</i> of everyone in your father's household (extended family).N½ZAbiathar told David that Saul had murdered the <e>Lord's</e> priests.l½ SZ<pb/>But one of the sons of Ahimelech the son of Ahitub, named Abiathar, escaped and fled to David.E¼ƒZAnd he struck Nob the city of the priests with the edge of the sword, both men and women, children and infants; also <i>he struck</i> oxen and donkeys and sheep with the edge of the sword._¼~ƒ9ZSo the king said to Doeg, "You turn around and attack the priests." And Doeg the Edomite turned and<f>[5]</f> attacked the priests, and that day he killed eighty-five men who wore the <n>[priest's]</n> linen ephod.‚M¼}…ZAnd the king said to the guards who stood around him, "Turn around and kill the priests of the LORD, because their<f>[4]</f> loyalty also is with David, and because they knew that he was fleeing and did not inform me." But the servants of the king were not willing to put out their hands to attack the <e>Lord's</e> priests.z¼|oZBut Saul said, "Be assured that you shall die, Ahimelech, you and all your father's household (extended family)."y¼{ƒmZDid I <i>just</i> begin to inquire of God for him today? Far be it from me! Do not let the king impute any <i>guilt</i> to his servant or to any of the household of my father, for your servant knows nothing at all about this entire matter."|¼zƒsZ<pb/>Then Ahimelech answered the king, "And who among all your servants is as faithful <i>and</i> trustworthy as David, who is the king's son-in-law, and who is captain over your guard <n>[and your confidant]</n>, and is honored in your house?p¼yƒ[Z
Saul said to him, "Why have you and the son of Jesse conspired against me, in that you have given him bread and a sword and have inquired of God for him, so he would rebel against me by lying in ambush, as <i>he does</i> this day?"p¼x[ZThen Saul said, "Listen now, son of Ahitub." He replied, "Here I am <n>[at your service]</n>, my lord."8¼w‚kZ<pb/>Then the king sent someone to call Ahimelech the priest, the son of Ahitub, and all his father's household, the priests who were at Nob; and all of them came to the king.r¼v_Z
Ahimelech inquired of the LORD for him, and gave him provisions and the sword of Goliath the Philistine."*¼u‚OZ	Then Doeg the Edomite, who was standing with Saul's servants, replied, "I saw the son of Jesse coming to Nob, to Ahimelech <n>[the priest]</n> the son of Ahitub.‚+¼t„QZFor all of you have conspired against me so that no one<f>[3]</f> informs me when my son <n>[Jonathan]</n> makes <i>a covenant</i> with the son of Jesse. None of you cares about me or informs me that my son has stirred up my servant against me to lie in ambush, as <i>he does</i> this day?"_¼sƒ9ZSaul said to his servants who were standing around him, "Listen now, you Benjamites! Will the son of Jesse also give every one of you fields and vineyards? Will he make you all commanders of thousands and hundreds?o¼rƒYZ<pb/>But Saul heard that David and the men who were with him had been discovered. Now Saul was in Gibeah, sitting under the tamarisk tree on the high place with his spear in his hand, and all his servants were standing around him.%¼q‚EZThen the prophet Gad said to David, "Do not stay in the stronghold; leave, and go into the land of Judah." So David left and went into the forest of Hereth.¼p‚ZThen he left them with the king of Moab; and they stayed with him all the time that David was in the<f>[2]</f> stronghold.]¼oƒ5Z<pb/>And David went from there to Mizpah of Moab; and he said to the king of Moab, "Please let my<f>[1]</f> father and my mother come <n>[out of Judah]</n> and stay with you until I know what God will do for me."K¼nƒZEveryone who was suffering hardship, and everyone who was in debt, and everyone who was discontented gathered to him; and he became captain over them. There were about four hundred men with him.+¼m	‚SZ<pb/>So David departed from there and escaped to the cave of Adullam; and when his brothers and all his father's house heard about it, they went down there to him.~¼lwZDo I lack madmen, that you bring this one to behave like a madman in my presence? Shall this one come into my house?"q¼k]ZThen Achish said to his servants, "Look, you see that the man is insane. Why have you brought him to me?9¼j‚mZ
So <n>[fearing for his life]</n> he changed his behavior in their sight, and acted insanely in their hands, and he scribbled on the doors of the gate, and drooled on his beard.\¼i3Z<pb/>David took these words to heart and was greatly afraid of Achish king of Gath.m¼hƒUZ<t>The servants of Achish said to him, "Is this not David the king of the land? Did they not sing in praise of this one as they danced, saying, </t><br/><t>'Saul has slain his thousands, </t><t>And David his ten thousands'?"</t>f¼gGZ
<pb/>Then David arose and fled from Saul that day, and went to Achish king of<f>[7]</f> Gath.‚'¼f„IZ	Then the priest said, "The sword of Goliath the Philistine, whom you killed in the Valley of Elah, is here wrapped in a cloth behind the ephod; if you would take it for yourself, do so. For there is no other here except for it." And David said, "There is none like that; give it to me."E¼eƒZ<pb/>David said to Ahimelech, "Do you not have a sword or spear here on hand? For I brought neither my sword nor my <n>[other]</n> weapons with me, because the king's business was urgent."¼d‚#Z<pb/>Now one of Saul's servants was there that day, detained before the LORD; his name was Doeg the Edomite, the chief of Saul's shepherds.r¼cƒ_ZSo the priest gave him the consecrated <i>bread</i>; for there was no bread there except the bread of the<f>[6]</f> Presence which was removed from before the LORD in order to put hot bread <i>in its place</i> when it was taken away.‚:¼b„oZDavid answered the priest, "Be assured that<f>[4]</f> women have been kept from us in these three days since I set out, and the<f>[5]</f> bodies of the young men were consecrated (ceremonially clean), although it was an ordinary (unconsecrated) journey; so how much more will their vessels be holy today?"S¼aƒ!ZThe priest answered David, "There is no ordinary (unconsecrated) bread on hand, but there is<f>[3]</f> consecrated bread; <n>[you may have it]</n> if only the young men have kept themselves from women."m¼`UZNow what <n>[food]</n> do you have on hand? Give me five loaves of bread, or whatever can be found."‚
¼_„ZDavid said to Ahimelech the priest, "The king has commissioned me with a matter and has told me, 'Let no one know anything about the matter for which I am sending you and with which I have commissioned you. I have directed the young men to a certain place.'G¼^	ƒZ<pb/>Then David went to<f>[1]</f> Nob to<f>[2]</f> Ahimelech the priest; and Ahimelech came trembling <n>[in fear]</n> to meet David and said to him, "Why are you alone, and no one with you?"‚¼]„5Z*Jonathan told David, "Go<f>[16]</f> in safety, inasmuch as we have sworn to each other in the name of the LORD, saying, 'The LORD shall be between me and you, and between my descendants and yours forever.'"<f>[17]</f> Then he stood and left, while Jonathan went into the city.‚¼\„#Z)As soon as the boy was gone, David got up from the south side <n>[beside the mound of stones]</n> and fell on his face to the ground <n>[in submission and respect]</n>, and bowed three times. Then they kissed one another and wept together, but David <i>wept</i> more.[¼[1Z(Jonathan gave his weapons to his boy and said to him, "Go, take them to the city."^¼Z7Z'But the boy was not aware of anything; only Jonathan and David knew about the matter.¼Y‚Z&And Jonathan called out after the boy, "Hurry, be quick, do not stay!" So Jonathan's boy picked up the arrow and came back to his master.¼X}Z%When the boy came to the place where Jonathan had shot the arrow, Jonathan called to him, "Is the arrow not beyond you?"¼W‚Z$And he said to his boy, "Run, please find the arrows which I am about to shoot." As the boy ran, he shot an arrow past him.u¼VeZ#<pb/>In the morning Jonathan went out to the field for the meeting with David, and a young boy was with him.U¼Uƒ%Z"Then Jonathan stood up from the table in the heat of anger, and ate no food on that second day of the new moon (month), for he grieved <i>and</i> worried about David because his father had dishonored him.¼T‚5Z!Then Saul hurled his spear at him to strike him down, so Jonathan knew <n>[without any doubt]</n> that his father had decided to put David to death.\¼S3Z Jonathan answered Saul his father, "Why must he be put to death? What has he done?"]¼Rƒ5ZFor as long as the son of Jesse lives on the earth, neither you <n>[as heir to the throne]</n> nor your kingdom will be established. So now, send <n>[someone]</n> and bring him to me, for he<f>[15]</f> must die."¼QƒyZThen Saul's anger burned against Jonathan and he said to him, "You son of a<f>[14]</f> wayward, rebellious woman! Do I not know that you have chosen the son of Jesse <n>[over me]</n> to your own shame, and to the shame of your mother's nakedness?‚¼P„+ZHe said, 'Please let me go because our family is holding a sacrifice in the city, and my brother has commanded me to attend. Now, if I have found favor in your eyes, please let me slip away so that I may see my brothers.' That is why he has not come to the king's table."d¼OCZJonathan answered Saul, "David earnestly asked me for permission <i>to go</i> to Bethlehem.[¼Nƒ1ZBut on the next day, the second <i>day</i> of the new moon, David's place was empty <n>[again]</n>; and Saul said to Jonathan his son, "Why has the son of Jesse not come to the meal, either yesterday or today?"
