%	3ºÄ½¢ŒðT¢                               ‚Àg„dI also gave you your master's house, and put your master's wives into your<f>[2]</f> care <i>and</i> under your protection, and I gave you the house (royal dynasty) of Israel and of Judah; and if <i>that had been</i> too little, I would have given you much more!.Àf‚Wd<pb/>Then Nathan said to David, "You are the man! Thus says the LORD, the God of Israel, 'I anointed you as king over Israel, and I spared you from the hand of Saul.Àe‚+dHe shall make restitution for the ewe lamb four times as much <n>[as the lamb was worth]</n>, because he did this thing and had no compassion."Àd‚+d<pb/>Then David's anger burned intensely against the man, and he said to Nathan, "As the LORD lives, the man who has done this deserves to die.‚Àc„d<t>"Now a traveler (visitor) came to the rich man, </t><t>And to avoid taking one from his own flock or herd </t><t>To prepare <n>[a meal]</n> for the traveler who had come to him, </t><t>He took the poor man's ewe lamb and prepared it for<f>[1]</f> his guest."</t>‚Àb„)d<t>But the poor man had nothing but one little ewe lamb </t><t>Which he had purchased and nourished; </t><t>And it grew up together with him and his children. </t><t>It ate his food, drank from his cup, it lay in his arms, </t><t>And was like a daughter to him.</t><br/>OÀad<t>"The rich man had a very large number of flocks and herds,</t><br/>1À`	‚_d<pb/><t>And the LORD sent Nathan <n>[the prophet]</n> to David. He came and said to him, </t><br/><t>"There were two men in a city, one rich and the other poor.</t><br/>rÀ_ƒ_dAnd when the time of mourning was past, David sent <i>word</i> and had her brought to his house, and she became his wife and bore him a son. But the thing that David had done <n>[with Bathsheba]</n> was evil in the sight of the LORD.vÀ^gd<pb/>When Uriah's wife <n>[Bathsheba]</n> heard that her husband Uriah was dead, she mourned for her husband.nÀ]ƒWdThen David said to the messenger, "Tell Joab this, 'Do not let this thing disturb you, for the sword devours one <n>[side]</n> as well as another. Strengthen your battle against the city and overthrow it'; and so encourage Joab."À\‚'dThen the archers shot at your servants from the wall. Some of the king's servants are dead, and your servant Uriah the Hittite is also dead."HÀ[ƒdThe messenger said to David, "The men indeed prevailed against us and came out to us in the field, but we were on them <i>and</i> pushed them as far as the entrance of the <n>[city]</n> gate.sÀZad<pb/>So the messenger left, and he came and told David everything that Joab had sent him <i>to report</i>.‚ÀY„dWho killed Abimelech the son of Jerubbesheth (Gideon)? Was it not a woman who threw an upper millstone on him from the wall so that he died at Thebez? Why did you go so near the wall?' Then you shall say, 'Your servant Uriah the Hittite is also dead.'"/ÀX‚Ydthen if the king becomes angry and he says to you, 'Why did you go so near to the city to fight? Did you not know that they would shoot <n>[arrows]</n> from the wall?qÀW]dAnd he commanded the messenger, "When you have finished reporting all the events of the war to the king,TÀV#dThen Joab sent <i>word</i> and informed David of all the events of the war.ÀU‚+dAnd the men of the city came out and fought against Joab, and some of the people among the servants of David fell; Uriah the Hittite also died."ÀT‚?dSo it happened that as Joab was besieging the city, he assigned Uriah to the place where he knew the <n>[enemy's]</n> valiant men were <i>positioned</i>.
