tion <i>and</i> act promptly, for at that time the LORD will have gone out before you to strike the army of the Philistines."¿\‚%dWhen David inquired of the LORD, He said, "You shall not go up, but circle around behind them and come at them in front of the balsam trees.Y¿[-d<pb/>The Philistines came up once again and spread out in the Valley of Rephaim.¿ZydThe Philistines abandoned their <n>[pagan]</n> idols there, so David and his men took them away <n>[to be burned]</n>.^¿Yƒ7dSo David came to Baal-perazim, and he defeated them there, and said, "The LORD has broken through my enemies before me, like a breakthrough of water." So he named that place Baal-perazim (master of breakthroughs).E¿XƒdDavid inquired of the LORD, saying, "Shall I go up against the Philistines? Will You hand them over to me?" And the LORD said to David, "Go up, for I will certainly hand them over to you."b¿W?dNow the Philistines had come and spread out <n>[for battle]</n> in the Valley of Rephaim.@¿V‚{dWhen the Philistines heard that David had been anointed king over Israel, all the Philistines went up to look for him, but he heard about it and went down to the<f>[4]</f> stronghold.(¿UMdElishama, Eliada, and Eliphelet.'¿TKdIbhar, Elishua, Nepheg, Japhia,n¿SWdAnd these are the names of those who were born to him in Jerusalem: Shammua, Shobab, Nathan, Solomon,¿R‚%d
<pb/>David took more<f>[3]</f> concubines and wives from Jerusalem, after he came from Hebron; and more sons and daughters were born to him.¿Q‚dAnd David knew that the LORD had established him as king over Israel, and that He had exalted his kingdom for His people Israel's sake.¿P‚1d<pb/>Now Hiram the king of Tyre sent messengers to David with cedar trees, carpenters, and stonemasons; and they built a house (palace) for David.a¿O=d
David became greater and greater, for the LORD, the God of hosts (armies), was with him.<¿N‚sd	So David lived in the stronghold and called it the City of David. And he built all around <n>[the surrounding area]</n> from the<f>[2]</f> Millo <n>[fortification]</n> and inward.‚t¿M…cdThen David said on that day, "Whoever strikes the Jebusites, let him go up through the <n>[underground]</n> water shaft to strike the lame and the blind, who are detested by David's soul <n>[because of their arrogance]</n>." So <n>[for that reason]</n> they say, "The blind or the lame (Jebusites) shall not come into the <n>[royal]</n> house <n>[of Israel]</n>."d¿LCdNevertheless, David captured the stronghold (fortress) of Zion, that is, the City of David.‚D¿K…d<pb/>Now the king and his men went to Jerusalem against the Jebusites, the inhabitants of the land, who said to David, "You shall not enter here, for the blind and the lame <n>[even the weakest among us]</n> will turn you away"; they thought, "David cannot come in here <n>[because the walls are impenetrable]</n>."¿J‚dIn Hebron he reigned over Judah seven years and six months, and in Jerusalem he reigned thirty-three years over all Israel and Judah.T¿I#dDavid was thirty years old when he became king, and he reigned forty years.=¿H‚udSo all the elders (tribal leaders) of Israel came to the king at Hebron, and King David made a covenant with them at Hebron before the LORD; and they anointed him king over Israel.c¿GƒAdIn times past, when Saul was king over us, it was you who led Israel out <n>[to war]</n> and brought Israel in <n>[from battle]</n>. And the LORD told you, 'You shall shepherd My people Israel and be ruler over them.'" ¿F	}d<pb/>Then<f>[1]</f> all the tribes of Israel came to David at Hebron and said, "Behold, we are your bone and your flesh.n¿EƒWdSo David commanded his young men, and they killed them and cut off their hands and feet and hung them beside the pool in Hebron. But they took Ish-bosheth's head and buried it in Hebron in the tomb of Abner <n>[his relative]</n>.=¿D‚udHow much more, when wicked men have killed a righteous <i>and</i> just man in his own house on his bed, shall I not require his blood from your hand and remove you from the earth?"&¿C‚Gd
when a man told me, 'Behold, Saul is dead,'<f>[2]</f> thinking that he was bringing good news, I seized and killed him in Ziklag, to reward him for his news.5¿B‚ed	<pb/>David replied to Rechab and Baanah his brother, the sons of Rimmon the Beerothite, and said to them, "As the LORD lives, who has redeemed my life from every adversity,‚¿A„dThey brought the head of Ish-bosheth to David at Hebron, and said to the king, "Look, the head of Ish-bosheth the son of Saul, your enemy, who sought your life; thus the LORD has granted my lord the king vengeance this day on Saul and on his descendants."h¿@ƒKdNow when they entered the house he was lying on his bed in his bedroom. They <n>[not only]</n> struck and killed him, <n>[but]</n> they also beheaded him. Then they took his head and traveled all night by way of the Arabah.B¿?‚dThey came into the interior of the house as if to get wheat <n>[for the soldiers]</n>, and they struck him in the stomach. Then Rechab and Baanah his brother escaped <n>[unnoticed]</n>.,¿>‚Sd<pb/>So the sons of Rimmon the Beerothite, Rechab and Baanah, left and came to the house of Ish-bosheth in the heat of the day while he was taking his midday rest.‚?¿=„yd<pb/>Jonathan, Saul's son, had a son whose feet were crippled. He was five years old when the news <n>[of the deaths]</n> of Saul and Jonathan came from Jezreel. And the boy's nurse picked him up and fled; but it happened that while she was hurrying to flee, he fell and became lame. His name was Mephibosheth._¿<9dand the Beerothites fled to Gittaim, and have been resident aliens there to this day).‚¿;„+dSaul's son had two men who were commanders of <n>[raiding]</n> bands <n>[of soldiers]</n>. One was named Baanah and the other Rechab, the sons of Rimmon the Beerothite of the sons (tribe) of Benjamin (for Beeroth is also considered part of <n>[the tribe of]</n> Benjamin,¿:	‚;d<pb/>When Saul's son Ish-bosheth <n>[king of Israel]</n>, heard that Abner had died in Hebron, he<f>[1]</f> lost courage, and all Israel was horrified.;¿9‚qd'Today I am weak, though anointed king; these men, the sons of Zeruiah, are too difficult for me. May the LORD repay the evildoer <n>[Joab]</n> in accordance with his wickedness!"z¿8od&Then the king said to his servants, "Do you not know that a prince and a great man has fallen this day in Israel?
¿7‚d%So all the people and all Israel understood that day that it had not been the will of the king to put Abner the son of Ner to death.
¿6‚d$And all the people took notice of it and it<f>[12]</f> pleased them, just as everything that the king did pleased all the people.K¿5ƒd#All the people came to urge David to eat food while it was still day; but David took an oath, saying, "May God do so to me, and more also, if I taste bread or anything else before the sun sets."4¿4‚cd"<t>"Your hands were not bound, nor your feet put in fetters; </t><t>As a man falls before the wicked, so you have fallen." </t>And all the people wept again over him.<br/>
