<t>"He bowed the heavens also, and came down </t><t>With thick darkness under His feet.</t><br/>Ã$‚d	<t>"Smoke went up out of His nostrils, </t><t>And devouring fire from His mouth; </t><t>Coals were kindled by it.</t><br/>Ã#‚!d<t>"Then the earth shook and quaked, </t><t>The foundations of the heavens trembled </t><t>And were shaken, because He was angry.</t><br/>RÃ"ƒd<t>"In my distress I called upon the LORD; </t><t>I cried out to my God, </t><t>And from His temple <n>[in the heavens]</n> He heard my voice; </t><t>My cry for help <i>came</i> into His ears.</t><br/>aÃ!=d<t>"The cords of Sheol surrounded me; </t><t>The snares of death confronted me.</t><br/>Ã ‚d<t>"For the waves of death encompassed me; </t><t>The torrents of<f>[2]</f> destruction overwhelmed <i>and</i> terrified me.</t><br/>mÃUd<t>"I call on the LORD, who is worthy to be praised, </t><t>And I am saved from my enemies.</t><br/>AÃ‚}d<t><f>[1]</f> My God, my rock, in whom I take refuge; </t><t>My shield and the horn of my salvation, my stronghold and my refuge, </t><t>My Saviorâ€”You save me from violence.</t><br/>wÃid<t>He said: </t><br/><t>"The LORD is my rock and my fortress <n>[on the mountain]</n> and my rescuer;</t><br/>Ã	‚!d<pb/>David spoke the words of this song to the LORD when the LORD rescued him from the hands of all his enemies and from the hand of Saul.ÃdThese four <n>[warriors]</n> were descended from the giant in Gath, and they fell by the hands of David and his servants.oÃYdAnd when he taunted <i>and</i> defied Israel, Jonathan the son of Shimei, David's brother, killed him.NÃƒdThere was war at Gath again, where there was a man of <i>great</i> stature who had six fingers on each hand and six toes on each foot, twenty-four in number; he also was a descendant of the giants.6Ã‚gdThere was war with the Philistines again at Gob, and Elhanan the son of Jaare-oregim, a Bethlehemite, killed Goliath the Gittite, whose spear shaft was like a weaver's beam.9Ã‚md<pb/>After this, there was war again with the Philistines at Gob (Gezer). At that time Sibbecai the Hushathite killed Saph (Sippai), who was among the descendants of the giant.cÃƒAdBut Abishai the son of Zeruiah came to David's aid, and struck and killed the Philistine. Then David's men swore to him, "You shall not go out again with us to battle, so that you do not extinguish the lamp of Israel."[Ãƒ1dThen Ishbi-benob, who was among the descendants of the giant, the weight of whose spear was three hundred <i>shekels</i> (six pounds) of bronze, was armed with a new <i>sword</i>, and he intended to kill David.#Ã‚Ad<pb/>Now the Philistines were at war again with Israel. David went down with his servants, and as they fought against the Philistines, David became weary.[Ãƒ1dThey buried the bones of Saul and Jonathan his son in the country of Benjamin in Zela, in the tomb of Kish his father; and they did all that the king commanded. After that, God was moved by prayer for the land./Ã‚Yd
He brought up the bones of Saul and of Jonathan his son from there, and they gathered the bones of those who had been hanged <n>[with their arms and legs broken]</n>.ÃƒydThen David went and took the bones of Saul and Jonathan his son from the men of Jabesh-gilead, who had stolen them from the open square of Beth-shan, where the Philistines had hanged them on the day when the Philistines had killed Saul in Gilboa.dÃCdDavid was told what Rizpah the daughter of Aiah, the<f>[4]</f> concubine of Saul, had done.‚QÃ…d
<pb/>Rizpah the daughter of Aiah took sackcloth and spread it out for herself on the rock, from the beginning of harvest <n>[in the spring]</n> until <n>[the autumn]</n> rain fell on them; and she allowed neither the birds of the sky to rest on their bodies by day, nor the beasts of the field <n>[to feed on them]</n> by night.yÃƒmd	He handed them over to the Gibeonites, and they hanged them on the hill before the LORD, and the seven died together. They were put to death in the first days of the grain harvest, the beginning of the barley harvest <n>[in the spring]</n>.tÃ
ƒcdSo the king took the two sons of Rizpah the daughter of Aiah, whom she bore to Saul, Armoni and Mephibosheth, and the five sons of<f>[3]</f> Merab the daughter of Saul, whom she had borne to Adriel the son of Barzillai the Meholathite.&Ã‚Gd<pb/>But the king spared Mephibosheth the son of Jonathan, the son of Saul, because of the <e>Lord's</e> oath that was between David and Saul's son Jonathan.‚!Ã„=dlet seven men <n>[chosen]</n> from his sons (descendants) be given to us and we will hang them before the LORD <n>[that is, put them on display, impaled with broken legs and arms]</n> in<f>[2]</f> Gibeah of Saul, the chosen one of the LORD." And the king said, "I will give them."Ã
‚dSo they said to the king, "The man who consumed us and planned to exterminate us from remaining in any territory of Israel,`Ã	ƒ;dThe Gibeonites said to him, "We will not accept silver or gold belonging to Saul or his household (descendants); nor is it for us to put any man to death in Israel." David said, "I will do for you whatever you say."Ã‚1dSo David said to the Gibeonites, "What should I do for you? How can I make it good so that you will bless the <e>Lord's</e> inheritance (Israel)?"‚PÃ…dSo the king called the Gibeonites and spoke to them (now the Gibeonites were not of the sons (descendants) of Israel but of the remnant (survivors) of the Amorites. The Israelites had sworn <n>[an oath]</n> to <n>[spare]</n> them, but Saul in his zeal for the sons of Israel and Judah had sought to strike down the Gibeonites).‚Ã	„d<pb/>There was famine in the days of David for three consecutive years; and David sought the presence (face) of the LORD <n>[asking the reason]</n>. The LORD replied, "It is because of Saul and his bloody house, because<f>[1]</f> he put the Gibeonites to death."3Ãcdalso Ira the Jairite was a priest to David.CÃdSheva was the scribe; and Zadok and Abiathar were priests;VÃ'dAdoram was over the forced labor; Jehoshaphat the son of Ahilud was recorder;DÃƒd<pb/>Now Joab was <n>[commander]</n> over the entire army of Israel; Benaiah the son of Jehoiada was <n>[commander]</n> over the Cherethites and Pelethites <n>[the king's bodyguards]</n>;‚sÃ…adThen the woman in her wisdom went to all the people <n>[to inform them of the agreement]</n>. And they beheaded Sheba the son of Bichri and threw his head <n>[down]</n> to Joab. So he blew the trumpet <n>[signaling the end of the attack]</n>, and they dispersed from the city, every man to his own tent. And Joab returned to Jerusalem to <n>[David]</n> the king.‚+Ã „QdThat is not true. But a man of the hill country of Ephraim, Sheba the son of Bichri by name, has lifted up his hand <n>[in rebellion]</n> against King David. Only hand him over, and I will leave the city." And the woman said to Joab, "Behold, his head shall be thrown to you over the wall."YÂ-dJoab answered, "Far be it, far be it from me that I would swallow up or destroy!5Â~‚edI am one of the peaceable and faithful in Israel. You are seeking to destroy a city, and a mother in Israel. Why would you swallow up (devour) the inheritance of the LORD?"Â}‚dThen she said, "In the past people used to say, 'They will certainly ask <i>advice</i> at Abel,' and so they settled <i>the dispute</i>.9Â|‚mdSo when he approached her, the woman asked, "Are you Joab?" He answered, "I am." Then she said to him, "Listen to the words of your maidservant." He answered, "I am listening."tÂ{cdThen a wise woman cried out from the city, "Hear, hear! Tell Joab, 'Come here so that I may speak to you.'"‚Âz„dAnd<f>[6]</f> the army of Joab came and besieged Sheba in Abel Beth-maacah, and they built up an<f>[7]</f> assault ramp against the city, and it stood against the outer rampart; and all the people who were with Joab were wreaking destruction to make the wall fall.Ây‚/d<pb/>Now Joab went through all the tribes of Israel to Abel, even Beth-maacah, and all the Berites; and they assembled and also went after Sheba.Âx‚d
When <n>[the body of]</n> Amasa was removed from the highway, everyone followed after Joab to pursue Sheba the son of Bichri.‚Âw„1dBut Amasa was wallowing in his blood in the middle of the highway. And when the man saw that all the people who came by stopped <n>[to look]</n>, he moved Amasa from the highway into the field and threw a garment over him when he saw that everyone who came by Amasa stopped.xÂvkdNow one of Joab's young men stood by him and said, "Whoever favors Joab and is for David, let him follow Joab!"‚IÂu…
