dSo David went up according to Gad's word, as the LORD commanded.DÄƒdThen Gad <n>[the prophet]</n> came to David that day and said to him, "Go up, set up an altar to the LORD on the threshing floor of Araunah the Jebusite <n>[where you saw the angel]</n>."‚JÄ…dWhen David saw the angel who was striking down the people, he spoke to the LORD and said, "Behold, I <n>[alone]</n> am the one who has sinned and done wrong; but these sheep (people of Israel), what have they done <n>[to deserve this]</n>? Please let Your hand be <n>[only]</n> against me and my father's house (family)."‚'Ä„IdWhen the <n>[avenging]</n> angel stretched out his hand toward Jerusalem to destroy it, the LORD relented from the disaster and said to the angel who destroyed the people, "It is enough! Now relax your hand." And the angel of the LORD was by the threshing floor of Araunah the Jebusite.EÄƒdSo the LORD sent a pestilence (plague) <n>[lasting three days]</n> upon Israel from the morning until the appointed time, and seventy thousand men of the people from Dan to Beersheba died.+Ä‚QdThen David said to Gad, "I am in great distress. Let us fall into the hands of the LORD, for His mercies are great, but do not let me fall into the hands of man."‚HÄ…d
So Gad came to David and told him, and said to him, "Shall seven years of famine come to you in your land? Or will you flee three months before your enemies as they pursue you? Or shall there be three days of pestilence (plague) in your land? Now consider this and decide what answer I shall return to Him who sent me."Ä‚)d"Go and say to David, 'Thus says the LORD, "I am giving you three <i>choices</i>; select one of them for yourself, and I will do it to you."'"nÄ WdWhen David got up in the morning, the word of the LORD came to the prophet Gad, David's seer, saying,{Ãƒqd
<pb/>But David's heart (conscience) troubled him after he had counted the people. David said to the LORD, "I have sinned greatly in what I have done. But now, O LORD, please take away the sin of Your servant, for I have acted very foolishly."'Ã~‚Id	And Joab gave the sum of the census of the people to the king. In Israel there were 800,000 valiant men who drew the sword, and the men of Judah were 500,000.Ã}‚)dSo when they had gone about through all the land <n>[taking the census]</n>, they came to Jerusalem at the end of nine months and twenty days.Ã|‚3dand they came to the stronghold of Tyre and to all the cities of the Hivites and Canaanites, and they went out to the south of Judah, to Beersheba.rÃ{_dThen they came to Gilead and to the land of Tahtim-hodshi, and they came to Dan-jaan and around to Sidon,:Ãz‚odThey crossed over the Jordan and camped in Aroer, on the south side of the city which is in the middle of the river valley <n>[of the Arnon]</n> toward Gad, and on toward Jazer.-Ãy‚UdNevertheless, the king's word prevailed against Joab and the commanders of the army. So they went from the king's presence to take a census of the people of Israel._Ãxƒ9dBut Joab said to the king, "May the LORD your God add to the people a hundred times as many as there are, and let the eyes of my lord the king see it; but why does my lord the king<f>[3]</f> want to do this thing?"‚Ãw„dSo the king said to Joab the commander of the army who was with him, "Go now through all the tribes of Israel, from Dan <n>[in the north]</n> to Beersheba <n>[in the south]</n>, and conduct a census of the people, so that I may know the number of the people."6Ãv	‚id<pb/>Now again the anger of the LORD burned against Israel, and<f>[1]</f> He incited David against them to say, "Go,<f>[2]</f> count <n>[the people of]</n> Israel and Judah."0Ãu]d'Uriah the Hittiteâ€”thirty-seven in all.+ÃtSd&Ira the Ithrite, Gareb the Ithrite,YÃs-d%Zelek the Ammonite, Naharai of Beeroth, armor bearer of Joab the son of Zeruiah,9Ãrod$Igal the son of Nathan of Zobah, Bani the Gadite,4Ãqed#Hezro (Hezrai) of Carmel, Paarai the Arbite,^Ãp7d"Eliphelet the son of Ahasbai the son of Maacah, Eliam the son of Ahithophel of Giloh,CÃod!Shammah the Hararite, Ahiam the son of Sharar the Ararite,:Ãnqd Eliahba of Shaalbon, the sons of Jashen, Jonathan,8ÃmmdAbi-albon the Arbathite, Azmaveth the Barhumite,;ÃlsdBenaiah of Pirathon, Hiddai of the brooks of Gaash,aÃk=dHeleb the son of Baanah of Netophah, Ittai the son of Ribai of Gibeah of the Benjamites,0Ãj]dZalmon the Ahohite, Maharai of Netophah,5ÃigdAbiezer of Anathoth, Mebunnai the Hushathite,:ÃhqdHelez the Paltite, Ira the son of Ikkesh of Tekoa,)ÃgOdShammah of Harod, Elika of Harod,qÃf]d<pb/>Asahel the brother of Joab was one of the thirty; <i>then</i> Elhanan the son of Dodo of Bethlehem,Ãe‚dHe was honored among the thirty, but he did not attain to the <n>[greatness of the]</n> three. David appointed him over his guard.kÃdQdThese things Benaiah the son of Jehoiada did, and gained a reputation beside the three mighty men.oÃcƒYdAnd he killed an Egyptian, an impressive <i>and</i> handsome man. The Egyptian had a spear in his hand, but Benaiah went down to him with a club, snatched the spear out of the Egyptian's hand and killed the man with his own spear.lÃbƒSd<pb/>Then Benaiah the son of Jehoiada, the son of a valiant man of Kabzeel, who had done many notable acts, killed two <n>[famous]</n> warriors of Moab. He also went down and killed a lion in the middle of a pit on a snowy day.Ãa‚dHe was the most honored of the thirty, so he became their commander; however, he did not attain to the <n>[greatness of the]</n> three.MÃ`ƒd<pb/>Now Abishai the brother of Joab the son of Zeruiah was chief of the<f>[4]</f> thirty. He wielded his spear against three hundred men and killed them, and gained a reputation beside the three.pÃ_ƒ[dAnd he said, "Far be it from me, O LORD, that I should drink this. <n>[Is it not the same as]</n> the blood of the men who went at the risk of their lives?" So he would not drink it. These things the<f>[3]</f> three mighty men did.uÃ^ƒedSo the three mighty men broke through the camp of the Philistines, and drew water from the well of Bethlehem by the gate, and carried and brought it to David. But he would not drink it, but poured it out <n>[in worship]</n> to the LORD.Ã]‚dAnd David had a craving and said, "Oh that someone would give me water to drink from the well of Bethlehem, which is by the gate!"eÃ\EdDavid was then in the stronghold, and the garrison of the Philistines was then in Bethlehem.8Ã[‚kd
<pb/>Then three of the thirty chief men went down and came to David at harvest time in the cave of Adullam, while an army of Philistines was encamped in the Valley of Rephaim.ÃZ‚%dBut he took his stand in the center of the plot and defended it and struck down the Philistines; and the LORD brought about a great victory.aÃYƒ=d<pb/>Next to Eleazar was Shammah the son of Agee the Hararite. The Philistines were gathered into an army where there was a plot of ground full of lentils, and the people <n>[of Israel]</n> fled from the Philistines.gÃXƒId
Eleazar stood up and struck down the Philistines until his hand was weary and clung to the sword. The LORD brought about a great victory that day; the people returned after him only to take the spoil <n>[of the slain]</n>.`ÃWƒ;d	Next to him was Eleazar the son of Dodo the son of Ahohi. He was one of the three mighty men with David when they taunted <i>and</i> defied the Philistines assembled there for battle, and the men of Israel had gone.‚ÃV„dThese are the names of the mighty men (warriors) whom David had: Josheb-basshebeth, a Tahchemonite, chief of the<f>[1]</f> captains, <i>also called</i> Adino the Eznite (spear) because of the<f>[2]</f> eight hundred men killed <n>[by him]</n> at one time.;ÃU‚qd<t>"But the man who touches them </t><t>Must be armed with iron and the shaft of a spear, </t><t>And they are utterly burned <i>and</i> consumed by fire in their place."</t><br/>ÃT‚!d<t>"But the wicked <i>and</i> worthless are all to be thrown away like thorns, </t><t>Because they cannot be taken with the hand;</t><br/>‚CÃS…d<t>"Truly is not my house so <n>[blessed]</n> with God? </t><t>For He has made an everlasting covenant with me, </t><t>Ordered in all things, and secured. </t><t>For will He not cause to grow <i>and</i> prosper </t><t>All my salvation and my every wish? </t><t>Will He not make it grow <i>and</i> prosper?</t><br/>RÃRƒd<t>Is like the morning light <i>when</i> the sun rises, </t><t>A morning without clouds, </t><t><i>When</i> the fresh grass <i>springs</i> out of the earth </t><t>Through sunshine after rain.'</t><br/>ÃQ‚9d<t>"The God of Israel, </t><t>The Rock of Israel spoke to me, </t><t>'He who rules over men righteously, </t><t>Who rules in the fear of God,</t><br/>_ÃP9d<t>"The Spirit of the LORD spoke by me, </t><t>And His word was on my tongue.</t><br/>nÃO	ƒYd<pb/><t>Now these are the last words of David. </t><br/><t>David the son of Jesse declares, </t><t>The man who was raised on high declares, </t><t>The anointed of the God of Jacob, </t><t>And the sweet psalmist of Israel,</t><br/>2ÃN‚_d3<t>"He is a tower of salvation <i>and</i> great deliverance to His king, </t><t>And shows lovingkindness to His anointed, </t><t>To David and his offspring forever."</t>ÃM‚d2<t>"For this I will give thanks <i>and</i> praise You, O LORD, among the nations; </t><t>I will sing praises to Your name.</t><br/>#ÃL‚Ad1<t>Who also brings me out from my enemies. </t><t>You even lift me above those who rise up against me; </t><t>You rescue me from the violent man.</t><br/>ÃK‚d0<t>"It is God who executes vengeance for me, </t><t>And brings down <n>[and disciplines]</n> the peoples under me,</t><br/>uÃJed/<t>"The LORD lives, and blessed be my rock, </t><t>And exalted be my God, the rock of my salvation.</t><br/>aÃI=d.<t>"Foreigners lose heart; </t><t>They come trembling out of their strongholds.</t><br/>pÃH[d-<t>"Foreigners pretend obedience to me; </t><t>As soon as they hear <n>[me]</n>, they obey me.</t><br/>:ÃG‚od,<t>"You also have rescued me from strife with my <n>[own]</n> people; </t><t>You have kept me as the head of the nations. </t><t>People whom I have not known served me.</t><br/>ÃF‚3d+<t>"Then I beat them as <n>[small as]</n> the dust of the earth; </t><t>I crushed and stamped them as the mire (dirt, mud) of the streets.</t><br/>ÃE‚7d*<t>"They looked, but there was no savior <i>for them</i>â€” </t><t><i>Even</i> to the LORD <n>[they looked]</n>, but He did not answer them.</t><br/>
