°	Ö	*R—FÌ,hÚ+                                                                                                                                                                 +Æ9‚Qn<pb/>"If a man sins against his neighbor and is made to take an oath <n>[of innocence]</n> and he comes to take the oath before Your altar in this house (temple),!Æ8‚=nListen to the prayer of Your servant and of Your people Israel which they pray toward this place. Hear in heaven, Your dwelling place; hear and forgive.eÆ7ƒEnthat Your eyes may be open toward this house night and day, toward the place of which You have said, 'My Name (Presence) shall be there,' that You may listen to the prayer which Your servant shall pray toward this place.@Æ6‚{nYet graciously consider the prayer of Your servant and his supplication, O LORD my God, to listen to the <n>[loud]</n> cry and to the prayer which Your servant prays before You today;Æ5‚3n<pb/>But will God indeed dwell on the earth? Behold, heaven and the highest heaven cannot contain You; how much less this house which I have built!wÆ4inNow, O God of Israel, please let Your word which You have spoken to Your servant David my father be confirmed.‚MÆ3…nNow therefore, O LORD, the God of Israel, keep for Your servant my father David that which You promised him when You said, 'You shall not be without a man (descendant) to sit on the throne of Israel, if only your sons take heed to their way <n>[of life]</n> to walk before Me <n>[according to my laws]</n> as you have done.'7Æ2‚inYou who have kept what You<f>[2]</f> promised to Your servant my father David. You have spoken with Your mouth and have fulfilled Your word with Your hand, as it is this day.TÆ1ƒ#nHe said, "O LORD, the God of Israel, there is no God like You in heaven above or on earth below, who keeps the covenant and shows lovingkindness to Your servants who walk before You with all their heart;(Æ0‚KnThen Solomon stood <n>[in the courtyard]</n> before the altar of the LORD in the presence of all the assembly of Israel and spread out his hands toward heaven.VÆ/ƒ'nThere I have made a place <n>[in the Holy of Holies]</n> for the ark, in which is the covenant (solemn agreement) of the LORD, which He made with our fathers when He brought them out of the land of Egypt."‚Æ.„nNow the LORD has fulfilled His word which He spoke; I have risen in the place of my father David and have taken my seat on the throne of Israel, just as the LORD promised, and have built the house (temple) for the Name of the LORD, the God of Israel. Æ-‚;nNevertheless, you shall not build the house, but your son, who shall be born to you, it is he who shall build it for My Name <n>[and My Presence]</n>.'Æ,‚'nBut the LORD said to my father David, 'Because it was in your heart to build a house for My Name, you did well, in that it was in your heart.Æ+‚nNow it was <n>[determined]</n> in the heart of my father David to build a house (temple) for the Name of the LORD, the God of Israel.‚
Æ*„n'Since the day that I brought My people Israel out of Egypt, I did not choose a <n>[particular]</n> city out of all the tribes of Israel <i>in which</i> to build a house so that My Name (Presence) would be in it, but I chose David to be over My people Israel.'Æ)‚nHe said, "Blessed be the LORD, the God of Israel, who spoke with His mouth to my father David and has fulfilled it with His hand, saying, Æ({n<pb/>Then the king turned around and blessed all the assembly of Israel, while all the assembly of Israel was standing.gÆ'In
<t>"I have certainly built You a lofty house, </t><t>A place for You to dwell in forever."</t>Æ&‚	n<t>Then Solomon said, </t><br/><t>"The LORD has said that He would dwell in the thick darkness <n>[of the cloud]</n>.</t><br/>BÆ%‚nso the priests could not stand <n>[in their positions]</n> to minister because of the cloud, for the glory <i>and</i> brilliance of the LORD had filled the <e>Lord's</e> house (temple).xÆ$kn
Now it happened that when the priests had come out of the Holy Place, the cloud filled the <e>Lord's</e> house,IÆ#ƒ
n	There was nothing in the ark except the two tablets of stone which Moses put there at Horeb (Sinai), where the LORD made a covenant with the Israelites when they came out of the land of Egypt.qÆ"ƒ]nThe poles were so long that the ends of the poles were visible from the Holy Place that was in front of the<f>[1]</f> Holy of Holies, but they were not visible from the outside; they are there to this day (the date of this writing).Æ!‚+nFor the cherubim spread their two wings over the place of the ark, and the cherubim covered the ark and its <n>[carrying]</n> poles from above.4Æ ‚cnThen the priests brought the ark of the covenant of the LORD to its place, into the inner sanctuary of the house, into the Holy of Holies, under the wings of the cherubim.EÆƒnKing Solomon and all the congregation of Israel, who had assembled before him, were with him before the ark, sacrificing sheep and oxen, so many that they could not be counted or numbered."Æ‚?nThey brought up the ark of the LORD and the Tent of Meeting and all the holy utensils that were in the tent; the priests and the Levites brought them up.HÆnAll the elders of Israel came, and the priests carried the ark.Æ‚nAll the men of Israel assembled before King Solomon at the feast in the month of Ethanim (September-October), that is, the seventh month.‚Æ	„#n<pb/>Then Solomon assembled the elders of Israel and all the heads of the tribes, the leaders of the fathers' <i>households</i> of the sons of Israel, to King Solomon in Jerusalem, to bring up the ark of the covenant of the Lord from the City of David, which is Zion.‚Æ„	n3<pb/>So all the work that King Solomon did on the house of the LORD was completed. Solomon brought in the things which David his father had dedicatedâ€”the silver, the gold, and the utensilsâ€”and he put them in the treasuries of the <e>Lord's</e> house.fÆƒGn2the cups, snuffers, bowls, spoons, firepansâ€”of pure gold; and the hinges of gold <n>[both]</n> for the doors of the inner house, the Holy of Holies, and for the doors of the house, the main room <n>[the Holy Place]</n>.8Æ‚kn1the lampstands of pure gold, five on the right side and five on the left, in front of the Holy of Holies (inner sanctuary); with the flowers, the lamps, and the tongs of gold;=Æ‚un0<pb/>Solomon made all the <n>[other]</n> furniture which was in the house of the LORD: the <n>[incense]</n> altar of gold; the table of gold on which was the bread of the Presence;Æ‚n/Solomon left all the utensils <i>unweighed</i>, because there were so many; the weight of the bronze could not be determined.wÆin.In the plain of the Jordan <n>[River]</n> the king cast them, in the clay ground between Succoth and Zarethan.Æ‚'n-the pails, the shovels, and the bowls; all these utensils which Hiram made for King Solomon in the house of the LORD were of polished bronze.;Æsn,one Sea (basin), and the twelve oxen under the Sea;8Æmn+the ten stands and the ten basins on the stands;7Æ‚in*and the four hundred pomegranates for the two networks, two rows of pomegranates for each network to cover the two bowls of the capitals that were on the tops of the pillars;KÆƒn)the two pillars and the <n>[two]</n> bowls of the capitals which were on the top of the two pillars, and the two networks to cover the two bowls of the capitals which were on top of the pillars;Æ‚5n(<pb/>Now Hiram made the basins and the shovels and the bowls. So Hiram finished all the work which he did for King Solomon on the house of the LORD:dÆƒCn'Then he placed the bases, five on the right <n>[or south]</n> side of the house and five on the left <n>[or north]</n> side; and he set the Sea <n>[of cast metal]</n> on the right side of the house toward the southeast.Æ
‚#n&<pb/>Then he made ten basins of bronze; each basin held forty baths and was four cubits, and there was one basin on each of the ten stands._Æ9n%He made the ten stands like this: they all had one casting, one measure, and one form.EÆƒn$And on the surface of its supports and its borders Hiram engraved cherubim, lions, and palm-shaped decorations, according to the <n>[available]</n> space for each, with borders all around.Æ
‚#n#On the top of the stand there was a circular piece half a cubit high, and on the top of the stand its supports and borders were part of it.tÆ	cn"Now there were four supports at the four corners of each stand; the supports were part of the stand itself.xÆkn!The wheels were made like a chariot wheel: their axles, their rims, their spokes, and their hubs were all cast.Æ‚n Underneath the borders were four wheels, and the axles of the wheels were on the stand. And the height of a wheel was a cubit and a half.cÆƒAnIts opening inside the crown at the top <i>measured</i> a cubit, and its opening was round like the design of a pedestal, a cubit and a half. Also on its opening were carvings, and their borders were square, not round.6Æ‚gnNow each stand had four bronze wheels with bronze axles, and its four feet had supports <n>[for a basin]</n>. Beneath the basin were cast supports with borders at each side.1Æ‚]nOn the borders between the frames were lions, oxen, and cherubim; and on the frames there was a pedestal above. Beneath the lions and oxen were borders of hanging work.PÆnThis was the design of the stands: they had borders between the frames.1Æ‚]n<pb/>Then Hiram made ten bronze stands <n>[for smaller basins]</n>; the length of each stand was four cubits, its width was four cubits and its height was three cubits.Æ‚nIt was a hand width thick, and its brim was made like the brim of a cup, like a lily blossom. It held two thousand<f>[4]</f> baths.5Æ ‚enIt stood on twelve oxen, three facing north, three west, three south, and three east; the Sea <i>was set</i> on top of them, and all their rear parts <i>pointed</i> inward.Å‚-nUnder its brim were gourds encircling it ten to a cubit, completely surrounding the Sea; the gourds were in two rows, cast in one piece with it.$Å~‚Cn<pb/>Now he made the Sea (basin) of cast <i>metal</i>, ten cubits from brim to brim, circular in form, five cubits high and thirty cubits in circumference.dÅ}CnOn the tops of the pillars was lily work (design). So the work of the pillars was finished.EÅ|ƒnHiram set up the pillars at the porch of the temple; he set up the right pillar and named it Jachin (may he establish), and he set up the left pillar and named it Boaz (in it is strength).@Å{‚{nThe capitals were on the two pillars and also above and close to the rounded projection which was beside the network. There were two hundred pomegranates in rows around both capitals.rÅz_nThe capitals which were upon the top of the pillars in the porch were of lily work (design), four cubits.NÅyƒnSo Hiram made the pillars <n>[in this manner]</n>, and two rows around on the one network to cover the capitals which were on the top of the pomegranates; and he did the same for the other capital.KÅxƒn<i>There were</i> nets of network (lattice-work) and twisted threads (wreaths) of chainwork for the capitals which were on the tops of the pillars, seven for one capital and seven for the other.=Åw‚unHe also made two capitals (crowns) of cast bronze to put on the tops of the pillars; the height of one capital was five cubits, and the height of the other capital was five cubits.,Åv‚Sn<pb/>He cast the two pillars of bronze; the one pillar was eighteen cubits high, and a <n>[measuring]</n> line of twelve cubits measured the circumference of both.‚Åu„7nHe was the son of a widow from the tribe of Naphtali, and his father was a man of Tyre, a craftsman in bronze. Hiram was filled with wisdom, understanding, and skill for doing any <n>[kind of]</n> work in bronze. So he came to King Solomon and did all his <n>[bronze]</n> work.nÅtWn
Now King Solomon sent <i>word</i> and brought<f>[3]</f> Hiram <n>[a skilled craftsman]</n> from Tyre..Ås‚WnSo the great courtyard all around had three rows of cut stone and a row of cedar beams like the inner courtyard of the house of the LORD, and the porch of the house.mÅrUnAnd above <n>[the foundation]</n> were expensive stones, stones cut according to measure, and cedar.tÅqcn
<pb/>The foundation was of expensive stones, large stones, stones of ten cubits and stones of eight cubits.NÅpƒn	<pb/>All these were of expensive stones, of stone cut according to measure, sawed with saws, inside and outside; even from the foundation to the coping, and from the outside to the great courtyard.DÅoƒn<pb/>His house where he was to live, the other courtyard behind the hall, was of similar workmanship. Solomon also made a house like this hall for Pharaoh's daughter, whom he had married.'Ån‚In<pb/>He made the hall for the throne where he was to judge, the Hall of Judgment; it was paneled with cedar from <n>[one]</n> floor to <n>[another]</n> floor.DÅmƒn<pb/>He also made the Hall of Pillars; its length was fifty cubits (75 ft.) and its width thirty cubits (45 ft.). There was a porch in front, and pillars and a threshold in front of them.Ål‚nAll the doorways and doorposts <n>[and windows]</n> had squared <n>[artistic]</n> frames, and window was opposite window in three tiers.oÅkYn<i>There were artistic window</i> frames in three rows, and window was opposite window in three tiers.
Åj‚nAnd it was covered with cedar <n>[as a roof]</n> on the supporting beams that were upon the forty-five pillars, fifteen in each row.{ÅiƒqnHe also built the House of the Forest of Lebanon; its length was a hundred<f>[2]</f> cubits (150 ft.), its width fifty cubits (75 ft.), and its height thirty cubits (45 ft.), upon four rows of cedar pillars, with cedar beams upon the pillars.
Åh	‚n<pb/>Now Solomon built his own house (palace)<f>[1]</f> in thirteen years, and he finished all of his house <n>[in that time]</n>.Ågƒyn&In the eleventh year <n>[of King Solomon's reign]</n> in the month of Bul (October-November), that is, the eighth month, the house was finished throughout all its parts and in accordance with all its specifications. So he built it in seven years.$Åf‚Cn%<pb/>In the fourth year <n>[of King Solomon's reign]</n> the foundation of the <e>Lord's</e> house was laid, in the <n>[second]</n> month, Ziv (April-May).\Åe3n$He built the inner courtyard with three rows of cut stone and a row of cedar beams.Åd‚/n#He carved cherubim, palm-shaped decorations, and open flowers <i>on the doors</i>, and overlaid them with gold evenly applied on the carved work.,Åc‚Sn"and two doors of cypress wood; the two leaves of the one door turned on pivots <i>and</i> were folding, and the two leaves of the other door also turned on pivots.Åb‚n!<pb/>Also he made for the entrance of the <n>[outer]</n> sanctuary (the Holy Place) four-sided doorposts (frames) of olive woodrÅaƒ_n So <i>he made</i> two doors of olive wood, and he carved on them carvings of cherubim, palm-shaped decorations, and open flowers; and overlaid them with gold; and he hammered out overlays of gold on the cherubim and palm decorations.,Å`‚Sn<pb/>For the entrance of the Holy of Holies he made two <n>[folding]</n> doors of olive wood, the lintel (header above the door) and five-sided doorposts (frames).lÅ_SnHe overlaid the floor of the house with gold, <n>[both]</n> the inner and outer <i>sanctuaries</i>.FÅ^ƒn<pb/>He carved all the walls of the house all around with carved engravings of cherubim, palm-shaped decorations, and open flowers, <n>[both]</n> the inner and the outer <i>sanctuaries</i>.5Å]gnSolomon also overlaid the cherubim with gold.‚<Å\„snHe put the cherubim <n>[above the ark]</n> inside the innermost room of the house, and their wings were spread out so that the wing of the one touched one wall, and the wing of the other cherub was touching the other wall; and their inner wings were touching<f>[8]</f> each other in the middle of the house.GÅ[	nthe height of the one cherub was ten cubits, as was the other.ÅZ‚nThe <n>[wingspan of the]</n> other cherub was also ten cubits. The measurements and cut (shape) of the two cherubim were the same;9ÅY‚mn<f>[7]</f> One wing of the cherub was five cubits long, and the other wing was <i>also</i> five cubits long; it was ten cubits from the tip of one wing to the tip of the other. ÅX{n<pb/>Within the Holy of Holies he made two<f>[6]</f> cherubim (sculptured figures) of olive wood, each ten cubits high.7ÅW‚inThen he overlaid the entire house with gold, until the whole house was finished. He also overlaid the entire <n>[incense]</n> altar which was by the Holy of Holies with gold.@ÅV‚{nSolomon overlaid the interior of the house with pure gold, and he drew<f>[5]</f> chains of gold across the front of the Holy of Holies (inner sanctuary), and he overlaid it with gold.NÅUƒnThe Holy of Holies was twenty cubits in length, twenty cubits in width, and twenty cubits in height (a cube), and he overlaid it with pure gold. He also overlaid the cedar altar <n>[with gold]</n>.xÅTknThen he prepared the Holy of Holies within the house in order to put the ark of the covenant of the LORD there.ÅS‚#nThe cedar on the house within had wood carvings <i>in the shape</i> of gourds and open flowers. Everything was cedar; no stone was visible.zÅRonThe <n>[rest of the]</n> house, that is, the temple in front of <i>the Holy of Holies</i>, was forty cubits long.7ÅQ‚inHe built twenty cubits on the rear of the house with boards of cedar from the floor to the ceiling; he built its interior as the <n>[inner]</n> sanctuary, the Holy of Holies.‚$ÅP„CnHe built the walls of the interior of the house <n>[that is, the Holy Place and the Holy of Holies]</n> with boards of cedar, from the floor of the house to the rafters of the ceiling. He overlaid the interior with wood, and he overlaid the floor of the house with boards of cypress.AÅOn<pb/>So Solomon built the house (temple) and finished it.eÅNEn
I will dwell among the sons (descendants) of Israel, and will not abandon My people Israel."|ÅMƒsn"<i>Concerning</i> this house which you are building, if you will walk in My statutes and execute My precepts and keep all My commandments by walking in them, then I will carry out My word (promises) with you which I made to David your father.>ÅLyn<pb/>Now the word of the LORD came to Solomon, saying,2ÅK‚_n
