o	ñó~{ÀóãGl                     YÉƒ-n<pb/>Then Elijah said to all the people, "Come near to me." So all the people approached him. And he repaired <i>and</i> rebuilt the <n>[old]</n> altar of the LORD that had been torn down <n>[by Jezebel]</n>.WÉƒ)nAs midday passed, they played the part of prophets <i>and</i> raved dramatically until the time for offering the <i>evening</i> sacrifice; but there was no voice, no one answered, and no one paid attention.<É‚snSo they cried out with a loud voice <n>[to get Baal's attention]</n> and cut themselves with swords and lances in accordance with their custom, until the blood flowed out on them.XÉƒ+nAt noon Elijah mocked them, saying, "Cry out with a loud voice, for he is a god; either he is occupied, or he is out <n>[at the moment]</n>, or he is on a journey. Perhaps he is asleep and must be awakened!"‚É„nSo they took the bull that was given to them and prepared it, and called on the name of Baal from morning until noon, saying, "O Baal, hear <i>and</i> answer us." But there was no voice and no one answered. And they leaped about the altar which they had made.IÉƒ
n<pb/>Elijah said to the prophets of Baal, "Choose one bull for yourselves and prepare it first, since there are many of you; and call on the name of your god, but put no fire <i>under it</i>."7É‚inThen you call on the name of your god, and I will call on the name of the LORD; and the god who answers by fire, He is God." And all the people answered, "It is well spoken."ÉƒynNow let them give us two oxen, and let them choose one ox for themselves and cut it in pieces, and lay it on the wood, but put no fire <i>under it</i>. I will prepare the other ox and lay it on the wood, and I will not put a fire <i>under it</i>.rÉ_nThen Elijah said to the people, "I alone remain a prophet of the LORD, while Baal's prophets are 450 men.zÉƒonElijah approached all the people and said, "How long will you<f>[3]</f> hesitate between two opinions? If the LORD is God, follow Him; but if Baal, follow him." But the people <n>[of Israel]</n> did not answer him <n>[so much as]</n> a word.{ÉqnSo Ahab sent <i>word</i> to all the Israelites and assembled the <n>[pagan]</n> prophets together at Mount Carmel.YÉƒ-nNow then, send <i>word</i> and gather to me all Israel at Mount Carmel, together with the 450 prophets of Baal and the 400 prophets of <n>[the goddess]</n> Asherah, who eat at <n>[Queen]</n> Jezebel's table."AÉ‚}nElijah said, "I have not brought disaster on Israel, but you and your father's household <i>have</i>, by abandoning (rejecting) the commandments of the LORD and by following the Baals.kÉQn<pb/>When Ahab saw Elijah, Ahab said to him, "Are you the one who is bringing disaster on Israel?"QÉnSo Obadiah went to meet Ahab and told him; and Ahab went to meet Elijah.}É
unElijah said, "As the LORD of hosts (armies) lives, before whom I stand, I will certainly show myself to Ahab today."`É;nAnd now you are saying, 'Go, tell your master, "Elijah is here"'; and he will kill me."aÉƒ=n
Has it not been told to my lord <n>[Elijah]</n> what I did when Jezebel killed the prophets of the LORD, how I hid a hundred of the <e>Lord's</e> prophets by fifties in a cave, and provided them with bread and water?}É
ƒunAnd as soon as I leave you, the Spirit of the LORD will carry you<f>[2]</f> to a place I do not know; so when I come to tell Ahab and he does not find you, he will kill me. Yet your servant has <n>[reverently]</n> feared the LORD from my youth.[É	1nAnd now you are saying, 'Go, tell your master, "Behold, Elijah <n>[is here]</n>."'gÉƒIn
As the LORD your God lives, there is no nation or kingdom where my master has not sent <i>messengers</i> to seek you; and when they said, 'He is not here,' Ahab made the kingdom or nation swear that they had not found you.sÉan	But he said, "What sin have I committed, that you would hand over your servant to Ahab to put me to death?KÉnHe answered him, "It is I. Go tell your master, 'Elijah is here.'"(É‚Kn<pb/>As Obadiah was on the way, behold, Elijah met him. He recognized him and fell face downward <n>[out of respect]</n> and said, "Is it you, my lord Elijah?"É‚nSo they divided the land between them to<f>[1]</f> survey it. Ahab went one way by himself and Obadiah went another way by himself.RÉƒnThen Ahab said to Obadiah, "Go into the land to all the sources of water and to all the streams; perhaps we may find grass and keep the horses and mules alive, and not have to kill some of the cattle.")É‚Mnfor when Jezebel destroyed the prophets of the LORD, Obadiah took a hundred prophets and hid them by fifties in a cave, and provided them with bread and water.)eÉEnAhab called Obadiah who was the governor of his house. (Now Obadiah feared the LORD greatly;VÉ 'nSo Elijah went to show himself to Ahab. Now the famine was severe in Samaria.<È	‚un<pb/>Now it happened after many days that the word of the Lord came to Elijah in the third year, saying, "Go, show yourself to Ahab, and I will send rain on the face of the earth."È~‚nThen the woman said to Elijah, "Now I know that you are a man of God and that the word of the LORD in your mouth is truth."8È}‚knElijah took the child and brought him down from the upper room into the <n>[lower part of the]</n> house and gave him to his mother; and Elijah said, "See, your son is alive."fÈ|GnThe LORD heard the voice of Elijah, and the life of the child returned to him and he revived. È{‚;nThen he stretched himself out upon the child three times, and called to the LORD and said, "O LORD my God, please let this child's life return to him."Èz‚/nHe called to the LORD and said, "O LORD my God, have You brought further tragedy to the widow with whom I am staying, by causing her son to die?""Èy‚?nHe said to her, "Give me your son." Then he took him from her arms and carried him up to the upper room where he was living, and laid him on his own bed.*Èx‚OnSo she said to Elijah, "What <n>[problem]</n> is there between you and me, O man of God? Have you come to me to bring my sin to mind and to put my son to death?"/Èw‚YnIt happened after these things, that the son of the woman, the mistress of the house, became sick; and his illness was so severe that there was no breath left in him.Èv‚!nThe bowl of flour was not exhausted nor did the jar of oil become empty, in accordance the word of the LORD which He spoke through Elijah.dÈuCnShe went and did as Elijah said. And she and he and her household ate for <i>many</i> days.TÈtƒ#nFor this is what the LORD God of Israel says: 'The bowl of flour shall not be exhausted nor shall the jar of oil be empty until the day that the LORD sends rain <n>[again]</n> on the face of the earth.'"FÈsƒn
Elijah said to her, "Do not fear; go and do as you have said. Just make me a little bread from it first and bring it out to me, and afterward you may make one for yourself and for your son.‚Èr„nBut she said, "As the LORD your God lives, I have no bread, only a handful of flour in the bowl and a little oil in the jar. See, I am gathering a few sticks so that I may go in and bake it for me and my son, that we may eat it <n>[as our last meal]</n> and die."pÈq[nAs she was going to get it, he called to her and said, "Please bring me a piece of bread in your hand."xÈpƒkn
So he set out and went to Zarephath, and when he came to the gate of the city, behold, a widow was there gathering sticks <n>[for firewood]</n>. He called out to her and said, "Please bring me a little water in a jar, so that I may drink."Èo‚n	"Arise, go to<f>[1]</f> Zarephath, which belongs to Sidon, and stay there. Behold, I have commanded a widow there to provide for you.";Ènsn<pb/>Then the word of the LORD came to him, saying,bÈm?nIt happened after a while that the brook dried up, because there was no rain in the land.Èl‚nAnd the ravens brought him bread and meat in the morning, and bread and meat in the evening; and he would drink from the brook.	Èk‚
nSo he went and did in accordance with the word of the LORD; he went and lived by the brook Cherith, which is east of the Jordan.rÈj_nYou shall drink from the brook, and I have commanded the ravens to sustain you there <n>[with food]</n>."|Èisn"Go from here and turn eastward and hide yourself by the brook Cherith, which is east of the Jordan <n>[River]</n>.5ÈhgnAnd the word of the LORD came to him, saying,WÈg	ƒ+n<pb/>Now Elijah the Tishbite, who was of the settlers of Gilead, said to Ahab, "As the LORD, the God of Israel lives, before whom I stand, there shall be neither dew nor rain these years, except by My word."‚Èf„1n"In his days, Hiel the Bethelite rebuilt Jericho. He laid its foundations with the <i>loss of</i> Abiram his firstborn, and set up its gates with the <i>loss of</i> his youngest son Segub, in accordance with the word of the LORD, which He spoke through Joshua the son of Nun.Èe‚n!Ahab also made the Asherah. Ahab did more to provoke the LORD God of Israel than all the kings of Israel who were before him.XÈd+n So he erected an altar for Baal in the house of Baal which he built in Samaria.vÈcƒgn<pb/>It came about, as if it had been a trivial thing for Ahab to walk in the sins of Jeroboam the son of Nebat, that he married<f>[3]</f> Jezebel the daughter of Ethbaal king of the Sidonians, and went and served Baal and worshiped him.uÈbenAhab the son of Omri did evil in the sight of the LORD more than all <n>[the kings]</n> who were before him.8Èa‚kn<pb/>Ahab the son of Omri became king over Israel in the thirty-eighth year of Asa king of Judah, and Ahab the son of Omri reigned over Israel in Samaria for twenty-two years.{È`qnSo Omri slept with his fathers <n>[in death]</n> and was buried in Samaria. Ahab his son became king in his place.(È_‚KnNow as for the rest of the acts of Omri which he did, and his might which he showed, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel?2È^‚_nHe walked in all the ways of Jeroboam the son of Nebat and in his sin, which he made Israel commit, provoking the LORD God of Israel, <n>[to anger]</n> with their idols.vÈ]gn<pb/>But Omri did evil in the sight of the LORD, and acted more wickedly than all who <i>came</i> before him.hÈ\ƒKnOmri bought the hill Samaria from Shemer for two talents of silver, and he built <i>a city</i> on the hill <n>[and fortified it]</n>, and named the city which he built<f>[2]</f> Samaria, after the owner of the hill, Shemer.È[‚-nIn the thirty-first year of Asa king of Judah, Omri became king over Israel <i>and reigned</i> for twelve years. He reigned six years at Tirzah.
ÈZ‚nBut the people who followed Omri prevailed over the people who followed Tibni the son of Ginath. So Tibni died and Omri became king.-ÈY‚Un<pb/>Then the people of Israel were divided in half. Half of the people followed Tibni the son of Ginath, to make him king, and the <i>other</i> half followed Omri.8ÈX‚knNow as for the rest of the acts of Zimri, and his <n>[treasonous]</n> conspiracy which he committed, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel?&ÈW‚Gnbecause of the sins he had committed, doing evil in the sight of the LORD, by walking in the way of Jeroboam, and because of his sin he caused Israel to sin.$ÈV‚CnWhen Zimri saw that the city was taken, he went into the fortress of the king's palace; and <i>while inside</i>, he set fire to the king's palace and died,]ÈU5nThen Omri went up from Gibbethon, and all Israel with him, and they besieged Tirzah.JÈTƒnand the people who were camped heard it said, "Zimri has conspired and has also struck down the king." So all Israel made Omri, the commander of the army, king over Israel that day in the camp.\ÈSƒ3n<pb/>In the twenty-seventh year of Asa king of Judah, Zimri reigned <n>[over Israel]</n> for seven days at Tirzah. Now the troops were camped against Gibbethon, <n>[a city]</n> which belonged to the Philistines,ÈR‚)nNow as for the rest of the acts of Elah and everything that he did, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel?+ÈQ‚Qn
for all the sins of Baasha and the sins of Elah his son, which they committed, and made Israel commit, provoking the LORD God of Israel to anger with their idols."ÈP‚?n<pb/>Thus Zimri destroyed the entire household of Baasha, in accordance with the word of the LORD which He spoke against Baasha through Jehu the prophet,IÈOƒ
nWhen he became king, as soon as he sat on his throne, he killed the entire household of Baasha; he did not leave a single male <n>[alive]</n>, either of his<f>[1]</f> relatives or his friends.ÈN‚n
Then Zimri came in and struck and killed Elah in the twenty-seventh year of Asa king of Judah, and became king in his place.MÈMƒn	His servant Zimri, commander of half his chariots, conspired against Elah. Now Elah was in Tirzah, getting drunk in the house of Arza, who was in charge of the <n>[king's]</n> household in Tirzah.ÈL‚#n<pb/>In the twenty-sixth year of Asa king of Judah, Elah the son of Baasha became king over Israel in Tirzah, <i>and reigned</i> two years.‚gÈK…InMoreover, the word of the LORD came against Baasha and his household through the prophet Jehu the son of Hanani, both for all the evil that Baasha did in the sight of the LORD in provoking Him to anger with the work of his hands (idolatry), in being like the house of Jeroboam, and because he <n>[willingly]</n> destroyed it (the family of Jeroboam).yÈJmnBaasha slept with his fathers <n>[in death]</n> and was buried in Tirzah. Elah his son became king in his place.ÈI‚7n<pb/>Now as for the rest of the acts of Baasha, what he did and his might, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel?ÈH‚5nThe dogs will eat anyone belonging to Baasha who dies in the city, and the birds of the sky will eat anyone belonging to him who dies in the field."ÈG‚3nbehold, I am going to sweep away Baasha and his household (family), and I will make your house (royal line) like that of Jeroboam the son of Nebat.mÈFƒUn"Because I exalted you <n>[Baasha]</n> from the dust and made you leader over My people Israel, and you walked in the way of Jeroboam and made My people Israel sin, provoking Me to anger with their sins <n>[of idol worship]</n>,\ÈE	5n<pb/>Now the word of the LORD came to Jehu the son of Hanani against Baasha, saying,ÈD‚n"He did evil in the sight of the LORD and walked in the way of Jeroboam and in his sin <n>[of idolatry]</n> with which he made Israel sin.5ÈC‚en!<pb/>In the third year of Asa king of Judah, Baasha the son of Ahijah became king over all Israel in Tirzah <n>[the capital city]</n>, <i>and reigned</i> twenty-four years.LÈBn There was war between Asa and Baasha king of Israel all their days.ÈA‚+n<pb/>Now as for the rest of Nadab's acts and everything that he did, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel?È@‚nbecause of the sins committed by Jeroboam and which he made Israel commit, and because he provoked the LORD God of Israel to anger.|È?ƒsnAs soon as he was king, Baasha struck down all the household of Jeroboam. He did not leave for Jeroboam anyone alive, but he destroyed them in accordance with the word of the LORD which He had spoken through His servant Ahijah the Shiloniteâ€”fÈ>Gn<pb/>So Baasha killed Nadab in the third year of Asa king of Judah, and reigned in his place.bÈ=ƒ?nBaasha the son of Ahijah of the house (tribe) of Issachar conspired against Nadab, and Baasha struck him down at Gibbethon, which belonged to the Philistines, while Nadab and all Israel were laying siege to Gibbethon.'È<‚InHe did evil in the sight of the LORD and walked in the way of his father <n>[Jeroboam]</n> and in his sin <n>[of idolatry]</n>, with which he made Israel sin.È;‚#nNow Nadab the son of Jeroboam began to reign over Israel in the second year of Asa king of Judah, and he reigned over Israel for two years. È:‚;nAsa slept with his fathers <n>[in death]</n> and was buried with his fathers in the city of his father David. Jehoshaphat his son reigned in his place.~È9ƒwnNow as for the rest of all the acts of Asa, all his might, everything that he did, and the cities which he built, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? But during the time of his old age he had a foot disease.‚È8„%nThen King Asa made a proclamation to all Judahâ€”none was exemptâ€”and they carried away the stones of Ramah and its timber with which Baasha had built <n>[the fortifications]</n>. And King Asa built with them <n>[border fortresses at]</n> Geba of Benjamin and Mizpah.`È7;nWhen Baasha heard about it, he stopped<f>[3]</f> fortifying Ramah and stayed in Tirzah.|È6ƒsnSo Ben-hadad listened to king Asa and sent the commanders of his armies against the cities of Israel, and conquered Ijon, Dan, Abel-beth-maacah, and all Chinneroth <n>[the region of the Sea of Galilee]</n>, along with all the land of Naphtali.‚È5„n"<i>Let there be</i> a treaty between me and you, <i>as there was</i> between my father and your father. Behold, I have sent you a gift of silver and gold; go, break your treaty with Baasha king of Israel, so that he will<f>[2]</f> withdraw from me."‚3È4„anThen Asa took all the silver and gold left in the treasuries of the house of the LORD and the treasuries of the palace of the king and handed them over to his servants. And King Asa sent them to Ben-hadad the son of Tabrimmon, the son of Hezion, king of Aram (Syria), who lived in Damascus, saying,9È3‚mnBaasha king of Israel went up against Judah and fortified Ramah <n>[north of Jerusalem]</n>, in order to prevent <i>anyone</i> from going out or coming in to Asa king of Judah.UÈ2%n<pb/>Now there was war between Asa and Baasha king of Israel all their days.3È1‚anHe brought the things which his father had dedicated and the things which he had dedicated into the house of the LORDâ€”silver, gold, and utensils <i>and</i> accessories.È0‚nBut the high places <n>[of idol worship]</n> were not removed. Nevertheless, Asa's heart was entirely devoted to the LORD all his days.xÈ/ƒkn
He also deposed his <n>[great-grand]</n>mother Maacah from <i>being</i> queen mother, because she had made a horrid (obscene, vulgar) image for <n>[the goddess]</n> Asherah. Asa cut down her horrid image, and burned it by the Brook Kidron.$È.‚CnHe expelled the male cult prostitutes (sodomites) from the land and removed all the idols that his fathers <n>[Solomon, Rehoboam, and Abijam]</n> had made.^È-7nAsa did what was right in the eyes of the LORD, as did his father (forefather) David. È,{n
He reigned forty-one years in Jerusalem. His <n>[great-grand]</n>mother was Maacah the daughter of Abishalom (Absalom).iÈ+Mn	<pb/>So in the twentieth year of Jeroboam king of Israel, Asa began to reign as king over Judah.È*‚nAbijam slept with his fathers <n>[in death]</n> and they buried him in the City of David. Asa his son became king in his place.LÈ)ƒn<pb/>Now as for the rest of the acts of Abijam and everything that he did, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? And there was war between Abijam and Jeroboam.nÈ(WnThere was war between Rehoboam <n>[Abijam's father]</n> and Jeroboam all the days of Rehoboam's life.]È'ƒ5nbecause David did what was right in the eyes of the LORD, and had not turned aside from anything that He commanded him all the days of his life, except in the matter of <n>[the betrayal of]</n> Uriah the Hittite.*È&‚OnNevertheless, for David's sake the LORD his God gave him a lamp (descendant on the throne) in Jerusalem, setting up his son after him and establishing Jerusalem,`È%ƒ;nHe walked in all the sins <n>[of idol worship]</n> that his father <n>[Rehoboam]</n> committed before him; and his heart was not entirely devoted to the LORD his God, like the heart of his father (forefather) David.}È$unHe reigned three years in Jerusalem. His mother was Maacah <n>[grand]</n>daughter of Abishalom (<f>[1]</f> Absalom).eÈ#	Gn<pb/>In the eighteenth year of King Jeroboam the son of Nebat, Abijam became king over Judah.SÈ"ƒ!nAnd Rehoboam slept with his fathers <n>[in death]</n> and was buried with his fathers in the City of David. His mother's name was Naamah the Ammonitess. Abijam (Abijah) his son became king in his place.FÈ!nThere was also war between Rehoboam and Jeroboam continually. È ‚;n<pb/>Now as for the rest of the acts of Rehoboam, and everything that he did, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah?