Elijah replied to the captain of fifty, "So if I am a man of God, then let fire come down from heaven and consume you and your fifty <n>[fighting men]</n>." Then fire fell from heaven and consumed him and his fifty.‚ÊG	„	x	<pb/>Then the king sent to Elijah a captain of fifty with his fifty <n>[fighting men to seize the prophet]</n>. And he went up to him, and behold, he was sitting on the top of a hill. And the captain said to him, "Man of God, the king says, 'Come down.'"3ÊF	‚cxThey answered him, "<i>He was</i> a<f>[4]</f> hairy man with a <n>[wide]</n> leather<f>[5]</f> band bound around his loins." And Ahaziah said, "It is Elijah the Tishbite."wÊE	kxThe king asked them, "What was the appearance of the man who came up to meet you and said these things to you?"‚AÊD	„xThey replied, "A man came up to meet us and said to us, 'Go, return to the king who sent you and tell him, "Thus says the LORD: 'Is it because there is no God in Israel that you send to inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron? Therefore you will not leave the bed on which you lie, but you will certainly die.'"'"pÊC	]x<pb/>When the messengers returned to Ahaziah, he said to them, "Why have you returned <n>[so soon]</n>?"ÊB	‚9xTherefore this is what the LORD says: 'You <n>[Ahaziah]</n> will not leave the bed on which you lie, but you will certainly die.'" So Elijah departed.‚
ÊA	„xBut the angel of the LORD said to Elijah the<f>[2]</f> Tishbite, "<f>[3]</f> Arise, go up to meet the messengers of the king of Samaria, and say to them, 'Is it because there is no God in Israel that you are going to inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron?'‚&Ê@	„IxAhaziah <n>[the king of Israel]</n> fell through the lattice (grid) in his upper chamber which was in Samaria, and became sick <n>[from the injury]</n>. So he sent messengers, saying to them, "Go, inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of<f>[1]</f> Ekron, if I will recover from this sickness."EÊ?			x<pb/>Now Moab rebelled against Israel after the death of Ahab.!Ê>‚=n5He served Baal and worshiped him, and he provoked the LORD God of Israel to anger, in accordance with everything that his father <n>[Ahab]</n> had done.YÊ=ƒ-n4He did evil in the sight of the LORD and walked in the <n>[idolatrous]</n> way of his father <n>[Ahab]</n> and of his mother <n>[Jezebel]</n>, and in the way of Jeroboam the son of Nebat, who made Israel sin.(Ê<‚Kn3<pb/>Ahaziah the son of Ahab became king over Israel in Samaria in the seventeenth year of Jehoshaphat king of Judah, and he reigned over Israel for two years.,Ê;‚Sn2Jehoshaphat slept with his fathers <n>[in death]</n> and was buried with his fathers in the city of his father David, and Jehoram his son became king in his place."Ê:‚?n1Then Ahaziah the son of Ahab said to Jehoshaphat, "Let my servants go with your servants in the ships," But Jehoshaphat was unwilling <i>and</i> refused.(Ê9‚Kn0Jehoshaphat had <n>[large cargo]</n> ships of Tarshish constructed to go to Ophir for gold, but they did not go, because the ships were wrecked at Ezion-geber.^Ê87n/<pb/>Now there was no king in Edom; a deputy (governor) was <n>[serving as]</n> king.Ê7‚n.And the remnant of the sodomites (male cult prostitutes) who remained in the days of his father Asa, Jehoshaphat expelled from the land.2Ê6‚_n-<pb/>Now the rest of the acts of Jehoshaphat, his might which he displayed and how he made war, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah?<Ê5un,Jehoshaphat also made peace with the king of Israel.dÊ4ƒCn+He walked in all the ways of Asa his father, without turning from them, doing right in the sight of the LORD. However, the high places were not removed; the people still sacrificed and burned incense on the high places.'Ê3‚In*Jehoshaphat was thirty-five years old when he became king, and he reigned twenty-five years in Jerusalem. His mother's name was Azubah the daughter of Shilhi.iÊ2Mn)Now Jehoshaphat the son of Asa became king over Judah in the fourth year of Ahab king of Israel.hÊ1Kn(So Ahab slept with his fathers <n>[in death]</n>, and Ahaziah his son became king in his place.OÊ0ƒn'Now the rest of Ahab's acts, and everything that he did, the ivory palace which he built and all the cities which he built, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel?OÊ/ƒn&They washed the chariot by the pool <n>[on the outskirts]</n> of Samaria, where the prostitutes bathed, and the dogs licked up his blood, in accordance with the word of the LORD which He had spoken._Ê.9n%<pb/>So the king died and was brought to Samaria, and they buried the king in Samaria.
Ê-‚n$Then about sundown a resounding cry passed throughout the army, saying, "Every man to his city and every man to his own country!"]Ê,ƒ5n#The battle raged that day, and <n>[Ahab]</n> the king was propped up in his chariot facing the Arameans (Syrians). And in the evening he died, and the blood from his wound ran down into the bottom of the chariot.kÊ+ƒQn"<pb/>But one man drew a bow at<f>[3]</f> random and struck the king of Israel in a joint of the armor. So he said to the driver of his chariot, "Turn around and take me out of the fight, because I have been seriously wounded."vÊ*gn!When the captains of the chariots saw that it was not the king of Israel, they turned back from pursuing him.9Ê)‚mn When the captains of the chariots saw Jehoshaphat, they said, "Surely it is the king of Israel." They turned to fight against him, and Jehoshaphat shouted out <n>[in fear]</n>.QÊ(ƒnNow the king of Aram (Syria) had commanded the thirty-two captains of his chariots, saying, "Do not fight with <n>[anyone, either]</n> small or great, but with <n>[Ahab]</n> the king of Israel alone."NÊ'ƒnThe king of Israel said to Jehoshaphat, "I will disguise myself and enter the battle, but you put on your <n>[royal]</n> clothing." So the king of Israel disguised himself and went into the battle.hÊ&KnSo <n>[Ahab]</n> the king of Israel and Jehoshaphat the king of Judah went up to Ramoth-gilead.|Ê%snMicaiah said, "If you indeed return safely, the LORD has not spoken by me." Then he said, "Listen, all you people."	Ê$‚
nand say, 'Thus says the king, "Put this man in prison, and feed him sparingly with the bread and water until I return safely."'"Ê#‚nThen the king of Israel said, "Take Micaiah and return him to Amon, the governor of the city, and to Joash, the king's son,$Ê"‚CnMicaiah said, "Behold, you shall see on that day <n>[of the king's defeat]</n> when you enter an inner room <n>[looking for a place]</n> to hide yourself."#Ê!‚An<pb/>But Zedekiah the son of Chenaanah approached and struck Micaiah on the cheek and said, "How did the Spirit of the LORD pass from me to speak to you?"Ê ‚%nNow then, behold, the LORD has put a deceiving spirit in the mouth of all these prophets; and the LORD has proclaimed disaster against you."JÊƒnThe LORD said to him, 'How?' And he said, 'I will go out and be a deceiving spirit in the mouth of all his prophets.' Then the LORD said, 'You are to entice him and also succeed. Go and do so.'\Ê3nThen a spirit came forward and stood before the LORD and said, 'I will entice him.'~ÊwnThe LORD said, 'Who will entice Ahab to go up and fall at Ramoth-gilead?' And one said this, while another said that.=Ê‚un<pb/>Micaiah said, "Therefore, hear the word of the LORD. I saw the LORD sitting on His throne, and all the host (army) of heaven standing by Him on His right hand and on His left.Ê‚n<pb/>Then the king of Israel said to Jehoshaphat, "Did I not tell you that he would not prophesy good concerning me, but evil?"xÊƒkn<t>And he said, </t><br/><t>"I saw all Israel </t><t>Scattered upon the mountains, </t><t>Like sheep that have no shepherd. </t><t>And the LORD said, </t><t>'These have no master. </t><t>Let each of them return to his house in peace.'"</t>Ê‚3nBut the king <n>[doubted him and]</n> said to him, "How many times must I make you swear to tell me nothing but the truth in the name of the LORD?"eÊƒEn<pb/>So when he came to the king, the king said to him, "Micaiah, shall we go against Ramoth-gilead in battle, or shall we not?" And he answered him, "Go up and be successful, for the LORD will hand it over to the king."VÊ'nBut Micaiah said, "As the LORD lives, I will speak what the LORD says to me."oÊƒYn
Then the messenger who went to summon Micaiah said to him, "<f>[2]</f> Listen carefully, the words of the prophets are unanimously favorable to the king. Please let your words be like the word of one of them, and speak favorably."3Ê‚anAll the prophets were prophesying in the same way <n>[to please Ahab]</n>, saying, "Go up to Ramoth-gilead and be successful, for the LORD will hand it over to the king."6Ê‚gnThen Zedekiah the son of Chenaanah made for himself horns of iron and said, "Thus says the LORD: 'With these you will gore the Arameans (Syrians) until they are destroyed.'"‚Ê„+n
Now the king of Israel and Jehoshaphat king of Judah were each sitting on his throne, dressed in <i>their</i> <n>[royal]</n> robes, <n>[in an open place]</n> at the threshing floor at the entrance of the gate of Samaria; and all the prophets were prophesying before them.hÊKn	Then the king of Israel summoned an officer and said, "Bring quickly Micaiah the son of Imlah."‚:Ê„onThe king of Israel said to Jehoshaphat, "There is one more man, Micaiah the son of Imlah, by whom we may inquire of the LORD, but I hate him, because he never prophesies good <i>news</i> for me, but <i>only</i> evil." But Jehoshaphat said, "May the king not say that <n>[Micaiah only tells bad news]</n>."tÊcnBut Jehoshaphat <n>[doubted and]</n> said, "Is there not another prophet of the LORD here whom we may ask?"nÊƒWnThen the king of Israel gathered the prophets together, about four hundred men, and said to them, "Shall I go to battle against Ramoth-gilead, or should I not?" And they said, "Go up, for the LORD has handed it over to the king."jÊOn<pb/>But Jehoshaphat said to the king of Israel, "Please inquire first for the word of the LORD."GÊ
