Two worthless <i>and</i> unprincipled men came in and sat down opposite him; and they testified against Naboth before the people, saying, "Naboth cursed <i>and</i> renounced God and the king." Then they brought him outside the city and stoned him to death.LÉxnThey proclaimed a fast and seated Naboth at the head of the people.:Éw‚onSo the men of his city, the elders and the nobles who lived there, did as Jezebel had sent <i>word</i> to them, just as it was written in the letters which she had sent to them.HÉvƒn
and seat two worthless <i>and</i> unprincipled men opposite him, and have them testify against him, saying, 'You<f>[2]</f> cursed God and the king.' Then take him out and stone him to death."lÉuSn	Now in the letters she wrote, "Proclaim a<f>[1]</f> fast and seat Naboth at the head of the people;Ét‚/n<pb/>So she wrote letters in Ahab's name and sealed them with his seal, and sent them to the elders and nobles who lived with Naboth in his city.+És‚QnJezebel his wife said to him, "Do you now reign over Israel? Get up, eat food, and let your heart rejoice; I will give you the vineyard of Naboth the Jezreelite."qÉrƒ]nAnd he said to her, "Because I spoke to Naboth the Jezreelite and said to him, 'Give me your vineyard for money; or if you prefer, I will give you <i>another</i> vineyard for it.' But he answered, 'I will not give you my vineyard.'"xÉqkn<pb/>Then Jezebel his wife came to him and asked him, "Why is your spirit so troubled that you have not eaten?"‚JÉp…nSo Ahab <n>[already upset by the Lord's message]</n> came into his house <n>[feeling more]</n> resentful and sullen because of what Naboth the Jezreelite had said to him; for he had said, "I will not give you the inheritance of my fathers." And he lay down on his bed and turned away his face, and would not eat any food.oÉoYnBut Naboth said to Ahab, "The LORD forbid me that I should give the inheritance of my fathers to you."‚Én„	nAhab spoke to Naboth, saying, "Give me your vineyard, so that I may have it for a garden of vegetables <i>and</i> herbs, because it is near my house. I will give you a better vineyard for it, or, if you prefer, I will give you what it is worth in money."(Ém	‚Mn<pb/>Now it came about after these things that Naboth the Jezreelite had a vineyard in Jezreel, close beside the <n>[winter]</n> palace of Ahab king of Samaria;[Él1n+So the king of Israel went to his house resentful and sullen, and came to Samaria.mÉkƒUn*He said to the king, "Thus says the LORD: 'Because you have released from your hand the man <n>[Ben-hadad]</n> whom I had devoted to destruction, your life shall be <i>required</i> for his life, and your people for his people.'"Éj‚n)Then the prophet quickly removed the bandage from his eyes, and <n>[Ahab]</n> the king of Israel recognized him as one of the prophets.>Éi‚wn(But while your servant was busy here and there, he <n>[escaped and]</n> was gone." And the king of Israel said to him, "Such is your own judgment (verdict); you have determined it."‚VÉh…'n'As the king passed by, the prophet called out to the king and said, "Your servant went out into the middle of the battle, and behold, a man turned aside and brought a man to me and said, 'Guard this man; if for any reason he is missing, then your life shall be <i>required</i> for his life, or else you shall pay a talent of silver.'Ég‚#n&So the prophet left and waited for King Ahab by the road, and disguised himself <n>[as a wounded soldier]</n> with a bandage over his eyes.sÉfan%Then the prophet found another man and said, "Please strike me." So the man struck him hard, wounding him.OÉeƒn$Then the prophet said to him, "Because you have not obeyed the voice of the LORD, behold, as soon as you leave me, a lion will kill you." And as soon as he left him, a lion found him and killed him.%Éd‚En#<pb/>Now a certain man of<f>[5]</f> the sons of the prophets said to another by the word of the LORD, "Please strike me." But the man refused to strike him.‚LÉc…n"Ben-hadad <n>[tempting him]</n> said to him, "I will restore the cities which my father took from your father; and you may set up bazaars (shops) of your own in Damascus, as my father did in Samaria." Then, <i>Ahab replied</i>, "I will let you go with this covenant (treaty)." So he made a covenant with him and let him go.{Ébƒqn!Now the men took it as a <i>good</i> omen, and quickly understanding his meaning said, "Yes, your brother Ben-hadad <i>lives</i>." Then the king said, "Go, bring him." Then Ben-hadad came out to him, and Ahab had him come up into the chariot.`Éaƒ;n So they put sackcloth around their loins and ropes on their necks, and came to the king of Israel and said, "Your servant Ben-hadad says, 'Please let me live.'" And Ahab asked, "Is he still alive? He is my brother."‚6É`„gn<pb/>But his servants said to him, "We have heard that the kings of the house (royal line) of Israel are merciful kings. Please let us put sackcloth around our<f>[4]</f> loins and ropes on our necks <n>[as symbols of submission]</n>, and go out to the king of Israel; perhaps he will spare your life."KÉ_ƒnBut the rest ran to the city of Aphek, and the <n>[city]</n> wall fell on 27,000 of the men who were left. Ben-hadad escaped and came into the city, going into an inner chamber <n>[to hide]</n>.9É^‚mnSo they camped opposite each other for seven days. Then on the seventh day the battle began, and the sons of Israel killed 100,000 of the Aramean foot soldiers in a single day.‚(É]„KnA man of God approached and said to the king of Israel, "Thus says the LORD, 'Because the Arameans have said, "The LORD is a god of the hills, but He is not a god of the valleys," I will give this great army into your hand, and you shall know <n>[by experience]</n> that I am the LORD.'"‚É\„nThe sons of Israel were counted and given provisions, and they went to meet them. The Israelites camped before the enemy like two<f>[3]</f> little flocks of goats <n>[with everything against them, except God]</n>, and the Arameans filled the country.EÉ[ƒnAt the first of the year <n>[in spring]</n>, Ben-hadad assembled <i>and</i> counted the Arameans (Syrians) and went up to Aphek <n>[east of the Sea of Galilee]</n> to fight against Israel.~ÉZƒwnand assemble an army like the army that you have lost <i>in battle</i>, horse for horse and chariot for chariot. Then we will fight against them in the plain, and surely we shall be stronger than they." And he listened to their words and did so.tÉYcnDo this: remove the <n>[thirty-two allied]</n> kings, each from his place, and put captains in their place,vÉXƒgn<pb/>Now the servants of the king of Aram said to him, "Israel's<f>[1]</f> god is a<f>[2]</f> god of the hills; that is why they were stronger than we. But let us fight against them in the plain, and surely we will be stronger than they.aÉWƒ=n<pb/>Then the prophet approached the king of Israel and said to him, "Go, strengthen yourself and observe and see what you have to do; for at the first of next year the king of Aram (Syria) will come up against you."
ÉV‚nThe king of Israel went out and struck <n>[the riders of]</n> the horses and chariots, and killed the Arameans in a great slaughter.ÉU‚)nAnd each one killed his man; and the Arameans (Syrians) fled and Israel pursued them. Ben-hadad king of Aram escaped on a horse with horsemen.tÉTcn<pb/>So these young men of the governors of the districts went out of the city, and the army followed them.YÉS-nAnd he said, "Whether they have come out for peace or for war, take them alive."%ÉR‚EnThe young men of the governors of the districts went out first; and Ben-hadad sent <i>men</i> out and they told him, saying, "Men have come out of Samaria."ÉQ‚!n<pb/>They went out at noon, while Ben-hadad was getting drunk in the temporary shelters, he and the thirty-two kings who were helping him.NÉPƒnThen Ahab assembled <i>and</i> counted the young men of the governors of the districts, and there were 232. After them he assembled <i>and</i> counted all the people, all the sons of Israel, 7,000.cÉOƒAnAhab said, "By whom?" And he said, "Thus says the LORD: 'By the young men <n>[the attendants or bodyguards]</n> of the governors of the districts.'" Then Ahab said, "Who shall begin the battle?" And he answered, "You."cÉNƒAn
Then a prophet approached Ahab king of Israel and said, "Thus says the LORD: 'Have you seen all this great army? Behold, I will hand them over to you, and you shall know <n>[without any doubt]</n> that I am the LORD.'"hÉMƒKnWhen Ben-hadad heard this message, as he and the kings were drinking in the temporary shelters, he said to his servants, "Station <i>yourselves</i>." So they stationed <i>themselves</i> against the city <n>[of Samaria]</n>.?ÉL‚ynThe king of Israel answered, "Tell him, 'A man who puts on <n>[his armor to go to battle]</n> should not boast like the man who takes it off <n>[after the battle has been won]</n>.'"EÉKƒn
Ben-hadad sent <i>word</i> to him and said, "May the gods do so to me, and more also, if there is enough dust left of Samaria for handfuls for all the <n>[armed]</n> people who follow me."‚ÉJ„!n	So he said to Ben-hadad's messengers, "Tell my lord the king, 'Every <i>demand</i> you first sent to your servant I will do, but I cannot do this <n>[additional]</n> thing <n>[as a condition of peace]</n>.'" And the messengers left; then they brought him word again.vÉIgnAll the elders and all the people said to him, "Do not listen or consent <n>[to this additional demand]</n>."‚ÉH„n<pb/>Then the king of Israel summoned all the elders of the land and said, "Please observe and see how this man is seeking our destruction. For he sent <i>messengers</i> to me for my wives, my children, my silver, and my gold, and I did not refuse him."oÉGƒYnbut about this time tomorrow I will send my servants to you, and they will search your house and the houses of your servants; and they will take with their hands (confiscate) whatever is desirable in your eyes and carry it away.'"6ÉF‚gnThe messengers returned and said, "Thus says Ben-hadad: 'I indeed sent <i>word</i> to you, saying, "You shall give me your silver, your gold, your wives, and your children,"ÉE‚nThe king of Israel <n>[conceded his defeat and]</n> answered, "By your word, my lord, O king, I am yours, and all that I have."ÉD‚n'Your silver and your gold are mine; your wives and your children, even the fairest, also are mine <n>[as conditions of peace]</n>.'"nÉCWnThen he sent messengers to the city to Ahab king of Israel; and he said to him, "Thus says Ben-hadad:kÉB	ƒSn<pb/>Ben-hadad king of Aram (Syria) gathered all his army together; thirty-two kings were <n>[allied]</n> with him, with horses and chariots. And he went up and besieged Samaria <n>[Israel's capital]</n>, and fought against it.‚ÉA„nSo Elisha left him and went back. Then he took a pair of oxen and sacrificed them and boiled their meat with the implements of the oxen <n>[as fuel]</n>, and gave <i>the meat</i> to the people, and they ate. Then he stood and followed Elijah, and served him.YÉ@ƒ-nHe left the oxen and ran after Elijah and said, "Please let me kiss my father and mother <n>[goodbye]</n>, then I will follow you." And he said to him, "Go on back; for what have I done to <n>[stop]</n> you?"gÉ?ƒIn<pb/>So Elijah departed from there and found Elisha the son of Shaphat, while he was plowing with twelve pairs <i>of oxen</i> before him, and he with the twelfth. Elijah went over to him and threw his mantle (coat) on him.É>‚'nYet I will leave 7,000 <n>[survivors]</n> in Israel, all the knees that have not bowed down to Baal and every mouth that has not kissed him."$É=‚CnIt shall come about that Jehu shall put to death whoever escapes from the sword of Hazael, and Elisha shall put to death whoever escapes the sword of Jehu."É<‚?nand you shall anoint Jehu the son of Nimshi as king over Israel; and anoint Elisha the son of Shaphat of<f>[5]</f> Abel-meholah as prophet in your place.,É;‚Sn<pb/>The LORD<f>[4]</f> said to him, "Go, return on your way to the Wilderness of Damascus; and when you arrive, you shall anoint Hazael as king over Aram (Syria);‚
É:„nHe said, "I have been very zealous for the LORD God of hosts (armies), because the sons of Israel have abandoned (broken) Your covenant, torn down Your altars and killed Your prophets with the sword. And I, only I, am left; and they seek to take away my life."WÉ9ƒ)n
When Elijah heard <i>the sound</i>, he wrapped his face in his mantle (cloak) and went out and stood in the entrance of the cave. And behold, a voice came to him and said, "What are you doing here, Elijah?"!É8‚=nAfter the earthquake, <n>[there was]</n> a fire, but the LORD was not in the fire; and after the fire, <n>[there was]</n> the sound of a gentle blowing.‚`É7…;n<pb/>So He said, "Go out and stand on the mountain before the LORD." And behold, the LORD was passing by, and a great and powerful wind was tearing out the mountains and breaking the rocks in pieces before the LORD; but the LORD was not in the wind. And after the wind, <n>[there was]</n> an earthquake, but the LORD was not in the earthquake.‚[É6…1n
He said, "I have been very<f>[3]</f> zealous (impassioned) for the LORD God of hosts (armies) <n>[proclaiming what is rightfully and uniquely His]</n>; for the sons of Israel have abandoned (broken) Your covenant, torn down Your altars, and killed Your prophets with the sword. And I, only I, am left; and they seek to take away my life."'É5‚In	There he came to a cave and spent the night in it; and behold, the word of the LORD <i>came</i> to him, and He said to him, "What are you doing here, Elijah?"É4‚#nSo he got up and ate and drank, and with the strength of that food he traveled forty days and nights to Horeb (Sinai), the mountain of God.3É3‚anThen the angel of the LORD came again a second time and touched him and said, "Get up, and eat, for the journey is too long for you <n>[without adequate sustenance]</n>."
É2‚nHe looked, and by his head there was a bread cake baked on hot coal, and a pitcher of water. So he ate and drank and lay down again.zÉ1onHe lay down and slept under the juniper tree, and behold, an angel touched him and said to him, "Get up and eat."uÉ0ƒenBut he himself traveled a day's journey into the wilderness, and he came and sat down under a juniper tree and asked <n>[God]</n> that he might die. He said, "It is enough; now, O LORD, take my life, for I am no better than my fathers."É/‚+nAnd Elijah was afraid and arose and ran for his life, and he came to<f>[2]</f> Beersheba which belongs to Judah, and he left his servant there.=É.‚unThen Jezebel sent a messenger to Elijah, saying, "So may the gods do to me, and even more, if by this time tomorrow I do not make your<f>[1]</f> life like the life of one of them."É-	‚n<pb/>Now Ahab told Jezebel all that Elijah had done, and how he had killed all the prophets <n>[of Baal]</n> with the sword.HÉ,ƒn.Then the hand of the LORD came upon Elijah <n>[giving him supernatural strength]</n>. He<f>[6]</f> girded up his loins and outran Ahab to the entrance of Jezreel <n>[nearly twenty miles]</n>.EÉ+ƒn-In a little while the sky grew dark with clouds and wind, and there were heavy showers. And Ahab mounted <i>and</i> rode <n>[his chariot]</n> and went <n>[inland]</n> to<f>[5]</f> Jezreel.tÉ*ƒcn,And at the seventh <i>time</i> the servant said, "A cloud as small as a man's hand is coming up from the sea." And Elijah said, "Go up, say to Ahab, 'Prepare <i>your chariot</i> and go down, so that the rain shower does not stop you.'"É)‚5n+and he said to his servant, "Go up, look toward the sea." So he went up and looked and said, "There is nothing." Elijah said, "Go back" seven times.É(‚3n*So Ahab went up to eat and to drink. And Elijah went up to the top of Carmel; and he crouched down to the earth and put his face between his knees,É'}n)<pb/>Now Elijah said to Ahab, "Go up, eat and drink, for there is the sound of the roar <i>of an abundance</i> of rain."YÉ&ƒ-n(Then Elijah said to them, "Seize the prophets of Baal; do not let one of them escape." They seized them; and Elijah brought them down to the brook Kishon, and <n>[as God's law required]</n> killed them there.xÉ%kn'When all the people saw it, they fell face downward; and they said, "The LORD, He is God! The LORD, He is God!")É$‚Mn&Then the fire of the LORD fell and consumed the burnt offering and the wood, and <i>even</i> the stones and the dust; it also licked up the water in the trench.É#‚1n%Answer me, O LORD, answer me, so that this people may know that You, O LORD, are God, and that You have turned their hearts back <n>[to You]</n>."‚8É"„kn$At the time of the offering of the <i>evening</i> sacrifice, Elijah the prophet approached <n>[the altar]</n> and said, "O LORD, the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel (Jacob), let it be known today that You are God in Israel and that I am Your servant and that I have done all these things at Your word.UÉ!%n#The water flowed around the altar, and he also filled the trench with water.oÉ ƒYn"And he said, "Fill four pitchers with water and pour it on the burnt offering and the wood." And he said, "Do it the second time." And they did it the second time. And he said, "Do it the third time." And they did it a third time.TÉ#n!Then he laid out the wood and cut the ox in pieces and laid it on the wood.!É‚=n So with the stones Elijah built an altar in the name of the LORD. He made a trench around the altar large enough to hold<f>[4]</f> two measures of seed.5É‚enThen Elijah took twelve stones in accordance with the number of the tribes of the sons of Jacob, to whom the word of the LORD had come, saying, "Israel shall be your name."
