Then the king of Israel sent <i>word</i> to the place about which Elisha had warned him; so he guarded himself there repeatedly.ËT‚7x	The man of God sent <i>word</i> to the king of Israel saying, "Be careful not to pass by this place, because the Arameans are pulling back to there."ËS‚5xNow the king of Aram (Syria) was making war against Israel, and he consulted with his servants, saying, "My camp shall be in such and such a place."YËR-xHe said, "Pick it up for yourself." So he reached out with his hand and took it.)ËQ‚MxThe man of God said, "Where did it fall?" When he showed him the place, Elisha cut off a stick and threw it in there, and made the iron <n>[axe head]</n> float.ËP‚9xBut it happened that as one was cutting down a beam, the axe head fell into the water; and he cried out and said, "Oh no, my master! It was borrowed!"jËOOxSo he went with them; and when they came to the Jordan, they cut down <n>[some of]</n> the trees.cËNAxThen one said, "Please be willing to go with your servants." So he answered, "I shall go."PËMƒxPlease let us go to the Jordan <n>[River]</n> and let each man take from there a beam <n>[for the building]</n>; and let us make a place there for ourselves where we may live." And he answered, "Go."ËL	‚x<pb/>Now the<f>[1]</f> sons of the prophets said to Elisha, "Look now, the place where we<f>[2]</f> live near you is too small for us.%ËK‚ExTherefore, the leprosy of Naaman shall cling to you and to your descendants forever." So Gehazi departed from his presence, a leper <i>as white</i> as snow.‚ËJ„x<pb/>Elisha said to him, "Did my heart not go <i>with you</i>, when the man turned from his chariot to meet you? Is it a <n>[proper]</n> time to accept money and clothing and olive orchards and vineyards and sheep and oxen and male and female servants?ËI‚xThen he went in and stood before his master. Elisha asked him, "Where have you been, Gehazi?" He said, "Your servant went nowhere."ËH‚3xWhen he came to the hill, he took them from their hand and put them in the house <n>[for safekeeping]</n>; and he sent the men away, and they left.oËGƒYxNaaman said, "Please take two talents." And he urged him <n>[to accept]</n>, and tied up two talents of silver in two bags with two changes of clothes and gave them to two of his servants; and they carried them in front of Gehazi.sËFƒaxAnd he said, "All is well. My master has sent me to say, 'Just now two young men of the sons of the prophets have come to me from the hill country of Ephraim. Please give them a<f>[5]</f> talent of silver and two changes of clothes.'"ËE‚xSo Gehazi pursued Naaman. When Naaman saw someone running after him, he got down from the chariot to meet him and said, "Is all well?"nËDƒWx<pb/>when Gehazi, the servant of Elisha the man of God, said, "My master has spared this Naaman the Aramean (Syrian), by not accepting from him what he brought. As the LORD lives, I will run after him and get something from him."eËCExElisha said to him, "Go in peace." So Naaman departed and was a good distance away from him,‚_ËB…9xIn this matter may the LORD pardon your servant: when my master <n>[the king]</n> goes into the house of <n>[his god]</n> Rimmon to worship there, and he leans on my hand and I bow in the house of Rimmon, when I bow down in the house of Rimmon, may the LORD pardon your servant in this matter <n>[of attending the king when he worships]</n>."‚ËA„'xNaaman said, "If not, then please, let your servant be given a load of<f>[4]</f> earth for a team of mules; for <n>[from this day on]</n> your servant will no longer offer a burnt offering nor a sacrifice to other gods, but only to the LORD, <n>[the God of Israel]</n>.Ë@‚	xBut Elisha said, "As the LORD lives, before whom I stand, I will accept nothing." He urged him to take it, but Elisha refused.yË?ƒmxThen Naaman returned to the man of God, he and all the people in his group, and stood before him. He said, "Behold, I know that there is no God in all the earth, except in Israel; so now accept a blessing <i>and</i> gift from your servant."3Ë>‚axSo he went down and plunged himself into the Jordan seven times, just as the man of God had said; and his flesh was restored like that of a little child and he was clean.]Ë=ƒ5x
Then his servants approached and said to him, "My father, if the prophet had told you <i>to do some</i> great thing, would you not have done it? How much more then, when he has said to you, 'Wash, and be clean?'"@Ë<‚{xAre not Abana and Pharpar, the rivers of Damascus <n>[in Aram]</n>, better than all the waters of Israel? Could I not wash in them and be clean?" So he turned and went away in a rage.wË;ƒixBut Naaman was furious and went away and said, "Indeed! I thought 'He would at least come out to <n>[see]</n> me and stand and call on the name of the LORD his God, and wave his hand over the place <n>[of leprosy]</n> and heal the leper.'Ë:‚7x
Elisha sent a messenger to him, saying, "Go and wash in the Jordan seven times, and your flesh will be restored to you and <i>you will</i> be clean."cË9Ax	So Naaman came with his horses and chariots and stopped at the entrance of Elisha's house.‚
Ë8„x<pb/>Now when Elisha the man of God heard that the king of Israel had torn his clothes, he sent <i>word</i> to the king, asking, "Why have you torn your clothes? Just let Naaman come to me, and he shall know that there is a <n>[true]</n> prophet in Israel."‚iË7…MxWhen the king of Israel read the letter, he<f>[3]</f> tore his clothes <n>[in shock and outrage at the request]</n> and said, "Am I God, to kill and to make alive, that this man sends to me <n>[a request]</n> to heal a man of his leprosy? Just consider <n>[what he is asking]</n> and see how he is seeking an opportunity <n>[for a battle]</n> with me."NË6ƒx<pb/>And he brought the letter to the king of Israel. It said, "And now when this letter comes to you, I will have sent my servant Naaman to you, so that<f>[2]</f> you may heal him of his leprosy."rË5ƒ_xThen the king of Aram (Syria) said, "Go now, and I will send a letter to the king of Israel (Jehoram the son of Ahab)." So he left and took with him ten talents of silver and 6,000 <i>shekels</i> of gold, and ten changes of clothing.|Ë4sxNaaman went in and told his master <n>[the king]</n>, "The girl who is from the land of Israel said such and such."Ë3‚/xShe said to her mistress, "I wish that my master <n>[Naaman]</n> were with the prophet who is in Samaria! Then he would heal him of his leprosy."=Ë2‚uxThe Arameans (Syrians) had gone out in bands <n>[as raiders]</n> and had taken captive a little girl from the land of Israel; and she waited on Naaman's wife <n>[as a servant]</n>.‚Ë1	„/x<pb/>Now Naaman, commander of the army of the king of Aram (Syria), was <i>considered</i> a great man by his king, and was highly respected because through Naaman the LORD had given victory to Aram (Syria). He was also a man of courage, <i>but he was</i> a<f>[1]</f> leper.nË0Wx,So he set it before them, and they ate and left <i>some</i>, in accordance with the word of the LORD.`Ë/ƒ;x+His servant said, "How am I to set <n>[only]</n> this before a hundred <n>[hungry]</n> men?" He said, "Give it to the people so that they may eat, for thus says the LORD, 'They shall eat and have <i>some</i> left.'"‚6Ë.„gx*<pb/>Now <n>[at another time]</n> a man from Baal-shalisha came and brought the man of God bread of the first fruits, twenty loaves of barley bread, and fresh ears of grain <n>[in the husk]</n> in his sack. And Elisha said, "Give it to the people <n>[affected by the famine]</n> so that they may eat."0Ë-‚[x)But he said, "Bring<f>[4]</f> flour." And he threw it into the pot and said, "Serve it for the people so that they may eat." Then there was nothing harmful in the pot.Ë,‚7x(So they served it for the men to eat. But as they ate the stew, they cried out, "O man of God, there is death in the pot." And they could not eat it.lË+ƒSx'Then one <n>[of them]</n> went into the field to gather herbs, and found a wild vine and gathered from it a lapful of wild gourds, and came and cut them up into the pot of stew, although they did not know <i>what they were</i>.QË*ƒx&Elisha came back to Gilgal during a famine in the land. The sons of the prophets were sitting before him, and he said to his servant, "Put on the large pot and cook stew for the sons of the prophets."Ë)‚x%She came and fell at his feet, bowing herself to the ground <n>[in respect and gratitude]</n>. Then she picked up her son and left.
Ë(‚x$Then Elisha called Gehazi and said, "Call this Shunammite." So he called her. And when she came to him, he said, "Pick up your son.";Ë'‚qx#Then he returned and walked in the house once back and forth, and went up <n>[again]</n> and stretched himself out on him; and the boy sneezed seven times and he opened his eyes.WË&ƒ)x"Then he went up and lay on the child and put his mouth on his mouth, his eyes on his eyes, and his hands on his hands. And as he stretched himself out on him <i>and</i> held him, the boy's skin became warm.UË%%x!So he went in, shut the door behind the two of them, and prayed to the LORD.WË$)x <pb/>When Elisha came into the house, the child was dead and lying on his bed.ZË#ƒ/xGehazi went on ahead of them and laid the staff on the boy's face, but there was no sound or response <n>[from the boy]</n>. So he turned back to meet Elisha and told him, "The boy has not awakened (revived)."
Ë"‚xThe mother of the child said, "As the LORD lives and as your soul lives, I will not leave you." So Elisha arose and followed her.‚eË!…Ex<pb/>Then he said to Gehazi, "<f>[3]</f> Gird up your loins (prepare now!) and take my staff in your hand, and go <n>[to the woman's house]</n>; if you meet any man <n>[along the way]</n>, do not greet him and if a man greets you, do not <n>[stop to]</n> answer him; and lay my staff on the face of the boy <n>[as soon as you reach the house]</n>."gË IxThen she said, "Did I ask for a son from my lord? Did I not say, 'Do not give me false hope'?"‚#Ë„AxWhen she came to the mountain to the man of God, she took hold of his feet. Gehazi approached to push her away; but the man of God said, "Let her alone, for her soul is desperate <i>and</i> troubled within her; and the LORD has hidden <i>the reason</i> from me and has not told me."!Ë‚=xPlease run now to meet her and ask her, 'Is it well with you? Is it well with your husband? Is it well with the child?'" And she answered, "It is well."FËƒx<t>So she set out and came to the man of God at Mount Carmel. </t><br/>When the man of God saw her at a distance, he said to Gehazi his servant, "Look, there is the Shunammite <i>woman</i>.Ë‚!xThen she saddled the donkey and said to her servant, "Drive <n>[the animal]</n> fast; do not slow down the pace for me unless I tell you."Ë‚xHe said, "Why are you going to him today? It is neither the New Moon nor the Sabbath." And she said, "<i>It will be</i> all right."Ë‚7xThen she called to her husband and said, "Please send me one of the servants and one of the donkeys, so that I may run to the man of God and return."Ë‚xShe went up and laid him on the bed of the man of God, and shut <i>the door</i> <n>[of the small upper room]</n> behind him and left.|ËsxWhen he had carried and brought him to his mother, he sat on her lap until noon, and <i>then</i> he<f>[2]</f> died.oËYxBut he said to his father, "My head, my head." The man said to his servant, "Carry him to his mother."_Ë9xWhen the child was grown, the day came that he went out to his father, to the reapers.{Ëqx<pb/>But the woman conceived and gave birth to a son at that season the next year, just as Elisha had said to her.Ë‚xElisha said, "At this season next year, you will embrace a son." She said, "No, my lord. O man of God, do not lie to your maidservant."cËAxHe said, "Call her." So Gehazi called her, and she <n>[came and]</n> stood in the doorway.~ËwxLater Elisha said, "What then is to be done for her?" Gehazi answered, "Well, she has no son and her husband is old."‚+Ë„Qx
Now he said to Gehazi, "Say to her now, 'You have gone to all this trouble for us; what can I do for you? Would you <i>like</i> to be mentioned to the king or to the captain of the army?'" She answered, "I live among my own people <n>[in peace and security and need no special favors]</n>."nËWxAnd he said to Gehazi his servant, "Call this Shunammite." So he called her and she stood before him.YË-x<pb/>One day he came there and turned in to the upper room and lay down to rest.]Ëƒ5x
Please, let us make a small, fully-walled upper room <n>[on the housetop]</n> and put a bed there for him, with a table, a chair, and a lampstand. Then whenever he comes to <i>visit</i> us, he can turn in there."pË
[x	She said to her husband, "Behold, I sense that this is a holy man of God who frequently passes our way.\Ëƒ3xNow there came a day when Elisha went over to Shunem, where there was a prominent <i>and</i> influential woman, and she persuaded him to eat a meal. Afterward, whenever he passed by, he stopped there for a meal.
Ë‚xThen she came and told the man of God. He said, "Go, sell the oil and pay your debt, and you and your sons can live on the rest."8Ë
‚kxWhen the containers were all full, she said to her son, "Bring me another container." And he said to her, "There is not a one left." Then the oil stopped <n>[multiplying]</n>.Ë	‚xSo she left him and shut the door behind her and her sons; they were bringing her <i>the containers</i> as she poured <n>[the oil]</n>.>Ë‚wxThen you shall go in and shut the door behind you and your sons, and pour out <n>[the oil you have]</n> into all these containers, and you shall set aside each one when it is full."tËcxThen he said, "Go, borrow containers from all your neighbors, empty containersâ€”and not <i>just</i> a few.ZËƒ/xElisha said to her, "What shall I do for you? Tell me, what do you have <n>[of value]</n> in the house?" She said, "Your maidservant has nothing in the house except a <n>[small]</n> jar of <n>[olive]</n> oil."‚LË	…x<pb/>Now one of the wives <i>of a man</i> of the<f>[1]</f> sons of the prophets cried out to Elisha <n>[for help]</n>, saying "Your servant my husband is dead, and you know that your servant <n>[reverently]</n> feared the LORD; but the creditor is coming to take my two sons to be his slaves <n>[in payment for a loan]</n>."‚yË…mxThen the king of Moab took his<f>[5]</f> eldest son, who was to reign in his place, and<f>[6]</f> offered him <n>[publicly]</n> as a burnt offering <n>[to Chemosh]</n> on the <n>[city]</n> wall <n>[horrifying everyone]</n>. And there was great wrath against Israel, and Israel's allies <n>[Judah and Edom]</n> withdrew from King Jehoram and returned to their own land.-Ë‚UxWhen the king of Moab saw that the battle was too fierce for him, he took with him seven hundred swordsmen to break through to the king of Edom; but they could not.ƒË†xThey destroyed the <n>[walls of the]</n> cities, and each man threw a stone on every piece of good land, covering it <n>[with stones]</n>. And they stopped up all the springs of water and cut down all the good trees, until they left <i>nothing</i> in Kir-hareseth <n>[Moab's capital city]</n> but its stones. Then the <n>[stone]</n> slingers surrounded the city and destroyed it.RËƒxBut when they came to the camp of Israel, the Israelites rose up and struck the Moabites, so that they fled before them; and they went forward into the land, killing the Moabites <n>[as they went]</n>.9Ë ‚mxAnd they said, "This is blood! Clearly the kings have fought together, and have killed one another. Now then, Moab, to the spoil <n>[and the plunder of the dead soldiers]</n>!"Ê‚3xWhen they got up early the <i>next</i> morning, the sun shone on the water, and the Moabites saw the water across from <i>them</i> as red as blood.‚Ê~„x<pb/>Now all the Moabites heard that the <n>[three]</n> kings had come up to fight against them, and all<f>[4]</f> who were able to put on armor, as well as those who were older, were summoned and stood <n>[together in battle formation]</n> at the border.1Ê}‚]xIt happened in the morning, when the sacrifice was offered, that suddenly water came <n>[miraculously]</n> from the area of Edom, and the country was filled with water.AÊ|‚}xYou shall strike every fortified city and every choice (principal) city, and cut down every good tree and stop up all sources of water, and ruin every good piece of land with stones.'"iÊ{MxThis is but a simple thing in the sight of the LORD; He will also hand over the Moabites to you.+Êz‚QxFor thus says the LORD, 'You will not see wind or rain, yet that valley will be filled with water, so you and your cattle and your <i>other</i> animals may drink.XÊy+xHe said, "Thus says the LORD, 'Make this valley (the Arabah) full of trenches.'Êx‚	xBut now bring me a musician." And it came about while the musician played, that the hand (power) of the LORD came upon Elisha.KÊwƒxElisha said, "As the LORD of hosts (armies) lives, before whom I stand, were it not that I have regard for Jehoshaphat king of Judah, I would not look at you nor see you <n>[king of Israel]</n>.‚cÊv…Ax
<pb/>Now Elisha said to the king of Israel, "What <i>business</i> do you have with me? Go to the prophets of your <n>[wicked]</n> father <n>[Ahab]</n> and to the prophets of your <n>[pagan]</n> mother <n>[Jezebel]</n>." But the king of Israel said to him, "No, for the LORD has called these three kings <i>together</i> to be handed over to Moab."Êu‚xJehoshaphat said, "The word of the LORD is with him." So the king of Israel and Jehoshaphat and the king of Edom went down to Elisha.zÊtƒoxBut Jehoshaphat said, "Is there no prophet of the LORD here from whom we may inquire of the LORD?" One of the servants of the king of Israel answered, "Elisha the son of Shaphat is here, who<f>[3]</f> used to pour water over Elijah's hands."|Êssx
Then the king of Israel said, "We are doomed, for the LORD has called these three kings to be handed over to Moab."KÊrƒx	<pb/>So the king of Israel went with the king of Judah and the king of Edom. They made a circuit of seven days' journey, but there was no water for the army or for the cattle that followed them.rÊq_xJehoram said, "Which way shall we go up?" Jehoshaphat answered, "The way through the Wilderness of Edom."‚	Êp„
xThen he went and sent <i>word</i> to Jehoshaphat king of Judah, saying, "The king of Moab has rebelled against me. Will you go with me to fight against Moab?" And he replied, "I will go; I am as you are, my people as your people, my horses as your horses."iÊoMxSo King Jehoram left Samaria at that time and assembled all <n>[the fighting men of]</n> Israel.RÊnxBut when Ahab died, the king of Moab rebelled against the king of Israel.4Êm‚cx<pb/><f>[2]</f> Mesha the king of Moab was a sheep breeder, and he used to pay <n>[an annual tribute]</n> to the king of Israel 100,000 lambs and the wool of 100,000 rams.Êl‚!xNevertheless, he continued in the <n>[idolatrous]</n> sins of Jeroboam the son of Nebat, who made Israel sin; he did not depart from them.Êk‚5xHe did evil in the sight of the LORD, but not like his father and mother; for he put away the <i>sacred</i> pillar of Baal that his father had made. Êj	‚=x<pb/>Jehoram the son of Ahab became king over Israel in Samaria in the<f>[1]</f> eighteenth year of Jehoshaphat king of Judah, and reigned twelve years.WÊi)xElisha went from there to Mount Carmel, and from there he returned to Samaria.0Êh‚[xWhen he turned around and looked at them, he cursed them in the name of the LORD. Then two female bears came out of the woods and tore to pieces forty-two of the boys.EÊgƒx<pb/>Then Elisha went up from Jericho to<f>[5]</f> Bethel. On the way, young boys came out of the city and mocked him and said to him, "Go up, you<f>[6]</f> baldhead! Go up, you baldhead!"dÊfCxSo the waters have been purified to this day, in accordance with the word spoken by Elisha.mÊeƒUxThen Elisha went to the spring of water and threw the salt in it and said, "Thus says the LORD: 'I <n>[not the salt]</n> have purified <i>and</i> healed these waters; there shall no longer be death or barrenness because of it.'"`Êd;xHe said, "Bring me a new jar, and put<f>[4]</f> salt in it." So they brought it to him.8Êc‚kx<pb/>Then the men of the city said to Elisha, "Look, this city is in a pleasant place, as my lord <n>[Elisha]</n> sees; but the water is bad and the land<f>[3]</f> is barren."yÊbmxThey returned to Elisha while he was staying at Jericho; and he said to them, "Did I not tell you, 'Do not go'?"AÊa‚}xBut when they urged him until he was embarrassed <n>[to refuse them]</n>, he said, "Send <i>them</i>." So they sent fifty men, and they searched for three days but did not find Elijah.‚*Ê`„OxThen they said to Elisha, "Behold now, there are among your servants fifty strong men; please let them go and search for your master. It may be that the Spirit of the LORD has taken him up and cast him on some mountain or into some valley." And he said, "You shall not send <i>anyone</i>."hÊ_ƒKxWhen the sons of the prophets who were <n>[watching]</n> opposite at Jericho saw him, they said, "The spirit of Elijah rests on Elisha." And they came to meet him and bowed down to the ground before him <n>[in respect]</n>.`Ê^ƒ;xHe took the mantle of Elijah that fell from him and struck the waters and said, "Where is the LORD, the God of Elijah?" And when he too had struck the waters, they divided this way and that, and Elisha crossed over.pÊ][x
He picked up the mantle of Elijah that fell off him, and went back and stood by the bank of the Jordan.XÊ\ƒ+xElisha saw it and cried out, "My father, my father, the chariot of Israel and its horsemen!" And he no longer saw Elijah. Then he took hold of his own clothes and tore them into two pieces <n>[in grief]</n>.AÊ[‚}xAs they continued along and talked, behold, a chariot of fire with horses of fire <n>[appeared suddenly and]</n> separated the two of them, and Elijah went up to heaven in a whirlwind.)ÊZ‚Mx
He said, "You have asked for a difficult thing. <i>However</i>, if you see me when I am taken from you, it shall be so for you; but if not, it shall not be so."RÊYƒx	<pb/>And when they had crossed over, Elijah said to Elisha, "Ask what I shall do for you before I am taken from you." And Elisha said, "Please let a double<f>[2]</f> portion of your spirit be upon me."/ÊX‚YxAnd Elijah took his mantle (coat) and rolled it up and struck the waters, and they were divided this way and that, so that the two of them crossed over on dry ground.ÊW‚9x<pb/>Fifty men of the sons of the prophets also went and stood opposite them <n>[to watch]</n> at a distance; and the two of them stood by the Jordan.CÊVƒxElijah said to him, "Please stay here, for the LORD has sent me to the Jordan." But he said, "As the LORD lives and as your soul lives, I will not leave you." So the two of them went on.^ÊUƒ7xThe sons of the prophets who were at Jericho approached Elisha and said to him, "Do you know that the LORD will take your master away from you today?" And he answered, "Yes, I know it; be quiet <n>[about it]</n>."JÊTƒx<pb/>Elijah said to him, "Elisha, please stay here, for the LORD has sent me to Jericho." But he said, "As the LORD lives and as your soul lives, I will not leave you." So they came to Jericho.dÊSƒCxNow the<f>[1]</f> sons of the prophets who were at Bethel came out to Elisha and said to him, "Do you know that the LORD will take your master away from you today?" He said, "Yes, I know it; be quiet <n>[about it]</n>."NÊRƒxAnd Elijah said to Elisha, "Please stay here, for the LORD has sent me to Bethel." But Elisha replied, "As the LORD lives and as your soul lives, I will not leave you." So they went down to Bethel.~ÊQ	yx<pb/>When the LORD was about to take Elijah up to heaven by a whirlwind, Elijah and Elisha were traveling from Gilgal.ÊP	‚xNow the rest of the acts of Ahaziah which he did, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel?‚eÊO	…GxSo Ahaziah <n>[the son of King Ahab]</n> died in accordance with the word of the LORD which Elijah had spoken. And because he had no son, Jehoram <n>[his younger brother]</n> became king <n>[of Israel, the northern kingdom]</n> in his place in the<f>[6]</f> second year of Jehoram the son of Jehoshaphat, king of Judah <n>[the southern kingdom]</n>.‚ÊN	„'xThen Elijah said to Ahaziah, "Thus says the LORD: 'Since you have sent messengers to inquire of Baal-zebub, god of Ekronâ€”is it because there is no God in Israel to inquire of His word?â€”therefore you will not leave the bed on which you lie, but will certainly die.'"
ÊM	‚xThe angel of the LORD said to Elijah, "Go down with him; do not be afraid of him." So he stood and went down with him to the king.ÊL	‚3xBehold, fire came down from heaven and consumed the first two captains of fifty with their fifties; but now let my life be precious in your sight."‚\ÊK	…5x
<pb/>So Ahaziah again sent a captain of a third fifty with his fifty <n>[fighting men]</n>. And the third captain of fifty went up and came bowed down on his knees before Elijah, and begged him <n>[for compassion]</n> and said to him, "O man of God, please let my life and the lives of your servants, these fifty, be precious in your sight.
