Õ8	‚ ‚<pb/>Satan <n>[the adversary]</n> stood up against Israel and incited David to<f>[1]</f> count <n>[the population of]</n> Israel.{Õ7o ‚These were descended from the giants in Gath, and they fell by the hand of David and by the hand of his servants.ZÕ6- ‚When he taunted Israel, Jonathan the son of Shimea, David's brother, killed him.gÕ5ƒG ‚Again there was war at Gath, where there was a man of <i>great</i> stature who had twenty-four fingers and toes, six <i>fingers on each hand</i> and six <i>toes on each foot</i>; and he also was descended from the giants.3Õ4‚_ ‚There was war again with the Philistines, and Elhanan the son of Jair killed Lahmi the brother of Goliath the Gittite, the shaft of whose spear was like a weaver's beam.PÕ3ƒ ‚<pb/>Now it came about after this that war broke out at Gezer with the Philistines; then Sibbecai the Hushathite killed Sippai, one of the descendants of the<f>[4]</f> giants, and they were subdued.nÕ2ƒU ‚He brought out the people who were in it, and<f>[3]</f> put them <n>[to work]</n> with saws, iron picks, and axes. David dealt <i>in this way</i> with all the Ammonite cities. Then David and all the people returned to Jerusalem.‚Õ1„! ‚David took the crown of their king from his head and found that it<f>[2]</f> weighed a talent of gold and that there was a precious stone in it; so it was set on David's head. He also brought a very great amount of spoil (plunder) out of the city <n>[of Rabbah]</n>.‚=Õ0	„u ‚<pb/>Then it happened at the end of the year,<f>[1]</f> at the time when kings go out <i>to battle</i>, Joab led out the army and ravaged <i>and</i> devastated the land of the Ammonites, and came and besieged Rabbah. But David stayed at Jerusalem <n>[with Bathsheba]</n>. Joab struck Rabbah and overthrew it.fÕ/ƒE ‚When the servants of Hadadezer saw that they had been defeated by Israel, they made peace with David and became subject to <i>and</i> served him. And the Arameans (Syrians) were not willing to help the Ammonites anymore.0Õ.‚Y ‚But the Arameans fled before Israel, and David killed of the Arameans 7,000 charioteers and 40,000 foot soldiers, and put to death Shophach the commander of the army.dÕ-ƒA ‚When this was told to David, he gathered all Israel and crossed the Jordan, and came upon them and drew up in formation against them. So when David drew up in battle array against the Arameans, they fought against him.xÕ,ƒi ‚<pb/>When the Arameans (Syrians) saw that they had been defeated by Israel, they sent messengers and brought out the Arameans who were beyond the <n>[Euphrates]</n> River, with Shophach the commander of the army of Hadadezer leading them.*Õ+‚M ‚When the Ammonites saw that the Arameans fled, they also fled before Abishai, Joab's brother, and entered the city <n>[Medeba]</n>. Then Joab came to Jerusalem.pÕ*Y ‚So Joab and the people who were with him approached the Arameans for battle, and they fled before him."Õ)‚= ‚
Be strong and let us show ourselves courageous for the sake of our people and for the cities of our God; and may the LORD do what is good in His sight."Õ(‚ ‚He said, "If the Arameans are too strong for me, then you shall help me; but if the Ammonites are too strong for you, I will help you.yÕ'k ‚The rest of the soldiers he placed in the hand of Abishai his brother, and they lined up against the Ammonites.RÕ&ƒ ‚
Now when Joab saw that the battle was set against him in the front and in the rear, he chose <i>warriors</i> from all the choice men of Israel and put them in formation against the Arameans (Syrians).3Õ%‚_ ‚	The Ammonites came out and lined up in battle formation at the entrance of the city <n>[Medeba]</n>, while the kings who had come were by themselves in the open country.\Õ$1 ‚When David heard <i>about it</i>, he sent Joab and all the army of courageous men.JÕ#ƒ
 ‚So they hired for themselves 32,000 chariots and the king of Maacah and his troops, who came and camped before Medeba. And the Ammonites gathered together from their cities and came to battle.^Õ"ƒ5 ‚<pb/>When the Ammonites saw that they had made themselves hateful to David, Hanun and his people sent 1,000 talents of silver to hire for themselves chariots and horsemen from Mesopotamia, Aram-maacah, and Zobah.‚Õ!„ ‚When David was told how the men were treated, he sent <i>messengers</i> to meet them, for they were very humiliated <i>and</i> ashamed <n>[to return]</n>. So the king said, "Stay in Jericho until your beards grow <n>[back]</n>, and <i>then</i> return."SÕ ƒ ‚Therefore Hanun took David's servants, shaved them <n>[cutting off half their beards]</n>, and cut off their garments in the middle as far as their buttocks, and sent them away <n>[in humiliation]</n>.‚Õ„ ‚But the leaders of the Ammonites said to Hanun, "<f>[1]</f> Do you think that David has sent people to console <i>and</i> comfort you because he honors your father? Have his servants not come to you to search and to overthrow and to spy out the land?"‚Õ„ ‚David said, "I will be kind (gracious) to Hanun son of Nahash, because his father was kind to me." So David sent messengers to comfort him concerning <n>[the death of]</n> his father. And the servants of David came to the land of the Ammonites to comfort Hanun.yÕ	m ‚<pb/>Now it came about after this, that Nahash king of the Ammonites died, and his son became king in his place.:Õ‚m ‚and Benaiah the son of Jehoiada was in charge of the<f>[4]</f> Cherethites and the Pelethites <n>[David's bodyguards]</n>, and the sons of David were chiefs at the king's side.Õw ‚Zadok the son of Ahitub and Abimelech the son of Abiathar were the priests, and Shavsha was secretary (state scribe);qÕ[ ‚Joab the son of Zeruiah was in command of the army, and Jehoshaphat the son of Ahilud was the recorder;nÕU ‚<pb/>So David reigned over all Israel and administered justice and righteousness for all his people.Õ‚ ‚
He put military outposts in Edom, and all the Edomites became David's servants. Thus the LORD helped David wherever he went.{Õo ‚<pb/>Also Abishai the son of Zeruiah <n>[David's half sister]</n> defeated 18,000 Edomites in the Valley of Salt.gÕƒG ‚King David also dedicated these to the LORD <n>[setting them apart for sacred use]</n>, with the silver and the gold which he brought from all the nations: from Edom, Moab, the Ammonites, the Philistines, and from Amalek.‚Õ„ ‚
he sent Hadoram his son to King David to greet him and to bless (congratulate) him, because he had fought against Hadadezer and had defeated him; for Hadadezer had been at war with Tou. And <i>Hadoram brought</i> all kinds of articles of gold and silver and bronze.lÕQ ‚	<pb/>When Tou king of Hamath heard how David had defeated all the army of Hadadezer king of Zobah,TÕƒ! ‚Likewise from Tibhath and from Cun, cities of Hadadezer, David brought a very large amount of bronze, with which Solomon later made the bronze Sea (large basin), the pillars, and the utensils of bronze.wÕg ‚David took the shields of gold which were carried by the servants of Hadadezer and brought them to Jerusalem.<Õ‚q ‚Then David put <i>military outposts</i> among the Arameans in Damascus; and the Arameans became David's servants and brought tribute. Thus the LORD helped David wherever he went.Õ‚ ‚<pb/>When the Arameans (Syrians) of<f>[3]</f> Damascus came to help Hadadezer king of Zobah, David killed 22,000 of the Arameans.}Õƒs ‚David took from him 1,000 chariots, 7,000 horsemen, and 20,000 foot soldiers (infantrymen). David also<f>[2]</f> hamstrung <n>[nearly]</n> all the chariot horses <n>[to make them lame and useless]</n>, but left enough of them for 100 chariots.Õ‚- ‚<pb/>David also defeated Hadadezer king of Zobah <i>as far as</i> Hamath, as he went to establish his<f>[1]</f> dominion to the Euphrates River.aÕ
; ‚He defeated Moab, and the Moabites became David's servants and brought tribute (gifts).Õ	‚5 ‚<pb/>After this it came about that David defeated and subdued the Philistines, and he took Gath and its villages out of the hand of the Philistines.1Õ‚[ ‚Therefore may it please You to bless the house (descendants) of Your servant, that it may continue before You forever; for what You bless, O LORD, is blessed forever."pÕ
Y ‚And now, O LORD, You are God, and you have spoken <i>and</i> promised this good thing to Your servant.1Õ	‚[ ‚For You, O my God, have revealed to Your servant that You will build for him a house (descendants); therefore Your servant has found <i>courage</i> to pray before You.jÕƒM ‚Let Your name <n>[and the character that it denotes]</n> endure and be magnified forever, saying, 'The LORD of hosts is the God of Israel, yes, a God to Israel; and the house of Your servant David is established before You.'Õ‚) ‚<pb/>"Therefore now, O LORD, let the word which You have spoken concerning Your servant and his house endure forever, and do as You have said._Õ7 ‚You made Your people Israel Your own people forever, and You, LORD, became their God.‚Õ„! ‚And what one nation on the earth is like Your people Israel, whom God went to redeem for Himself as a people, to make a name for Yourself by great and awesome <i>and</i> terrible things, by driving out nations from before Your people, whom You redeemed out of Egypt?Õw ‚O LORD, there is no one like You, nor is there any God except You, according to all that we have heard with our ears.!Õ‚; ‚O LORD, for Your servant's sake, and in accordance with Your own heart, You have accomplished all this greatness, to make known all these great things.qÕ[ ‚What more can David say to You for the honor <i>granted to</i> Your servant? For You know Your servant.oÕƒW ‚This was a small thing in Your eyes, O God; but You have spoken of Your servant's house for a great while to come, and have regarded me according to the standard <i>and</i> estate of a man of high degree (prominence), O LORD God.(Õ ‚I ‚Then David the king went in and sat before the LORD and said, "Who am I, O LORD God, and what is my house <i>and</i> family that You have brought me this far?bÔ= ‚According to all these words and according to all this vision, so Nathan spoke to David.Ô~‚ ‚But I will settle<f>[3]</f> him in My house and in My kingdom forever, and his throne shall be established forevermore."'"2Ô}‚] ‚
I will be his father and he shall be My son; and I will not take My steadfast love <i>and</i> mercy away from him, as I took it from him (King Saul) who was before you.MÔ| ‚He shall build Me a house, and I will establish his throne forever.rÔ{ƒ] ‚And it shall come to pass that when your days are completed and you must go to be with your fathers <n>[in death]</n>, I will raise up <i>one of</i> your descendants after you, one of your own sons; and I will establish his kingdom.dÔzƒA ‚
<t>since the time that I commanded judges to be over My people Israel. And I will humble <i>and</i> subdue all your enemies. </t><br/>"Furthermore, I tell you that the LORD will build you a house (a blessed posterity).zÔyƒm ‚	I will appoint a place for My people Israel, and will plant them, so that they may live in their own place and not be moved again <n>[nor tremble with fear]</n>; and the<f>[2]</f> wicked will not waste (persecute) them anymore, as formerly,3Ôx‚_ ‚I have been with you wherever you have gone, and I have cut off all your enemies from before you; and I will make your name like the name of the great ones of the earth.CÔw‚ ‚Now, therefore, this is what you shall say to My servant David: 'Thus says the LORD of hosts, "I took you from the pasture, from following the sheep, to be leader over My people Israel.FÔvƒ ‚Wherever I have walked with all Israel, did I say a word to any of the judges of Israel, whom I commanded to shepherd My people, saying, 'Why have you not built for Me a house of cedar?'"'LÔuƒ ‚for I have not dwelt in a house since the day that I brought Israel up <n>[from Egypt]</n> until this day, but I have gone from tent to tent and from <i>one</i> dwelling place <i>to another</i>.nÔtU ‚"Go and tell David My servant, 'Thus says the LORD, "You shall not build a house for Me to dwell in;]Ôs3 ‚<pb/>But it came about that same night that the word of God came to Nathan, saying,dÔrA ‚Then Nathan said to David, "<f>[1]</f> Do all that is in your heart, for God is with you."9Ôq	‚m ‚<pb/>As David sat in his house (palace), he said to Nathan the prophet, "Behold, I live in a house of cedars, while the ark of the covenant of the LORD is under tent curtains."xÔpi ‚+<pb/>Then all the people departed, each to his house, and David returned <n>[home]</n> to bless his household.cÔoƒ? ‚*With them were Heman and Jeduthun <i>with</i> trumpets and cymbals for those who <i>were to</i> sound aloud, and instruments for <n>[accompanying]</n> the songs of God. And the sons of Jeduthun were to be at the gate.%Ôn‚C ‚)With them were Heman and Jeduthun, and the rest who were chosen and designated by name, to give thanks to the LORD, for His lovingkindness endures forever.IÔmƒ ‚(to offer burnt offerings to the LORD on the altar of burnt offering continually, morning and evening, in accordance with all that is written in the Law of the LORD, which He commanded Israel.$Ôl‚A ‚'<i>David left</i> Zadok the priest and his relatives the priests before the dwelling place (tabernacle) of the LORD in the high place which was at Gibeon,~Ôku ‚&and Obed-edom with his sixty-eight relatives; also Obed-edom the son of Jeduthun, and Hosah, were to be gatekeepers.%Ôj‚C ‚%So David left Asaph and his relatives there before the ark of the covenant of the LORD to minister before the ark continually, as each day's work required;
Ôi‚ ‚$<t>Blessed be the LORD, the God of Israel, </t><t>Forever and ever. </t>And all the people said, "Amen," and praised the LORD.<br/>OÔhƒ ‚#<t>Then say, "Save us, O God of our salvation; </t><t>Gather us together and rescue us from the nations, </t><t>That we may give thanks to Your holy name, </t><t>And glory in Your praise."</t><br/>vÔge ‚"<t>O give thanks to the LORD, for <i>He is</i> good; </t><t>For His lovingkindness endures forever.</t><br/>Ôf‚ ‚!<t>Then the trees of the forest will sing for joy before the LORD; </t><t>For He comes to judge <i>and</i> govern the earth.</t><br/>}Ôes ‚ <t>Let the sea roar, and all the things that fill it; </t><t>Let the field rejoice, and all that is in it.</t><br/>Ôd‚ ‚<t>Let the heavens be glad, and let the earth rejoice; </t><t>And let them say among the nations, "The LORD reigns."</t><br/>
Ôc‚
 ‚<t>Tremble <n>[reverently]</n> before Him, all the earth; </t><t>The world is firmly established, it will not be moved.</t><br/>OÔbƒ ‚<t>Ascribe to the LORD the glory <i>and</i> honor due His name; </t><t>Bring an offering <n>[of thanksgiving]</n>, and come before Him; </t><t>Worship the LORD in the splendor of holiness.</t><br/>Ôa‚ ‚<t>Ascribe to the LORD, O families of the peoples, </t><t>Ascribe to the LORD glory <i>and</i> honor and strength.</t><br/>Ô`‚ ‚<t>Splendor and majesty are <n>[found]</n> in His presence; </t><t>Strength and joy are <n>[found]</n> in His place (sanctuary).</t><br/>xÔ_i ‚<t>For all the gods of the peoples are <n>[lifeless]</n> idols, </t><t>But the LORD made the heavens.</t><br/>Ô^‚+ ‚<t>For great is the LORD, and greatly to be praised; </t><t>He is also to be feared <n>[with awe-filled reverence]</n> above all gods.</t><br/>hÔ]I ‚<t>Declare His glory among the nations, </t><t>His marvelous works among all peoples.</t><br/>vÔ\e ‚<t>Sing to the LORD, all the earth; </t><t>Proclaim the good news of His salvation from day to day.</t><br/>YÔ[+ ‚<t>"Do not touch My anointed ones, </t><t>And do My prophets no harm."</t><br/>ÔZ‚1 ‚<t>He allowed no man to oppress <i>or</i> exploit them, </t><t>And, He reproved <i>and</i> punished kings for their sakes, <i>saying</i>,</t><br/>uÔYc ‚<t>When they wandered from nation to nation, </t><t>And from <i>one</i> kingdom to another people,</t><br/>_ÔX7 ‚<t>When you were few in number, </t><t>Even a very few, and strangers in it,</t><br/>ÔW‚ ‚<t>Saying, "To you I will give the land of Canaan, </t><t>As the portion of your possession <i>and</i> inheritance."</t><br/>mÔVS ‚<t>He confirmed it as a statute to Jacob, </t><t>And to Israel as an everlasting covenant,</t><br/>sÔU_ ‚<t><i>The covenant</i> which He made with Abraham, </t><t>And His oath (sworn promise) to Isaac.</t><br/>ÔT‚ ‚<t>Be mindful of His covenant forever, </t><t>The promise which He commanded <i>and</i> established to a thousand generations,</t><br/>XÔS) ‚<t>He is the LORD our God; </t><t>His judgments are in all the earth.</t><br/>iÔRK ‚
<t>O seed of Israel His servant, </t><t><f>[2]</f> Children of Jacob, His chosen ones!</t><br/>ÔQ‚ ‚<t>Remember <n>[with gratitude]</n> His marvelous deeds which He has done, </t><t>His miracles and the judgments from His mouth,</t><br/>~ÔPu ‚<t>Seek the LORD and His strength; </t><t>Seek His face continually <n>[longing to be in His presence]</n>.</t><br/>gÔOG ‚
<t>Glory in His holy name; </t><t>Let the hearts of those who seek the LORD rejoice.</t><br/>XÔN) ‚	<t>Sing to Him, sing praises to Him; </t><t>Speak of all His wonders.</t><br/>qÔM[ ‚<t>O give thanks to the LORD, call on His name; </t><t>Make His deeds known among the peoples.</t><br/>ÔL‚# ‚<pb/><t>Then on that day David first entrusted to Asaph and his relatives to give thanks to the LORD <n>[as their chief task]</n>.</t><br/>vÔKe ‚and Benaiah and Jahaziel the priests <i>blew</i> trumpets continually before the ark of the covenant of God.uÔJƒc ‚Asaph the chief, and second to him Zechariah, <i>then</i> Jeiel (Jaaziel), Shemiramoth, Jehiel, Mattithiah, Eliab, Benaiah, Obed-edom, and Jeiel with musical instruments, harps and lyres; also Asaph <i>played</i> loud-sounding cymbals;8ÔI‚i ‚<pb/>He appointed some of the Levites to minister before the ark of the LORD and to profess <n>[God's name]</n> and to<f>[1]</f> thank and praise the LORD, the God of Israel:
ÔH‚ ‚He distributed to everyone in Israel, both man and woman, to everyone a loaf of bread, a portion <i>of meat</i>, and a raisin cake.ÔG‚ ‚When David had finished offering the burnt offerings and the peace offerings, he blessed the people in the name of the LORD.(ÔF	‚K ‚<pb/>So they brought the ark of God and set it inside the tent which David had pitched for it, and they offered burnt offerings and peace offerings before God.‚ÔE„1 ‚<pb/>It happened that as the ark of the covenant of the LORD came to the City of David, Michal <n>[David's wife]</n> the daughter of Saul, looking down through a window, saw King David leaping and dancing <n>[in celebration]</n>; and<f>[2]</f> she despised him in her heart.8ÔD‚i ‚Thus all Israel brought up the ark of the covenant of the LORD with shouting, and with the sound of the horn, with trumpets, with loud-sounding cymbals, with harps and lyres.fÔCƒE ‚David was clothed with a robe of fine linen, as were all the Levites who carried the ark, and the singers and Chenaniah, director of the music of the singers. David also wore an ephod (a priestly upper garment) of linen.1ÔB‚[ ‚Because God was helping the Levites who carried the ark of the covenant of the LORD <n>[to do it carefully and safely]</n>, they sacrificed seven bulls and seven rams..ÔA‚U ‚<pb/>So David, with the elders of Israel and the captains over thousands, went to bring up the ark of the covenant of the LORD from the house of Obed-edom with joy.FÔ@ƒ ‚Shebaniah, Joshaphat, Nethanel, Amasai, Zechariah, Benaiah, and Eliezer the priests blew the trumpets before the ark of God. Obed-edom and Jehiah (Jeiel) were also gatekeepers for the ark.<Ô?s ‚Berechiah and Elkanah were gatekeepers for the ark.Ô>‚ ‚Chenaniah, leader of the Levites, was <i>in charge of</i> the singing; he gave instruction in singing because he was skilled.(Ô=‚I ‚and Mattithiah, Eliphelehu, Mikneiah, Obed-edom, Jeiel, and Azaziah were to lead with lyres set to Sheminith <n>[that is, the eighth string, a low pitch]</n>.:Ô<‚m ‚and Zechariah, Aziel, Shemiramoth, Jehiel, Unni, Eliab, Maaseiah, and Benaiah <n>[were to play]</n> with<f>[1]</f> harps <i>tuned</i> to Alamoth <n>[that is, a high pitch]</n>;jÔ;M ‚So the singers, Heman, Asaph, and Ethan <i>were appointed</i> to sound aloud the bronze cymbals;VÔ:ƒ% ‚and with them their relatives of the second rank: Zechariah, Ben, Jaaziel, Shemiramoth, Jehiel, Unni, Eliab, Benaiah, Maaseiah, Mattithiah, Eliphelehu, and Mikneiah, Obed-edom, and Jeiel, the gatekeepers.5Ô9‚c ‚So the Levites appointed Heman the son of Joel, and from his relatives, Asaph the son of Berechiah; and from the sons of Merari their relatives, Ethan the son of Kushaiah,hÔ8ƒI ‚<pb/>Then David told the chiefs of the Levites to appoint their relatives as the singers, with instruments of musicâ€”harps, lyres, and cymbalsâ€”to play loudly <i>and</i> to raise sounds of joy <n>[with their voices]</n>.Ô7‚ ‚The Levites carried the ark of God on their shoulders with the poles, as Moses commanded in accordance with the word of the LORD.zÔ6m ‚So the priests and the Levites consecrated (dedicated) themselves to bring up the ark of the LORD God of Israel.GÔ5ƒ ‚
Because you did not <n>[carry it as God directed]</n> the first time, the LORD our God made an <n>[angry]</n> outburst against us, for we did not seek Him in accordance with the ordinance."‚Ô4„ ‚and he said to them, "You are the heads of the fathers' <i>households</i> of the Levites; consecrate yourselves, both you and your relatives, so that you may bring up the ark of the LORD God of Israel, to <i>the place</i> that I have prepared for it.Ô3‚ ‚<pb/>Then David called for Zadok and Abiathar the priests, and for the Levitesâ€”Uriel, Asaiah, Joel, Shemaiah, Eliel, and Amminadab,PÔ2 ‚
of the sons of Uzziel, Amminadab the chief, with 112 of his relatives.KÔ1 ‚	of the sons of Hebron, Eliel the chief, with 80 of his relatives;RÔ0 ‚of the sons of Elizaphan, Shemaiah the chief, with 200 of his relatives;LÔ/ ‚of the sons of Gershom, Joel the chief, with 130 of his relatives;MÔ. ‚of the sons of Merari, Asaiah the chief, with 220 of his relatives;LÔ- ‚of the sons of Kohath, Uriel the chief, with 120 of his relatives;DÔ, ‚David gathered together the sons of Aaron and the Levites: Ô+y ‚And David assembled all Israel at Jerusalem to bring up the ark of the LORD to the place which he had prepared for it."Ô*‚= ‚Then David said, "No one is to carry the ark of God except the Levites; for the LORD chose them to carry the ark of God and to minister to Him forever."Ô)	‚ ‚<pb/><i>David</i> built houses for himself in the City of David; and he prepared a place for the ark of God and pitched a tent for it.cÔ(? ‚Then David's fame spread into all the lands; and the LORD caused all nations to fear him.Ô' ‚So David did just as God had commanded him, and they struck down the army of the Philistines from Gibeon as far as Gezer.;Ô&‚o ‚It shall be when you hear the sound of marching in the tops of the balsam trees, then you shall go out to battle, for God has gone out before you to strike the Philistine army."!Ô%‚; ‚So David inquired again of God, and God said to him, "Do not go up after them; circle around behind them and come at them in front of the balsam trees.>Ô$w ‚
<pb/>The Philistines again made a raid in the valley.Ô#‚/ ‚The Philistines abandoned their gods (idols) there; so David gave a command and they were burned in a fire <n>[as the Law of Moses required]</n>.pÔ"ƒY ‚So Israel came up to Baal-perazim, and David defeated the Philistines there. Then David said, "God has broken through my enemies by my hand, like the breakthrough of waters." Therefore they named that place<f>[2]</f> Baal-perazim.5Ô!‚c ‚
So David inquired of God, "Shall I go up against the Philistines? And will You hand them over to me?" Then the LORD said to him, "Go up, and I will hand them over to you."PÔ  ‚	Now the Philistines had come and made a raid in the Valley of Rephaim.-Ô‚S ‚When the Philistines heard that David had been anointed king over all Israel, they all went up in search of David; and he heard about it and went out against them.+ÔQ ‚Elishama, Beeliada, and Eliphelet.Ô9 ‚Nogah, Nepheg, Japhia,!Ô= ‚Ibhar, Elishua, Elpelet, Ôy ‚Now these are the<f>[1]</f> names of the children born <n>[to him]</n> in Jerusalem: Shammua, Shobab, Nathan, Solomon,lÔQ ‚<pb/>Then David took more wives at Jerusalem, and he became the father of more sons and daughters.0Ô‚Y ‚And David understood that the LORD had established <i>and</i> confirmed him as king over Israel, for his kingdom was highly exalted for the sake of His people Israel.Ô	‚ ‚<pb/>Now Hiram king of Tyre sent messengers to David with cedar timbers, masons and carpenters, to build a house (palace) for him.1Ô‚[ ‚
