	nA¥ÅöƒˆÂ                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Bß)‚} –Blessed be the LORD, the God of our fathers <n>[said Ezra]</n>, who put <i>such a thing</i> as this in the king's heart, to adorn <i>and</i> glorify the house of the LORD in Jerusalem,wß(ƒg –Whoever does not observe <i>and</i> practice the law of your God and the law of the king, let judgment be executed upon him strictly <i>and</i> promptly, whether it be for death or banishment or confiscation of property or imprisonment."‚oß'…W –<pb/>"You, Ezra, are to appoint magistrates and judges in accordance with the wisdom <i>and</i> instruction of your God which is in your hand, so that they may judge all the people who are in <i>the province</i> west of the <n>[Euphrates]</n> River; <i>appoint</i> those who know the laws of your God; and you may teach anyone who does not know <i>them</i>.Kß&ƒ –We also inform you that it is not authorized to impose tax, tribute, or toll on any of the priests, Levites, singers, doorkeepers, temple servants or <i>other</i> servants of this house of God.\ß%ƒ1 –Whatever is commanded by the God of heaven, let it be done diligently <i>and</i> with enthusiasm for the house of the God of heaven, so that there will not be wrath against the kingdom of the king and his sons.ß$‚) –<i>even</i> up to 100 talents of silver, 100 kors (measures) of wheat, 100 baths of wine, 100 baths of <n>[olive]</n> oil, and salt as needed.‚)ß#„K –<pb/>"And I, Artaxerxes the king, issue a decree to all the treasurers <i>in the provinces</i><f>[1]</f> west of the <n>[Euphrates]</n> River, that whatever Ezra the priest, the scribe of the Law of the God of heaven, may require of you, it shall be done diligently <i>and</i> at onceâ€”ß"‚ –The rest of the things required for the house of your God, which you may have occasion to provide, provide it from the royal treasury.ß!‚- –As for the utensils which are given to you for the service of the house of your God, deliver <n>[those]</n> in full before the God of Jerusalem.!ß ‚; –And whatever seems good to you and to your brothers to do with the rest of the silver and the gold, you may do in accordance with the will of your God.Qßƒ –Therefore with this money, you shall diligently buy bulls, rams, and lambs, with their grain offerings and drink offerings, and offer them on the altar of the house of your God which is in Jerusalem.Aß‚{ –and all the silver and gold which you find in the whole province of Babylon, along with the freewill offering of the people and of the priests for the house of their God in Jerusalem.&ß‚E –and to bring <n>[with you]</n> the silver and gold which the king and his advisers have freely offered to the God of Israel, whose dwelling is in Jerusalem,!ß‚; –For you are sent by the king and his seven advisers to inquire about Judah and Jerusalem in accordance with the Law of your God, which is in your hand,&ß‚E –
I have issued a decree that all of the people of Israel and their priests and the Levites in my kingdom who are willing to go to Jerusalem, may go with you.ß‚ –"Artaxerxes, king of kings, to Ezra the priest, the scribe of the Law of the God of heaven, perfect <i>peace</i> (greetings). And nowHßƒ	 –Now this is a copy of the decree that King Artaxerxes gave to Ezra the priest, the scribe <n>[who was]</n> learned in the words of the commandments of the LORD and of His statutes to Israel:-ß‚S –
For Ezra had set his heart (resolved) to study <i>and</i> interpret the Law of the LORD, and to practice it and teach <i>His</i> statutes and ordinances in Israel.4ß‚a –	For on the first of the first month he started out from Babylon, and on the first of the fifth month he arrived in Jerusalem, because the good hand of his God was on him.Yß+ –<pb/>Ezra came to Jerusalem in the fifth month of the seventh year of the king.Xßƒ) –Some of the sons of Israel, with some of the priests and Levites, the singers and gatekeepers, and the temple servants also went up <n>[from Babylon]</n> to Jerusalem in the seventh year of King Artaxerxes.oßƒW –this Ezra went up from Babylon. He was a scribe skilled in the Law (the five books) of Moses, which the LORD God of Israel had given; and the king granted him everything that he asked, for the hand of the LORD his God was on him.kßO –the son of Abishua, the son of Phinehas, the son of Eleazar, the son of Aaron the chief priestâ€”@ß{ –the son of Zerahiah, the son of Uzzi, the son of Bukki,Fß –the son of Amariah, the son of Azariah, the son of Meraioth,Aß} –the son of Shallum, the son of Zadok, the son of Ahitub,;ß	‚q –<pb/>Now after this, in the reign of Artaxerxes I <n>[son of Ahasuerus (Xerxes)]</n> king of Persia, Ezra the son (descendant) of Seraiah, the son of Azariah, the son of Hilkiah,‚0ß„Y –They observed the Feast of Unleavened Bread for seven days with joy, for the LORD had caused them to rejoice and had turned the heart of<f>[4]</f> the king of Assyria toward them, so that he encouraged them <i>and</i> strengthened their hands in the work of the house of God, the God of Israel.‚"ß
„= –The <i>Passover</i> was eaten by the sons (descendants) of Israel (Jacob) who returned from exile and by all those who had separated themselves from the <n>[ceremonial]</n> uncleanness of the nations of the land to <i>join</i> them, <i>in order</i> to seek the LORD God of Israel.nßƒU –For the priests and the Levites had purified themselves together; all of them were <n>[ceremonially]</n> clean. So they slaughtered the Passover <i>lamb</i> for all the exiles, for their brothers the priests, and for themselves.ißK –The <n>[former]</n> exiles kept the Passover on the fourteenth <n>[day]</n> of the first month.(ß
‚I –Then they appointed the priests to their divisions and the Levites in their orders for the service of God at Jerusalem, as it is written in the book of Moses.?ß	‚w –They offered at the dedication of this house of God 100 bulls, 200 rams, 400 lambs, and, as a sin offering for all Israel, 12 male goats, according to the number of Israel's tribes.ß‚7 –<pb/>And all Israelâ€”the priests, the Levites, and the rest of the <n>[former]</n> exilesâ€”celebrated the dedication of this house of God with joy.ß} –This temple was finished on the third day of the month of Adar, in the<f>[3]</f> sixth year of the reign of King Darius.‚ß„! –And the Jewish elders built and prospered through the prophesying of Haggai the prophet and Zechariah the son of Iddo. They finished their building as commanded by the God of Israel and in accordance with the decree of Cyrus and Darius and Artaxerxes king of Persia.fßƒE –
Then Tattenai, governor of <i>the province</i> west of the <n>[Euphrates]</n> River, with Shethar-bozenai and their associates carried out the decree with due diligence, just as King Darius had sent <i>and</i> commanded.‚ ß„9 –May the God who has caused His<f>[2]</f> Name to dwell there overthrow any king or people who attempts to alter <i>this command</i>, so as to destroy this house of God in Jerusalem. I, Darius, have issued this decree; let it be carried out quickly <i>and</i> with due diligence.‚ß„ –I have also issued a decree that <i>if there is</i> any man who violates this edict, a beam of timber shall be pulled from his house <n>[and set up]</n>; then he shall be impaled on it, and his house shall be turned into a refuse heap for this <n>[violation]</n>.
