A  the sons of Parosh, 2,172;gáƒG  <t>the ones who came with<f>[2]</f> Zerubbabel, Jeshua,<f>[3]</f> Nehemiah, Azariah, Raamiah, Nahamani, Mordecai, Bilshan, Mispereth, Bigvai, Nehum, and Baanah. </t><br/>The men of the people of Israel<f>[4]</f> numbered:yáƒk  <pb/>These are the sons (descendants, people) of the province who came up from the captivity of the exiles whom Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon had deported <n>[to Babylon]</n>; they returned to Jerusalem and to Judah, each to his city,‚ á
ƒy  <pb/>Then my God put it into my heart to assemble the nobles, the officials, and the people to be registered by genealogy. Then I found the register of the genealogy of those who came <n>[from Babylon]</n> first, and I found the following record:~á	u  Now the city was spacious and large, but there were few people in it and the houses had not <n>[yet]</n> been built.‚<á„q  I said to them, "Do not let the gates of Jerusalem be opened until the<f>[1]</f> sun is hot; and while the watchmen are still standing <i>guard</i>, have them shut and bar the doors. Appoint guards from the residents of Jerusalem, each at his post <n>[on the wall]</n>, and each in front of his own house.">á‚u  I put my brother Hanani, with Hananiah the commander of the fortress, in charge of Jerusalem, for Hananiah was a more faithful and God-fearing man than many <n>[of the others]</n>.
á	‚  <pb/>Now when the wall had been rebuilt and I had set up the doors, and the gatekeepers, singers, and Levites had been appointed,á‚'  Also, they were speaking about Tobiah's good deeds in my presence, and reporting to him what I said. Then Tobiah sent letters to frighten me.Máƒ  For<f>[5]</f> many in Judah were bound by oath to him because he was the son-in-law of Shecaniah the son of Arah, and his son Jehohanan had married the daughter of Meshullam the son of Berechiah.á{  Moreover, in those days many letters went from the nobles of Judah to Tobiah, and Tobiah's <i>letters</i> came to them.eáƒC  When all our enemies heard <i>about it</i>, and all the <n>[Gentile]</n> nations around us saw it,<f>[4]</f> they lost their confidence; for they recognized that this work had been accomplished with the help of our God.rá]  So the wall was finished on the twenty-fifth <n>[day]</n> of <n>[the month]</n> Elul, in fifty-two days.Ná ƒ  Remember, O My God, Tobiah and Sanballat in regard to these actions of theirs, and also <n>[remember]</n> the prophetess Noadiah and the rest of the prophets who were <i>trying to</i> frighten me.Màƒ  
He was hired for this reason, that I would be frightened and do as he said and sin, so that they would have <n>[grounds to make]</n> a malicious report in order to censure <i>and</i> disgrace me.à~‚  Then I realized that God had not sent him, but he spoke this prophecy against me because Tobiah and Sanballat had hired him.6à}‚e  But I said, "Should a man like me flee <n>[in fear and hide]</n>? Should<f>[3]</f> someone like me enter the temple <n>[for sanctuary]</n> to save his life? I will not go."‚fà|…E  
<pb/>When I went into the house of Shemaiah the son of Delaiah, the son of Mehetabel, who was<f>[1]</f> confined at home, he said, "Let us meet <n>[and take refuge]</n> together in the house of God,<f>[2]</f> within the temple, and let us shut the doors of the temple, because they are coming to kill you, and they are coming to kill you at night."+à{‚O  	For they all wanted to frighten us, thinking, "They will become discouraged with the work and it will not be done." But now, <n>[O God,]</n> strengthen my hands.àz‚  I sent <i>a message</i> to him, saying, "Such things as you are saying have not been done; you are inventing them in your own mind."‚ày„  Also <n>[it is reported that]</n> you have appointed prophets to make a proclamation concerning you in Jerusalem, saying, 'There is a king in Judah!' And now these things will be reported to the <n>[Persian]</n> king. So come now, and let us consult together."‚àx„  In it was written, "It is reported among the <n>[neighboring]</n> nations, and Gashmu is saying that you and the Jews are planning to revolt, and that is the reason you are rebuilding the wall. And according to these reports, you are to be their king.pàwY  Then Sanballat sent his servant to me in the same way the fifth time, with an open letter in his hand.bàv=  They sent <i>word</i> to me four times in this way, and I answered them in the same way..àu‚U  So I sent messengers to them, saying, "I am doing a great work and cannot come down. Why should the work stop while I leave to come down to <n>[meet with]</n> you?" àt‚9  Sanballat and Geshem sent <i>word</i> to me, saying, "Come, let us meet together at Chephirim in the plain of Ono." But they were planning to harm me.]às	ƒ5  <pb/>Now when Sanballat, Tobiah, Geshem the Arab, and the rest of our enemies heard that I had rebuilt the wall, and that there was no breach left in it, although at that time I had not set up doors in the gates,_àr7  Remember me, O my God, for good, <i>according to</i> all I have done for this people.‚Iàq…  Now the following were prepared for each day: one ox and six choice sheep; also fowls (poultry) were prepared for me; and in intervals of ten days all sorts of wine <i>was provided</i> in abundance. Yet for all this, I did not demand the governor's food <i>allowance</i>, because the servitude was heavy on this people.àp‚#  Moreover, there were at my table a hundred and fifty Jews and officials, besides those who came to us from the nations that were around us.ào‚  I also applied myself to the work on this wall; we did not buy any land, and all my servants were gathered together there for the work.‚Ràn…  But the former governors who were <n>[in office]</n> before me put heavy burdens on the people and took food and wine from them in addition to forty shekels of silver <n>[as an excessive monthly salary]</n>; even their servants assumed authority over the people. But I did not do so because of the <n>[reverent]</n> fear of God.‚àm„  Moreover, from the day that I was appointed to be their governor in the land of Judah, from the twentieth year to the thirty-second year of King Artaxerxes, for twelve years, neither I nor my relatives have eaten the governor's food <i>allowance</i>.‚Zàl…-  
I also<f>[2]</f> shook out the<f>[3]</f> front of my garment and said, "So may God shake out every man from his house and from his possessions who does not keep this promise; like this may he be shaken out and emptied." And all the assembly said, "Amen!" And they praised the LORD. Then the people acted in accordance with this promise.]àkƒ3  Then they said, "We will give it back and not require anything from them. We will do exactly as you say." Then I called the priests and took an oath from them that they would act in accordance with this promise._àjƒ7  Please, give back to them this very day their fields, their vineyards, their olive groves, and their houses, and also a hundredth part of the money, the grain, the new wine, and the oil that you are lending them."
ài‚  
And likewise I, my brothers, and my servants are lending them money and grain. Please, let us stop <n>[charging]</n> this interest.$àh‚A  	So I said, "What you are doing is not good. Should you not walk in the fear of our God to prevent the taunting by the <n>[pagan]</n> nations, our enemies?‚.àg„U  I said to them, "According to our ability we have redeemed (purchased back) our Jewish brothers who were sold to the <n>[Gentile]</n> nations; now would you even sell your brothers, that they might be sold to us?" Then they were silent and could not find a <n>[single]</n> word <i>to say</i>.Wàfƒ'  I thought it over and then challenged the nobles and the rulers. I said to them, "You are exacting usury (excessive interest) from your own brother (relative)." So I held a great assembly to confront them.fàeE  <pb/>Then I was very angry when I heard their outcry and these words <n>[of accusation]</n>.‚qàd…[  Now our flesh (skin) is the same as that of our brothers (relatives), and our children are like their children, yet here we are forcing (selling) our sons and our daughters to be slaves; and some of our daughters are forced into bondage <i>already</i>, and<f>[1]</f> we are powerless <n>[to redeem them]</n> because our fields and vineyards belong to others." àc‚9  There were also <i>others</i> who were saying, "We have borrowed money on our fields and vineyards for the <n>[Persian]</n> king's <n>[heavy]</n> tax.
