<t>Because it did not shut the doors of my <i>mother's</i> womb, </t><t>Nor hide trouble from my eyes.</t>;är‚o Ü	<t>"Let the stars of its early dawn be dark; </t><t>Let <i>the morning</i> wait in vain for the light, </t><t>Let it not see the eyelids of morning (the day's dawning),</t><br/>väqe Ü<t>"Let those curse it<f>[2]</f> who curse the day, </t><t>Who are skilled in rousing up Leviathan.</t><br/>päpY Ü<t>"Behold, let that night be barren <n>[and empty]</n>; </t><t>Let no joyful voice enter it.</t><br/>0äo‚Y Ü<t>"As for that night, let darkness seize it; </t><t>Let it not rejoice among the days of the year; </t><t>Let it not be counted in the number of the months.</t><br/>3än‚_ Ü<t>"Let darkness and gloom claim it for their own; </t><t>Let a cloud settle upon it; </t><t>Let all that blackens the day terrify it (the day that I was born).</t><br/>väme Ü<t>"May that day be darkness; </t><t>Let God above not care about it, </t><t>Nor light shine on it.</t><br/>äl‚ Ü<pb/><t>"Let the day on which I was born perish, </t><t>And the night which announced: 'There is a man-child conceived.'</t><br/>(äkK Ü<t><f>[1]</f> And Job said,</t>Väj	' Ü<pb/>After this, Job opened his mouth and cursed the day of his <i>birth</i>.äi‚1 Ü
