<t>From the fruit of his mouth a <n>[wise]</n> man enjoys good, </t><t>But the desire of the treacherous is for violence.</t><br/>h‚m	ƒK ð
<pb/><t>A wise son heeds <i>and</i> accepts <n>[and is the result of]</n> his father's discipline <i>and</i> instruction, </t><t>But a scoffer does not listen to reprimand <i>and</i> does not learn from his errors.</t><br/>
‚l‚
 ð<t>In the way of righteousness is life, </t><t>And in its pathway there is no death <n>[but immortalityâ€”eternal life]</n>.</t>Q‚kƒ ð<t>The lazy man does not catch <i>and</i> roast his prey, </t><t>But the precious possession of a <n>[wise]</n> man is diligence <n>[because he recognizes opportunities and seizes them]</n>.</t><br/>w‚jg ð<t>The righteous man is a guide to his neighbor, </t><t>But the way of the wicked leads them astray.</t><br/>r‚i] ð<t>Anxiety in a man's heart weighs it down, </t><t>But a good (encouraging) word makes it glad.</t><br/>‚hw ð<t>The hand of the diligent will rule, </t><t>But the negligent <i>and</i> lazy will be put to forced labor.</t><br/>5‚g‚c ð<t>A shrewd man is reluctant to display his knowledge <n>[until the proper time]</n>, </t><t>But the heart of <n>[over-confident]</n> fools proclaims foolishness.</t><br/>|‚fq ð<t>Lying lips are extremely disgusting to the LORD, </t><t>But those who deal faithfully are His delight.</t><br/>c‚e? ð<t>No harm befalls the righteous, </t><t>But the wicked are filled with trouble.</t><br/>o‚dW ð<t>Deceit is in the heart of those who devise evil, </t><t>But counselors of peace have joy.</t><br/>‚c} ð<t>Truthful lips will be established forever, </t><t>But a lying tongue is <n>[credited]</n> only for a moment.</t><br/>‚b ð<t>There is one who speaks rashly like the thrusts of a sword, </t><t>But the tongue of the wise brings healing.</t><br/>‚a‚# ð<t>He who speaks truth <n>[when he testifies]</n> tells what is right, </t><t>But a false witness utters deceit <n>[in court]</n>.</t><br/>.‚`‚U ð<t>The <n>[arrogant]</n> fool's anger is quickly known <n>[because he lacks self-control and common sense]</n>, </t><t>But a prudent man ignores an insult.</t><br/>>‚_‚u ð<t>The way of the <n>[arrogant]</n> fool <n>[who rejects God's wisdom]</n> is right in his own eyes, </t><t>But a wise <i>and</i> prudent man is he who listens to counsel.</t><br/>$‚^‚A ð<t>A man will be satisfied with good from the fruit of his words, </t><t>And the deeds of a man's hands will return to him <n>[as a harvest]</n>.</t><br/>‚]‚' ð
<t>An evil man is <n>[dangerously]</n> ensnared by the transgression of his lips, </t><t>But the righteous will escape from trouble.</t><br/>‚\{ ð<t>The wicked desire the plunder of evil men, </t><t>But the root of the righteous yields <i>richer</i> fruit.</t><br/>%‚[‚C ð<t>He who tills his land will have plenty of bread, </t><t>But he who follows worthless <i>things</i> lacks common sense <i>and</i> good judgment.</t><br/>‚Z‚	 ð
<t>A righteous man has kind regard for the life of his animal, </t><t>But even the compassion of the wicked is cruel.</t><br/>H‚Yƒ	 ð	<t><f>[1]</f> Better is he who is lightly esteemed and has a servant, </t><t>Than he who <n>[boastfully]</n> honors himself <n>[pretending to be what he is not]</n> and lacks bread.</t><br/> ‚X‚9 ð<t>A man will be commended according to his insight <i>and</i> sound judgment, </t><t>But the one who is of a perverse mind will be despised.</t><br/>1‚W‚[ ð<t>The wicked are overthrown <n>[by their evil]</n> and are no more, </t><t>But the house of the <n>[consistently]</n> righteous will stand <n>[securely]</n>.</t><br/>G‚Vƒ ð<t>The <n>[malevolent]</n> words of the wicked lie in wait for <n>[innocent]</n> blood <n>[to slander]</n>, </t><t>But the mouth of the upright will rescue <i>and</i> protect them.</t><br/>B‚U‚} ð<t>The thoughts <i>and</i> purposes of the <n>[consistently]</n> righteous are just (honest, reliable), </t><t>But the counsels <i>and</i> schemes of the wicked are deceitful.</t><br/>M‚Tƒ ð<t>A virtuous <i>and</i> excellent wife <n>[worthy of honor]</n> is the crown of her husband, </t><t>But she who shames him <n>[with her foolishness]</n> is like rottenness in his bones.</t><br/>‚S‚ ð<t>A man will not be established by wickedness, </t><t>But the root of the <n>[consistently]</n> righteous will not be moved.</t><br/>t‚Ra ð<t>A good man will obtain favor from the LORD, </t><t>But He will condemn a man who devises evil.</t><br/>‚Q	‚1 ð<pb/><t>Whoever loves instruction <i>and</i> discipline loves knowledge, </t><t>But he who hates reproof <i>and</i> correction is stupid.</t><br/>?‚P‚w ð<t>If the righteous will be rewarded on the earth <n>[with godly blessings]</n>, </t><t>How much more <n>[will]</n> the wicked and the sinner <n>[be repaid with punishment]</n>!</t>C‚O‚ ð<t>The fruit of the <n>[consistently]</n> righteous is a tree of life, </t><t>And he who is wise captures <i>and</i> wins souls <n>[for Godâ€”he gathers them for eternity]</n>.</t><br/>‚N‚7 ð<t>He who troubles (mismanages) his own house will inherit the wind (nothing), </t><t>And the foolish will be a servant to the wise-hearted.</t><br/>R‚Mƒ ð<t>He who leans on <i>and</i> trusts in <i>and</i> is confident in his riches will fall, </t><t>But the righteous <n>[who trust in God's provision]</n> will flourish like a <i>green</i> leaf.</t><br/>‚L‚ ð<t>He who diligently seeks good seeks favor <i>and</i> grace, </t><t>But he who seeks evil, evil will come to him.</t><br/>8‚K‚i ð<t>The people curse him who holds back grain <n>[when the public needs it]</n>, </t><t>But a blessing <n>[from God and man]</n> is upon the head of him who sells it.</t><br/>R‚Jƒ ð<t>The generous man <n>[is a source of blessing and]</n> shall be prosperous <i>and</i> enriched, </t><t>And he who waters will himself be watered <n>[reaping the generosity he has sown]</n>.</t><br/>m‚IƒS ð<t>There is the one who <n>[generously]</n> scatters <n>[abroad]</n>, and yet increases all the more; </t><t>And there is the one who withholds what is justly due, <i>but it results</i> only in want <i>and</i> poverty.</t><br/>z‚Hm ð<t>The desire of the righteous brings only good, </t><t>But the expectation of the wicked brings wrath.</t><br/>%‚G‚C ð<t>As a ring of gold in a swine's snout, </t><t>So is a beautiful woman who is without discretion <n>[her lack of character mocks her beauty]</n>.</t><br/> ‚Fy ð<t>Assuredly, the evil man will not go unpunished, </t><t>But the descendants of the righteous will be freed.</t><br/>=‚E‚s ð<t>The perverse in heart are repulsive <i>and</i> shamefully vile to the LORD, </t><t>But those who are blameless <i>and</i> above reproach in their walk are His delight!</t><br/>‚D‚ ð<t>He who is steadfast in righteousness <i>attains</i> life, </t><t>But he who pursues evil <i>attains</i> his own death.</t><br/>Q‚Cƒ ð<t>The wicked man earns deceptive wages, </t><t>But he who sows righteousness <i>and</i> lives his life with integrity will have a true reward <n>[that is both permanent and satisfying]</n>.</t><br/>;‚B‚o ð<t>The merciful <i>and</i> generous man benefits his soul <n>[for his behavior returns to bless him]</n>, </t><t>But the cruel <i>and</i> callous man does himself harm.</t><br/>‚A‚ ð<t>A gracious <i>and</i> good woman attains honor, </t><t>And ruthless men attain riches <n>[but not respect]</n>.</t><br/>g‚@ƒG ð<t>He who puts up security <i>and</i> guarantees a debt for an outsider will surely suffer <n>[for his foolishness]</n>, </t><t>But he who hates (declines) being a guarantor is secure <n>[from its penalties]</n>.</t><br/>g‚?ƒG ð<t>Where there is no <n>[wise, intelligent]</n> guidance, the people fall <n>[and go off course like a ship without a helm]</n>, </t><t>But in the abundance of <n>[wise and godly]</n> counselors there is victory.</t><br/>‚>‚ ð
<t>He who goes about as a gossip reveals secrets, </t><t>But he who is trustworthy <i>and</i> faithful keeps a matter hidden.</t><br/>o‚=W ð<t>He who despises his neighbor lacks sense, </t><t>But a man of understanding keeps silent.</t><br/>‚<‚- ð<t>By the blessing <n>[of the influence]</n> of the upright the city is exalted, </t><t>But by the mouth of the wicked it is torn down.</t><br/>	‚;‚ ð
<t>When it goes well for the righteous, the city rejoices, </t><t>And when the wicked perish, there are shouts of joy.</t><br/>‚:‚5 ð	<t>With his mouth the godless man destroys his neighbor, </t><t>But through knowledge <i>and</i> discernment the righteous will be rescued.</t><br/>n‚9U ð<t><f>[1]</f> The righteous is rescued from trouble, </t><t>And the wicked takes his place.</t><br/>	‚8‚ ð<t>When the wicked man dies, his expectation will perish; </t><t>And the hope of <n>[godless]</n> strong men perishes.</t><br/>‚7‚ ð<t>The righteousness of the upright will rescue them, </t><t>But the treacherous will be caught by their own greed.</t><br/>"‚6‚= ð<t>The righteousness of the blameless will smooth their way <i>and</i> keep it straight, </t><t>But the wicked will fall by his own wickedness.</t><br/>‚5‚ ð<t>Riches will not provide security in the day of wrath <i>and</i> judgment, </t><t>But righteousness rescues from death.</t><br/>‚4‚3 ð<t>The integrity <i>and</i> moral courage of the upright will guide them, </t><t>But the crookedness of the treacherous will destroy them.</t><br/>‚:‚3„m ð<t>When pride comes <n>[boiling up with an arrogant attitude of self-importance]</n>, then come dishonor <i>and</i> shame, </t><t>But with the humble <n>[the teachable who have been chiseled by trial and who have learned to walk humbly with God]</n> there is wisdom <i>and</i> soundness of mind.</t><br/>
