<t>Drive out the scoffer, and contention will go away; </t><t>Even strife and dishonor will cease.</t><br/>k…O ð	<t>He who is generous will be blessed, </t><t>For he gives some of his food to the poor.</t><br/>'… ‚G ð<t>He who sows injustice will reap <n>[a harvest of]</n> trouble, </t><t>And the rod of his wrath <n>[with which he oppresses others]</n> will fail.</t><br/>e„C ð<t>The rich rules over the poor, </t><t>And the borrower is servant to the lender.</t><br/>H„~ƒ	 ð<t>Train up a child in the way he should go <n>[teaching him to seek God's wisdom and will for his abilities and talents]</n>, </t><t>Even when he is old he will not depart from it.</t><br/>‚„}„ ð<t>Thorns and snares are in the way of the obstinate <n>[for their lack of honor and their wrong-doing traps them]</n>; </t><t>He who guards himself <n>[with godly wisdom]</n> will be far from them <i>and</i> avoid the consequences they suffer.</t><br/>D„|ƒ ð<t>The reward of humility <n>[that is, having a realistic view of one's importance]</n> and the <n>[reverent, worshipful]</n> fear of the LORD </t><t>Is riches, honor, and life.</t><br/>g„{ƒG ð<t>A prudent <i>and</i> far-sighted <i>person</i> sees the evil <n>[of sin]</n> and hides himself <n>[from it]</n>, </t><t>But the naive continue on and are punished <n>[by suffering the consequences of sin]</n>.</t><br/>e„zC ð<t>The rich and poor have a common bond; </t><t>The LORD is the Maker of them all.</t><br/>]„y	ƒ5 ð<pb/><t>A <i>good</i> name <n>[earned by honorable behavior, godly wisdom, moral courage, and personal integrity]</n> is more desirable than great riches; </t><t>And favor is better than silver and gold.</t><br/>{„xo ð<t>The horse is prepared for the day of battle, </t><t>But deliverance <i>and</i> victory belong to the LORD.</t>„w‚ ð<t>There is no <n>[human]</n> wisdom or understanding </t><t>Or counsel <n>[that can prevail]</n> against the LORD.</t><br/>7„v‚g ð<t>A wicked man puts on a bold face, </t><t>But as for the upright, he considers, directs, <i>and</i> establishes his way <n>[with the confidence of integrity]</n>.</t><br/>„u‚ ð<t>A false witness will perish, </t><t>But a man who listens <i>to the truth</i> will speak forever <i>and</i> go unchallenged.</t><br/>T„tƒ! ð<t>The sacrifice of the wicked is detestable <i>and</i> offensive <n>[to the LORD]</n>. </t><t>How much more <n>[unacceptable and insulting can it be]</n> when he brings it with evil intention?</t><br/>)„s‚K ð<t>He craves all the day long <n>[and does no work]</n>, </t><t>But the righteous <n>[willingly]</n> gives and does not withhold <n>[what he has]</n>.</t><br/>]„r3 ð<t>The desire of the lazy kills him, </t><t>For his hands refuse to labor;</t><br/>~„qu ð<t>"Proud," "Haughty," "Scoffer," are his names </t><t>Who acts with overbearing <i>and</i> insolent pride.</t><br/>a„p; ð<t>He who guards his mouth and his tongue </t><t>Guards himself from troubles.</t><br/>„o‚	 ð<t>A wise man scales the city <n>[walls]</n> of the mighty </t><t>And brings down the stronghold in which they trust.</t><br/>s„n_ ð<t>He who earnestly seeks righteousness and loyalty </t><t>Finds life, righteousness, and honor.</t><br/>M„mƒ ð<t>There is precious treasure and oil in the house of the wise <n>[who prepare for the future]</n>, </t><t>But a short-sighted <i>and</i> foolish man swallows it up <i>and</i> wastes it.</t><br/>r„l] ð<t>It is better to dwell in a desert land </t><t>Than with a contentious and troublesome woman.</t><br/>'„k‚G ð<t>The wicked become a ransom for the righteous, </t><t>And the treacherous in the place of the upright <n>[for they fall into their own traps]</n>.</t><br/>I„jƒ ð<t>He who loves <n>[only selfish]</n> pleasure <i>will become</i> a poor man; </t><t>He who loves <i>and</i> is devoted to<f>[1]</f> wine and <n>[olive]</n> oil will not become rich.</t><br/>„i‚ ð<t>A man who wanders from the way of understanding (godly wisdom) </t><t>Will remain in the assembly of the dead.</t><br/>'„h‚G ð<t>When justice is done, it is a joy to the righteous (the upright, the one in right standing with God), </t><t>But to the evildoers it is disaster.</t><br/>u„gc ð<t>A gift in secret subdues anger, </t><t>And a bribe <n>[hidden]</n> in the pocket, strong wrath.</t><br/>t„fa ð
<t>Whoever shuts his ears at the cry of the poor </t><t>Will cry out himself and not be answered.</t><br/>„ew ð<t>The righteous one keeps an eye on the house of the wickedâ€” </t><t>How the wicked are cast down to ruin.</t><br/>G„dƒ ð<t>When the scoffer is punished, the naive <n>[observes the lesson and]</n> becomes wise; </t><t>But when the wise <i>and</i> teachable person is instructed, he receives knowledge.</t><br/>„c‚! ð
<t>The soul of the wicked desires evil <n>[like an addictive substance]</n>; </t><t>His neighbor finds no compassion in his eyes.</t><br/>>„b‚u ð	<t>It is better to live in a corner of the housetop <n>[on the flat roof, exposed to the weather]</n> </t><t>Than in a house shared with a quarrelsome (contentious) woman.</t><br/> „ay ð<t>The way of the guilty is <n>[exceedingly]</n> crooked, </t><t>But as for the pure, his conduct is upright.</t><br/>4„`‚a ð<t>The violence of the wicked will <n>[return to them and]</n> drag them away <n>[like fish caught in a net]</n>, </t><t>Because they refuse to act with justice.</t><br/> „_y ð<t>Acquiring treasures by a lying tongue </t><t>Is a fleeting vapor, the seeking <i>and</i> pursuit of death.</t><br/>„^‚3 ð<t>The plans of the diligent lead surely to abundance <i>and</i> advantage, </t><t>But everyone who acts in haste comes surely to poverty.</t><br/>/„]‚W ð<t>Haughty <i>and</i> arrogant eyes and a proud heart, </t><t>The lamp of the wicked <n>[their self-centered pride]</n>, is sin <n>[in the eyes of God]</n>.</t><br/>„\‚! ð<t>To do righteousness and justice </t><t>Is more acceptable to the LORD than sacrifice <n>[for wrongs repeatedly committed]</n>.</t><br/>„[‚7 ð<t>Every man's way is right in his own eyes, </t><t>But the LORD weighs <i>and</i> examines the hearts <n>[of people and their motives]</n>.</t><br/>	„Z	‚
 ð<pb/><t>The king's heart is like channels of water in the hand of the LORD; </t><t>He turns it whichever way He wishes.</t><br/>e„YC ð<t>Blows that wound cleanse away evil, </t><t>And strokes reach to the innermost parts.</t>2„X‚] ð<t>The glory of young men is their <n>[physical]</n> strength, </t><t>And the honor of aged men is their gray head <n>[representing wisdom and experience]</n>.</t><br/>„W‚! ð<t>Loyalty <i>and</i> mercy, truth <i>and</i> faithfulness, protect the king, </t><t>And he upholds his throne by lovingkindness.</t><br/>#„V‚? ð<t>The<f>[2]</f> spirit (conscience) of man is the lamp of the LORD, </t><t>Searching <i>and</i> examining all the innermost parts of his being.</t><br/>;„U‚o ð<t>A wise king sifts out the wicked <n>[from among the good]</n> </t><t>And drives the <n>[threshing]</n> wheel over them <n>[to separate the chaff from the grain]</n>.</t><br/>R„Tƒ ð<t>It is a trap for a man to <n>[<f>[1]</f> speak a vow of consecration and]</n> say rashly, "It is holy!" </t><t>And <n>[not until]</n> afterward consider <n>[whether he can fulfill it]</n>.</t><br/>„S‚ ð<t>Man's steps are ordered <i>and</i> ordained by the LORD. </t><t>How then can a man <n>[fully]</n> understand his way?</t><br/>„R ð<t>Differing weights are detestable <i>and</i> offensive to the LORD, </t><t>And fraudulent scales are not good.</t><br/>
„Q‚ ð<t>Do not say, "I will repay evil"; </t><t>Wait <n>[expectantly]</n> for the LORD, and He will rescue <i>and</i> save you.</t><br/>„P‚ ð<t>An inheritance hastily gained <n>[by greedy, unjust means]</n> at the beginning </t><t>Will not be blessed in the end.</t><br/>	„O‚ ð<t>Whoever curses his father or his mother, </t><t>His lamp <n>[of life]</n> will be extinguished in time of darkness.</t><br/>„N‚/ ð<t>He who goes about as a gossip reveals secrets; </t><t>Therefore do not associate with a gossip <n>[who talks freely or flatters]</n>.</t><br/>l„MQ ð<t>Plans are established by counsel; </t><t>So make war <n>[only]</n> with wise guidance.</t><br/>N„Lƒ ð<t>Food gained by deceit is sweet to a man, </t><t>But afterward his mouth will be filled with gravel <n>[just as sin may be sweet at first, but later its consequences bring despair]</n>.</t><br/>E„Kƒ ð<t><n>[The judge tells the creditor]</n>, "Take the clothes of one who is surety for a stranger; </t><t>And hold him in pledge <n>[when he guarantees a loan]</n> for foreigners."</t><br/>!„J‚; ð<t>There is gold, and an abundance of pearls, </t><t>But the lips of knowledge are a vessel of preciousness <n>[the most precious of all]</n>.</t><br/>Y„Iƒ+ ð<t>"It is <n>[almost]</n> worthless, it is <n>[almost]</n> worthless," says the buyer <n>[as he negotiates the price]</n>; </t><t>But when he goes his way, then he boasts <n>[about his bargain]</n>.</t><br/>6„H‚e ð
<t>Do not love <n>[excessive]</n> sleep, or you will become poor; </t><t>Open your eyes <n>[so that you can do your work]</n> and you will be satisfied with bread.</t><br/>t„Ga ð<t>The hearing ear and the seeing eye, </t><t>The <n>[omnipotent]</n> LORD has made both of them.</t><br/>}„Fs ð<t>Even a boy is known <i>and</i> distinguished by his acts, </t><t>Whether his conduct is pure and right.</t><br/>4„E‚a ð
<t>Differing weights <n>[one for buying and another for selling]</n> and differing measures, </t><t>Both of them are detestable <i>and</i> offensive to the LORD.</t><br/>\„D1 ð	<t>Who can say, "I have cleansed my heart, </t><t>I am pure from my sin?"</t><br/>1„C‚[ ð<t>A <n>[discerning]</n> king who sits on the throne of judgment </t><t>Sifts all evil <n>[like chaff]</n> with his eyes <n>[and cannot be easily fooled]</n>.</t><br/>~„Bƒu ð<t>The righteous man who walks in integrity <i>and</i> lives life in accord with his <n>[godly]</n> beliefsâ€” </t><t>How blessed <n>[happy and spiritually secure]</n> are his children after him <n>[who have his example to follow]</n>.</t><br/>„A‚ ð<t>Many a man proclaims his own loyalty <i>and</i> goodness, </t><t>But who can find a faithful <i>and</i> trustworthy man?</t><br/>„@‚' ð<t>A plan (motive, wise counsel) in the heart of a man is like water in a deep well, </t><t>But a man of understanding draws it out.</t><br/>3„?‚_ ð<t>The lazy man does not plow when the winter <n>[planting]</n> season arrives; </t><t>So he begs at the <n>[next]</n> harvest and has nothing <n>[to reap]</n>.</t><br/>b„>ƒ= ð<t>It is an honor for a man to keep away from strife <n>[by handling situations with thoughtful foresight]</n>, </t><t>But any fool will <n>[start a]</n> quarrel <n>[without regard for the consequences]</n>.</t><br/>„=‚ ð<t>The terror of a king is like the roaring of a lion; </t><t>Whoever provokes him to anger forfeits his own life.</t><br/> „<	{ ð<pb/><t>Wine is a mocker, strong drink a riotous brawler; </t><t>And whoever is intoxicated by it is not wise.</t><br/>v„;e ð<t>Judgments are prepared for scoffers, </t><t>And beatings for the backs of <n>[thickheaded]</n> fools.</t>„:w ð<t>A wicked <i>and</i> worthless witness mocks justice, </t><t>And the mouth of the wicked spreads iniquity.</t><br/>„9‚ ð<t>Cease listening, my son, to instruction <i>and</i> discipline </t><t><i>And you will</i> stray from the words of knowledge.</t><br/>z„8m ð<t>He who assaults his father and chases away his mother </t><t>Is a son who brings shame and disgrace.</t><br/>x„7ƒi ð<t>Strike a scoffer <n>[for refusing to learn]</n>, and the naive may <n>[be warned and]</n> become prudent; </t><t>Reprimand one who has understanding <i>and</i> a teachable spirit, and he will gain knowledge <i>and</i> insight.</t><br/>„6 ð<t>The lazy man buries his hand in the <n>[food]</n> dish, </t><t>But will not even bring it to his mouth again.</t><br/>{„5o ð<t>The fear of the LORD <i>leads</i> to life, </t><t>So that one may sleep satisfied, untouched by evil.</t><br/>'„4‚G ð<t>That which is desirable in a man is his loyalty <i>and</i> unfailing love, </t><t>But it is better to be a poor man than a <n>[wealthy]</n> liar.</t><br/>
„3‚
 ð<t>Many plans are in a man's mind, </t><t>But it is the <e>Lord's</e> purpose for him that will stand (be carried out).</t><br/>„2‚ ð<t>Listen to counsel, receive instruction, <i>and</i> accept correction, </t><t>That you may be wise in the time to come.</t><br/>‚„1„+ ð<t><i>A man of</i> great anger will bear the penalty <n>[for his quick temper and lack of self-control]</n>; </t><t>For if you rescue him <n>[and do not let him learn from the consequences of his action]</n>, you will only have to rescue him over and over again.</t><br/>L„0ƒ ð<t>Discipline <i>and</i> teach your son while there is hope, </t><t>And do not <n>[indulge your anger or resentment by imposing inappropriate punishment nor]</n> desire his destruction.</t><br/>„/‚# ð<t>He who is gracious <i>and</i> lends a hand to the poor lends to the LORD, </t><t>And the LORD will repay him for his good deed.</t><br/><„.‚q ð<t>He who keeps <i>and</i> obeys the commandment <n>[of the LORD]</n> keeps (guards) his own life, </t><t>But he who is careless of his ways <i>and</i> conduct will die.</t><br/>„-‚ ð<t>Laziness casts one into a deep sleep <n>[unmindful of lost opportunity]</n>, </t><t>And the idle person will suffer hunger.</t><br/>2„,‚] ð<t>House and wealth are the inheritance from fathers, </t><t>But a wise, understanding, <i>and</i> sensible wife is <n>[a gift and blessing]</n> from the LORD.</t><br/>5„+‚c ð
<t>A foolish (ungodly) son is destruction to his father, </t><t>And the contentions of a <n>[quarrelsome]</n> wife are like a constant dripping <n>[of water]</n>.</t><br/>&„*‚E ð<t>The king's wrath <i>terrifies</i> like the roaring of a lion, </t><t>But his favor is as <n>[refreshing and nourishing as]</n> dew on the grass.</t><br/>h„)ƒI ð<t>Good sense <i>and</i> discretion make a man slow to anger, </t><t>And it is his honor <i>and</i> glory to overlook a transgression <i>or</i> an offense <n>[without seeking revenge and harboring resentment]</n>.</t><br/>j„(M ð
<t>Luxury is not fitting for a fool; </t><t>Much less for a slave to rule over princes.</t><br/>j„'M ð	<t>A false witness will not go unpunished, </t><t>And he who breathes lies will perish.</t><br/>$„&‚A ð<t>He who gains wisdom <i>and</i> good sense loves (preserves) his own soul; </t><t>He who keeps understanding will find good <i>and</i> prosper.</t><br/>,„%‚Q ð<t>All the brothers of a poor man hate him; </t><t>How much more do his friends abandon him! </t><t>He pursues <i>them with</i> words, but they are gone.</t><br/>
„$‚
 ð<t>Many will seek the favor of a generous <i>and</i> noble man, </t><t>And everyone is a friend to him who gives gifts.</t><br/>r„#] ð<t>A false witness will not go unpunished, </t><t>And he who breathes out lies will not escape.</t><br/>d„"A ð<t>Wealth makes many friends, </t><t>But a poor man is separated from his friend.</t><br/>p„!ƒY ð<t>The foolishness of man undermines his way <n>[ruining whatever he undertakes]</n>; </t><t>Then his heart is resentful <i>and</i> rages against the LORD <n>[for, being a fool, he blames the LORD instead of himself]</n>.</t><br/>h„ ƒI ð<t>Also it is not good for a person to be without knowledge, </t><t>And he who hurries with his feet <n>[acting impulsively and proceeding without caution or analyzing the consequences]</n> sins (misses the mark).</t><br/>+„	‚Q ð<pb/><t>Better is a poor man who walks in his integrity </t><t>Than a <n>[rich]</n> man who is twisted in his speech and is a <n>[shortsighted]</n> fool.</t><br/>t„ƒa ð<t>The man of <i>too many</i> friends <n>[chosen indiscriminately]</n> will be broken in pieces <i>and</i> come to ruin, </t><t>But there is a <n>[true, loving]</n> friend who <n>[is reliable and]</n> sticks closer than a brother.</t>S„ ð<t>The poor man pleads, </t><t>But the rich man answers roughly.</t><br/>„‚5 ð<t>He who finds a <n>[<f>[2]</f> true and faithful]</n> wife finds a good thing </t><t>And obtains favor <i>and</i> approval from the LORD.</t><br/>;„‚o ð<t>Death and life are in the power of the tongue, </t><t>And those who love it <i>and</i> indulge it will eat its fruit <i>and</i> bear the consequences of their words.</t><br/>„‚ ð<t>A man's stomach will be satisfied with the fruit of his mouth; </t><t>He will be satisfied with the consequence of his words.</t><br/>/„‚W ð<t>A brother offended <i>is harder to win</i> over than a fortified city, </t><t>And contentions <n>[separating families]</n> are like the bars of a castle.</t><br/>u„c ð<t><f>[1]</f> To cast lots puts an end to quarrels </t><t>And decides between powerful contenders.</t><br/>|„q ð<t>The first one to plead his case seems right, </t><t><i>Until</i> another comes and cross-examines him.</t><br/> „y ð<t>A man's gift <n>[given in love or courtesy]</n> makes room for him </t><t>And brings him before great men.</t><br/>„‚ ð<t>The mind of the prudent <n>[always]</n> acquires knowledge, </t><t>And the ear of the wise <n>[always]</n> seeks knowledge.</t><br/>w„g ð<t>The spirit of a man sustains him in sickness, </t><t>But as for a broken spirit, who can bear it?</t><br/>o„W ð
<t>He who answers before he hears <n>[the facts]</n>â€” </t><t>It is folly and shame to him.</t><br/>„‚ ð<t>Before disaster the heart of a man is haughty <i>and</i> filled with self-importance, </t><t>But humility comes before honor.</t><br/>„‚3 ð<t>The rich man's wealth is his strong city, </t><t>And like a high wall <n>[of protection]</n> in his own imagination <i>and</i> conceit.</t><br/>„‚+ ð
<t>The name of the LORD is a strong tower; </t><t>The righteous runs to it and is safe <i>and</i> set on high <n>[far above evil]</n>.</t><br/>]„3 ð	<t>He who is careless in his work </t><t>Is a brother to him who destroys.</t><br/>T„ƒ! ð<t>The words of a whisperer (gossip) are like dainty morsels <n>[to be greedily eaten]</n>; </t><t>They go down into the innermost chambers of the body <n>[to be remembered and mused upon]</n>.</t><br/>`„
9 ð<t>A fool's mouth is his ruin, </t><t>And his lips are the snare of his soul.</t><br/>o„W ð<t>A fool's lips bring contention <i>and</i> strife, </t><t>And his mouth invites a beating.</t><br/>
„‚ ð<t>To show respect to the wicked person is not good, </t><t>Nor to push aside <i>and</i> deprive the righteous of justice.</t><br/>p„
ƒY ð<t>The words of a man's mouth are like deep waters <n>[copious and difficult to fathom]</n>; </t><t>The fountain of <n>[mature, godly]</n> wisdom is like a bubbling stream <n>[sparkling, fresh, pure, and life-giving]</n>.</t><br/>N„	ƒ ð<t>When the wicked man comes <n>[to the depth of evil]</n>, contempt <n>[of all that is pure and good]</n> also comes, </t><t>And with inner baseness (dishonor) comes outer shame (scorn).</t><br/>P„ƒ ð<t>A <n>[closed-minded]</n> fool does not delight in understanding, </t><t>But only in revealing his personal opinions <n>[unwittingly displaying his self-indulgence and his stupidity]</n>.</t><br/>"„	‚? ð<pb/><t>He who <n>[willfully]</n> separates himself <n>[from God and man]</n> seeks his own desire, </t><t>He quarrels against all sound wisdom.</t><br/>S„ƒ ð<t>Even a <n>[callous, arrogant]</n> fool, when he keeps silent, is considered wise; </t><t>When he closes his lips he is regarded as sensible (prudent, discreet) <i>and</i> a man of understanding.</t>@„‚y ð<t>He who has knowledge restrains <i>and</i> is careful with his words, </t><t>And a man of understanding <i>and</i> wisdom has a cool spirit (self-control, an even temper).</t><br/>v„e ð<t>It is also not good to fine the righteous, </t><t>Nor to strike the noble for their uprightness.</t><br/>„ ð<t>A foolish son is a grief <i>and</i> anguish to his father </t><t>And bitterness to her who gave birth to him.</t><br/>X„ƒ) ð<t><n>[Skillful and godly]</n> wisdom is in the presence of a person of understanding <n>[and he recognizes it]</n>, </t><t>But the eyes of a <n>[thickheaded]</n> fool are on the ends of the earth.</t><br/>z„m ð<t>A wicked man receives a bribe from the <n>[hidden]</n> pocket </t><t>To pervert the ways of justice.</t><br/>„ ‚ ð<t>A happy heart is good medicine <i>and</i> a joyful mind causes healing, </t><t>But a broken spirit dries up the bones.</t><br/>@ƒ‚y ð<t>He who becomes the parent of a fool <n>[who is spiritually blind]</n> does so to his sorrow, </t><t>And the father of a fool <n>[who is spiritually blind]</n> has no joy.</t><br/>~ƒ~u ð<t>He who has a crooked mind finds no good, </t><t>And he who is perverted in his language falls into evil.</t><br/>Aƒ}‚{ ð<t>He who loves transgression loves strife <i>and</i> is quarrelsome; </t><t>He who <n>[proudly]</n> raises his gate seeks destruction <n>[because of his arrogant pride]</n>.</t><br/>ƒ|‚5 ð<t>A man lacking common sense gives a pledge </t><t>And becomes guarantor <n>[for the debt of another]</n> in the presence of his neighbor.</t><br/>^ƒ{5 ð<t>A friend loves at all times, </t><t>And a brother is born for adversity.</t><br/>ƒz‚ ð<t>Why is there money in the hand of a fool to buy wisdom, </t><t>When he has no common sense <i>or</i> even a heart for it?</t><br/>{ƒyo ð<t>He who justifies the wicked, and he who condemns the righteous </t><t>Are both repulsive to the LORD.</t><br/>vƒxƒe ð<t>The beginning of strife is like letting out water <n>[as from a small break in a dam; first it trickles and then it gushes]</n>; </t><t>Therefore<f>[1]</f> abandon the quarrel before it breaks out <i>and</i> tempers explode.</t><br/>`ƒw9 ð
<t>Whoever returns evil for good, </t><t>Evil will not depart from his house.</t><br/>ƒv‚) ð<t>Let a man meet a <n>[ferocious]</n> bear robbed of her cubs </t><t>Rather than the <n>[angry, narcissistic]</n> fool in his folly.</t><br/>tƒua ð<t>A rebellious man seeks only evil; </t><t>Therefore a cruel messenger will be sent against him.</t><br/>ƒt‚! ð
<t>A reprimand goes deeper into one who has understanding <i>and</i> a teachable spirit </t><t>Than a hundred lashes into a fool.</t><br/>%ƒs‚C ð	<t>He who covers <i>and</i> forgives an offense seeks love, </t><t>But he who repeats <i>or</i> gossips about a matter separates intimate friends.</t><br/>ƒrw ð<t>A bribe is like a bright, precious stone in the eyes of its owner; </t><t>Wherever he turns, he prospers.</t><br/>ƒq‚+ ð<t>Excellent speech does not benefit a fool <n>[who is spiritually blind]</n>, </t><t>Much less do lying lips <i>benefit</i> a prince.</t><br/>
