 ð <pb/><t>If you have foolishly exalted yourself, </t><t>Or if you have plotted <i>evil, put your</i> hand on your mouth.</t><br/>{‡o ð<t>The<f>[5]</f> strutting rooster, the male goat also, </t><t>And the king <i>when his</i> army is with him.</t>f‡E ð<t>The lion, which is mighty among beasts </t><t>And does not turn back before any;</t><br/> ‡y ð<pb/><t>There are three things which are stately in step, </t><t>Even four which are stately in their stride:</t><br/>^‡ 5 ð<t>You may grasp the lizard with your hands, </t><t>Yet it is in kings' palaces.</t>X†) ð<t>The locusts have no king, </t><t>Yet all of them go out in groups;</t><br/>t†~a ð<t>The<f>[4]</f> shephanim are not a mighty folk, </t><t>Yet they make their houses in the rocks;</t><br/>i†}K ð<t>The ants are not a strong people, </t><t>Yet they prepare their food in the summer;</t><br/>s†|_ ð<pb/><t>There are four things that are small on the earth, </t><t>But they are exceedingly wise:</t><br/>†{‚ ð<t>Under an unloved woman when she gets married, </t><t>And <i>under</i> a maidservant when she supplants her mistress.</t>†z‚	 ð<t>Under a servant when he reigns, </t><t><i>Under</i> a <n>[spiritually blind]</n> fool when he is filled with food,</t><br/>†y{ ð<pb/><t>Under three things the earth is disquieted <i>and</i> quakes, </t><t>And under four it cannot bear up:</t><br/>†x‚ ð<t>This is the way of an adulterous woman: </t><t>She eats and wipes her mouth </t><t>And says, "I have done no wrong."</t>8†w‚i ð<t>The way of an eagle in the air, </t><t>The way of a serpent on a rock, </t><t>The way of a ship in the middle of the sea, </t><t>And the way of a man with a maid.</t><br/>†v‚/ ð<pb/><t>There are three things which are too astounding <i>and</i> unexpectedly wonderful for me, </t><t>Four which I do not understand:</t><br/>(†u‚I ð<t>The eye that mocks a father </t><t>And scorns a mother, </t><t>The ravens of the valley will pick it out, </t><t>And the young vultures will devour it.</t>†t‚5 ð<t><f>[3]</f> Sheol, and the barren womb, </t><t>Earth that is never satisfied with water, </t><t>And fire that never says, "It is enough."</t><br/>1†s‚[ ð<pb/><t>The leech has two daughters, </t><t>"Give, give!" </t><t>There are three things that are never satisfied, </t><t>Four that do not say, "It is enough":</t><br/>S†rƒ ð<t>There is a generation (class of people) whose teeth are like swords </t><t>And whose jaw teeth are like knives, </t><t>To devour the afflicted from the earth </t><t>And the needy from among men.</t>†q‚% ð
<t>There is a generation (class of people)â€”oh, how lofty are their eyes! </t><t>And their eyelids are raised <i>in arrogance</i>.</t><br/>	†p‚ ð<t>There is a generation (class of people) that is pure in its own eyes, </t><t>Yet is not washed from its filthiness.</t><br/>†ow ð<pb/><t>There is a generation (class of people) that curses its father </t><t>And does not bless its mother.</t><br/>Y†nƒ+ ð
<pb/><t>Do not slander <i>or</i> malign a servant before his master <n>[stay out of another's personal life]</n>, </t><t>Or he will curse you <n>[for your interference]</n>, and you will be found guilty.</t>+†m‚O ð	<t>So that I will not be full and deny You and say, "Who is the LORD?" </t><t>Or that I will not be poor and steal, </t><t>And so profane the name of my God.</t>†l‚% ð<t>Keep deception and lies far from me; </t><t>Give me neither poverty nor riches; </t><t>Feed me with the food that is my portion,</t><br/>f†kE ð<pb/><t>Two things I have asked of You; </t><t>Do not deny them to me before I die:</t><br/>f†jE ð<t>Do not add to His words, </t><t>Or He will reprove you, and you will be found a liar.</t>'†i‚G ð<pb/><t>Every word of God is tested <i>and</i> refined <n>[like silver]</n>; </t><t>He is a shield to those who trust <i>and</i> take refuge in Him.</t><br/>‚!†h„; ð<t>Who has ascended into heaven and descended? </t><t>Who has gathered the wind in His fists? </t><t>Who has bound the waters in His garment? </t><t>Who has established all the ends of the earth? </t><t>What is His name, and what is His Son's name? </t><t>Certainly you know!</t>!†g‚; ð<t>I have not learned <n>[skillful and godly]</n> wisdom, </t><t>Nor do I have knowledge of the Holy One <n>[who is the source of wisdom]</n>.</t><br/>@†f‚y ð<pb/><t>Surely<f>[2]</f> I am more brutish <i>and</i> stupid than any man, </t><t>And I do not have the understanding of a man <n>[for I do not know what I do not know]</n>.</t><br/>	†e	‚
 ð<pb/><t>The words of<f>[1]</f> Agur the son of Jakeh, the oracle: </t><br/><t>The man says to Ithiel, to Ithiel and to Ucal:</t>†d‚% ð<t>An unjust man is repulsive to the righteous, </t><t>And he who is upright in the way <n>[of the LORD]</n> is repulsive to the wicked.</t>b†c= ð<t>Many seek the ruler's favor, </t><t>But justice for man comes from the LORD.</t><br/>†b‚5 ð<t>The fear of man brings a snare, </t><t>But whoever trusts in <i>and</i> puts his confidence in the LORD will be exalted <i>and</i> safe.</t><br/>N†aƒ ð<t>Whoever is partner with a thief hates his own life; </t><t>He hears the curse <n>[when swearing an oath to testify]</n>, but discloses nothing <n>[and commits perjury by omission]</n>.</t><br/>†`‚# ð<t>A man's pride <i>and</i> sense of self-importance will bring him down, </t><t>But he who has a humble spirit will obtain honor.</t><br/>†_‚ ð<t>An angry man stirs up strife, </t><t>And a hot-tempered <i>and</i> undisciplined man commits many transgressions.</t><br/>j†^M ð<t>He who pampers his slave from childhood </t><t>Will find him to be a son in the end.</t><br/>\†]ƒ1 ð<t>Do you see a <n>[conceited]</n> man who speaks quickly <n>[offering his opinions or answering without thinking]</n>? </t><t>There is more hope for a <n>[thickheaded]</n><f>[1]</f> fool than for him.</t><br/>†\‚5 ð<t>A servant will not be corrected by words <i>alone</i>; </t><t>For though he understands, he will not respond <n>[nor pay attention]</n>.</t><br/>D†[ƒ ð<t>Where there is no vision <n>[no revelation of God and His word]</n>, the people are unrestrained; </t><t>But happy <i>and</i> blessed is he who keeps the law <n>[of God]</n>.</t><br/>l†ZQ ð<t>Correct your son, and he will give you comfort; </t><t>Yes, he will delight your soul.</t><br/>†Y‚ ð<t>When the wicked are in authority, transgression increases, </t><t>But the righteous will see the downfall of the wicked.</t><br/>†X‚ ð<t>The rod and reproof (godly instruction) give wisdom, </t><t>But a child who gets his own way brings shame to his mother.</t><br/>†W{ ð<t>If a king faithfully <i>and</i> truthfully judges the poor, </t><t>His throne shall be established forever.</t><br/>z†Vm ð
<t>The poor man and the oppressor have this in common: </t><t>The LORD gives light to the eyes of both.</t><br/>†U‚ ð<t>If a ruler pays attention to lies <n>[and encourages corruption]</n>, </t><t>All his officials <i>will become</i> wicked.</t><br/>-†T‚S ð<t>A <n>[shortsighted]</n> fool always loses his temper <i>and</i> displays his anger, </t><t>But a wise man <n>[uses self-control and]</n> holds it back.</t><br/>†S‚ ð
<t>The bloodthirsty hate the blameless <n>[because of his integrity]</n>, </t><t>But the upright are concerned for his life.</t><br/>[†Rƒ/ ð	<t>If a wise man has a controversy with a foolish <i>and</i> arrogant man, </t><t>The foolish man <n>[ignores logic and fairness and]</n> only rages or laughs, and there is no peace (rest, agreement).</t><br/>)†Q‚K ð<t>Scoffers set a city afire <n>[by stirring up trouble]</n>, </t><t>But wise men turn away anger <n>[and restore order with their good judgment]</n>.</t><br/>†P‚ ð<t>The righteous man cares for the rights of the poor, </t><t>But the wicked man has no interest in such knowledge.</t><br/>!†O‚; ð<t>By his wicked plan an evil man is trapped, </t><t>But the righteous man sings and rejoices <n>[for his plan brings good things to him]</n>.</t><br/>†N‚ ð<t>A man who flatters his neighbor <n>[with smooth words intending to do harm]</n> </t><t>Is spreading a net for his own feet.</t><br/>}†Ms ð<t>The king establishes (stabilizes) the land by justice, </t><t>But a man who takes bribes overthrows it.</t><br/>"†L‚= ð<t>A man who loves <n>[skillful and godly]</n> wisdom makes his father joyful, </t><t>But he who associates with prostitutes wastes his wealth.</t><br/>.†K‚U ð<t>When the righteous are in authority <i>and</i> become great, the people rejoice; </t><t>But when the wicked man rules, the people groan <i>and</i> sigh.</t><br/>2†J	‚_ ð<pb/><t>He who hardens his neck <i>and</i> refuses instruction after being often reproved (corrected, criticized), </t><t>Will suddenly be broken beyond repair.</t><br/>6†I‚e ð<t>When the wicked rise <n>[to power]</n>, men hide themselves; </t><t>But when the wicked perish, the <n>[consistently]</n> righteous increase <i>and</i> become great.</t>†H‚ ð<t>He who gives to the poor will never want, </t><t>But he who shuts his eyes <n>[from their need]</n> will have many curses.</t><br/>5†G‚c ð<t>He who trusts confidently in his own heart is a <n>[dull, thickheaded]</n> fool, </t><t>But he who walks in <n>[skillful and godly]</n> wisdom will be rescued.</t><br/>†F‚ ð<t>An arrogant <i>and</i> greedy man stirs up strife, </t><t>But he who trusts in the LORD will be blessed <i>and</i> prosper.</t><br/>/†E‚W ð<t>He who robs his father or his mother </t><t>And says, "This is no sin," </t><t>Is <n>[not only a thief but also]</n> the companion of a man who destroys.</t><br/>†D‚5 ð<t>He who <n>[appropriately]</n> reprimands a <n>[wise]</n> man will afterward find more favor </t><t>Than he who flatters with the tongue.</t><br/>†C‚ ð<t>He who has an evil <i>and</i> envious eye hurries to be rich </t><t>And does not know that poverty will come upon him.</t><br/>&†B‚E ð<t>To have regard for one person over another <i>and</i> to show favoritism is not good, </t><t>Because for a piece of bread a man will transgress.</t><br/>†A‚ ð<t>A faithful (right-minded) man will abound with blessings, </t><t>But he who hurries to be rich will not go unpunished.</t><br/>1†@‚[ ð<t>He who cultivates his land will have plenty of bread, </t><t>But he who follows worthless people <i>and</i> frivolous pursuits will have plenty of poverty.</t><br/>†?‚ ð<t>He who walks blamelessly <i>and</i> uprightly will be kept safe, </t><t>But he who is crooked (perverse) will suddenly fall.</t><br/>+†>‚O ð<t>A man who is burdened with the guilt of human blood (murder) </t><t>Will be a fugitive until death; let no one support him <i>or</i> give him refuge.</t><br/>e†=ƒC ð<t>A leader who is a great oppressor lacks understanding <i>and</i> common sense <n>[and his wickedness shortens his days]</n>, </t><t>But he who hates unjust gain will <n>[be blessed and]</n> prolong his days.</t><br/>j†<M ð<t>Like a roaring lion and a charging bear </t><t>Is a wicked ruler over a poor people.</t><br/>Z†;ƒ- ð<t>Blessed <i>and</i> favored by God is the man who fears <n>[sin and its consequence]</n> at all times, </t><t>But he who hardens his heart <n>[and is determined to sin]</n> will fall into disaster.</t><br/>5†:‚c ð
<t>He who conceals his transgressions will not prosper, </t><t>But whoever confesses and<f>[2]</f> turns away from his sins will find compassion <i>and</i> mercy.</t><br/>+†9‚O ð<t>When the righteous triumph, there is great glory <i>and</i> celebration; </t><t>But when the wicked rise <n>[to prominence]</n>, men hide themselves.</t><br/>i†8ƒK ð<t>The rich man <n>[who is conceited and relies on his wealth instead of God]</n> is wise in his own eyes, </t><t>But the poor man who has understanding <n>[because he relies on God]</n> is able to see through him.</t><br/>†7‚1 ð
<t>He who leads the upright astray on an evil path </t><t>Will himself fall into his own pit, </t><t>But the blameless will inherit good.</t><br/>†6‚' ð	<t>He who turns his ear away from listening to the law <n>[of God and man]</n>, </t><t>Even his prayer is repulsive <n>[to God]</n>.</t><br/>†5‚ ð<t>He who increases his wealth by interest and usury (excessive interest) </t><t>Gathers it for him who is gracious to the poor.</t><br/>@†4‚y ð<t>He who keeps the law <n>[of God and man]</n> is a wise <i>and</i> discerning son, </t><t>But he who is a companion of gluttons humiliates his father <n>[and himself]</n>.</t><br/>	†3‚ ð<t>Better is the poor who walks in his integrity </t><t>Than he who is crooked <i>and</i> two-faced though he is rich.</t><br/>†2‚ ð<t>Evil men do not understand justice, </t><t>But they who long for <i>and</i> seek the LORD understand it fully.</t><br/>4†1‚a ð<t>Those who<f>[1]</f> set aside the law <n>[of God and man]</n> praise the wicked, </t><t>But those who keep the law <n>[of God and man]</n> struggle with them.</t><br/>†0{ ð<t>A poor man who oppresses <i>and</i> exploits the lowly </t><t>Is like a sweeping rain which leaves no food.</t><br/>†/‚1 ð<t>When a land does wrong, it has many princes, </t><t>But when the ruler is a man of understanding and knowledge, its stability endures.</t><br/>s†.	a ð<pb/><t>The wicked flee when no one pursues them, </t><t>But the righteous are as bold as a lion.</t><br/> †-‚9 ð<t>And <i>there will be</i> enough goats' milk for your food, </t><t>For the food of your household, </t><t>And for the maintenance of your maids.</t> †,y ð<t>The lambs will <i>supply wool</i> for your clothing, </t><t>And the goats will bring the price of a field.</t><br/>w†+g ð<t>When the grass is gone, the new growth is seen, </t><t>And herbs of the mountain are gathered in,</t><br/>k†*O ð<t>For riches are not forever, </t><t>Nor does a crown <i>endure</i> to all generations.</t><br/>t†)a ð<pb/><t>Be diligent to know the condition of your flocks, </t><t>And pay attention to your herds;</t><br/>:†(‚m ð<t>Even though you pound a <n>[hardened, arrogant]</n> fool <n>[who rejects wisdom]</n> in a mortar with a pestle like grain, </t><t>Yet his foolishness will not leave him.</t>n†'ƒU ð<t>The refining pot is for silver and the furnace for gold <n>[to separate the impurities of the metal]</n>, </t><t>And each is tested by the praise given to him <n>[and his response to it, whether humble or proud]</n>.</t><br/>†&‚! ð<t>Sheol (the place of the dead) and Abaddon (the underworld) are never satisfied; </t><t>Nor are the eyes of man ever satisfied.</t><br/>e†%C ð<t>As in water face <i>reflects</i> face, </t><t>So the heart of man reflects man.</t><br/>†$‚- ð<t>He who tends the fig tree will eat its fruit, </t><t>And he who faithfully protects <i>and</i> cares for his master will be honored.</t><br/>†#‚ ð<t>As iron sharpens iron, </t><t>So one man sharpens <n>[and influences]</n> another <n>[through discussion]</n>.</t><br/>†"‚) ð<t>Whoever attempts to restrain her <n>[criticism]</n> might as well try to stop the wind, </t><t>And grasps oil with his right hand.</t><br/>y†!k ð<t>A constant dripping on a day of steady rain </t><t>And a contentious (quarrelsome) woman are alike;</t><br/>M† ƒ ð<t>He who blesses his neighbor with a loud voice early in the morning, </t><t>It will be counted as a curse to him <n>[for it will either be annoying or his purpose will be suspect]</n>.</t><br/>‚†ƒ} ð
<t><n>[The judge tells the creditor,]</n> "Take the garment of one who is surety (guarantees a loan) for a stranger; </t><t>And hold him in pledge when he is surety for an immoral woman <n>[for it is unlikely the debt will be repaid]</n>."</t><br/>U†ƒ# ð<t>A prudent man sees evil and hides himself <i>and</i> avoids it, </t><t>But the naive <n>[who are easily misled]</n> continue on and are punished <n>[by suffering the consequences of sin]</n>.</t><br/>	†‚ ð<t>My son, be wise, and make my heart glad, </t><t>That I may reply to him who reproaches (reprimands, criticizes) me.</t><br/>`†ƒ9 ð
<t>Do not abandon your own friend and your father's friend, </t><t>And do not go to your brother's house in the day of your disaster. </t><t>Better is a neighbor who is near than a brother who is far away.</t><br/>†‚	 ð	<t>Oil and perfume make the heart glad; </t><t>So does the sweetness of a friend's counsel that comes from the heart.</t><br/>†‚ ð<t>Like a bird that wanders from her nest <n>[with its comfort and safety]</n>, </t><t>So is a man who wanders from his home.</t><br/>q†[ ð<t>He who is satisfied loathes honey, </t><t>But to the hungry soul any bitter thing is sweet.</t><br/>G†ƒ ð<t>Faithful are the wounds of a friend <n>[who corrects out of love and concern]</n>, </t><t>But the kisses of an enemy are deceitful <n>[because they serve his hidden agenda]</n>.</t><br/>p†Y ð<t>Better is an open reprimand <n>[of loving correction]</n> </t><t>Than love that is hidden.</t><br/>†‚7 ð<t>Wrath is cruel and anger is an overwhelming flood, </t><t>But who is able to endure <i>and</i> stand before <n>[the sin of]</n> jealousy?</t><br/>#†‚? ð<t>Stone is heavy and the sand weighty, </t><t>But a fool's <n>[unreasonable]</n> wrath is heavier <i>and</i> more burdensome than both of them.</t><br/>o†W ð<t>Let another praise you, and not your own mouth; </t><t>A stranger, and not your own lips.</t><br/>g†	I ð<pb/><t>Do not boast about tomorrow, </t><t>For you do not know what a day may bring.</t><br/>s†_ ð<t>A lying tongue hates those it wounds <i>and</i> crushes, </t><t>And a flattering mouth works ruin.</t>8†‚i ð<t>Whoever digs a pit <n>[for another man's feet]</n> will fall into it, </t><t>And he who rolls a stone <n>[up a hill to do mischief]</n>, it will come back on him.</t><br/>†‚5 ð<t><i>Though his</i> hatred covers itself with guile <i>and</i> deceit, </t><t>His malevolence will be revealed openly before the assembly.</t><br/>#†‚? ð<t>When he speaks graciously <i>and</i> kindly <n>[to conceal his malice]</n>, do not trust him, </t><t>For seven abominations are in his heart.</t><br/>l†Q ð<t>He who hates, disguises it with his lips, </t><t>But he stores up deceit in his heart.</t><br/>c†
ƒ? ð<t>Like a <n>[common]</n> clay vessel covered with the silver dross <n>[making it appear silver when it has no real value]</n> </t><t>Are burning lips <n>[murmuring manipulative words]</n> and a wicked heart.</t><br/>T†ƒ! ð<t>The words of a whisperer (gossip) are like dainty morsels <n>[to be greedily eaten]</n>; </t><t>They go down into the innermost chambers of the body <n>[to be remembered and mused upon]</n>.</t><br/>t†a ð<t>Like charcoal to hot embers and wood to fire, </t><t>So is a contentious man to kindle strife.</t><br/>†
‚ ð<t>For lack of wood the fire goes out, </t><t>And where there is no whisperer <n>[who gossips]</n>, contention quiets down.</t><br/>~†	u ð<t>So is the man who deceives his neighbor (acquaintance, friend) </t><t>And then says, "Was I not joking?"</t><br/>T†! ð<t>Like a madman who throws </t><t>Firebrands, arrows, and death,</t><br/>9†‚k ð<t>Like one who grabs a dog by the ears <n>[and is likely to be bitten]</n> </t><t>Is he who, passing by, stops to meddle with a dispute that is none of his business.</t><br/>†‚ ð<t>The lazy person is wiser in his own eyes </t><t>Than seven <n>[sensible]</n> men who can give a discreet answer.</t><br/>%†‚C ð<t>The lazy person buries his hand in the dish <n>[losing opportunity after opportunity]</n>; </t><t>It wearies him to bring it back to his mouth.</t><br/>†} ð<t>As the door turns on its hinges, </t><t>So does the lazy person on his bed <n>[never getting out of it]</n>.</t><br/>_†ƒ7 ð
<t>The lazy person <n>[who is self-indulgent and relies on lame excuses]</n> says, "There is a lion in the road! </t><t>A lion is in the open square <n>[and if I go outside to work I will be killed]</n>!"</t><br/>/†‚W ð<t>Do you see a man <n>[who is unteachable and]</n> wise in his own eyes <i>and</i> full of self-conceit? </t><t>There is more hope for a fool than for him.</t><br/>h†I ð<t>Like a dog that returns to his vomit </t><t>Is a fool who repeats his foolishness.</t><br/>;† ‚o ð
<t>Like a <n>[careless]</n> archer who <n>[shoots arrows wildly and]</n> wounds everyone, </t><t>So is he who hires a fool or those who <n>[by chance just]</n> pass by.</t><br/>E…ƒ ð	<t>Like a thorn that goes <n>[without being felt]</n> into the hand of a drunken man, </t><t>So is a proverb in the mouth of a fool <n>[who remains unaffected by its wisdom]</n>.</t><br/>2…~‚] ð<t>Like one who <n>[absurdly]</n> binds a stone in a sling <n>[making it impossible to throw]</n>, </t><t>So is he who <n>[absurdly]</n> gives honor to a fool.</t><br/>…}‚/ ð<t>Like the legs which are useless to the lame, </t><t>So is a proverb in the mouth of a fool <n>[who cannot learn from its wisdom]</n>.</t><br/>B…|‚} ð<t>He who sends a message by the hand of a fool </t><t>Cuts off <i>his own</i> feet (sabotages himself) and drinks the violence <n>[it brings on himself as a consequence]</n>.</t><br/>E…{ƒ ð<t>Answer <n>[and correct the erroneous concepts of]</n> a fool according to his folly, </t><t>Otherwise he will be wise in his own eyes <n>[if he thinks you agree with him]</n>.</t><br/>H…zƒ	 ð<t>Do not answer <n>[nor pretend to agree with the frivolous comments of]</n> a <n>[closed-minded]</n> fool according to his folly, </t><t>Otherwise you, even you, will be like him.</t><br/>
…y‚
 ð<t>A whip for the horse, a bridle for the donkey, </t><t>And a rod for the backs of fools <n>[who refuse to learn]</n>.</t><br/>7…x‚g ð<t>Like the sparrow in her wandering, like the swallow in her flying, </t><t>So the curse without cause does not come <i>and</i> alight <n>[on the undeserving]</n>.</t><br/>…w	‚ ð<pb/><t>Like snow in summer and like rain in harvest, </t><t>So honor is not fitting for a <n>[shortsighted]</n><f>[1]</f> fool.</t><br/>D…vƒ ð<t>Like a city that is broken down and without walls <n>[leaving it unprotected]</n> </t><t>Is a man who has no self-control over his spirit <n>[and sets himself up for trouble]</n>.</t>j…uM ð<t>It is not good to eat much honey, </t><t>Nor is it glorious to seek one's own glory.</t><br/> …t‚9 ð<t>Like a muddied fountain and a polluted spring </t><t>Is a righteous man who yields <i>and</i> compromises his integrity before the wicked.</t><br/>d…sA ð<t>Like cold water to a thirsty soul, </t><t>So is good news from a distant land.</t><br/>>…r‚u ð<t>It is better to live in a corner of the housetop <n>[on the flat roof, exposed to the weather]</n> </t><t>Than in a house shared with a quarrelsome (contentious) woman.</t><br/>m…qS ð<t>The north wind brings forth rain; </t><t>And a backbiting tongue, an angry countenance.</t><br/> …py ð<t>For in doing so, you will<f>[2]</f> heap coals of fire upon his head, </t><t>And the LORD will reward you.</t><br/>{…oo ð<t>If your enemy is hungry, give him bread to eat; </t><t>And if he is thirsty, give him water to drink;</t><br/>^…nƒ5 ð<t>Like one who takes off a garment in cold weather, or like <n>[a reactive, useless mixture of]</n> vinegar on soda, </t><t>Is he who <n>[thoughtlessly]</n> sings <n>[joyful]</n> songs to a heavy heart.</t><br/>{…mo ð<t>Like a broken tooth or an unsteady foot </t><t>Is confidence in an unfaithful man in time of trouble.</t><br/>
…l‚ ð<t>Like a club and a sword and a piercing arrow </t><t>Is a man who testifies falsely against his neighbor (acquaintance).</t><br/>{…ko ð<t>Let your foot seldom be in your neighbor's house, </t><t>Or he will become tired of you and hate you.</t><br/> …j‚9 ð<t>Have you found <n>[pleasure sweet like]</n> honey? Eat only as much as you need, </t><t>Otherwise, being filled excessively, you vomit it.</t><br/>'…i‚G ð<t>By patience <i>and</i> a calm spirit a ruler may be persuaded, </t><t>And a soft <i>and</i> gentle tongue breaks the bone <n>[of resistance]</n>.</t><br/>~…hu ð<t>Like clouds and wind without rain </t><t>Is a man who boasts falsely of gifts <n>[he does not give]</n>.</t><br/>Q…gƒ ð
<t>Like the cold of snow <n>[brought from the mountains]</n> in the time of harvest, </t><t>So is a faithful messenger to those who send him; </t><t>For he refreshes the life of his masters.</t><br/>…f‚ ð<t>Like an earring of gold and an ornament of fine gold </t><t>Is a wise reprover to an ear that listens <i>and</i> learns.</t><br/>o…eW ð<pb/><t>Like apples of gold in settings of silver </t><t>Is a word spoken at the right time.</t><br/>…d‚ ð
<t>Or he who hears it will shame you </t><t>And the rumor about you <n>[and your action in court]</n> will have no end.</t>…c‚ ð	<t>Argue your case with your neighbor himself <n>[before you go to court]</n>; </t><t>And do not reveal another's secret,</t><br/>q…bƒ[ ð<pb/><t>Do not rush out to argue <i>your</i> case <n>[before magistrates or judges]</n>; </t><t>Otherwise what will you do in the end <n>[when your case is lost and]</n> </t><t>When your neighbor (opponent) humiliates you?</t><br/>0…a‚Y ð<t>For it is better that it be said to you, "Come up here," </t><t>Than for you to be placed lower in the presence of the prince, </t><t>Whom your eyes have seen.</t>…`‚/ ð<t>Do not be boastfully ambitious <i>and</i> claim honor in the presence of the king, </t><t>And do not stand in the place of great men;</t><br/>|…_q ð<t>Take away the wicked from before the king, </t><t>And his throne will be established in righteousness.</t><br/>!…^‚; ð<t>Take away the dross from the silver, </t><t>And there comes out <n>[the pure metal for]</n> a vessel for the silversmith <n>[to shape]</n>.</t><br/>…]‚ ð<t>As the heavens for height and the earth for depth, </t><t>So the hearts <i>and</i> minds of kings are<f>[1]</f> unsearchable.</t><br/>…\w ð<pb/><t>It is the glory of God to conceal a matter, </t><t>But the glory of kings is to search out a matter.</t><br/>l…[	S ð<pb/><t>These are also the proverbs of Solomon, which the men of Hezekiah king of Judah copied:</t>a…Z; ð"<t>Then your poverty will come as a robber, </t><t>And your want like an armed man.</t>…Y{ ð!<t>"Yet a little sleep, a little slumber, </t><t>A little folding of the hands to rest <n>[and daydream]</n>,"</t><br/>a…X; ð <t>When I saw, I considered it well; </t><t>I looked and received instruction.</t><br/>…W‚3 ð<t>And, behold, it was all overgrown with thorns, </t><t>And nettles were covering its surface, </t><t>And its stone wall was broken down.</t><br/>…V‚! ð<pb/><t>I went by the field of the lazy man, </t><t>And by the vineyard of the man lacking understanding <i>and</i> common sense;</t><br/>r…U] ð<t>Do not say, "I will do to him as he has done to me; </t><t>I will pay the man back for his deed."</t>3…T‚_ ð<pb/><t>Do not be a witness against your<f>[3]</f> neighbor without cause, </t><t>And do not deceive with your lips <n>[speak neither lies nor half-truths]</n>.</t><br/>,…S‚Q ð<pb/><t>Prepare your work outside </t><t>And get it ready for yourself in the field; </t><t><f>[2]</f> Afterward build your house <i>and</i> establish a home.</t>…R‚ ð<t>He kisses the lips <n>[and wins the hearts of people]</n> </t><t>Who gives a right <i>and</i> straightforward answer.</t>G…Qƒ ð<t>But to those <n>[honorable judges]</n> who rebuke the <i>wicked</i>, it will go well with them <i>and</i> they will find delight, </t><t>And a good blessing will come upon them.</t><br/>…Pw ð<t>He who says to the wicked, "You are righteous," </t><t>Peoples will curse him, nations will denounce him;</t><br/>q…O[ ð<pb/><t>These also are sayings of the wise: </t><t>To show partiality in judgment is not good.</t><br/>!…N‚; ð<t>For their tragedy will rise suddenly, </t><t>And who knows the punishment that both <n>[the LORD and the king]</n> will bring on the rebellious?</t>,…M‚Q ð<pb/><t>My son, fear the LORD and the king; </t><t>And do not associate with those who are given to change <n>[of allegiance, and are revolutionary]</n>,</t><br/>m…LS ð<t>For there will be no future for the evil man; </t><t>The lamp of the wicked will be put out.</t>e…KC ð<pb/><t>Do not get upset because of evildoers, </t><t>Or be envious of the wicked,</t><br/>v…Je ð<t>Or the LORD will see your gloating and be displeased, </t><t>And turn His anger away from your enemy.</t>+…I‚O ð<pb/><t>Do not rejoice <i>and</i> gloat when your enemy falls, </t><t>And do not let your heart be glad <n>[in self-righteousness]</n> when he stumbles,</t><br/>…H‚% ð<t>For a righteous man falls seven times, and rises again, </t><t>But the wicked stumble in <i>time of</i> disaster <i>and</i> collapse.</t>…G‚ ð<pb/><t>Do not lie in wait, O wicked man, against the dwelling of the righteous; </t><t>Do not destroy his resting place;</t><br/>‚…Fƒ} ð<t>Know that <n>[skillful and godly]</n> wisdom is <n>[so very good]</n> for your life <i>and</i> soul; </t><t>If you find wisdom, then there will be a future <i>and</i> a reward, </t><t>And your hope <i>and</i> expectation will not be cut off.</t>…E‚ ð
<pb/><t>My son, eat honey, because it is good, </t><t>And the drippings of the honeycomb are sweet to your taste.</t><br/>‚Y…D…+ ð<t>If you <n>[claim ignorance and]</n> say, "See, we did not know this," </t><t>Does He not consider it who weighs <i>and</i> examines the hearts <i>and</i> their motives? </t><t>And does He not know it who guards your life <i>and</i> keeps your soul? </t><t>And will He not repay <n>[you and]</n> every man according to his works?</t>%…C‚C ð<pb/><t>Rescue those who are being taken away to death, </t><t>And those who stagger to the slaughter, Oh hold them back <n>[from their doom]</n>!</t><br/>j…BM ð
<pb/><t>If you are slack (careless) in the day of distress, </t><t>Your strength is limited.</t>Z…A- ð	<t>The devising of folly is sin, </t><t>And the scoffer is repulsive to men.</t>m…@S ð<pb/><t>He who plans to do evil </t><t>Will be called a schemer <i>or</i> deviser of evil.</t><br/>=…?‚s ð<pb/><t>Wisdom is too exalted for a <n>[hardened, arrogant]</n><f>[1]</f> fool; </t><t>He does not open his mouth in the gate <n>[where the city's rulers sit in judgment]</n>.</t>…>‚' ð<t>For by wise guidance you can wage your war, </t><t>And in an abundance of <n>[wise]</n> counselors there is victory <i>and</i> safety.</t>
