But it will not be well for the evil man, nor will he lengthen his days like a shadow, because he does not fear God.‚5ˆ?„c úThough a sinner does evil a hundred <i>times</i> and his life <n>[seemingly]</n> is prolonged <n>[in spite of his wickedness]</n>, still I know that it will be well with those who <n>[reverently]</n> fear God, who fear <i>and</i> worship Him openly <n>[realizing His omnipresence and His power]</n>.ˆ> úBecause the sentence against an evil act is not executed quickly, the hearts of the sons of men are fully set to do evil.‚2ˆ=„] ú
<pb/>So then, I have seen the wicked buried, those who used to go in and out of the holy place <n>[but did not thereby escape their doom]</n>, and they are <n>[praised in spite of their evil and]</n> soon forgotten in the city where they did such things. This too is futility (vanity, emptiness).9ˆ<‚k ú	<pb/>All this I have seen while applying my mind to every deed that is done under the sun. There is a time in which one man has exercised power over others to their detriment.‚8ˆ;„i ú<t>There is no man who has power <i>and</i> authority over the wind to restrain the wind, </t><t>Nor does he have authority over the day of death; </t><t>There is no discharge <n>[from service]</n> during time of war, </t><t>And evil will not rescue those who <n>[actively seek to]</n> practice it.</t>wˆ:g ú<t>For no one knows what will happen; </t><t>So who can tell him how <i>and</i> when it will happen?</t><br/>^ˆ9ƒ5 ú<t>For there is a<f>[2]</f> proper time and <n>[appropriate]</n> procedure for every delight, </t><t>Though mankind's misery <i>and</i> trouble lies heavily upon him <n>[who rebels against the king]</n>.</t><br/>aˆ8ƒ; ú<t>Whoever keeps <i>and</i> observes a <i>royal</i> command will experience neither trouble <i>nor</i> misery; </t><t>For a wise heart will know the<f>[1]</f> proper time and <n>[appropriate]</n> procedure.</t><br/>
ˆ7‚ ú<pb/><t>For the word of a king is authoritative <i>and</i> powerful, </t><t>And who will say to him, "What are you doing?"</t><br/>ˆ6‚ ú<t>Do not be in a hurry to get out of his presence. Do not join in a malevolent matter, for the king will do whatever he pleases.</t>ˆ5‚ ú<pb/>I counsel you to keep the command of the king because of the oath before God <n>[by which you swore loyalty to him]</n>.7ˆ4	‚i ú<pb/><t>Who is like the wise man? </t><t>And who knows the interpretation of a matter? </t><t>A man's wisdom illumines his face, </t><t>And causes his stern face to beam.</t>Aˆ3‚{ úBehold, I have found only this <n>[as a reason]</n>: God made man upright <i>and</i> uncorrupted, but they <n>[both men and women]</n> have sought out many devices <n>[for evil]</n>."\ˆ2ƒ1 úwhich I am still seeking but have not found. I have found one<f>[2]</f> man among a thousand <n>[who pleases God]</n>, but I have not found <n>[such]</n> a woman among all these <n>[a thousand in my harem]</n>.ˆ1‚ ú<pb/>"Behold, I have discovered this," says the Preacher, "while <i>adding</i> one thing to another to find an explanation,‚jˆ0…M úAnd I discovered that <n>[of all irrational sins none has been so destructive in beguiling one away from God as immoral women for]</n> more bitter than death is the woman whose heart is <n>[composed of]</n> snares and nets, and whose hands are chains. Whoever pleases God will escape from her, but the sinner will be taken captive by her <n>[evil]</n>.‚
ˆ/„ úI turned around <i>and</i> directed my heart to know, to investigate and to seek <n>[skillful and godly]</n> wisdom and the reason for things, and to know that wickedness is folly and that foolishness is madness <n>[leading to stupidity and recklessness]</n>.ˆ.‚ úWhatever has been is far off, deeply remote and exceedingly mysterious. Who can discover it <n>[for it is beyond the grasp of man]</n>?ˆ-‚ ú<pb/>I have tested all this with wisdom. I said, "I will be wise <n>[independently of God]</n>," but true wisdom was far from me.Gˆ, úfor you also know that you too have cursed others many times.rˆ+] úAlso, do not take seriously everything that is said, so that you will not hear your servant cursing you,gˆ*G úIndeed, there is not a righteous man on earth who <i>always</i> does good and who never sins.Vˆ)% ú<pb/>Wisdom strengthens the wise man more than ten rulers who are in a city.cˆ(ƒ? úIt is good that you take hold of one thing (righteousness) and also not let go of the other (wisdom); for the one who fears <i>and</i> worships God <n>[with awe-filled reverence]</n> will come forth with both of them.uˆ'c úDo not be excessively <i>or</i> willfully wicked and do not be a fool. Why should you die before your time?%ˆ&‚C úDo not be excessively righteous <n>[like those given to self-conceit]</n>, and do not be overly wise (pretentious)â€”why should you bring yourself to ruin?‚ˆ%„ ú<pb/>I have seen everything during my <n>[fleeting]</n> days of futility; there is a righteous man who perishes in <n>[spite of]</n> his righteousness, and there is a wicked man who<f>[1]</f> lives a long <i>life</i> in <n>[spite of]</n> his wickedness.dˆ$ƒA ú<t>In the day of prosperity be joyful, </t><t>But in the day of adversity consider that </t><t>God has made the one as well as the other, </t><t>So that man will not find out anything <i>that will be</i> after him.</t>^ˆ#5 ú
<t>Consider the work of God: </t><t>Who can make straight what He has bent?</t><br/>Xˆ"ƒ) ú<t>For wisdom is a protection <i>even as</i> money is a protection, </t><t>But the <n>[excellent]</n> advantage of knowledge is that wisdom shields <i>and</i> preserves the lives of its possessors.</t><br/>ˆ!‚ ú<t>Wisdom along with an inheritance is good </t><t>And an <n>[excellent]</n> advantage for those who see the sun.</t><br/>ˆ ‚ ú
<t>Do not say, "Why were the old days better than these?" </t><t>For it is not from wisdom that you ask about this.</t><br/>oˆW ú	<t>Do not be eager in your heart to be angry, </t><t>For anger dwells in the heart of fools.</t><br/>ˆ‚ ú<t>The end of a matter is better than its beginning; </t><t>Patience of spirit is better than haughtiness of spirit (pride).</t><br/>ˆ ú<t>For oppression makes a wise man foolish, </t><t>And a bribe corrupts the <n>[good judgment of the]</n> heart.</t><br/>+ˆ‚O ú<t>For like the crackling of <n>[burning]</n> thorn bushes under a pot, </t><t>So is the laughter of the fool; </t><t>And this too is vanity (futility).</t><br/>3ˆ‚_ ú<t>It is better to listen to the rebuke of the wise man <i>and</i> pursue wisdom </t><t>Than for one to listen to the song of fools <i>and</i> pursue stupidity.</t><br/>.ˆ‚U ú<t>The heart of the wise <n>[learns when it]</n> is in the house of mourning, </t><t>But the heart of fools is <n>[senseless]</n> in the house of pleasure.</t><br/>&ˆ‚E ú<t>Sorrow is better than laughter, </t><t>For when a face is sad (deep in thought) the heart may be happy <n>[because it is growing in wisdom]</n>.</t><br/>‚ ˆƒy ú<t>It is better to go to the house of mourning </t><t>Than to go to the house of feasting, </t><t>For that <n>[day of death]</n> is the end of every man, </t><t>And the living will take it to heart <i>and</i> solemnly ponder its meaning.</t><br/>ˆ	‚ ú<pb/><t>A good name is better than precious perfume, </t><t>And the day of one's death better than the day of one's birth.</t><br/>ƒ ˆ…y úFor who <n>[<f>[1]</f> limited by human wisdom]</n> knows what is good for man during his lifetime, during the few days of his futile life? He spends them like a shadow <n>[staying busy, but achieving nothing of lasting value]</n>. For who can tell a man what will happen after him <n>[to his work, his treasure, his plans]</n> under the sun <n>[after his life is over]</n>?fˆE úFor there are many other words that increase futility. What then is the advantage for a man?4ˆ‚a ú
<pb/>Whatever exists has already been named <n>[long ago]</n>, and it is known what <n>[a frail being]</n> man is; for he cannot dispute with Him who is mightier than he.'ˆ‚G ú	What the eyes see <n>[enjoying what is available]</n> is better than <n>[craving]</n> what the soul desires. This too is futility and chasing after the wind.‚8ˆ„i úFor what advantage has the wise man over the fool <n>[for being worldly-wise is not the secret to happiness]</n>? What <i>advantage</i> has the poor man who has learned how to walk <n>[publicly]</n> among the living <n>[with men's eyes on him; for being poor is not the secret to happiness either]</n>?ˆ‚1 ú<pb/>All the labor of man is for his mouth <n>[for self-preservation and enjoyment]</n>, and yet the desire <n>[of his soul]</n> is not satisfied.8ˆ‚i úEven if the <i>other</i> man lives a thousand years twice over and yet has seen no good <i>and</i> experienced no enjoymentâ€”do not both go to one place <n>[the grave]</n>?"oˆW úIt has not seen the sun nor had any knowledge; yet it has more rest <i>and</i> is better off than he.zˆm úfor the miscarriage comes in futility (in vain) and passes into obscurity; and its name is covered in obscurity.‚Hˆ
…	 úIf a man fathers a hundred <i>children</i> and lives many years, however many they may be, but his soul is not satisfied with good things and he is not respected <i>and</i> is not given a <i>proper</i> burial <n>[he is not laid to rest in the sepulcher of his fathers]</n>, then I say, "Better the miscarriage than he,‚zˆ…m úa man to whom God has given riches and wealth and honor, so that he lacks nothing of all that he desires, yet God has not given him the power <i>or</i> capacity to enjoy them <n>[all those things which are gifts from God]</n>, but a stranger <n>[in whom he has no interest succeeds him and]</n> enjoys them. This is vanity and it is a <n>[cause of]</n> great distress.]ˆ	5 ú<pb/>There is an evil which I have seen under the sun, and it weighs heavily on men:Lˆ
ƒ úFor he will not often consider the <n>[troubled]</n> days of his life, because God keeps him occupied <i>and</i> focused on the joy of his heart <n>[and the tranquility of God indwells him]</n>.‚	ˆ	„ úAlso, every man to whom God has given riches and possessions, He has also given the power <i>and</i> ability to enjoy them and to receive <n>[this as]</n> his <n>[allotted]</n> portion and to rejoice in his laborâ€”this is the gift of God <n>[to him]</n>.‚ˆƒ{ ú<pb/>Behold, here is what I have seen to be good and fitting: to eat and drink, and to find enjoyment in all the labor in which he labors under the sun during the few days of his life which God gives himâ€”for this is his <n>[allotted]</n> reward.ˆ‚ úAll of his life he also eats in darkness <n>[cheerlessly, without sweetness and light]</n>, with great frustration, sickness, and anger.ˆ úThis also is a grievous evilâ€”exactly as he was born, so he shall die. So what advantage has he who labors for the wind? ˆ‚9 úAs he came naked from his mother's womb, so he will return as he came; and he will take away nothing from all his labor that he can carry in his hand.(ˆ‚I úFor when those riches are lost in bad investments and he becomes the father of a son, then there is nothing in his hand <n>[for the support of the child]</n>.ˆ‚ ú
<pb/>There is a grievous evil which I have seen under the sun: riches being kept <i>and</i> hoarded by their owner to his own misery.2ˆ‚] úThe sleep of a working man is sweet, whether he eats little or much; but the full stomach (greed) of the rich <n>[who hungers for even more]</n> will not let him sleep.ˆ‚! úWhen good things increase, those who consume them increase. So what advantage is there to their owners except to see them with their eyes?ˆ ‚ ú
He who loves money will not be satisfied with money, nor he who loves abundance <i>with its</i> gain. This too is vanity (emptiness).Q‡ ú	After all, a king who cultivates the field is an advantage to the land.‚'‡~„G ú<pb/>If you see the oppression of the poor and the denial of justice and righteousness in the province, do not be shocked at the sight <n>[of corruption]</n>; for a higher official watches over another official, and there are higher ones over them <n>[looking out for one another]</n>.?‡}‚w úFor in a multitude of dreams and in a flood of words there is worthlessness. Rather <n>[reverently]</n> fear God <n>[and worship Him with awe-filled respect, knowing who He is]</n>.c‡|ƒ? úDo not allow your speech to cause you to sin, and do not say before the messenger (priest) <i>of God</i> that it was a mistake. Why should God be angry because of your voice (words) and destroy the work of your hands?T‡{! úIt is better that you should not vow than that you should vow and not pay.8‡z‚i ú<pb/>When you make a vow <i>or</i> a pledge to God, do not put off paying it; for <i>God takes</i> no pleasure in fools <n>[who thoughtlessly mock Him]</n>. Pay what you vow.`‡y9 úFor the dream comes through much effort, and the voice of the fool through many words.W‡xƒ' úDo not be hasty with your mouth <n>[speaking careless words or vows]</n> or impulsive in thought to bring up a matter before God. For God is in heaven and you are on earth; therefore let your words be few.‡w	ƒy ú<pb/>Guard your steps <i>and</i> focus on what you are doing as you go to the house of God and draw near to listen rather than to offer the <n>[careless or irreverent]</n> sacrifice of fools; for they are too ignorant to know they are doing evil.A‡v‚{ úThere is no end to all the people; to all who were before them. Yet those who come later will not be happy with him. Surely this also is vanity (emptiness) and chasing after the wind.‡u úI have seen all the living under the sun join with the second youth (the king's acknowledged successor) who replaces him.
‡t‚ úfor the poor youth has <n>[used his wisdom and]</n> come out of prison to become king, even though he was born poor in his kingdom.6‡s‚e ú
<pb/>A poor yet wise youth is better than an old and foolish king who<f>[2]</f> no longer knows how to receive instruction <i>and</i> counsel (friendly reproof, warning)â€”‡r} úAnd though one can overpower him who is alone, two can resist him. A cord of three <i>strands</i> is not quickly broken.e‡qC úAgain, if two lie down together, then they keep warm; but how can one be warm <i>alone</i>?(‡p‚I ú
for if<f>[1]</f> either of them falls, the one will lift up his companion. But woe to him who is alone when he falls and does not have another to lift him up.b‡o= ú	<pb/>Two are better than one because they have a more satisfying return for their labor;‚r‡n…] úThere was a certain manâ€”without a dependent, having neither a child nor a brother, yet there was no end to all his labor. Indeed, his eyes were not satisfied with riches <i>and he never asked</i>, "For whom do I labor and deprive myself of pleasure?" This too is vanity (a wisp of smoke, self-conceit); yes, it is a painful effort <i>and</i> an unhappy task.a‡m; ú<pb/>Then I looked again at vanity under the sun <n>[in one of its peculiar forms]</n>.v‡le úOne hand full of rest <i>and</i> patience is better than two fists full of labor and chasing after the wind.‡k úThe fool folds his hands <n>[together]</n> and consumes his own flesh <n>[destroying himself by idleness and apathy]</n>.P‡jƒ ú<pb/>I have seen that every <n>[effort in]</n> labor and every skill in work <i>comes from</i> man's rivalry with his neighbor. This too is vanity (futility, false pride) and chasing after the wind.‡i‚ úBut better off than either of them is the one who has not yet been born, who has not seen the evil deeds that are done under the sun.‡h úSo I congratulated <i>and</i> thought more fortunate are those who are already dead than the living who are still living.‚'‡g	„I ú<pb/>Then I looked again <i>and</i> considered all the acts of oppression that were being practiced under the sun. And behold <i>I saw</i> the tears of the oppressed and they had no one to comfort them; and on the side of their oppressors was power, but they had no one to comfort them.m‡fƒS úSo I have seen that there is nothing better than that a man should be happy in his own works <i>and</i> activities, for that is his portion (share). For who will bring him <n>[back]</n> to see what will happen after he is gone?t‡ea úWho knows if the spirit of man ascends upward and the spirit of the animal descends downward to the earth?V‡d% úAll go to the same place. All came from the dust and all return to the dust.‚‡c„/ úFor the <n>[earthly]</n> fate of the sons of men and the fate of animals is the same. As one dies, so dies the other; indeed, they all have the same breath and there is no preeminence <i>or</i> advantage for man <n>[in and of himself]</n> over an animal, for all is vanity.4‡b‚a úI said to myself regarding the sons of men, "God is surely testing them in order for them to see that <n>[by themselves, without God]</n> they are <n>[only]</n> animals."‡a‚/ úI said to myself, "God will judge both the righteous and the wicked," for there is a time <n>[appointed]</n> for every matter and for every deed.‡`‚/ ú<pb/>Moreover, I have seen under the sun that in the place of justice there is wickedness, and in the place of righteousness there is wickedness.‡_‚1 úThat which is has already been, and that which will be has already been, for God seeks what has passed by <n>[so that history repeats itself]</n>.s‡^ƒ_ úI know that whatever God does, it endures forever; nothing can be added to it nor can anything be taken from it, for God does it so that men will fear <i>and</i> worship Him <n>[with awe-filled reverence, knowing that He is God]</n>.‡]} ú
and also that every man should eat and drink and see <i>and</i> enjoy the good of all his laborâ€”it is the gift of God.p‡\Y ú<pb/>I know that there is nothing better for them than to rejoice and to do good as long as they live;‚e‡[…C úHe has made everything beautiful <i>and</i> appropriate in its time. He has also planted eternity <n>[a sense of divine purpose]</n> in the human heart <n>[a mysterious longing which nothing under the sun can satisfy, except God]</n>â€”yet man cannot find out (comprehend, grasp) what God has done (His overall plan) from the beginning to the end.f‡ZE ú
I have seen the task which God has given to the sons of men with which to occupy themselves.P‡Y ú	<pb/>What profit is there for the worker from that in which he labors?_‡X7 ú<t>A time to love and a time to hate; </t><t>A time for war and a time for peace.</t>‡W} ú<t>A time to<f>[1]</f> tear apart and a time to sew together; </t><t>A time to keep silent and a time to speak.</t><br/>u‡Vc ú<t>A time to search and a time to give up as lost; </t><t>A time to keep and a time to throw away.</t><br/>
‡U‚ ú<t>A time to throw away stones and a time to gather stones; </t><t>A time to embrace and a time to refrain from embracing.</t><br/>e‡TC ú<t>A time to weep and a time to laugh; </t><t>A time to mourn and a time to dance.</t><br/>k‡SO ú<t>A time to kill and a time to heal; </t><t>A time to tear down and a time to build up.</t><br/>|‡Rq ú<pb/><t>A time to be born and a time to die; </t><t>A time to plant and a time to uproot what is planted.</t><br/>‡Q	‚+ ú<pb/><t>There is a season (a time appointed) for everything and a time for every delight <i>and</i> event <i>or</i> purpose under heavenâ€”</t>l‡PƒQ úFor to the person who pleases Him God gives wisdom, knowledge, and joy; but to the sinner He gives the work of gathering and collecting so that he may give to one who pleases God. This too is vanity and chasing after the wind.@‡O{ úFor who can eat and who can have enjoyment without Him?+‡N‚O ú<pb/>There is nothing better for a man than to eat and drink and assure himself that there is good in his labor. Even this, I have seen, is from the hand of God.‡M úFor all his days his work is painful and sorrowful; even at night his mind does not rest. This too is vanity (worthless).
‡L‚ úFor what does a man get from all his labor and from the striving <i>and</i> sorrow of his heart with which he labors under the sun?.‡K‚U úFor there is a man who has labored with wisdom and knowledge and skill, yet gives his legacy to one who has not labored for it. This too is vanity and a great evil.z‡Jm úSo I turned aside and let my heart despair over all the fruit of my labor for which I had labored under the sun.^‡Iƒ5 úAnd who knows whether he will be a wise man or a fool? Yet he will have control over all the fruit of my labor for which I have labored by acting wisely under the sun. This too is vanity (futility, self-conceit).‡H‚! úSo I hated all the fruit (gain) of my labor for which I had labored under the sun, because I must leave it to the man who will succeed me.‡G‚- úSo I hated life, for the work which had been done under the sun caused me only great sorrow; because all is futility and chasing after the wind.D‡Fƒ úFor there is no <n>[more]</n> lasting remembrance of the wise man than of the fool, since in the days to come all will be long forgotten. And how does the wise man die? Even as the fool!L‡Eƒ úThen I said to myself, "As it happens to the fool, so death will also happen to me. What use is it then for me to be extremely wise?" Then I said in my heart, "This too is vanity (meaningless)."‡D‚# úThe wise man's eyes are in his head, but the fool walks in darkness; and yet I know that <n>[in the end]</n> one fate happens to them both.6‡C‚e ú
Then I saw that <n>[even secular]</n> wisdom <n>[that brings sorrow]</n> is better than <n>[the pleasures of]</n> folly <i>and</i> self-indulgence as light excels darkness.0‡B‚Y úSo I turned to consider <n>[secular]</n> wisdom, madness, and folly; for what will the man do who succeeds the king? Nothing <i>except</i> what has already been done.B‡A‚} úThen I considered all which my hands had done and labored to do, and behold, all was vanity and chasing after the wind and there was no profit (nothing of lasting value) under the sun.Q‡@ƒ ú
Whatever my eyes looked at with desire I did not refuse them. I did not withhold from my heart any pleasure, for my heart was pleased because of all my labor; and this was my reward for all my labor.z‡?m ú	<pb/>So I became great and excelled more than all who preceded me in Jerusalem. My wisdom also remained with me.k‡>ƒO úAlso, I collected for myself silver and gold and the treasure of kings and provinces. I provided for myself male singers and female singers, and the delights <i>and</i> pleasures of menâ€”<f>[1]</f> many<f>[2]</f> concubines.‡=‚- úI bought male and female slaves and had slaves born in my house. I also possessed herds and flocks larger than any who preceded me in Jerusalem.h‡<I úI made pools of water for myself from which to water the forest <i>and</i> make the trees bud.`‡;9 úI made gardens and orchards for myself and I planted in them all kinds of fruit trees;X‡:) úI made great works: I built houses for myself; I planted vineyards for myself;‚&‡9„E úI explored with my mind how to gratify myself with wine while <n>[at the same time]</n> having my mind remain steady <i>and</i> guide me wisely; and how to take control of foolishness, until I could see what was good for the sons of men to do under heaven all the days of their lives.Z‡8- úI said of laughter, "It is madness," and of pleasure, "What does it accomplish?"O‡7	ƒ ú<pb/>I said to myself, "Come now, I will test you with pleasure <i>and</i> gratification; so enjoy yourself <i>and</i> have a good time." But behold, this too was vanity (futility, meaninglessness).‡6	‚ úFor in much <n>[human]</n> wisdom there is much displeasure <i>and</i> exasperation; increasing knowledge increases sorrow.y‡5	ƒm úAnd I set my mind to know <n>[practical]</n> wisdom and to discern <n>[the character of]</n> madness and folly <n>[in which men seem to find satisfaction]</n>; I realized that this too is a futile grasping <i>and</i> chasing after the wind.‚‡4	„ ú<pb/>I spoke with my heart, saying, "Behold, I have acquired great <n>[human]</n> wisdom <i>and</i> experience, more than all who were over Jerusalem before me; and my mind has observed a wealth of <n>[moral]</n> wisdom and <n>[scientific]</n> knowledge."k‡3	Q úWhat is crooked cannot be straightened and what is defective <i>and</i> lacking cannot be counted.‡2	‚' úI have seen all the works which have been done under the sun, and behold, all is vanity, a futile grasping <i>and</i> chasing after the wind.‚!‡1	„= ú
And I set my mind to seek and explore by <n>[man's]</n><f>[2]</f> wisdom all <n>[human activity]</n> that has been done under heaven. It is a miserable business <i>and</i> a burdensome task which<f>[3]</f> God has given the sons of men with which to be busy <i>and</i> distressed.A‡0	 úI, the Preacher, have been king over Israel in Jerusalem.R‡/	ƒ ú<t>There is no remembrance of earlier things, </t><t>Nor also of the later things that are to come; </t><t>There will be for them no remembrance </t><t>By generations who will come after them.</t><br/>[‡.	ƒ1 ú
<t>Is there anything of which it can be said, </t><t>"See this, it is new"? </t><t>It has already existed for <n>[the vast]</n> ages <n>[of time recorded or unrecorded]</n> </t><t>Which were before us.</t><br/>B‡-	‚ ú	<t>That which has been is that which will be <n>[again]</n>, </t><t>And that which has been done is that which will be done again. </t><t>So there is nothing new under the sun.</t><br/>C‡,	ƒ ú<t>All things are wearisome <i>and</i> all words are frail; </t><t>Man cannot express it. </t><t>The eye is not satisfied with seeing, </t><t>Nor is the ear filled with hearing.</t><br/>‡+	‚9 ú<t>All the rivers flow into the sea, </t><t>Yet the sea is not full. </t><t>To the place where the rivers flow, </t><t>There they flow again.</t><br/>D‡*	ƒ ú<t>The wind blows toward the south, </t><t>Then circles toward the north; </t><t>The wind circles <i>and</i> swirls endlessly, </t><t>And on its circular course the wind returns.</t><br/>p‡)	[ ú<t>Also, the sun rises and the sun sets; </t><t>And hurries to the place where it rises again.</t><br/>l‡(	S ú<t>One generation goes and another generation comes, </t><t>But the earth remains forever.</t><br/>	‡'	‚
 ú<pb/><t>What advantage does man have from all his work </t><t>Which he does<f>[1]</f> under the sun (while earthbound)?</t><br/>u‡&	ƒe ú<pb/><t>"Vanity of vanities," says the Preacher. </t><t>"Vanity of vanities! All <n>[that is done without God's guidance]</n> is vanity <n>[futile, meaninglessâ€”a wisp of smoke, a vapor that vanishes, merely chasing the wind]</n>."</t>S‡%		# ú<pb/><t>The words of the Preacher, the son of David, king in Jerusalem.</t>‡${ ð<t>Give her of the product of her hands, </t><t>And let her own works praise her in the gates <n>[of the city]</n>.</t>|‡#ƒq ð<t>Charm <i>and</i> grace are deceptive, and <n>[superficial]</n> beauty is vain, </t><t>But a woman who fears the LORD <n>[reverently worshiping, obeying, serving, and trusting Him with awe-filled respect]</n>, she shall be praised.</t><br/>8‡"‚i ð<t>"Many daughters have done nobly, <i>and</i> well <n>[with the strength of character that is steadfast in goodness]</n>, </t><t><f>[6]</f> But you excel them all."</t><br/>‡!‚7 ð<t>Her children rise up and call her blessed (happy, prosperous, to be admired); </t><t>Her husband also, and he praises her, <i>saying</i>,</t><br/>v‡ e ð<t>She looks well to how things go in her household, </t><t>And does not eat the bread of idleness.</t><br/>.‡‚U ð<t>She opens her mouth in <n>[skillful and godly]</n> wisdom, </t><t>And the teaching of kindness is on her tongue <n>[giving counsel and instruction]</n>.</t><br/>D‡ƒ ð<t>Strength and dignity are her clothing <i>and</i> her position is strong and secure; </t><t>And she smiles at the future <n>[knowing that she and her family are prepared]</n>.</t><br/>y‡k ð<t>She makes <n>[fine]</n> linen garments and sells them; </t><t>And supplies sashes to the merchants.</t><br/>z‡m ð<t>Her husband is known in the <n>[city's]</n> gates, </t><t>When he sits among the elders of the land.</t><br/>+‡‚O ð<t>She makes for herself coverlets, cushions, <i>and</i> rugs of tapestry. </t><t>Her clothing is linen, pure <i>and</i> fine, and purple <n>[wool]</n>.</t><br/>‡‚1 ð<t>She does not fear the snow for her household, </t><t>For all in her household are clothed in <n>[expensive]</n> scarlet <n>[wool]</n>.</t><br/>‡} ð<t>She opens <i>and</i> extends her hand to the poor, </t><t>And she reaches out her filled hands to the needy.</t><br/>&‡‚E ð<t>She stretches out her hands to the<f>[5]</f> distaff, </t><t>And her hands hold the spindle <n>[as she spins wool into thread for clothing]</n>.</t><br/>3‡‚_ ð<t>She sees that her gain is good; </t><t>Her lamp does not go out, but it burns continually through the night <n>[she is prepared for whatever lies ahead]</n>.</t><br/> ‡‚9 ð<t>She equips herself with strength <n>[spiritual, mental, and physical fitness for her God-given task]</n> </t><t>And makes her arms strong.</t><br/>>‡‚u ð<t>She considers a field before she buys <i>or</i> accepts it <n>[expanding her business prudently]</n>; </t><t>With her profits she plants fruitful vines in her vineyard.</t><br/>
