<t>"I am my beloved's, </t><t>And his desire is for me.</t><br/>\‰eƒ1	<t>'And your kisses like the best wine!'" </t><pb/><i>(The Shulammite Bride)</i> <t>"It goes <i>down</i> smoothly <i>and</i> sweetly for my beloved, </t><t>Gliding gently over his lips while he sleeps.</t><br/>M‰dƒ<t>"I said, 'I will climb the palm tree; </t><t>I will grasp its branches. </t><t>Let your breasts be like clusters of the grapevine, </t><t>And the fragrance of your breath like apples,</t><br/>‰c<t>"Your stature is like that of a palm tree </t><t>And your breasts <i>like its</i> clusters <n>[of dates]</n>.</t><br/>h‰bI<t>"How beautiful and how delightful you are, </t><t>My love, with all your delights!</t><br/>;‰a‚o<t>"Your head crowns you like Mount Carmel, </t><t>And the flowing hair of your head like purple threads; </t><t>I, the king, am held captive by your<f>[1]</f> tresses.</t><br/>h‰`ƒI<t>"Your neck is like a tower of ivory, </t><t>Your eyes the <n>[sparkling]</n> pools of Heshbon </t><t>By the gate of Bath-rabbim. </t><t>Your nose is like the tower of Lebanon </t><t>Which looks toward Damascus.</t><br/>Z‰_-<t>"Your two breasts are like two fawns, </t><t>The twins of a gazelle.</t><br/>!‰^‚;<t>"Your navel is a round goblet </t><t>Which never lacks mixed wine. </t><t>Your belly is like a heap of wheat </t><t>Surrounded with lilies.</t><br/>9‰]	‚m<pb/><t>"How beautiful are your feet in sandals, </t><t>O prince's daughter! </t><t>The curves of your hips are like jewels, </t><t>The work of the hands of an artist.</t><br/>o‰\ƒW
<pb/><i>(The Chorus)</i> <t>"Return, return, O Shulammite; </t><t>Return, return, that we may gaze at you." </t><pb/><i>(The Bridegroom)</i> <t>"Why should you gaze at the Shulammite, </t><t>As at the dance of the two armies?</t>,‰[‚Q<t>"Before I was aware <n>[of what was happening]</n>, my desire had brought me </t><t>Into the area of the princes of my people <n>[the king's retinue]</n>."</t>B‰Z‚}<t>"I went down to the orchard of nut trees </t><t>To see the flowers of the valley, </t><t>To see whether the grapevine had budded </t><t>And the pomegranates were in flower.</t><br/>O‰Yƒ
<pb/><t>'Who is this that looks down like the dawn, </t><t>Fair <i>and</i> beautiful as the full moon, </t><t>Clear <i>and</i> pure as the sun, </t><t>As majestic as an army with banners?'</t><br/>‚U‰X…#	<t>But my dove, my perfect one, stands alone <n>[above them all]</n>; </t><t>She is her mother's only <i>daughter</i>; </t><t>She is the pure <i>child</i> of the one who bore her. </t><t>The maidens saw her and called her blessed <i>and</i> happy, </t><t>The queens and the concubines also, and they praised her, <i>saying</i>,</t>q‰W[<t>"There are sixty queens and eighty<f>[2]</f> concubines, </t><t>And maidens without number;</t><br/>`‰V9<t>"Your temples are like a slice of the pomegranate </t><t>Behind your veil.</t><br/>:‰U‚m<t>"Your teeth are like a flock of ewes </t><t>Which have come up from their washing, </t><t>All of which bear twins, </t><t>And not one among them has lost her young.</t><br/>‚‰T„<t>"Turn your <n>[flashing]</n> eyes away from me, </t><t>For they have confused <i>and</i> overcome me; </t><t>Your hair is like <n>[the shimmering black fleece of]</n> a flock of <n>[Arabian]</n> goats </t><t>That have descended from Mount Gilead.</t><br/>4‰S‚a<pb/><i>(The Bridegroom)</i> <t>"You are as beautiful as<f>[1]</f> Tirzah, my darling, </t><t>As lovely as Jerusalem, </t><t>As majestic as an army with banners!</t><br/>s‰R_<t>"I am my beloved's and my beloved is mine, </t><t>He who feeds <i>his flock</i> among the lilies."</t>H‰Qƒ	<pb/><i>(The Shulammite Bride)</i> <t>"My beloved has gone down to his garden, </t><t>To the beds of balsam, </t><t>To feed <i>his flock</i> in the gardens </t><t>And gather lilies.</t><br/>D‰P	ƒ<pb/><pb/><i>(The Chorus)</i> <t>"Where has your beloved gone, </t><t>O most beautiful among women? </t><t>Where is your beloved hiding himself, </t><t>That we may seek him with you?"</t>E‰Oƒ<t>"His mouth is <i>full of</i> sweetness; </t><t>Yes, he is altogether lovely <i>and</i> desirable. </t><t>This is my beloved and this is my friend, </t><t>O daughters of Jerusalem."</t>R‰Nƒ<t>"His legs are <n>[strong and steady]</n> pillars of alabaster </t><t>Set upon pedestals of fine gold. </t><t>His appearance is like Lebanon, </t><t>Stately <i>and</i> choice as the cedars.</t><br/>‰M‚#<t>"His hands are rods of gold </t><t>Set with beryl; </t><t>His abdomen is a figure of carved ivory </t><t>Inlaid with sapphires.</t><br/>)‰L‚K
