<pb/>He who digs a pit <n>[for others]</n> may fall into it, and a serpent may bite him who breaks through a <n>[stone]</n> wall.cˆ]? ú
I have seen slaves <i>riding</i> on horses and princes walking like slaves on the ground.nˆ\U ú
folly is set in many exalted places <i>and</i> in great dignity while the rich sit in humble places.iˆ[K ú
<pb/>There is an evil I have seen under the sun, like an error which proceeds from the rulerâ€”)ˆZ‚K ú
If the temper of the ruler rises against you, do not leave your post <n>[showing resistance]</n>, because composure <i>and</i> calmness prevent great offenses.ˆY‚7 ú
Even when a fool walks along the road, his <n>[common]</n> sense <i>and</i> good judgment fail him and he demonstrates to everyone that he is a fool.DˆXƒ ú
A wise man's heart <i>turns him</i> toward the right <n>[which is the way of blessing]</n>, but a fool's heart <i>turns him</i> toward the left <n>[which is the way of condemnation]</n>.ˆW	‚9 ú
<pb/>Dead flies make the oil of the perfumer give off a foul odor; so a little foolishness <n>[in one who is esteemed]</n> outweighs wisdom and honor.RˆV ú	Wisdom is better than weapons of war, but one sinner destroys much good.tˆUa ú	The words of wise men heard in quietness are <i>better</i> than the shouting of one who rules among fools.ˆTw ú	But I say that wisdom is better than strength, though the poor man's wisdom is despised and his words are not heeded.ˆS‚ ú	But there was found in it a poor wise man, and by his wisdom he rescued the city. Yet no man <n>[seriously]</n> remembered that poor man.
ˆR‚ ú	There was a little city with few men in it and a great king came against it and besieged it and built great battlements against it.kˆQO ú	
<pb/>This <n>[illustration of]</n> wisdom I have also seen under the sun, and great it was to me:`ˆPƒ9 ú	For man also does not know his time <n>[of death]</n>; like fish caught in a treacherous net, and birds caught in the snare, so the sons of men are ensnared in an evil time when a dark cloud suddenly falls on them.‚ˆO„ ú	<pb/>I again saw under the sun that the race is not to the swift and the battle is not to the strong, and neither is bread to the wise nor riches to those of intelligence <i>and</i> understanding nor favor to men of ability; but time and chance overtake them all.HˆNƒ	 ú	
Whatever your hand finds to do, do it with all your might; for there is no activity or planning or knowledge or wisdom in Sheol (the nether world, the place of the dead) where you are going.‚ˆMƒ ú		Live joyfully with the wife whom you love all the days of your fleeting life which He has given you under the sunâ€”all the days of vanity <i>and</i> futility. For this is your reward in life and in your work in which you have labored under the sun.ˆL‚ ú	Let your clothes always be white <n>[with purity]</n>, and do not let the oil <n>[of gladness]</n> be lacking on your head._ˆKƒ7 ú	<pb/>Go <i>your way</i>, eat your bread with joy and drink your wine with a cheerful heart <n>[if you are righteous, wise, and in the hands of God]</n>; for God has already approved <i>and</i> accepted your works.1ˆJ‚[ ú	Indeed their love, their hatred and their zeal have already perished, and they will no longer have a share <n>[in this age]</n> in anything that is done under the sun.!ˆI‚; ú	For the living know that they will die; but the dead know nothing, and they no longer have a reward <n>[here]</n>, for the memory of them is forgotten.ˆH‚ ú	<n>[There is no exemption,]</n> but whoever is joined with all the living, has hope; surely a live dog is better than a dead lion._ˆGƒ7 ú	This evil is in all that is done under the sun, that one fate comes to all. Also, the hearts of the sons of men are full of evil, and madness is in their hearts while they live, and afterwards they go to the dead.‚SˆF… ú	<pb/>It is the same for all. There is one fate for the righteous and for the wicked; for the good, for the clean and for the unclean; for the man who offers sacrifices and for the one who does not sacrifice. As the good man is, so is the sinner; as he who swears <i>an oath</i> is, so is he who is afraid to swear <i>an oath</i>.‚ˆE	„ ú	<pb/>For I have taken all this to heart, exploring <i>and</i> examining it all, how the righteous (those in right standing with God) and the wise and their deeds are in the hands of God. No man knows whether it will be love or hatred; anything awaits him.‚+ˆD„O úand I saw all the work of God, I <i>concluded</i> that man cannot discover the work that is done under the sun. Even though man may labor in seeking, he will not discover; and <n>[more than that]</n>, though a wise man thinks <i>and</i> claims he knows, he will not be able to find it out.7ˆC‚g ú<pb/>When I applied my mind to know wisdom and to see the activities <n>[of mankind]</n> that take place upon the earthâ€”how some men seem to sleep neither day nor nightâ€”‚ˆB„ úThen I commended pleasure <i>and</i> enjoyment, because a man <n>[without God]</n> has no better thing under the sun than to eat and to drink and to be merry, for this will stand by him in his toil through the days of his life which God has given him under the sun.
