	qN¶ï/Œ§ÞA’ü˜                                                                        `m9"&Hezekiah also had said, "What is the sign that I will go up to the house of the LORD?"l‚"&<pb/>Now Isaiah had said, "Have them take a cake of figs and rub it <n>[as an ointment]</n> on the inflamed spot, that he may recover."*k‚M"&<t>"The LORD is ready to save me; </t><t>Therefore we will play my songs on stringed instruments </t><t>All the days of our lives at the house of the LORD."</t>j‚)"&<t>"It is the living who give praise <i>and</i> thanks to You, as I do today; </t><t>A father tells his sons about Your faithfulness.</t><br/>Diƒ"&<t>"For Sheol cannot praise <i>or</i> thank You, </t><t>Death cannot praise You <i>and</i> rejoice in You; </t><t>Those who go down to the pit cannot hope for Your faithfulness.</t><br/>`hƒ9"&<t>"Indeed, it was for my own well-being that I had such bitterness; </t><t>But You have loved back my life from the pit of nothingness (destruction), </t><t>For You have cast all my sins behind Your back.</t><br/>g‚5"&<t>"O Lord, by <i>these</i> things men live, </t><t>And in all these is the life of my spirit; </t><t>Restore me to health and let me live!</t><br/>;f‚o"&<pb/><t>"What shall I say? </t><t>For He has spoken to me, and He Himself has done it; </t><t>I will wander aimlessly all my years because of the bitterness of my soul.</t><br/>Be‚}"&<t>"Like a swallow, like a crane, so I chirp; </t><t>I coo like a dove. </t><t>My eyes look wistfully upward; </t><t>O Lord, I am oppressed, take my side <i>and</i> be my security.</t>d‚"&
<t>"I lay down until morning. </t><t>Like a lion, so He breaks all my bones; </t><t>From day until night You bring me to an end.</t><br/>‚c„5"&<t>"My dwelling (body) is pulled up and removed from me like a shepherd's tent; </t><t>I have rolled up my life as a weaver <n>[rolls up the finished web]</n>. </t><t>He cuts me free from the warp <n>[of the loom]</n>; </t><t>From day to night You bring me to an end.</t><br/>%b‚C"&<t>I said, "I will not see the LORD </t><t>The LORD in the land of the living; </t><t>I will no longer see man among the inhabitants of the world.</t><br/>@a‚y"&
<pb/><t>I said, "In mid-life </t><t>I am to go through the gates of Sheol (the place of the dead), </t><t>I am to be summoned, <i>deprived of</i> the remainder of my years."</t><br/>`‚"&	<pb/><t><i>This is the</i> writing of Hezekiah king of Judah after he had been sick and had recovered from his illness:</t>‚_„5"&Listen carefully, I will turn the shadow on the stairway <n>[denoting the time of day]</n> ten steps backward, <i>the shadow</i> on the stairway (<f>[1]</f> sundial) of Ahaz." And the sunlight went ten steps backward on the stairway where it had <n>[previously]</n> gone down.q^["&<pb/>"This shall be the sign to you from the LORD, that the LORD will do this thing that He has spoken:]{"&I will rescue you and this city from the hand of the king of Assyria; and I will defend this city <n>[Jerusalem]</n>."'E\ƒ"&"Go and say to Hezekiah, 'For the LORD, the God of David your father says this, "I have heard your prayer, I have seen your tears; listen carefully, I will add fifteen years to your life.?[y"&<pb/>Then the word of the LORD came to Isaiah, saying,rZƒ]"&and said, "Please, O LORD, just remember how I have walked before You in faithfulness <i>and</i> truth, and with a whole heart <n>[absolutely devoted to You]</n>, and have done what is good in Your sight." And Hezekiah wept greatly.KY"&Then Hezekiah turned his face to the wall and prayed to the LORD,‚X	„/"&<pb/>In those days Hezekiah <n>[king of Judah]</n> became sick and was at the point of death. And Isaiah the prophet, the son of Amoz, came to him and said, "For the LORD says this, 'Set your house in order <i>and</i> prepare a will, for you shall die; you will not live.'"‚ Wƒy"%&It came to pass as he was worshiping in the house of Nisroch his god, that Adrammelech and Sharezer his sons killed him with the sword; and they escaped into the land of Ararat <n>[in Armenia]</n>. And Esarhaddon his son became king in his place.TV!"%%So Sennacherib king of Assyria departed and returned and lived at Nineveh.RUƒ"%$And the<f>[5]</f> angel of the LORD went out and<f>[6]</f> struck 185,000 in the camp of the Assyrians; and when the <n>[surviving]</n> men got up early the next morning, <i>they saw</i> all the dead.iTK"%#For I will defend this city to save it, for My own sake and for the sake of My servant David.'"{So"%"By the way that he came, by the same way he will return, and he will not come into this city,' declares the LORD.KRƒ"%!<pb/>"Therefore, the LORD says this concerning the king of Assyria, 'He will not come into this city or shoot an arrow there, or come before it with shield, or raise an assault ramp against it.	Q‚"% For out of Jerusalem will come a remnant and from Mount Zion a band of survivors. The zeal of the LORD of hosts will do this."'jPM"%The surviving remnant of the house of Judah will again take root downward and bear fruit upward.‚&O„E"%<pb/>"This shall be the sign <n>[of these things]</n> to you <n>[Hezekiah]</n>: you are to eat this year<f>[4]</f> what grows of itself, and in the second year that which springs from the same, and in the third year you are to sow and harvest, and plant vineyards and eat their fruit.lNƒQ"%<t>"Because your raging against Me </t><t>And your arrogance has come up to My ears, </t><t>I will put My hook in your nose </t><t>And My bridle in your<f>[3]</f> mouth, </t><t>And I will turn you back by the way you came.</t>'M‚G"%<t>"But I know your sitting down </t><t>And your going out and your coming in <n>[every detail of your life]</n>, </t><t>And your raging against Me.</t><br/>‚L„3"%<t>"Therefore their inhabitants had little power, </t><t>They were terrorized and shamed; </t><t>They were like the grass of the field and the green vegetation, </t><t>Like grass on the housetops <i>and</i> like a field <n>[of grain]</n> scorched before it is grown.</t><br/>‚/K„W"%<t>"Have you not heard <n>[says the God of Israel]</n> </t><t>That I did it long ago, </t><t>That I planned it in ancient times? </t><t>Now I have brought it to pass, </t><t>That you <n>[king of Assyria]</n> would <n>[be My instrument to]</n> turn fortified cities into ruinous heaps.</t><br/>1J‚["%<t>'I dug <i>wells</i> and drank <n>[foreign]</n> waters, </t><t>And with the sole of my feet I dried up </t><t>All the canals <n>[of the Nile]</n> of Egypt.'</t><br/>ƒI†"%<t>"Through your servants you have taunted <i>and</i> defied the Lord, </t><t>And you have said, 'With my many chariots I have gone up to the heights of the mountains, </t><t>To the remotest parts of Lebanon. </t><t>I cut down its tallest cedars and its choicest cypress trees; </t><t>And I will go to its remotest height, its most luxuriant <i>and</i> thickest forest.</t><br/>@H‚y"%<t>"Whom have you taunted and blasphemed? </t><t>And against whom have you raised your voice </t><t>And haughtily lifted up your eyes? </t><t>Against the Holy One of Israel!</t><br/>‚G„"%<t>this is the word that the LORD has spoken against him: </t><br/><t>"She has shown contempt for you and mocked you, </t><t>The Virgin Daughter of Zion (Jerusalem); </t><t>She has shaken her head behind you, </t><t>The Daughter of Jerusalem!</t><br/>5F‚c"%Then Isaiah son of Amoz sent <i>word</i> to Hezekiah, saying, "For the LORD, the God of Israel says this, 'Because you have prayed to Me about Sennacherib king of Assyria,&E‚E"%Now, O LORD our God, save us from his hand so that all the kingdoms of the earth may know <i>and</i> fully realize that You alone, LORD, are<f>[2]</f> God."1D‚["%and have cast the gods <n>[of those peoples]</n> into the fire, for they were not gods but the work of men's hands, wood and stone. Therefore they have destroyed them.jCM"%It is true, O LORD, that the kings of Assyria have laid waste all the countries and their lands,.B‚U"%Incline Your ear, O LORD, and hear; open Your eyes, O LORD, and see; and hear all the words of Sennacherib that he has sent to taunt <i>and</i> defy the living God.4A‚a"%"O LORD of hosts, God of Israel, who is enthroned <i>above</i> the cherubim, You are the God, You alone, of all the kingdoms of the earth. You have made heaven and earth.0@["%And Hezekiah prayed to the LORD saying,?‚7"%Then Hezekiah took the letter from the hand of the messengers and read it, and he went up to the house of the LORD and spread it out before the LORD.4>‚a"%
Where is the king of Hamath, the king of Arpad <n>[of northern Syria]</n>, the king of the city of Sepharvaim, <n>[the king of]</n> Hena, or <n>[the king of]</n> Ivvah?'"2=‚]"%Did the gods of the nations which my fathers destroyed rescue themâ€”<f>[1]</f> Gozan, Haran <n>[of Mesopotamia]</n>, Rezeph, and the sons of Eden who were in Telassar?
