J	<pb/>On that day <n>[swift]</n> messengers will go from Me in ships to frighten the careless <i>and</i> unsuspecting Ethiopians, and there will be anguish <i>and</i> trembling in them as in the day of <n>[judgment for]</n> Egypt; for behold, it is coming!"9¥]‚kJ<t>"And they will know <n>[without any doubt]</n> that I am the LORD, </t><t>When I have set a fire in Egypt </t><t>And all her helpers are shattered <i>and</i> destroyed.</t>L¥\ƒJ<t>"And they will be desolate </t><t>In the midst of countries that are desolated; </t><t>And her cities will be </t><t>Among cities that are devastated <n>[by plunder and slavery]</n>.</t><br/>‚/¥[„WJ<pb/><t>'Thus says the LORD, </t><t>"Those who uphold <i>and</i> support Egypt will fall </t><t>And the pride of her power will come down; </t><t>From Migdol <n>[in the north]</n> to Syene <n>[in the south]</n> </t><t>They will fall within her by the sword," </t><t>Says the Lord GOD.</t><br/>Y¥Zƒ+J<pb/><t>Ethiopia (Cush), Put, Lud, all<f>[1]</f> Arabia, Libya (Cub) and the people of the land of the covenant <n>[the Jews who had taken refuge in Egypt]</n> will fall with the Egyptians by the sword."</t>‚¥Y„7J<t>"A sword will come upon Egypt, </t><t>And anguish <i>and</i> trembling will be in Ethiopia (Cush), </t><t>When the slain fall in Egypt </t><t>And they <n>[of Babylon]</n> carry away her great mass of people <i>and</i> her riches </t><t>And her foundations are torn down.</t>"¥X‚=J<t>"For the day is near, </t><t>Even the day of the LORD is near, </t><t>It will be a cloudy day; </t><t>A time <i>of doom</i> for the nations.</t><br/>t¥WaJ<t>"Son of man, prophesy and say, 'Thus says the Lord GOD, </t><br/><t>"Wail, 'Alas for the day!'</t><br/>;¥V	sJ<pb/>The word of the LORD came again to me, saying,]¥Uƒ3J<pb/>"In that day I will make a horn sprout for the house of Israel, and I will open your mouth among them, and they will know <n>[without any doubt]</n> that I am the LORD <n>[when I renew their strength]</n>."¥T‚JI have given him the land of Egypt for the hard work which he did <n>[against Tyre]</n>, because they did it for Me," says the Lord GOD.k¥SƒOJTherefore thus says the Lord GOD, "Behold, I will give the land of Egypt to Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon. And he will carry off her wealth and capture her spoil and seize her plunder; and it shall be the wages for his army.ƒ`¥R‡9J"Son of man, Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon made his army<f>[3]</f> work hard <n>[at My command]</n> against Tyre; every <n>[soldier's]</n> head became<f>[4]</f> bald and every shoulder was worn <i>and</i> peeled <n>[from carrying loads of dirt and stones for siege works]</n>. Yet he had no wages from Tyre <n>[in proportion to the time and labor expended during the siege]</n>, either for himself or his army, for the work that he had done against it <n>[for Me]</n>."=¥Q‚sJ<pb/>In the twenty-seventh year <n>[after King Jehoiachin was taken to Babylon]</n>, in the first <i>month</i>, on the first of the month, the word of the LORD came to me, saying,s¥Pƒ_JAnd Egypt will never again have the confidence of the house of Israel; their wickedness will be remembered whenever Israel looks toward them <n>[for help]</n>. Then they will know <n>[without any doubt]</n> that I am the Lord GOD."'"B¥O‚}JIt will be the lowliest of the kingdoms, and it will<f>[2]</f> never again exalt itself above the nations; I will diminish the Egyptians so they will never again rule over the nations.C¥N‚JI will reverse the fortunes of Egypt <n>[as I will that of Israel]</n> and cause them to return to the land of Pathros, the land of their origin, and there they will be a lowly kingdom.¥M‚J
<pb/>'For thus says the Lord GOD, "At the end of forty years I will gather the Egyptians from the nations among whom they were scattered.‚!¥L„;JSo I will make the land of Egypt a desolation <n>[plundered and ruined]</n> among desolated lands; and her cities, among cities that are laid waste, will be desolate forty years. I will scatter the Egyptians among the nations and <n>[I will]</n> disperse them through the lands."¥K}JNo man's foot will pass through it, no animal's foot will pass through it, and it will not be inhabited for forty years.‚%¥J„CJ
behold (hear this), therefore, I am against you and against your rivers (the Nile, its tributaries), and I will make the land of Egypt a complete waste and a desolation, from <n>[northern]</n> Migdol to <n>[southern]</n><f>[1]</f> Syene, even as far as the border of Ethiopia (Cush).M¥IƒJ	<t>and the land of Egypt will be a desolation and a wasteland. And they will know <n>[without any doubt]</n> that I am the LORD. </t><br/>"Because you said, 'The Nile is mine and I have made it,'¥H‚	J<pb/>'Therefore thus says the Lord GOD, "Behold, I am going to bring a sword on you and cut off (destroy) both man and animal,>¥G‚uJ<t>"When they (Israel) grasped you (Egypt) by the hand, </t><t>You broke and tore apart their hands; </t><t>When they leaned on you, </t><t>You broke and strained their backs."</t>[¥Fƒ/J<t>"And all the inhabitants of Egypt will know <n>[without any doubt]</n> that I am the LORD, </t><t>Because they have been <n>[only]</n> a staff made of <n>[fragile]</n> reeds to the house of Israel.</t><br/>‚¥E„)J<t>"I will abandon you to the wilderness, you and all the fish of your rivers; </t><t>You will fall on the open field; and you will not be gathered up or buried. </t><t>I have given you as food to the <n>[wild]</n> animals of the earth and the birds of the sky.</t><br/>‚¥Dƒ{J<t>"I will put hooks in your jaws </t><t>And <n>[I will]</n> make the fish of your rivers stick to your scales. </t><t>And I will pull you up from the midst of your rivers, </t><t>And all the fish of your rivers will stick to your scales.</t><br/>‚¥C„!J<t>Speak and say, 'Thus says the Lord GOD, </t><br/><t>"Behold, I am against you, Pharaoh king of Egypt, </t><t>The great monster that lies in the midst of his rivers, </t><t><n>[Boastfully]</n> declaring, 'My Nile is my own, and I have made it for myself.'</t><br/>r¥B]J"Son of man, set your face toward Pharaoh king of Egypt, and prophesy against him and against all Egypt.E¥A	ƒJ<pb/>In the tenth year <n>[of the captivity of King Jehoiachin by the king of Babylon]</n>, in the tenth <i>month</i>, on the twelfth of the month, the word of the LORD came to me, saying,‚¥@„JThey shall live in it securely; and they will build houses, plant vineyards and live securely when I execute judgment on all those around them who despise them. Then they will know <n>[with clarity and confidence]</n> that I am the LORD their God."'"}¥?ƒsJ'Thus says the Lord GOD, "When I gather the house of Israel from the nations among whom they are scattered, and I manifest my Holiness in them in the sight of the nations, then they will live in their own land which I gave to My servant Jacob.`¥>ƒ9J<pb/>And there will no longer be a briar or a painful thorn to prick the house of Israel from all those around them who treated them with contempt; then they will know <n>[with clarity]</n> that I am the Lord GOD."‚¥=ƒ}J<t>"For I will send virulent disease to her </t><t>And blood into her streets, </t><t>And the wounded will fall in her midst </t><t>By the sword upon her from every side, </t><t>And they shall know <n>[without any doubt]</n> that I am the LORD.</t>‚_¥<…7J<t>and say, 'Thus says the Lord GOD, </t><br/><t>"Behold, I am against you, O Sidon, </t><t>And I will show My glory <i>and</i> be glorified in your midst. </t><t>Then they will know <n>[by personal experience]</n> that I am the LORD when I bring judgment <i>and</i> punishment on her, </t><t>And I will manifest My holiness in her.</t><br/>K¥;J"Son of man, set your face toward Sidon, and prophesy against her7¥:iJAgain the word of the LORD came to me, saying,>¥9‚uJ<t>"All the peoples (nations) who knew you </t><t>Are appalled at you; </t><t>You have come to a horrible <i>and</i> terrifying end </t><t>And will forever cease to be."'"</t><br/>‚l¥8…QJ<t>"You profaned your sanctuaries </t><t>By the great quantity of your sins <i>and</i> the enormity of your guilt, </t><t>By the unrighteousness of your trade. </t><t>Therefore I have brought forth a fire from your midst; </t><t>It has consumed you, </t><t>And I have reduced you to ashes on the earth </t><t>In the sight of all who look at you.</t><br/>{¥7ƒoJ<t>"Your heart was proud <i>and</i> arrogant because of your beauty; </t><t>You destroyed your wisdom for the sake of your splendor. </t><t>I cast you to the ground; </t><t>I lay you before kings, </t><t>That they might look at you.</t><br/>‚h¥6…IJ<t>"Through the abundance of your commerce </t><t>You were internally filled with lawlessness <i>and</i> violence, </t><t>And you sinned; </t><t>Therefore I have cast you out as a profane <i>and</i> unholy thing </t><t>From the mountain of God. </t><t>And I have destroyed you, O covering cherub, </t><t>From the midst of the stones of fire.</t><br/>¥5‚/J<t>"You were blameless in your ways </t><t>From the day you were created </t><t>Until unrighteousness <i>and</i> evil were found in you.</t><br/>o¥4ƒWJ<t>"You were the anointed cherub who covers <i>and</i> protects, </t><t>And I placed you there. </t><t>You were on the holy mountain of God; </t><t>You walked in the midst of the stones of fire <n>[sparkling jewels]</n>.</t><br/>ƒ.¥3†UJ
<t>"You were in<f>[1]</f> Eden, the garden of God; </t><t>Every precious stone was your covering: </t><t>The ruby, the topaz, and the diamond; </t><t>The beryl, the onyx, and the jasper; </t><t>The lapis lazuli, the turquoise, and the emerald; </t><t>And the gold, the workmanship of your<f>[2]</f> settings and your sockets, </t><t>Was in you. </t><t>They were prepared </t><t>On the day that you were created.</t><br/>‚$¥2„AJ<t>"Son of man, take up a dirge (funeral poem to be sung) for the king of Tyre and say to him, 'Thus says the Lord GOD, </t><br/><t>"You had the full measure of perfection <i>and</i> the finishing touch <n>[of completeness]</n>, </t><t>Full of wisdom and perfect in beauty.</t><br/><¥1sJ<pb/>Again the word of the LORD came to me, saying,"¥0‚=J
<t>'You will die the death of the uncircumcised <n>[barbarian]</n> </t><t>By the hand of strangers, </t><t>For I have spoken!' says the Lord GOD."'"</t>o¥/ƒWJ	<t>'Will you still say, "I am a god," </t><t>In the presence of him who kills you? </t><t>But you are <n>[only]</n> a man <n>[made of earth]</n> and not God, </t><t>In the hands of those who wound <i>and</i> profane you.</t><br/>+¥.‚OJ<t>'They will bring you down to the pit <n>[of destruction]</n>, </t><t>And you will die the death of all those who die </t><t>In the heart of the seas.</t><br/>‚¥-„!J<t>Therefore, behold, I will bring strangers (Babylonians) upon you, </t><t>The most ruthless <i>and</i> violent of the nations. </t><t>And they will draw their swords </t><t>Against the beauty of your wisdom <n>[O Tyre]</n> </t><t>And defile your splendor.</t><br/>M¥,ƒJ<pb/><t>Therefore thus says the Lord GOD, </t><br/><t>'Because you have imagined your mind <n>[to be]</n> </t><t>Like the mind of God <n>[having thoughts and plans like God Himself]</n>,</t><br/>:¥+‚mJ<t>By your great wisdom and by your trade </t><t>You have increased your riches <i>and</i> power, </t><t>And your heart is proud <i>and</i> arrogant because of your wealth;</t>P¥*ƒJ<t>With your <n>[own]</n> wisdom and with your <n>[own]</n> understanding </t><t>You have acquired your riches <i>and</i> power </t><t>And have brought gold and silver into your treasuries;</t><br/>¥)‚-J<t>Behold, you are <n>[imagining yourself]</n> wiser than Daniel; </t><t>There is no secret <n>[you think]</n> that is hidden from you;</t><br/>ƒ^¥(‡5J<t>"Son of man, say to the prince of Tyre, 'Thus says the Lord GOD, </t><br/><t>"Because your heart is lifted up </t><t>And you have said <i>and</i> thought, 'I am a god, </t><t>I sit in the seat of the gods </t><t>In the heart of the seas'; </t><t>Yet you are <n>[only]</n> a man <n>[weak, feeble, made of earth]</n> and not God, </t><t>Though you <n>[imagine yourself to be more than mortal and]</n> think your mind is as <n>[wise as]</n> the mind of Godâ€”</t><br/>;¥'	sJ<pb/>The word of the LORD came again to me, saying,L¥&ƒJ$<t>'The merchants among the people hiss at you <n>[with malicious joy]</n>; </t><t>You have become a horror <i>and</i> a source of terrors. </t><t>You will cease to be<f>[6]</f> forever.'"'"</t>X¥%ƒ)J#<t>'All the inhabitants of the coastlands </t><t>Are aghast <i>and</i> appalled at you, </t><t>And their kings are horribly frightened <i>and</i> shudder; </t><t>Their faces twitch <i>and</i> pale.</t><br/>&¥$‚EJ"<t>'Now you are shattered by the seas </t><t>In the depths of the waters; </t><t>Your merchandise and all your crew </t><t>Have gone down with you.</t><br/>u¥#ƒcJ!<t>'When your wares went out to the sea, </t><t>You met the desire, <i>and</i> the demand, <i>and</i> the necessities of many people; </t><t>You enriched the kings of the earth </t><t>With your abundant wealth and merchandise.</t><br/>h¥"ƒIJ <t>"In their wailing they will take up a dirge (funeral poem to be sung) for you </t><t>And sing a dirge for you: </t><t>'Who is like Tyre, </t><t>Like her who is silent <n>[destroyed]</n> in the midst of the sea?</t><br/>f¥!ƒEJ<t>"And they will make themselves <n>[completely]</n> bald for you </t><t>And wrap themselves in sackcloth; </t><t>And in bitterness of soul they will weep for you </t><t>With bitter mourning <i>and</i> wailing.</t><br/>Q¥ ƒJ<t>And they will make their voice heard <n>[as they wail loudly]</n> over you </t><t>And they will cry bitterly. </t><t>They will throw dust on their heads; </t><t>They will wallow in ashes.</t><br/>-¥‚SJ<t>"All who handle the oar, </t><t>The mariners and all the pilots of the sea </t><t>Will come down from their ships; </t><t>They will stand on the shore,</t><br/>*¥‚MJ<t>"The pasture lands <i>and</i> the countryside will shake </t><t>At the <n>[piercing]</n> sound of the <n>[hopeless, wailing]</n> cry of your pilots.</t><br/>‚G¥…J<t>"Your riches, your wares, your merchandise </t><t>Your oarsmen and your pilots, </t><t>Your caulkers, your dealers in merchandise, </t><t>And all your men of war who are with you, </t><t>With all your company that is in your midst, </t><t>Will sink in the heart of the seas </t><t>On the day of your ruin.</t><br/>>¥‚uJ<pb/><t>"Your rowers have brought you </t><t>Into great <i>and</i> deep waters; </t><t>The east wind has broken <i>and</i> shipwrecked you </t><t>In the heart of the seas.</t><br/>b¥ƒ=J<t>The ships of Tarshish were the caravans for your merchandise, </t><br/><t>And you <n>[Tyre]</n> were replenished and very glorious <n>[heavily laden with an imposing fleet]</n> </t><t>In the heart of the seas.</t>‚¥„%JThey traded with you in choice fabrics <i>and</i> garments, in clothes of blue <i>or</i> violet and embroidered work, and in <n>[treasures of]</n> multi-colored damask <i>and</i> knotted carpets bound with tightly wound cords, <i>which were</i> among your merchandise.¥‚JHaran and Canneh and Eden <n>[in Mesopotamia]</n>, the merchants of Sheba <n>[on the Euphrates]</n>, Asshur and Chilmad traded with you.B¥‚}JThe merchants of Sheba and Raamah <n>[in Arabia]</n>, they traded with you; they paid for your wares with the choicest of all kinds of spices and all kinds of precious stones and gold.¥‚7JArabia and all the princes of Kedar, they were your customers for lambs, rams, and goats <n>[favored by you]</n>; for these they were your customers.=¥uJDedan traded with you in saddle blankets for riding.¥‚+JVedan and Javan traded with yarn from Uzal <n>[in Arabia]</n> for your wares; wrought iron, cassia, and sweet cane were among your merchandise.W¥ƒ'J<f>[5]</f> Damascus traded with you because of the abundance of your handiworks and the immense wealth of every kind, with the wine of Helbon <n>[Aleppo]</n> and the white wool <n>[of Sachar in Syria]</n>."¥‚=JJudah and the land of Israel, they were your traders; with the wheat of Minnith <n>[in Ammon]</n>, cakes, honey, oil, and balm they paid for your goods.@¥‚yJ<f>[4]</f> Aram traded with you because of the abundance of the goods you made. They paid for your merchandise with emeralds, purple, embroidered work, fine linen, coral, and rubies.#¥‚?JThe men of<f>[3]</f> Dedan were your traders. Many coastlands were your markets; ivory tusks and ebony they brought to you in payment <i>or</i> as gifts.w¥gJThose from Beth-togarmah (Armenia) traded for your wares with <n>[chariot]</n> horses, war horses, and mules..¥‚UJ
Javan (Greece), Tubal and Meshech (Asia Minor) traded with you; with the lives of men <n>[taken as slaves]</n> and vessels of bronze they paid for your merchandise.H¥ƒ	J<pb/>"Tarshish <n>[in Spain]</n> was your customer <i>and</i> traded with you because of the abundance of your riches of all kinds; with silver, iron, tin, and lead they paid for your wares.O¥
ƒJThe men of Arvad with your army were upon your walls, all around, and the Gammadim (men of valor) were in your towers. They hung their shields on your walls, all around; they perfected your beauty.¥‚)J
<pb/>"Persia and Lud and Put (Libya) were in your army as your men of war. They hung the shield and the helmet on you; they gave you splendor.W¥ƒ'J	<t>"The elders of<f>[2]</f> Gebal and its skilled <i>and</i> wise men were with you, repairing your leaks; </t><t>All the ships of the sea with their mariners were with you to deal in your merchandise.</t>:¥
‚mJ<t>"The inhabitants of Sidon and <n>[the island]</n> of Arvad were your oarsmen; </t><t>Your skilled <i>and</i> wise men, O Tyre, were with you; they were your pilots.</t><br/>‚¥	„J<t>"Your sail was of fine embroidered linen from Egypt </t><t>So that it became your distinguishing mark (insignia); </t><t>Your <n>[ship's]</n> awning <n>[which covered you]</n> was blue and purple from the coasts of Elishah <n>[of Asia Minor]</n>.</t><br/>#¥‚?J<t>"Of the oaks of Bashan they have made your oars; </t><t>They have made your deck of boxwood from the coastlands of Cyprus, inlaid with ivory.</t><br/>¥‚1J<t>"They have made all your planks of fir trees from<f>[1]</f> Senir; </t><t>They have taken a cedar from Lebanon to make a mast for you.</t><br/>r¥]J<t>"Your borders are in the heart of the seas; </t><t>Your builders have perfected your beauty.</t><br/>O¥ƒJ<t>and say to Tyre, who lives at the entrance to the sea, merchant of the peoples to many coastlands, 'Thus says the Lord GOD, </t><br/><t>"O Tyre, you have said, 'I am perfect in beauty.'</t><br/>S¥J"Now you, son of man, take up a dirge (funeral poem to be sung) for Tyre,;¥	sJ<pb/>The word of the LORD came to me again, saying,¥‚JI will bring terrors on you and you will be no more. Though you will be sought, yet you will never be found again," says the Lord GOD.‚\¥…1Jthen I will bring you down with those who descend into the pit (the place of the dead), to the people of old, and I will make you <n>[Tyre]</n> live in the depths of the earth, like the ancient ruins, with those who go down to the pit, so that you will not be inhabited; but I will set glory <i>and</i> splendor in the land of the living.3¥ ‚_J<pb/>For thus says the Lord GOD, "When I make you a desolate city, like the cities which are not inhabited, when I bring up the deep over you and great waters cover you,/¤‚WJ<t>'Now the coastlands will tremble </t><t>On the day of your fall; </t><t>Yes, the coastlands which are by the sea </t><t>Will be terrified at your departure.'"</t>‚R¤~…J<t>They will take up a dirge (funeral poem to be sung) for you and say to you, </t><br/><t>'How you have perished <i>and</i> vanished, O renowned city, </t><t>From the seas, O renowned city, </t><t>Which was mighty on the sea, </t><t>She and her inhabitants, </t><t>Who imposed her terror </t><t>On all who lived there!</t><br/>‚¤}ƒJThen all the princes of the sea will go down from their thrones and remove their robes and take off their embroidered garments. They will clothe themselves with trembling; they will sit on the ground, tremble again and again, and be appalled at you.)¤|‚KJ<pb/>Thus says the Lord GOD to Tyre, "Shall not the coastlands shake at the sound of your fall when the wounded groan, when the slaughter occurs in your midst?:¤{‚mJI will make you <n>[Tyre]</n> a<f>[4]</f> bare rock; you will be a dry place on which to spread nets. You will never be rebuilt, for I the LORD have spoken," says the Lord GOD.\¤z1J
So I will silence your songs, and the sound of your lyres will no longer be heard.b¤yƒ=JAlso they will take your riches as spoil and plunder your merchandise, and tear down your walls and your pleasant houses, and throw your stones and your timber and the debris <n>[from your city]</n> out in the water.9¤x‚kJWith the hoofs of his horses Nebuchadnezzar will trample all your streets; with the sword he will kill your people, and your strong pillars (obelisks) will fall to the ground.t¤wƒaJ
Because of the great number of his horses, their dust will cover you; your walls <n>[O Tyre]</n> will shake from the noise of the horsemen and the wagons and the chariots when he enters your gates as men enter a city that is breached.¤v‚J	He will direct the <n>[shocking]</n> blow of his battering rams against your walls, and he will tear down your towers with his crowbars.`¤uƒ9JHe will kill your daughters on the mainland with the sword, and he shall make siege walls against you and build a siege ramp against you and raise <n>[a roof of]</n> large shields <n>[as a defense]</n> against you.S¤tƒJ<pb/>For thus says the Lord GOD, "Behold, I will bring upon Tyre from the north<f>[3]</f> Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon, king of kings, with horses and chariots and with horsemen and a great army.,¤s‚QJAlso Tyre's daughters (towns, villages)<f>[2]</f> on the mainland will be killed by the sword, and they will know <n>[without any doubt]</n> that I am the LORD.'";¤r‚oJHer island in the midst of the sea will become a dry place to spread nets, for I have spoken,' says the Lord GOD, 'and she will become a prey <i>and</i> a spoil for the nations.2¤q‚]JThey will destroy the walls of Tyre and break down her towers; and I will scrape her dust <i>and</i> debris from her and make her as bare as <n>[the top of]</n> a rock.'¤p‚GJtherefore, thus says the Lord GOD, 'Behold, I am against you, O Tyre, and I will cause many nations to come up against you, as the sea makes its waves crest.C¤o‚J"Son of man, because<f>[1]</f> Tyre has said against Jerusalem, 'Aha! The gateway of the people is broken; she is open to me. I will be filled, <i>now that</i> she is a desolate waste,'&¤n	‚GJ<pb/>Now in the eleventh year, on the first <n>[day]</n> of the month <n>[after the capture of King Jehoiachin]</n>, the word of the LORD came to me, saying,D¤mƒJI will execute great vengeance on them with wrathful rebukes <i>and</i> chastisements and they will know <n>[without any doubt]</n> that I am the LORD when I lay My vengeance on them."'">¤l‚uJtherefore thus says the Lord GOD, "Behold, I will stretch out My hand against the Philistines, and I will cut off the<f>[5]</f> Cherethites and destroy the remnant of the seacoast.d¤kƒAJ'Thus says the Lord GOD, "Because the<f>[4]</f> Philistines have acted revengefully and have taken vengeance <n>[contemptuously]</n> with malice in their hearts to destroy with everlasting hostility <i>and</i> hatred,"a¤jƒ;JI will take My vengeance on Edom by the hand of My people Israel. Therefore, they will act in Edom in accordance with My anger and My wrath, and they will know <i>and</i> experience My vengeance," says the Lord GOD.
