<t>You who <n>[self-confidently]</n> rejoice in<f>[2]</f> Lo-debar (Nothing), </t><t>Who say, "Have we not by our own strength taken<f>[3]</f> Karnaim (Strength) for ourselves?"</t><br/>_¯?ƒ7r<pb/><t>Do horses run on rocks? </t><t>Do men plow rocks with oxen? <n>[Of course not!]</n> </t><t>Yet you have turned justice into poison </t><t>And the fruit of righteousness into wormwood (bitterness),</t><br/>¯>‚r<t>For behold, the LORD is going to command that the great house be smashed to pieces and the small house to fragments.</t>ƒ5¯=†cr
Then one's uncle, or his undertaker, shall come to bring the <n>[disease-infected]</n> body out of the house, <n>[to cremate it]</n>, and he will say to another in the farthest part of the house, "Is there anyone else with you?" And that one will say, "No one." Then he will respond, "Hush! Keep quiet! For the name of the LORD is not to be mentioned <n>[even casually, for fear that we might invoke even more punishment]</n>."¯<‚r	<pb/>And it shall come to pass that if there remain ten men in one house, they shall die <n>[by the pestilence that comes with war]</n>.ƒ¯;†r<pb/><t>The Lord<f>[1]</f> GOD has sworn <n>[an oath]</n> by Himselfâ€”the LORD God of hosts, says: </t><t>"I loathe <i>and</i> reject the <n>[self-centered]</n> arrogance of Jacob (Israel), </t><t>And I hate his palaces <i>and</i> citadels; </t><t>Therefore, I shall hand over the <n>[idolatrous]</n> city <n>[of Samaria]</n> with all that it contains <n>[to the Assyrian invaders]</n>."</t>Z¯:ƒ-r<t>Therefore, they will now go into exile with the first of the captives, </t><t>And the cultic revelry <i>and</i> banqueting of those who lounge around <n>[on their luxurious couches]</n> will pass away.</t>c¯9ƒ?r<t>Who drink wine from sacrificial bowls </t><t>And anoint themselves with the finest oils <n>[reflecting their unrestrained celebration]</n>; </t><t>Yet they are not grieved over the ruin of Joseph (Israel).</t><br/>|¯8qr<t>Who improvise to the sound of the harpâ€” </t><t>Like David they have composed songs for themselvesâ€”</t><br/>N¯7ƒr<pb/><t>Those who lie on <n>[luxurious]</n> beds of ivory </t><t>And lounge around out on their couches, </t><t>And eat lambs from the flock </t><t>And calves from the midst of the stall,</t><br/>j¯6Mr<t>Do you put off the day of punishment, </t><t>Yet cause the seat of violence to come near?</t>‚7¯5„gr<t>Go over to Calneh <n>[in Babylonia]</n> and look, </t><t>And from there go <n>[north of Damascus]</n> to the great city of Hamath; </t><t>Then go down to Gath of the Philistines. </t><t>Are they better than these kingdoms <n>[of yours]</n>, </t><t>Or is their territory greater than yours?</t><br/>‚¯4	„r<pb/><t>Woe (judgment is coming) to those who are at ease <i>and</i> carefree in Zion (Judah) </t><t>And to those on the mountain of Samaria who feel secure, </t><t>The distinguished men of the foremost of nations, </t><t>To whom the house of Israel comes.</t><br/>z¯3mrTherefore, I will send you to go into exile far beyond Damascus," says the LORD, whose name is the God of hosts.X¯2ƒ)rYou carried along your king Sikkuth and Kayyun <n>[your man-made gods of Saturn]</n>, your images of your star-god which you made for yourselves <n>[but you brought Me none of the appointed sacrifices]</n>.¯1‚%r<pb/>"Did you bring Me sacrifices and grain offerings during those forty years in the wilderness, O house of Israel? <n>[Certainly not!]</n>
