A man <i>was there</i> whose hand was withered. And they asked Jesus, "Is it lawful <i>and</i> permissible to heal on the Sabbath?"â€”<i>they asked this</i> so that they might accuse Him <i>and</i> bring charges into court.:·KoÖ	<pb/>Leaving there, He went into their synagogue.;·JqÖ<J>For the Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath."</J>g·IƒGÖ<J>And if you had only known what this statement means, '<e>I desire compassion</e> <n>[for those in distress]</n>,<f>[4]</f><e>and not</e> <n>[animal]</n> <e>sacrifice</e>,' you would not have condemned the innocent.</J>O·HÖ<J>But I tell you that something greater than the temple is here.</J> ·G‚9Ö<J>Or have you not read in the Law, that on the Sabbath the priests in the temple break <n>[the sanctity of]</n> the Sabbath and yet are innocent?</J>7·F‚gÖ<J>how he went into the house of God, and they ate the<f>[3]</f> consecrated bread, which was not lawful for him to eat nor for those with him, but for the priests only?</J>·E‚%ÖHe said to them, <J>"Have you not read <n>[in the Scriptures]</n> what David did when he was hungry, he and those who accompanied himâ€”</J>·D‚	ÖBut when the Pharisees saw this, they said to Him, "Look! Your disciples are doing what<f>[2]</f> is unlawful on the Sabbath."3·C	‚aÖ<pb/>At that particular time Jesus went through the grainfields on the<f>[1]</f> Sabbath, and His disciples were hungry and began to pick the heads of grain and eat them.N·BÖ<J>For My yoke is easy <n>[to bear]</n> and My burden is light."</J>Q·AƒÖ<J>Take My yoke upon you and learn from Me <n>[following Me as My disciple]</n>, for I am gentle and humble in heart, and <e>you will find rest</e> (renewal, blessed quiet) <e>for your souls</e>.</J>F·@ƒÖ<pb/><J>"Come to Me, all who are weary and heavily burdened <n>[by religious rituals that provide no peace]</n>, and I will give you rest <n>[refreshing your souls with salvation]</n>.</J>‚:·?„mÖ<J>All things have been handed over to Me by My Father; and no one fully knows <i>and</i> accurately understands the Son except the Father; and no one fully knows <i>and</i> accurately understands the Father except the Son, and anyone to whom the Son <n>[deliberately]</n> wills to reveal <i>Him</i>.</J>K·>Ö<J>Yes, Father, for this way was well-pleasing in Your sight.</J>‚X·=…)ÖAt that time Jesus said, <J>"I praise You, Father, Lord of heaven and earth <n>[I openly and joyfully acknowledge Your great wisdom]</n>, that You have hidden these things <n>[these spiritual truths]</n> from the wise and intelligent and revealed them to infants <n>[to new believers, to those seeking God's will and purpose]</n>.</J>y·<kÖ<J>But I say to you, it will be more tolerable for the land of Sodom on the day of judgment, than for you."</J>‚·;„Ö<J>And you, Capernaum, are you to be exalted to heaven <n>[for your apathy and unresponsiveness]</n>? You will descend to Hades (the realm of the dead); for if the miracles done in you had been done in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.</J>·:‚-Ö<J>Nevertheless I say to you, it will be more tolerable for <n>[the pagan cities of]</n> Tyre and Sidon on the day of judgment than for you.</J>‚s·9…_Ö<J>"Woe (judgment is coming) to you,<f>[3]</f> Chorazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the miracles done in you had been done in Tyre and Sidon <n>[cities of the Gentiles]</n>, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes <n>[their hearts would have been changed and they would have expressed sorrow for their sin and rebellion against God]</n>.</J>6·8‚eÖThen He began to denounce <n>[the people in]</n> the cities in which most of His miracles were done, because they did not repent <n>[and change their hearts and lives]</n>.‚:·7„mÖ<J>The Son of Man came eating and drinking <n>[with others]</n>, and they say, 'Look! A glutton and a drunkard, a friend of ta