<J>I assure you <i>and</i> most solemnly say to you, wherever this gospel <n>[of salvation]</n> is preached in the whole world, what this woman has done will also be told in memory of her <n>[for her act of love and devotion]</n>."</J>^¼5Ö<J>When she poured this perfume on My body, she did it to prepare Me for burial.</J>X¼)Ö<J>For you always have the poor with you; but you will not always have Me.</J>"¼‚=Ö
But Jesus, aware <n>[of the malice]</n> of this <n>[remark]</n>, said to them, <J>"Why are you bothering the woman? She has done a good thing to Me.</J>o¼ WÖ	For this <i>perfume</i> might have been sold at a high price and <i>the money</i> given to the poor."»‚ÖBut when the<f>[6]</f> disciples saw it they were indignant <i>and</i> angry, saying, "Why all this waste <n>[of money]</n>?'»~‚GÖa<f>[4]</f> woman came to Him with an alabaster vial of very expensive<f>[5]</f> perfume and she poured it on Jesus' head as He reclined <i>at the table</i>.`»}9ÖNow when Jesus was <n>[back]</n> in Bethany, at the home of<f>[3]</f> Simon the leper,{»|oÖBut they said, "It must not be during the festival (Passover), otherwise there might be a riot among the people."G»{Öand plotted together to arrest Jesus by stealth and kill Him.7»z‚gÖ<pb/>Then the chief priests and the elders of the people gathered in the courtyard of the <n>[elegant home of the Jewish]</n> high priest, whose name was<f>[2]</f> Caiaphas,»y‚Ö<J>"You know that the Passover is coming in two days, and the Son of Man is to be betrayed <i>and</i> handed over for crucifixion."</J>Y»x	-Ö<pb/>When Jesus had finished this<f>[1]</f> discourse, He said to His disciples,s»wƒ_Ö.<J>Then these <n>[unbelieving people]</n> will go away into eternal (unending) punishment, but those who are righteous <i>and</i> in right standing with God <n>[will go, by His remarkable grace]</n> into eternal (unending) life."</J>T»vƒ!Ö-<J>Then He will reply to them, 'I assure you <i>and</i> most solemnly say to you, to the extent that you did not do it for one of the least of these <n>[my followers]</n>, you did not do it for Me.'</J>C»u‚Ö,<J>Then they also <n>[in their turn]</n> will answer, 'Lord, when did we see You hungry, or thirsty, or as a stranger, or naked, or sick, or in prison, and did not minister to You?'</J>:»t‚mÖ+<J>I was a stranger, and you did not invite Me in; naked, and you did not clothe Me; sick, and in prison, and you did not visit Me <n>[with help and ministering care]</n>.'</J>z»smÖ*<J>for I was hungry, and you gave Me <i>nothing</i> to eat; I was thirsty, and you gave Me nothing to drink;</J>.»r‚UÖ)<pb/><J>"Then He will say to those on His left, 'Leave Me, you cursed ones, into the eternal fire which has been prepared for the devil and his angels (demons);</J>d»qƒAÖ(<J>The King will answer and say to them, 'I assure you <i>and</i> most solemnly say to you, to the extent that you did it for one of these brothers of Mine, <i>even</i> the least <i>of them</i>, you did it for Me.'</J>N»pÖ'<J>And when did we see You sick, or in prison, and come to You?'</J>d»oAÖ&<J>And when did we see You as a stranger, and invite You in, or naked, and clothe You?</J>»n‚Ö%<J>Then the righteous will answer Him, 'Lord, when did we see You hungry, and feed You, or thirsty, and give You something to drink?</J>H»mƒ	Ö$<J><i>I was</i> naked, and you clothed Me; I was sick, and you visited Me <n>[with help and ministering care]</n>; I was in prison, and you came to Me <n>[ignoring personal danger]</n>.'</J>'»l‚GÖ#<J>For I was hungry, and you gave Me something to eat; I was thirsty, and you gave Me <i>something</i> to drink; I was a stranger, and you invited Me in;</J>h»kƒIÖ"<pb/><J>"Then the King will say to those on His right, 'Come, you blessed of My Father <n>[you favored of God, appointed to eternal salvation]</n>, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world.</J>»j‚Ö!<J>and He will put the sheep on His right <n>[the place of honor]</n>, and the goats on His left <n>[the place of rejection]</n>.</J>1»i‚[Ö <J>All the nations will be gathered before Him <n>[for judgment]</n>; and He will separate them from one another, as a shepherd separates his sheep from the goats;</J>»h‚+Ö<J>"But when the Son of Man comes in His glory <i>and</i> majesty and all the angels with Him, then He will sit on the throne of His glory.</J>e»gƒCÖ<J>And throw out the worthless servant into the outer darkness; in that place <n>[of grief and torment]</n> there will be weeping <n>[over sorrow and pain]</n> and grinding of teeth <n>[over distress and anger]</n>.</J>‚|»f…qÖ<pb/><J>"For to everyone who has <n>[and values his blessings and gifts from God, and has used them wisely]</n>, more will be given, and <n>[he will be richly supplied so that]</n> he will have an abundance; but from the one who does not have <n>[because he has ignored or disregarded his blessings and gifts from God]</n>, even what he does have will be taken away.</J>c»e?Ö<J>So take the talent away from him, and give it to the one who has the ten talents.'</J>»d‚Ö<J>Then you ought to have put my money with the bankers, and at my return I would have received my <i>money</i> back with interest.</J><»c‚qÖ<pb/><J>"But his master answered him, 'You wicked, lazy servant, you knew that I reap <n>[the harvest]</n> where I did not sow and gather where I did not scatter <i>seed</i>.</J>»b‚Ö<J>So I was afraid <n>[to lose the talent]</n>, and I went and hid your talent in the ground. See, you have what is your own.'</J>y»aƒkÖ<pb/><J>"The one who had received one talent also came forward, saying, 'Master, I knew you to be a harsh <i>and</i> demanding man, reaping <n>[the harvest]</n> where you did not sow and gathering where you did not scatter <i>seed</i>.</J>W»`ƒ'Ö<J>His master said to him, 'Well done, good and faithful servant. You have been faithful <i>and</i> trustworthy over a little, I will put you in charge of many things; share in the joy of your master.'</J><»_‚qÖ<pb/><J>"Also the one who had the two talents came forward, saying, 'Master, you entrusted two talents to me. See, I have <n>[made a profit and]</n> gained two more talents.'</J>W»^ƒ'Ö<J>His master said to him, 'Well done, good and faithful servant. You have been faithful <i>and</i> trustworthy over a little, I will put you in charge of many things; share in the joy of your master.'</J>S»]ƒÖ<J>And the one who had received the five talents came and brought him five more, saying, 'Master, you entrusted to me five talents. See, I have <n>[made a profit and]</n> gained five more talents.'</J>r»\]Ö<pb/><J>"Now after a long time the master of those servants returned and settled accounts with them.</J>z»[mÖ<J>But the one who had received the one went and dug <i>a hole</i> in the ground and hid his master's money.</J>Y»Z+Ö<J>Likewise the one who had two <n>[made a profit and]</n> gained two more.</J>»Y‚Ö<J>The one who had received the five talents went at once and traded with them, and he <n>[made a profit and]</n> gained five more.</J>!»X‚;Ö<J>To one he gave five<f>[2]</f> talents, to another, two, and to another, one, each according to his own ability; and then he went on his journey.</J> »W‚9Ö<J>"For it is just like a man who was <i>about</i> to take a journey, and he called his servants together and entrusted them with his possessions.</J>»V‚1Ö
<J>Therefore, be on the alert <n>[be prepared and ready]</n>, for you do not know the day nor the hour <n>[when the Son of Man will come]</n>.</J>»U‚	Ö<J>But He replied, 'I assure you <i>and</i> most solemnly say to you, I do not know you <n>[we have no relationship]</n>.'</J>c»T?Ö<J>Later the others also came, and said, 'Lord, Lord, open <n>[the door]</n> for us.'</J>@»S‚yÖ
<J>But while they were going away to buy <i>oil</i>, the bridegroom came, and those who were ready went in with him to the wedding feast; and the door was shut <i>and</i> locked.</J>,»R‚QÖ	<J>But the wise replied, 'No, otherwise there will not be enough for us and for you, <i>too</i>; go instead to the dealers and buy <i>oil</i> for yourselves.'</J>~»QuÖ<J>But the foolish <i>virgins</i> said to the wise, 'Give us some of your oil, because our lamps are going out.'</J>»P‚Ö<J>Then all those virgins got up and put their own lamps in order <n>[trimmed the wicks and added oil and lit them]</n>.</J>y»OkÖ<J>But at midnight there was a shout, 'Look! The bridegroom <n>[is coming]</n>! Go out to meet <i>him</i>.'</J>g»NGÖ<J>Now while the bridegroom was delayed, they all began to nod off, and they fell asleep.</J>H»M	Ö<J>but the wise took flasks of oil along with their lamps.</J>k»LOÖ<J>For when the foolish took their lamps, they did not take any <n>[extra]</n> oil with them,</J>»K‚'Ö<J>Five of them were foolish <n>[thoughtless, silly, and careless]</n>, and five were wise <n>[far-sighted, practical, and sensible]</n>.</J>
»J	‚Ö<pb/><J>"Then the kingdom of heaven will be like ten virgins, who took their lamps and went to<f>[1]</f> meet the bridegroom.</J>C»I‚Ö3<J>and will cut him in two and put him with the hypocrites; in that place there will be weeping <n>[over sorrow and pain]</n> and grinding of teeth <n>[over distress and anger]</n></J>.»HÖ2<J>the master of that servant will come on a day when he does not expect him and at an hour of which he is not aware,</J>\»G1Ö1<J>and begins to beat his fellow servants and to eat and drink with drunkards;</J>»F‚Ö0<J>But if that servant is evil and says in his heart, 'My master is taking his time <n>[he will not return for a long while]</n>,'</J>x»EiÖ/<J>I assure you <i>and</i> most solemnly say to you that he will put him in charge of all his possessions.</J>j»DMÖ.<J>Blessed is that <n>[faithful]</n> servant when his master returns and finds him doing so.</J>O»CƒÖ-<pb/><J>"Who then is the faithful and wise servant whom his master has put in charge of his household to give the others <n>[in the house]</n> their food <i>and</i> supplies at the proper time?</J>»B‚Ö,<J>Therefore, you <n>[who follow Me]</n> must also be ready; because the Son of Man is coming at an hour when you do not expect Him.</J>M»AƒÖ+<J>But understand this: If the head of the house had known what time of the night the thief was coming, he would have been on the alert and would not have allowed his house to be broken into.</J>.»@‚UÖ*<J>"So be alert <n>[give strict attention, be cautious and active in faith]</n>, for you do not know which day <n>[whether near or far]</n> your Lord is coming.</J>}»?sÖ)<J>Two women <i>will be</i> grinding at the mill; one will be taken <n>[for judgment]</n> and one will be left.</J>»>Ö(<J>At that time two men will be in the field; one will be<f>[8]</f> taken <n>[for judgment]</n> and one will be left.</J>/»=‚WÖ'<J>and they did not know <i>or</i> understand until the flood came and swept them all away; so will the coming of the Son of Man be <n>[unexpected judgment]</n>.</J>-»<‚SÖ&<J>For as in those days before the flood they were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, until the <n>[very]</n> day when Noah entered the ark,</J>c»;?Ö%<J>For the coming of the Son of Man (the Messiah) will be just like the days of Noah.</J>'»:‚GÖ$<pb/><J>"But of that <n>[exact]</n> day and hour no one knows, not even the angels of heaven, nor the Son <n>[in His humanity]</n>, but the Father alone.</J>h»9IÖ#<J>Heaven and earth <n>[as now known]</n> will pass away, but My words will not pass away.</J>E»8ƒÖ"<J>I assure you <i>and</i> most solemnly say to you, this generation <n>[the people living when these signs and events begin]</n> will not pass away until all these things take place.</J>»7‚/Ö!<J>so you, too, when you see all<f>[6]</f> these things <n>[taking place]</n>, know for certain that He is near, right<f>[7]</f> at the door.</J>»6‚5Ö <J>"Now learn this lesson from the fig tree: As soon as its young shoots become tender and it puts out its leaves, you know that summer is near;</J>I»5ƒÖ<J>And He will send His angels with <e>a loud trumpet</e> and <e>they will gather together</e> His elect (God's chosen ones) from the four winds, from one end of the heavens to the other.</J>ƒ	»4†Ö<J>And at that time the sign of the Son of Man <n>[coming in His glory]</n> will appear in the sky, and then all the tribes of the earth <n>[and especially Israel]</n> will mourn <n>[regretting their rebellion and rejection of the Messiah]</n>, and they will see the <e>Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven</e> with power and great glory <n>[in brilliance and splendor]</n>.</J>_»3ƒ7Ö<J>"Immediately after the tribulation of those days <e>the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not provide its light, and the stars will fall</e> from the sky, and the powers of the heavens will be shaken.</J>Z»2-Ö<J>Wherever the corpse is, there the<f>[5]</f> vultures will flock together.</J>@»1‚yÖ<J>For just as the lightning comes from the east and flashes as far as the west, so will be the coming <n>[in glory]</n> of the Son of Man <n>[everyone will see Him clearly]</n>.</J>1»0‚[Ö<J>So if they say to you, 'Look! He is in the wilderness,' do not go out there, or, 'Look! He is in the inner rooms <n>[of a house]</n></J>,' <J>do not believe it.</J>=»/uÖ<J>Listen carefully, I have told you in advance.</J>3».‚_Ö<J>For false Christs and false prophets will appear and they will provide great signs and wonders, so as to deceive, if possible, even the elect (God's chosen ones).</J>!»-‚;Ö<J>Then if anyone says to you <n>[during the great tribulation]</n>, 'Look! Here is the Christ,' or 'There <i>He is</i>,' do not believe <i>it</i>.</J>@»,‚yÖ<J>And if those days <n>[of tribulation]</n> had not been cut short, no human life would be saved; but for the sake of the elect (God's chosen ones) those days will be shortened.</J>R»+ƒÖ<J>For<f>[4]</f> at that time there will be a great tribulation (pressure, distress, oppression), such as has not occurred since the beginning of the world until now, nor ever will <n>[again]</n>.</J> »*‚9Ö<J>Pray that your flight <n>[from persecution and suffering]</n> will not be in winter, or on a Sabbath <n>[when Jewish laws prohibit travel]</n>.</J>e»)CÖ<J>And woe to those who are pregnant and to those who are nursing babies in those days!</J>M»(Ö<J>"Whoever is in the field must not turn back to get his coat.</J>»'‚#Ö<J>"Whoever is on the housetop must not go down to get the things that are in his house <n>[because there will not be enough time]</n>.</J>[»&/Ö<J>then let those who are in Judea flee to the mountains <n>[for refuge]</n>.</J>w»%ƒgÖ<J>"So when you see the<f>[2]</f><e>abomination of desolation</e> <n>[the appalling sacrilege that astonishes and makes desolate]</n>, spoken of by the prophet Daniel, standing in the Holy Place (let the<f>[3]</f> reader understand),</J>A»$‚{Ö<J>This good news of the kingdom <n>[the gospel]</n> will be preached throughout the whole world as a testimony to all the nations, and then the end <n>[of the age]</n> will come.</J>o»#WÖ
<J>But the one who endures <i>and</i> bears up <n>[under suffering]</n> to the end will be saved.</J>Z»"-Ö<J>Because lawlessness is increased, the love of most people will grow cold.</J>A»!}Ö<J>Many false prophets will appear and mislead many.</J>‚ » „9Ö
<J>At that time many will be offended <i>and</i> repelled <n>[by their association with Me]</n> <i>and</i> will fall away <n>[from the One whom they should trust]</n> and will betray one another <n>[handing over believers to their persecutors]</n> and will hate one another.</J>'»‚GÖ	<pb/><J>"Then they will hand you over to <n>[endure]</n> tribulation, and will put you to death, and you will be hated by all nations because of My name.</J>»‚7Ö<J>But all these things are <i>merely</i> the beginning of birth pangs <n>[of the intolerable anguish and the time of unprecedented trouble]</n>.</J>»‚Ö<J>For nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom, and there will be famines and earthquakes in various places.</J>H»ƒ	Ö<J>You will <i>continually</i> hear of wars and rumors of wars. See that you are not frightened, for <i>those things</i> must take place, but that is not yet the end <n>[of the age]</n>.</J>W»ƒ'Ö<J>For many will come in My name <n>[misusing it, and appropriating the strength of the name which belongs to Me]</n>, saying, 'I am the Christ (the Messiah, the Anointed),' and they will mislead many.</J>}»sÖ<pb/>Jesus answered, <J>"Be careful that no one misleads you <n>[deceiving you and leading you into error]</n>.</J>‚»„#Ö<pb/>While Jesus was seated on the Mount of Olives, the disciples came to Him privately, and said, "Tell us, when will this <n>[destruction of the temple]</n> take place, and what will be the sign of Your coming, and of the end (completion, consummation) of the age?"?»‚wÖAnd He said to them, <J>"Do you see all these things? I assure you <i>and</i> most solemnly say to you, not one stone here will be left on another, which will not be torn down."</J>K»	ƒÖ<pb/>Jesus left the temple <i>area</i> and was going on His way when His disciples came up to Him to call His attention to the <n>[magnificent and massive]</n><f>[1]</f> buildings of the temple.X»ƒ)Ö'<J>For I say to you, you will not see Me again <n>[ministering to you publicly]</n> until you say, '<e>Blessed</e> <n>[to be celebrated with praise]</n> <e>is He who comes in the name of the Lord</e>!'"</J>»‚Ö&<J>Listen carefully: your house is being left to you desolate <n>[completely abandoned by God and destitute of His protection]</n>!</J>‚6»„eÖ%<J>"<f>[7]</f> O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, who murders the prophets and stones <n>[to death]</n> those <n>[messengers]</n> who are sent to her <n>[by God]</n>! How often I wanted to gather your children together <n>[around Me]</n>, as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you were unwilling.</J>3»‚_Ö$<J>I assure you <i>and</i> most solemnly say to you, <n>[the judgment for]</n> all these things <n>[these vile and murderous deeds]</n> will come on this generation.</J>‚»„Ö#<J>so that on you will come <i>the guilt of</i> all the blood of the righteous shed on earth, from the blood of righteous<f>[6]</f> Abel to the blood of Zechariah <n>[the priest]</n>, the son of Berechiah, whom you murdered between the temple and the altar.</J>‚]»…3Ö"<pb/><J>"Therefore, take notice, I am sending you prophets and wise men <n>[interpreters, teachers]</n> and scribes <n>[men educated in the Mosaic Law and the writings of the prophets]</n>; some of them you will kill and <i>even</i> crucify, and some you will flog in your synagogues, and pursue <i>and</i> persecute from city to city,</J>e»CÖ!<J>You<f>[5]</f> serpents, you spawn of vipers, how can you escape the penalty of hell?</J>o»WÖ <J>Fill up, then, the <n>[allotted]</n> measure <i>of the guilt</i> of your fathers' <i>sins</i>.</J>s»_Ö<J>So you testify against yourselves that you are the descendants of those who murdered the prophets.</J>$»
‚AÖ<J>and you say, 'If we had been <i>living</i> in the days of our fathers, we would not have joined them in <i>shedding</i> the blood of the prophets.'</J>@»‚yÖ<pb/><J>"Woe to you, <n>[self-righteous]</n> scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you build tombs for the prophets and decorate <i>and</i> adorn the monuments of the righteous,</J>»‚Ö<J>So you, also, outwardly seem to be just <i>and</i> upright to men, but inwardly you are full of hypocrisy and lawlessness.</J>e»
ƒCÖ<pb/><J>"Woe to you, <n>[self-righteous]</n> scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs which look beautiful on the outside, but inside are full of dead men's bones and everything unclean.</J>‚»	ƒ{Ö<J>You <n>[spiritually]</n> blind Pharisee, first clean the inside of the cup and of the plate <n>[examine and change your inner self to conform to God's precepts]</n>, so that the outside <n>[your public life and deeds]</n> may be clean also.</J>w»ƒgÖ<pb/><J>"Woe to you, <n>[self-righteous]</n> scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you clean the outside of the cup and of the plate, but inside they are full of extortion <i>and</i> robbery and self-indulgence (unrestrained greed).</J>F»ƒÖ<J>You <n>[spiritually]</n> blind guides, who strain out a gnat <n>[consuming yourselves with miniscule matters]</n> and swallow a camel <n>[ignoring and violating God's precepts]</n>!</J>ƒ&»†EÖ<pb/><J>"Woe to you, <n>[self-righteous]</n> scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you give a tenth (tithe) of your mint and dill and cumin <n>[focusing on minor matters]</n>, and have neglected the weightier <n>[more important moral and spiritual]</n> provisions of the Law: justice and mercy and faithfulness; but these are the <n>[primary]</n> things you ought to have done without neglecting the others.</J>}»sÖ<J>And whoever swears <n>[an oath]</n> by heaven, swears both by the throne of God and by Him who sits upon it.</J>»}Ö<J>And whoever swears <n>[an oath]</n> by the sanctuary of the temple, swears by it and by Him who dwells within it.</J>»‚Ö<J>Therefore, whoever swears <n>[an oath]</n> by the altar, swears both by it and by everything <n>[offered]</n> on it.</J>»‚Ö<J>You <n>[spiritually]</n> blind men, which is more important, the offering, or the altar that sanctifies the offering?</J>‚»„!Ö<J>And <n>[you scribes and Pharisees say]</n>, 'Whoever swears <n>[an oath]</n> by the altar, that is nothing (non-binding), but whoever swears <n>[an oath]</n> by the offering on it, he is obligated <n>[as a debtor to fulfill his vow and keep his promise]</n>.'</J>» ‚Ö<J>You fools and blind men! Which is more important, the gold or the sanctuary of the temple that sanctified the gold?</J>‚#º„?Ö<pb/><J>"Woe to you, blind guides, who say, 'Whoever swears <n>[an oath]</n> by the sanctuary of the temple, that is nothing (non-binding); but whoever swears <n>[an oath]</n> by the gold of the temple is obligated <n>[as a debtor to fulfill his vow and keep his promise]</n>.'</J>‚º~„Ö<pb/><J>"Woe to you, <n>[self-righteous]</n> scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you travel over sea and land to make a single proselyte (convert to Judaism), and when he becomes a convert, you make him twice as much a son of hell as you are.</J>Xº}ƒ)Ö<J><f>[4]</f><n>[Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you swallow up widows' houses, and to cover it up you make long prayers; therefore you will receive the greater condemnation.]</n></J>‚º|„5Ö
<J>"But woe (judgment is coming) to you, <n>[self-righteous]</n> scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you shut off the kingdom of heaven in front of people; for you do not enter yourselves, nor do you allow those who are <n>[in the process of]</n> entering to do so.</J>oº{WÖ<J>Whoever exalts himself shall be humbled; and whoever humbles himself shall be raised to honor.</J>@ºz{Ö<J>But the greatest among you will be your servant.</J>ºy{Ö
<J>Do not <i>let yourselves</i> be called leaders <i>or</i> teachers; for One is your Leader (Teacher), the Christ.</J>ºx‚Ö	<J>Do not call <i>anyone</i> on earth <n>[who guides you spiritually]</n> your father; for One is your Father, He who is in heaven.</J>zºwmÖ<J>But do not be called Rabbi (Teacher); for One is your Teacher, and you are all <n>[equally]</n> brothers.</J>ºv‚Ö<J>and to be greeted <n>[with respect]</n> in the market places <i>and</i> public forums, and to have people call them Rabbi.</J>Hºuƒ	Ö<J>They love the place of distinction <i>and</i> honor at feasts and the best seats in the synagogues <n>[those on the platform near the scrolls of the Law, facing the congregation]</n>,</J>DºtƒÖ<J>They do all their deeds to be seen by men; for they make their<f>[2]</f> phylacteries (tefillin) wide <n>[to make them more conspicuous]</n> and make their<f>[3]</f> tassels long.</J>SºsƒÖ<J>The scribes and Pharisees tie up<f>[1]</f> heavy loads <n>[that are hard to bear]</n> and place them on men's shoulders, but they themselves will not lift a finger <n>[to make them lighter]</n>.</J>ºr‚5Ö<J>so practice and observe everything they tell you, but do not do as they do; for they preach <n>[things]</n>, but do not practice <i>them</i>.</J>ºq‚	Ösaying: <J>"The scribes and Pharisees have seated themselves in Moses' chair <n>[of authority as teachers of the Law]</n>;</J>Aºp	Ö<pb/>Then Jesus spoke to the crowds and to His disciples,
