Ö"they offered Him wine mixed with gall (myrrh, a bitter-tasting narcotic) to drink; but after tasting it, He refused to drink it.`¼c9Ö!And when they came to a place called Golgotha, which means<f>[7]</f> Place of a Skull,,¼b‚QÖ <pb/>Now as they were coming out, they found a man of<f>[4]</f> Cyrene named<f>[5]</f> Simon, whom they forced into service<f>[6]</f> to carry the cross of Jesus.¼a‚/ÖAfter they finished ridiculing Him, they stripped Him of the <i>scarlet</i> robe and put His own clothes on Him, and led Him away to crucify Him.[¼`/ÖThey spat on Him, and took the reed and struck Him <i>repeatedly</i> on the head.^¼_ƒ5ÖAnd after twisting together a crown of thorns, they put it on His head, and put a reed in His right hand <n>[as a scepter]</n>. Kneeling before Him, they ridiculed Him, saying, "Hail (rejoice), King of the Jews!"^¼^5ÖThey stripped him and put a scarlet<f>[3]</f> robe on Him <n>[as a king's robe]</n>.¼]‚ÖThen the governor's soldiers took Jesus into<f>[2]</f> the Praetorium, and they gathered the whole Roman cohort around Him.¼\ÖSo he set Barabbas free for them; but after having Jesus severely whipped (scourged), he handed Him over to be crucified.t¼[aÖAnd all the people answered, "Let <n>[the responsibility for]</n> His blood be on us and on our children!"‚9¼Z„kÖ<pb/>So when Pilate saw that he was getting nowhere, but rather that a riot was breaking out, he took water and washed his hands <n>[to ceremonially cleanse himself of guilt]</n> in the presence of the crowd, saying, "I am innocent of this <n>[righteous]</n> Man's blood; see <i>to that</i> yourselves." ¼YyÖAnd he said, "Why, what has He done that is evil?" But they continued shouting all the louder, "Let Him be crucified!" ¼XyÖPilate said to them, "Then what shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ?" They all replied, "Let Him be crucified!"v¼WeÖThe governor said to them, "Which of the two do you wish me to set free for you?" And they said, "Barabbas."r¼V]ÖBut the chief priests and the elders persuaded the crowds to ask for Barabbas and to put Jesus to death.c¼Uƒ?Ö<pb/>While he was seated on the judgment seat, his wife sent him <i>a message</i>, saying, "Have nothing to do with that righteous <i>and</i> innocent Man; for last night I suffered greatly in a dream because of Him."y¼TkÖFor Pilate knew that it was because of jealousy that the chief priests and elders had handed Jesus over to him.(¼S‚IÖSo when they had assembled <n>[for this purpose]</n>, Pilate said to them, "Whom do you want me to set free for you? Barabbas, or Jesus who is called Christ?"~¼RuÖAnd at that time they were holding a notorious prisoner <n>[guilty of insurrection and murder]</n>, called Barabbas.¼Q‚Ö<pb/>Now at the feast <n>[of the Passover]</n> the governor was in the habit of setting free any one prisoner whom the people chose.w¼PgÖBut Jesus did not reply to him, not even to a single accusation, so that the governor was greatly astonished.e¼OCÖ
Then Pilate said to Him, "Do You not hear how many things they are testifying against You?"k¼NOÖBut when the charges were brought against Him by the chief priests and elders, He did not answer.G¼MƒÖNow Jesus stood before <n>[Pilate]</n> the governor, and the governor asked Him, "Are you the King of the Jews?" <n>[In affirmation]</n> Jesus said to him, <J>"<i>It is as</i> you say."</J>U¼L#Ö
<e>and they gave them for the Potter's Field, as the Lord directed me</e>."?¼K‚wÖ	Then the words spoken by Jeremiah the prophet were fulfilled: "<e>And they took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of Him on whom a price had been set</e> by the sons of Israel;X¼J)ÖTherefore that piece of ground has been called the Field of Blood to this day.n¼IUÖSo after consultation they used the money to buy the Potter's Field as a burial place for strangers.-¼H‚SÖThe chief priests, picking up the pieces of silver, said, "It is not lawful to put these in the treasury <n>[of the temple]</n>, because it is the price of blood."{¼GoÖAnd throwing the pieces of silver into the temple sanctuary, he left; and went away and<f>[1]</f> hanged himself.x¼FiÖsaying, "I have sinned by betraying innocent blood." They replied, "What is that to us? See to that yourself!"0¼E‚YÖ<pb/>When Judas, His betrayer, saw that Jesus was condemned, he was gripped with remorse and returned the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and the elders, ¼D‚9Öso they bound Him, and led Him away and handed Him over to Pilate the governor <n>[of Judea, who had the authority to condemn prisoners to death]</n>.‚¼C	„Ö<pb/>When it was morning, all the chief priests and the elders of the people (Sanhedrin, Jewish High Court) conferred together against Jesus, <n>[plotting how]</n> to put Him to death <n>[since under Roman rule they had no power to execute anyone]</n>;T¼Bƒ!ÖKAnd Peter remembered the <n>[prophetic]</n> words of Jesus, when He had said, <J>"Before a rooster crows, you will deny Me three times."</J> And he went outside and wept bitterly <n>[in repentance]</n>.0¼A‚YÖJThen he began to curse <n>[that is, to invoke God's judgment on himself]</n> and swear <n>[an oath]</n>, "I do not know the man!" And at that moment a rooster crowed.!¼@‚;ÖIAfter a little while the bystanders came up and said to Peter, "Surely you are one of them too; for even your <n>[Galilean]</n> accent gives you away."G¼?ÖHAnd again he denied it with an oath, "I do not know the man."¼>‚5ÖGAnd when he had gone out to the gateway, another <i>servant-girl</i> saw him and she said to the bystanders, "This man was with Jesus the Nazarene."_¼=7ÖFBut he denied it before them all, saying, "I do not know what you are talking about."
¼<‚ÖENow Peter was sitting outside in the courtyard, and a servant-girl came up to him and said, "You too were with Jesus the Galilean."k¼;OÖDsaying, "<f>[31]</f> Prophesy to us, You Christ (Messiah, Anointed); who was it that struck You?"`¼:9ÖC<pb/>Then they spat in His face and struck Him with their fists; and some slapped Him,Z¼9-ÖBWhat do you think?" They answered, "<f>[30]</f> He deserves to be put to death."‚¼8ƒ{ÖA<pb/>Then the high priest tore his<f>[29]</f> robes <n>[in mock horror]</n> and exclaimed, "He has blasphemed <n>[by making Himself God's equal]</n>! What further need have we of witnesses <i>or</i> evidence? See, you have now heard the blasphemy.‚>¼7„uÖ@Jesus said to him, <J>"You have <n>[in fact]</n> said it; but more than that I tell you <n>[regardless of what you do with Me now]</n>, in the future you will see <n>[Me revealed as]</n> <e>the Son of Man seated at the</e><f>[28]</f><e>right hand of Power</e>, and <e>coming on the clouds of heaven</e>."</J>9¼6‚kÖ?But Jesus kept silent. And the high priest said to Him, "I call on You to swear a binding oath by the living God, that you tell us whether You are the Christ, the Son of God."	¼5‚Ö>The high priest stood up and said to Jesus, "Have You no answer to give? What is it that these men are testifying against You?"	¼4‚Ö=and testified, "This man said, '<f>[26]</f> I am able to<f>[27]</f> tear down the temple of God and rebuild it in three days.'"c¼3?Ö<They found none, even though many false witnesses came forward. At last two came forward,O¼2ƒÖ;<pb/>Now the chief priests and the whole Council (Sanhedrin, Jewish High Court) tried to get false witnesses to testify against Jesus, so that they might <n>[have a reason to]</n> put Him to death.F¼1ƒÖ:But Peter followed Him at a distance as far as the courtyard of the <n>[elegant home of the Jewish]</n> high priest, and went inside, and sat with the<f>[25]</f> 