X	‡êádý‰Áó&Ã×                                                                                                                   gÅ9ƒGêNow when Jesus stepped out on land, He was met by a man from the city <n>[of Gerasa]</n> who was possessed with demons. For a long time he had worn no clothes, and was not living in a house, but among the<f>[3]</f> tombs._Å87êThen they sailed to the<f>[2]</f> country of the Gerasenes, which is east of Galilee.gÅ7ƒGêAnd He said to them, <J>"Where is your faith <n>[your confidence in Me]</n>?"</J> They were afraid and astonished, saying to one another, "Who then is this, that He commands even the winds and the sea, and they obey Him?"\Å6ƒ1êThey came to Jesus and woke Him, saying, "Master, Master, we are about to die!" He got up and rebuked the wind and the raging, violent waves, and they ceased, and it became calm <n>[a perfect peacefulness]</n>.IÅ5ƒêBut as they were sailing, He fell asleep. And a fierce gale of wind swept down <n>[as if through a wind tunnel]</n> on the lake, and they <i>began</i> to be swamped, and were in great danger.CÅ4‚êNow on one of <i>those</i> days Jesus and His disciples got into a boat, and He said to them, <J>"Let us cross over to the other side of the lake (Sea of Galilee)."</J> So they set out.pÅ3YêBut He answered, <J>"My mother and My brothers are these who listen to the word of God and do it!"</J>cÅ2?êAnd He was told, "Your mother and Your brothers are standing outside, asking to see You."yÅ1kê<pb/>Then Jesus' mother and His brothers came up toward Him, but they could not reach Him because of the crowd.‚Å0„ê<J>So be careful how you listen; for whoever has <n>[a teachable heart]</n>, to him <i>more</i> <n>[understanding]</n> will be given; and whoever does not have <n>[a longing for truth]</n>, even what he thinks he has will be taken away from him."</J>Å/‚)ê<J>For there is nothing hidden that will not become evident, nor <i>anything</i> secret that will not be known and come out into the open.</J>LÅ.ƒê<J>"Now no one lights a lamp and then covers it with a container <n>[to hide it]</n>, or puts it under a bed; instead, he puts it on a lampstand, so that those who come in may see the light.</J>?Å-‚wê<J>But as for that <i>seed</i> in the good soil, these are the ones who have heard the word with a good and noble heart, and hold on to it tightly, and bear fruit with patience.</J>oÅ,ƒWê<J>The <i>seed</i> which fell among the thorns, these are the ones who have heard, but as they go on their way they are suffocated with the anxieties and riches and pleasures of this life, and they bring no fruit to maturity.</J>‚.Å+„Uê
<J>Those on the rocky <i>soil are</i> the people who, when they hear, receive <i>and</i> welcome the word with joy; but these have no <i>firmly grounded</i> root. They believe for a while, and in time of trial <i>and</i> temptation they fall away <n>[from Me and abandon their faith]</n>.</J>bÅ*ƒ=ê<J>Those beside the road are the people who have heard; then the devil comes and takes the message <n>[of God]</n> away from their hearts, so that they will not believe <n>[in Me as the Messiah]</n> and be saved.</J>
Å)‚
ê<pb/><J>"Now <n>[the meaning of]</n> the parable is this: The seed is the word of God <n>[concerning eternal salvation]</n>.</J>‚!Å(„;ê
And He said, <J>"To you <n>[who have been chosen]</n> it has been granted to know <i>and</i> recognize the<f>[1]</f> mysteries of the kingdom of God, but to the rest it is in parables, so that <i>though</i> <e>seeing they may not see, and hearing they may not understand</e>.</J>QÅ'ê	<pb/>Now His disciples <i>began</i> asking Him what this parable meant.ZÅ&ƒ-ê<J>And some fell into good soil, and grew up and produced a crop a hundred times as great."</J> As He said these things, He called out, <J>"He who has ears to hear, let him hear <i>and</i> heed My words."</J>kÅ%Oê<J>Other <i>seed</i> fell among the thorns, and the thorns grew up with it and choked it out.</J>!Å$‚;ê<J>And some <i>seed</i> fell on <n>[shallow soil covering]</n> the rocks, and as soon as it sprouted, it withered away, because it had no moisture.</J>#Å#‚?ê<J>"The sower went out to sow his seed; and as he sowed, some fell beside the road and it was trampled underfoot, and the birds of the sky ate it up.</J>Å"‚êWhen a large crowd was gathering together, and people from city after city were coming to Him, He spoke <n>[to them]</n> using a parable:VÅ!ƒ%êand Joanna, the wife of Chuza, Herod's household steward, and Susanna, and many others who were contributing to their support out of their private means <n>[as was the custom for a rabbi's disciples]</n>.@Å ‚yêand <i>also</i> some women who had been healed of evil spirits and diseases: Mary, called Magdalene <n>[from the city of Magdala in Galilee]</n>, from whom seven demons had come out,VÅ	ƒ'ê<pb/>Soon afterward, Jesus <i>began</i> going around from one city and village to another, preaching and proclaiming <i>the good news of</i> the kingdom of God. The twelve <n>[disciples]</n> were with Him,Å‚1ê2Jesus said to the woman, <J>"Your faith <n>[in Me]</n> has saved you; go in peace <n>[free from the distress experienced because of sin]</n>."</J>Å‚ê1Those who were reclining <i>at the table</i> with Him began saying among themselves, "Who is this who even forgives sins?">Åwê0Then He said to her, <J>"Your sins are forgiven."</J>Å‚ê/<J>Therefore I say to you, her sins, which are many, are forgiven, for she loved much; but he who is forgiven little, loves little."</J>Å‚+ê.<J>You did not <n>[even]</n> anoint My head with <n>[ordinary]</n> oil, but she has anointed My feet with <n>[costly and rare]</n> perfume.</J>{Åoê-<J>You gave Me no <n>[welcoming]</n> kiss, but from the moment I came in, she has not ceased to kiss My feet.</J>‚FÅ…ê,Then turning toward the woman, He said to Simon, <J>"Do you see this woman? I came into your house <n>[but you failed to extend to Me the usual courtesies shown to a guest]</n>; you gave Me no water for My feet, but she has wet My feet with her tears and wiped them with her hair <n>[demonstrating her love]</n>.</J>Å{ê+Simon answered, "The one, I take it, for whom he forgave more." Jesus said to him, <J>"You have decided correctly."</J>
Å‚
ê*<J>When they had no means of repaying <n>[the debts]</n>, he freely forgave them both. So which of them will love him more?"</J>zÅmê)<J>"A certain moneylender had two debtors: one owed him five hundred<f>[8]</f> denarii, and the other fifty.</J>Å‚ê(Jesus, answering, said to the Pharisee, <J>"Simon, I have something to say to you."</J> And he replied, "Teacher, say it."‚Å„ê'Now when <n>[Simon]</n> the Pharisee who had invited Him saw this, he said to himself, "If this Man were a prophet He would know who and what sort of woman this is who is touching Him, that she is a <n>[notorious]</n> sinner <n>[an outcast, devoted to sin]</n>."‚ Å„9ê&and standing behind Him at His feet, weeping, she began wetting His feet with her tears, and wiped them with the hair of her head, and <n>[respectfully]</n> kissed His feet <n>[as an act signifying both affection and submission]</n> and<f>[7]</f> anointed them with the perfume.gÅƒGê%Now there was a woman in the city who was <n>[known as]</n> a<f>[5]</f> sinner; and<f>[6]</f> when she found out that He was reclining <i>at the table</i> in the Pharisee's house, she brought an alabaster vial of perfume;%Å‚Cê$One of the Pharisees asked Jesus to eat with him, and He went into the Pharisee's house <n>[in the region of Galilee]</n> and reclined <i>at the table</i>.(Å‚Iê#<J>Yet wisdom is vindicated <i>and</i> shown to be right by all her children <n>[by the lifestyle, moral character, and good deeds of her followers]</n>."</J>ZÅƒ-ê"<J>The Son of Man has come eating and drinking, and you say, 'Look, a man who is a glutton and a <n>[heavy]</n> wine-drinker, a friend of tax collectors and sinners <n>[including non-observant Jews]</n>.'</J>uÅ
cê!<J>For John the Baptist has come neither eating bread nor drinking wine, and you say, 'He has a demon!'</J>‚AÅ„{ê <J>They are like children sitting in the market place and calling to one another, and saying, 'We played the flute for you <n>[pretending to be at a wedding]</n>, and you did not dance; we sang a dirge <n>[pretending to be at a funeral]</n>, and you did not weep <n>[so nothing we did appealed to you]</n>.'</J>Å‚ê<pb/><J>"To what then shall I compare the people of this generation <n>[who set aside God's plan]</n>, and what are they like?</J>5Å
‚cêBut the Pharisees and the lawyers <n>[who were experts in the Mosaic Law]</n> annulled <i>and</i> set aside God's purpose for themselves, not having been baptized by John.$Å	‚AêAll the people and the tax collectors who heard Jesus, acknowledged <n>[the validity of]</n> God's justice, having been baptized with the baptism of John.3Å‚_ê<pb/><J>I tell you, among those born of women there is no one greater than John; yet he who is least in the kingdom of God is greater <n>[in privilege]</n> than he."</J>_Åƒ7ê<t><J>This is the one of whom it is written <n>[by the prophet Malachi]</n>,</J> </t><br/><t>'<J><e>Behold, I send My messenger ahead of You</e>,</J> </t><t><J><e>Who will prepare Your way before You</e>.'</J></t>KÅƒê<J>But what did you <n>[really]</n> go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I say to you, and one<f>[4]</f> far more <n>[eminent and remarkable]</n> than a prophet <n>[who foretells the future]</n>.</J>NÅƒê<J>But what did you go out to see? A man dressed in soft clothing <n>[entirely unsuited for the harsh wilderness]</n>? Those who wear splendid clothing and live in luxury are in royal palaces!</J>5Å‚cê<pb/>When John's messengers left, Jesus began speaking to the crowds about John: <J>"What did you go out to the wilderness to see?<f>[3]</f> A reed shaken by the wind?</J>eÅCê<J>Blessed <n>[joyful, spiritually favored]</n> is he who does not take offense at Me."</J>‚Å„/êSo He replied to them, <J>"Go and tell John about everything you have seen and heard: the <e>blind receive sight</e>, the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead are raised up, and the <e>poor have the good news</e> (gospel) <e>preached to them</e>.</J>:Å‚mêAt that very hour Jesus healed many <i>people</i> of sicknesses and infirmities and evil spirits; and He gave <n>[the gracious gift of]</n> sight to many <i>who were</i> blind.$Å ‚AêSo the men came to Jesus and said, "John the Baptist sent us to You to ask, 'Are You the Expected One (the Messiah), or should we look for someone else?'"%Ä‚CêJohn called two of his disciples and sent them to the Lord, saying, "Are You the<f>[2]</f> Expected One (the Messiah), or should we look for someone else?"VÄ~%êJohn's disciples brought word to him <n>[in prison]</n> of all these things.aÄ};êThis news about Him spread through all of Judea and in all the surrounding countryside.‚Ä|„êFear <i>and</i> profound awe gripped them all, and they <i>began</i> glorifying <i>and</i> honoring <i>and</i> praising God, saying, "A great prophet has arisen among us!" and, "God has visited His people <n>[to help and care for and provide for them]</n>!"gÄ{GêThe man who was dead sat up and began to speak. And <i>Jesus</i> gave him back to his mother.;Äz‚oêAnd He came up and touched the bier <n>[on which the body rested]</n>, and the pallbearers stood still. And He said, <J>"Young man, I say to you, arise <n>[from death]</n>!"</J>rÄy]ê
When the Lord saw her, He felt <n>[great]</n> compassion for her, and said to her, <J>"Do not weep."</J>RÄxƒêNow as He approached the city gate, a dead man was being carried outâ€”the only son of his mother, and she was a widow. And a large crowd from the city was with her <n>[in the funeral procession]</n>.Äw‚ê<pb/>Soon afterward Jesus went to a city called Nain <n>[near Nazareth]</n>, and His disciples and a large crowd accompanied Him.kÄvOê
When the messengers who had been sent returned to the house, they found the slave in good health.SÄuƒê	Now when Jesus heard this, He was amazed at him, and turned and said to the crowd that was following Him, <J>"I say to you, not even in Israel have I found such great faith <n>[as this man's]</n>."</J>HÄtƒ	êFor I also am a man subject to authority, with soldiers under me; and I say to this one, 'Go,' and he goes, and to another, 'Come,' and he comes, and to my slave, 'Do this,' and he does it."}ÄssêTherefore I did not even consider myself worthy to come to You. But just speak a word, and my slave will be healed.IÄrƒêAnd Jesus went with them. But when He was near the house, the centurion sent friends to Him, saying, "Lord, do not trouble Yourself further, for I am not worthy for You to come under my roof.aÄq;êbecause he loves our nation and he built us our synagogue <n>[at his own expense]</n>."Äp‚êWhen they reached Jesus, they pleaded with Him earnestly <n>[to come]</n>, saying, "He is worthy for You to do this for him,Äo‚êWhen the centurion heard about Jesus, he sent some Jewish elders to Him, asking Him to come and save the life of his slave.Än‚ê<pb/>Now a <n>[Roman]</n><f>[1]</f> centurion's slave, who was highly regarded by him, was sick and on the verge of death.nÄm	Wê<pb/>After He had finished all that He had to say in the hearing of the people, He went to Capernaum.‚*Äl„Mê1<J>But the one who has <n>[merely]</n> heard and has not practiced <n>[what I say]</n>, is like a <n>[foolish]</n> man who built a house on the ground without <i>any</i> foundation, and the torrent burst against it; and it immediately collapsed, and the ruin of that house was great."</J>‚0Äk„Yê0<J>he is like a <n>[far-sighted, practical, and sensible]</n> man building a house, who dug deep and laid a foundation on the rock; and when a flood occurred, the torrent burst against that house and yet could not shake it, because it had been securely built <i>and</i> founded on the rock.</J>rÄj]ê/<J>Everyone who comes to Me and listens to My words and obeys them, I will show you whom he is like:</J>XÄi)ê.<J>"Why do you call Me, 'Lord, Lord,' and do not practice what I tell you?</J>‚^Äh…5ê-<J>The <n>[intrinsically]</n> good man produces what is good <i>and</i> honorable <i>and</i> moral out of the good treasure <n>[stored]</n> in his heart; and the <n>[intrinsically]</n> evil <i>man</i> produces what is wicked <i>and</i> depraved out of the evil <n>[in his heart]</n>; for his mouth speaks from the overflow of his heart.</J>/Äg‚Wê,<J>For each tree is known <i>and</i> identified by its own fruit. For figs are not picked from thorn bushes, nor is a cluster of grapes picked from a briar bush.</J>Äf}ê+<J>For there is no good tree which produces bad fruit, nor, on the other hand, a bad tree which produces good fruit.</J>‚PÄe…ê*<J>How can you say to your brother, 'Brother, allow me to take out the speck that is in your eye,' when you yourself do not see the log that is in your own eye? You hypocrite (play actor, pretender), first take the log out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to take out the speck that is in your brother's eye.</J>Äd‚ê)<J>Why do you look at the speck that is in your brother's eye, but do not notice <i>or</i> consider the log that is in your own eye?</J>Äc‚	ê(<J>A student is not superior to his teacher; but everyone, after he has been completely trained, will be like his teacher.</J>Äb‚)ê'<pb/>He also told them a parable: <J>"Can a blind man guide <n>[another]</n> blind man? Will they not both fall into a hole in the ground?</J>‚0Äa„Yê&<J>Give, and it will be given to you. They will pour into your lap a good measureâ€”pressed down, shaken together, and running over <n>[with no space left for more]</n>. For with the standard of measurement you use <n>[when you do good to others]</n>, it will be measured to you in return."</J>‚jÄ`…Mê%<pb/><J>"<f>[12]</f> Do not judge <n>[others self-righteously]</n>, and you will not be judged; do not condemn <n>[others when you are guilty and unrepentant]</n>, and you will not be condemned <n>[for your hypocrisy]</n>; pardon <n>[others when they truly repent and change]</n>, and you will be pardoned <n>[when you truly repent and change]</n>.</J>sÄ__ê$<J>Be merciful (responsive, compassionate, tender) just as your <n>[heavenly]</n> Father is merciful.</J>‚]Ä^…3ê#<J>But love <n>[that is, unselfishly seek the best or higher good for]</n> your enemies, and do good, and lend,<f>[11]</f> expecting nothing in return; for your reward will be great (rich, abundant), and you will be sons of the Most High; because He Himself is kind <i>and</i> gracious <i>and</i> good to the ungrateful and the wicked.</J>QÄ]ƒê"<J>If you lend <n>[money]</n> to those from whom you expect to receive <n>[it back]</n>, what credit is that to you? Even sinners lend to sinners expecting to receive back the same <i>amount</i>.</J>vÄ\eê!<J>If you do good to those who do good to you, what credit is that to you? For even sinners do the same.</J>Ä[‚ê <J>If you <n>[only]</n> love those who love you, what credit is that to you? For even sinners love those who love them.</J>FÄZê<J>Treat others the same way you want them to treat you.</J>vÄYeê<J>Give to everyone who asks of you.<f>[10]</f> Whoever takes away what is yours, do not demand it back.</J>‚ÄX„ê<J>Whoever<f>[9]</f> strikes you on the cheek, offer him the other one also <n>[simply ignore insignificant insults or losses and do not bother to retaliateâ€”maintain your dignity]</n>. Whoever takes away your coat, do not withhold your shirt from him either.</J>hÄWIê<J>bless <i>and</i> show kindness to those who curse you, pray for those who mistreat you.</J>uÄVƒcê<pb/><J>"But I say to you who hear <n>[Me and pay attention to My words]</n>:<f>[8]</f> Love <n>[that is, unselfishly seek the best or higher good for]</n> your enemies, <n>[make it a practice to]</n> do good to those who hate you,</J>#ÄU‚?ê<J>Woe <i>to you</i> when all the people speak well of you <i>and</i> praise you, for their fathers used to treat the false prophets in the same way.</J>gÄTƒGê<J>Woe to you who are well-fed (gorged, satiated) now, for you will be hungry. Woe <i>to you</i> who laugh now <n>[enjoying a life of self-indulgence]</n>, for you will mourn and weep <n>[and deeply long for God]</n>.</J>‚ÄS„'ê<J>But<f>[7]</f> woe (judgment is coming) to you who are rich <n>[and place your faith in possessions while remaining spiritually impoverished]</n>, for you are <n>[already]</n> receiving your comfort in full <n>[and there is nothing left to be awarded to you]</n>.</J>:ÄR‚mê<J>Rejoice on that day and leap for joy, for your reward in heaven is great <n>[absolutely inexhaustible]</n>; for their fathers used to treat the prophets in the same way.</J>‚ÄQ„5ê<J>Blessed <n>[morally courageous and spiritually alive with life-joy in God's goodness]</n> are you when people hate you, and exclude you <n>[from their fellowship]</n>, and insult you, and scorn your name as evil because of <n>[your association with]</n> the Son of Man.</J>‚|ÄP…qê<J>Blessed <n>[joyful, nourished by God's goodness]</n> are you who hunger now <n>[for righteousness, actively seeking right standing with God]</n>, for you will be <n>[completely]</n> satisfied. Blessed <n>[forgiven, refreshed by God's grace]</n> are you who weep now <n>[over your sins and repent]</n>, for you will laugh <n>[when the burden of sin is lifted]</n>.</J>‚PÄO…êAnd looking toward His disciples, He began<f>[6]</f> speaking: <J>"Blessed <n>[spiritually prosperous, happy, to be admired]</n> are you <i>who are</i> poor <n>[in spirit, those devoid of spiritual arrogance, those who regard themselves as insignificant]</n>, for the kingdom of God is yours <n>[both now and forever]</n>.</J>{ÄNoêAll the people were trying to touch Him, because <n>[healing]</n> power was coming from Him and healing them all.ÄM‚'êwho had come to listen to Him and to be healed of their diseases. Even those who were troubled by unclean spirits (demons) were being healed.iÄLƒKê<pb/>Then Jesus came down with them and stood on a level place; and <i>there was</i> a large crowd of His disciples, and a vast multitude of people from all over Judea and Jerusalem and the coastal region of Tyre and Sidon,ÄK‚êJudas <n>[also called Thaddaeus]</n> <i>the son</i> of James, and Judas Iscariot, who became a traitor <n>[to the Lord]</n>.ÄJ‚êand Matthew (Levi, the tax collector) and Thomas; and<f>[5]</f> James <i>the son</i> of Alphaeus, and Simon who was called the Zealot;2ÄI‚]êSimon, whom He also named Peter, and his brother Andrew; and <n>[the brothers]</n><f>[4]</f> James and John; and Philip, and Bartholomew <n>[also called Nathanael]</n>;,ÄH‚Qê
When day came, He called His disciples and selected twelve of them, whom He also named<f>[3]</f> apostles (special messengers, personally chosen representatives):qÄG[êNow at this time Jesus went off to the mountain to pray, and He spent the whole night in prayer to God.*ÄF‚MêBut the scribes and Pharisees were filled with senseless rage <n>[and lacked spiritual insight]</n>, and discussed with one another what they might do to Jesus.ÄE‚#ê
After looking around at them all, He said to the man, <J>"Stretch out your hand!"</J> And he did, and his hand was <n>[fully]</n> restored.ÄD‚%ê	Then Jesus said to them, <J>"I ask you directly: Is it lawful to do good on the Sabbath or to do evil, to save a life or to destroy it?"</J>#ÄC‚?êBut He was aware of their thoughts, and He said to the man with the withered hand, <J>"Get up and come forward."</J> So he got up and stood <i>there</i>.cÄBƒ?êThe scribes and the Pharisees were watching Him closely <n>[with malicious intent]</n>, to see if He would <n>[actually]</n> heal <n>[someone]</n> on the Sabbath, so that they might find <i>a reason</i> to accuse Him.}ÄAsê<pb/>On another Sabbath He went into the synagogue and taught, and a man was present whose right hand was withered.oÄ@WêJesus was saying to them, <J>"The Son of Man (the Messiah) is Lord <n>[even]</n> of the Sabbath."</J>nÄ?ƒUê<J>how he went into the house of God, and took and ate the<f>[2]</f> consecrated bread, which is not lawful <n>[for anyone]</n> to eat except the priests alone, and <n>[how he also]</n> gave it to the men who were with him?"</J>Ä>‚7êJesus replied to them, <J>"Have you not even read <n>[in the Scriptures]</n> what David did when he was hungry, he and those who were with himâ€”</J>hÄ=IêBut some of the Pharisees said, "Why are you doing what<f>[1]</f> is unlawful on the Sabbath?"AÄ<	‚}ê<pb/>One Sabbath while Jesus was passing through fields of standing grain, it happened that His disciples were picking the heads of grain, rubbing them in their hands, and eating them.mÄ;Sê'<J>And no one, after drinking old <i>wine</i>, wishes for new; for he says, 'The old is fine.'"</J>>Ä:wê&<J>But new wine must be put into fresh wineskins.</J>UÄ9ƒ#ê%<J>And no one puts new wine into old<f>[3]</f> wineskins; otherwise the new <n>[fermenting]</n> wine will <n>[expand and]</n> burst the skins and it will be spilled out, and the skins will be ruined.</J>YÄ8ƒ+ê$He also told them a parable: <J>"No one tears a piece of</J> <J>cloth from a new garment and puts it on an old one; otherwise he will both tear the new, and the piece from the new will not match the old.</J>Ä7‚+ê#<J>But days <n>[for mourning]</n> will come when the bridegroom is <n>[forcefully]</n> taken away from them. They will fast in those days."</J>sÄ6_ê"Jesus said to them, <J>"Can you make the wedding guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them?</J>cÄ5ƒ?ê!<pb/>Then they said to Him, "The disciples of John <n>[the Baptist]</n> often practice fasting and offer prayers <n>[of special petition]</n>, and so do the <i>disciples</i> of the Pharisees; but Yours eat and drink."sÄ4ƒ_ê <J>I did not come to call the <n>[self-proclaimed]</n> righteous <n>[who see no need to repent]</n>, but sinners to repentance <n>[to change their old way of thinking, to turn from sin and to seek God and His righteousness]</n>."</J>Ä3‚	êAnd Jesus replied to them, <J>"It is not those who are healthy who need a physician, but <n>[only]</n> those who are sick.</J>‚Ä2„	êThe Pharisees and their scribes <n>[seeing those with whom He was associating]</n> <i>began</i> murmuring in discontent to His disciples, asking, "Why are you eating and drinking with the tax collectors and sinners <n>[including non-observant Jews]</n>?"3Ä1‚_ê<pb/>Levi (Matthew) gave a great banquet for Him at his house; and there was a large crowd of tax collectors and others who were reclining <i>at the table</i> with them.mÄ0SêAnd he left everything behind and got up and <i>began</i> to follow Jesus <n>[as His disciple]</n>.‚Ä/„êAfter this Jesus went out and noticed a tax collector named Levi (Matthew) sitting at the tax booth; and He said to him, <J>"Follow Me <n>[as My disciple, accepting Me as your Master and Teacher and walking the same path of life that I walk]</n>."</J>KÄ.ƒêThey were all astonished, and they <i>began</i> glorifying God; and they were filled with <n>[reverential]</n> fear and kept saying, "We have seen wonderful <i>and</i> incredible things today!"yÄ-kêHe immediately stood up before them, picked up his stretcher, and went home glorifying <i>and</i> praising God.nÄ,ƒUê<J>But, in order that you may know that the Son of Man (the Messiah) has authority <i>and</i> power on earth to forgive sins"</J>â€”He said to the paralyzed man, <J>"I say to you, get up, pick up your stretcher and go home."</J>eÄ+Cê<J>Which is easier, to say, 'Your sins are forgiven you,' or to say, 'Get up and walk'?</J>Ä*‚êBut Jesus, knowing their <n>[hostile]</n> thoughts, answered them, <J>"Why are you questioning <n>[these things]</n> in your hearts?</J>‚]Ä)…3êThe scribes and the Pharisees began to consider <i>and</i> question <n>[the implications of what He had said]</n>, saying, "Who is this man who speaks blasphemies <n>[by claiming the rights and prerogatives of God]</n>? Who can forgive sins <n>[that is, remove guilt, nullify sin's penalty, and assign righteousness]</n> except God alone?"Ä(‚êWhen Jesus saw their <n>[active]</n> faith <n>[springing from confidence in Him]</n>, He said, <J>"Man, your sins are forgiven."</J>‚Ä'„êBut finding no way to bring him in because of the crowd, they went up on the<f>[2]</f> roof <n>[and removed some tiles to make an opening]</n> and lowered him through the tiles with his stretcher, into the middle <i>of the crowd</i>, in front of Jesus.Ä&‚ê<i>Some</i> men came carrying on a stretcher a man who was paralyzed, and they tried to bring him in and lay him down in front of Jesus.mÄ%ƒSê<pb/>One day as He was teaching, there were Pharisees and teachers of the Law sitting there who had come from every village of Galilee and Judea and from Jerusalem. And the power of the Lord was <i>present</i> with Him to heal.cÄ$?êBut Jesus Himself would often slip away to the wilderness and pray <n>[in seclusion]</n>.Ä#‚	êBut the news about Him was spreading farther, and large crowds kept gathering to hear Him and to be healed of their illnesses.‚Ä"„êJesus ordered him to tell no one <n>[that he might happen to meet]</n>, <J>"But go and show yourself to the priest, and make an offering for your purification, just as Moses commanded, as a testimony (witness) to them <n>[that this is a work of Messiah]</n>."</J>Ä!‚ê
And Jesus reached out His hand and touched him, saying, <J>"I am willing; be cleansed."</J> And immediately the leprosy left him.yÄ ƒkêWhile Jesus was in one of the cities, there came a man covered with <n>[an advanced case of]</n> leprosy; and when he saw Jesus, he fell on his face and begged Him, saying, "Lord, if You are willing, You can make me clean <i>and</i> well."?Ä‚wêAfter they had brought their boats to land, they left everything and<f>[1]</f> followed Him <n>[becoming His disciples, believing and trusting in Him and following His example]</n>.5Ä‚cê
and so were James and John, sons of Zebedee, who were partners with Simon <n>[Peter]</n>. Jesus said to Simon, <J>"Have no fear; from now on you will be catching men!"</J>lÄQê	For he and all his companions were completely astounded at the catch of fish which they had taken; ÄyêBut when Simon Peter saw this, he fell down at Jesus' knees, saying, "Go away from me, for I am a sinful man, O Lord!"0Ä‚Yêso they signaled to their partners in the other boat to come and help them. And they came and filled both of the boats <n>[with fish]</n>, so that they began to sink.}ÄsêWhen they had done this, they caught a great number of fish, and their nets were <n>[at the point of]</n> breaking;ZÄƒ-êSimon replied, "Master, we worked hard all night <n>[to the point of exhaustion]</n> and caught nothing <n>[in our nets]</n>, but at Your word I will <n>[do as you say and]</n> lower the nets <n>[again]</n>." Ä‚9êWhen He had finished speaking, He said to Simon <n>[Peter]</n>, <J>"Put out into the deep water and lower your nets for a catch <n>[of fish]</n>."</J>9Ä‚kêHe got into one of the boats, which was Simon's, and asked him to put out a little distance from the shore. And He sat down and <i>began</i> teaching the crowds from the boat.Ä‚êthat He saw two boats lying at the edge of the lake, but the fishermen had gotten out of them and were washing their nets.1Ä	‚]ê<pb/>Now it happened that while Jesus was standing by the Lake of Gennesaret (Sea of Galilee), with the people crowding all around Him and listening to the word of God;xÄiê,<pb/>So He continued preaching in the synagogues of Judea <n>[the country of the Jews, including Galilee]</n>.Ä‚+ê+But He said, <J>"I must preach <n>[the good news of]</n> the kingdom of God to the other cities also, because I was sent for this purpose."</J>VÄƒ%ê*<pb/>When daybreak came, Jesus left <n>[Simon Peter's house]</n> and went to a secluded place; and the crowds were searching for Him, and <n>[they]</n> came to Him and tried to keep Him from leaving them.VÄƒ%ê)Demons also were coming out of many people, shouting, "You are the Son of God!" But He rebuked them and would not allow them to speak, because they knew that He was the Christ (the Messiah, the Anointed).‚Ä„+ê(<pb/>While the sun was setting <n>[marking the end of the Sabbath day]</n>, all those who had any <i>who were</i> sick with various diseases brought them to Jesus; and laying His hands on each one of them, He was healing them <n>[exhibiting His authority as Messiah]</n>.Ä‚!ê'Standing over her, He rebuked the fever, and it left her; and immediately she got up and <i>began</i> serving them <n>[as her guests]</n>.2Ä‚]ê&Then Jesus got up and left the synagogue and went to Simon's (Peter's) house. Now Simon's mother-in-law was suffering from a high fever, and they asked Him to help her._Ä
7ê%And the news about Him spread into every place in the surrounding district (Galilee).EÄƒê$They were all astonished <i>and</i> in awe, and <i>began</i> saying to one another, "What is this message? For with authority and power He commands the unclean spirits and they come out!"
