<J>No servant can serve two masters; for either he will hate the one and love the other, or he will stand devotedly by the one and despise the other. You cannot serve <i>both</i> God and mammon <n>[that is, your earthly possessions or anything else you trust in and rely on instead of God]</n>."</J>dÈ!ƒAê<J>And if you have not been faithful in <i>the use of</i> that <n>[earthly wealth]</n> which belongs to another <n>[whether God or man, and of which you are a trustee]</n>, who will give you that which is your own?</J>
È ‚ê<J>Therefore if you have not been faithful in the <i>use of</i> earthly wealth, who will entrust the true <i>riches</i> to you?</J>$È‚Aê
<pb/><J>"He who is faithful in a very little thing is also faithful in much; and he who is dishonest in a very little thing is also dishonest in much.</J>‚9È„kê	<J>And I tell you <n>[learn from this]</n>, make friends for yourselves <n>[for eternity]</n> by means of the<f>[4]</f> wealth of unrighteousness <n>[that is, use material resources as a way to further the work of God]</n>, so that when it runs out, they will welcome you into the eternal dwellings.</J>‚uÈ…cê<J>And his master commended the unjust manager <n>[not for his misdeeds, but]</n> because he had acted shrewdly <n>[by preparing for his future unemployment]</n>; for the sons of this age <n>[the non-believers]</n> are shrewder in relation to their own kind <n>[that is, to the ways of the secular world]</n> than are the sons of light <n>[the believers]</n>.</J>*È‚Mê<J>Then he said to another, 'And how much do you owe?' He said, 'A hundred<f>[3]</f> measures of wheat.' He said to him, 'Take your bill, and write eighty.'</J>%È‚Cê<J>He said, 'A hundred<f>[1]</f> measures of <n>[olive]</n> oil.' And he said to him, 'Take your bill, and sit down quickly and write<f>[2]</f> fifty.'</J>{Èoê<J>So he summoned his master's debtors one by one, and he said to the first, 'How much do you owe my master?'</J>%È‚Cê<J>I know what I will do, so that when I am removed from the management, people <n>[who are my master's debtors]</n> will welcome me into their homes.'</J>ZÈƒ-ê<J>The manager <n>[of the estate]</n> said to himself, 'What will I do, since my master is taking the management away from me? I am not strong enough to dig <n>[for a living]</n>, and I am ashamed to beg.</J>?È‚wê<J>So he called him and said to him, 'What is this I hear about you? Give an accounting of your management <n>[of my affairs]</n>, for you can no longer be <n>[my]</n> manager.'</J>‚È	„ê<pb/>Now Jesus was also saying to the disciples, <J>"There was a certain rich man who had a manager <n>[of his estate]</n>, and accusations <n>[against this man]</n> were brought to him, that this man was squandering his <n>[master's]</n> possessions.</J>4È‚aê <J>But it was fitting to celebrate and rejoice, for this brother of yours was <n>[as good as]</n> dead and <i>has begun</i> to live. He was lost and has been found.'"</J>eÈCê<J>The father said to him, 'Son, you are always with me, and all that is mine is yours.</J>!È‚;ê<J>but when this <n>[other]</n> son of yours arrived, who has devoured your estate with immoral women, you slaughtered that fattened calf for him!'</J>|Èƒqê<J>But he said to his father, 'Look! These many years I have served you, and I have never neglected <i>or</i> disobeyed your command. Yet you have never given me <n>[so much as]</n> a young goat, so that I might celebrate with my friends;</J>)È‚Kê<J>But the elder brother became angry <i>and</i> deeply resentful and was not willing to go in; and his father came out and <i>began</i> pleading with him.</J>È‚/ê<J>And he said to him, 'Your brother has come, and your father has killed the fattened calf because he has received him back safe and sound.'</J>qÈ[ê<J>So he summoned one of the servants and <i>began</i> asking what this <n>[celebration]</n> meant.</J>È‚ê<pb/><J>"Now his older son was in the field; and when he returned and approached the house, he heard music and dancing.</J>È
‚ê<J>for this son of mine was <n>[as good as]</n> dead and is alive again; he was lost and has been found.' So they began to celebrate.</J>{Èoê<J>And bring the fattened calf and slaughter it, and let us <n>[invite everyone and]</n> feast and celebrate;</J>OÈƒê<J>But the father said to his servants, 'Quickly bring out the best robe <n>[for the guest of honor]</n> and put it on him; and give him a<f>[2]</f> ring for his hand, and sandals for his feet.</J>È
‚ê<J>And the son said to him, 'Father, I have sinned against heaven and in your sight; I am no longer worthy to be called your son.'</J>FÈ	ƒê<J>So he got up and came to his father. But while he was still a long way off, his father saw him and was moved with compassion <i>for him</i>, and ran and embraced him and kissed him.</J>rÈ]ê<J>I am no longer worthy to be called your son; <n>[just]</n> treat me like one of your hired men."'</J>Èê<J>I will get up and go to my father, and I will say to him, "Father, I have sinned against heaven and in your sight.</J>*È‚Mê<J>But when he <n>[finally]</n> came to his senses, he said, 'How many of my father's hired men have more than enough food, while I am dying here of hunger!</J>;È‚oê<J>He would have gladly eaten the <n>[carob]</n> pods that the pigs were eating <n>[but they could not satisfy his hunger]</n>, and no one was giving <i>anything</i> to him.</J>È‚ê<J>So he went and forced himself on one of the citizens of that country, who sent him into his fields to<f>[1]</f> feed pigs.</J>È‚ê<J>Now when he had spent everything, a severe famine occurred in that country, and he began to do without <i>and</i> be in need.</J>NÈƒê
<J>A few days later, the younger son gathered together everything <n>[that he had]</n> and traveled to a distant country, and there he wasted his fortune in reckless <i>and</i> immoral living.</J>6È‚eê<J>The younger of them <n>[inappropriately]</n> said to his father, 'Father, give me the share of the property that falls to me.' So he divided the estate between them.</J>:È oêThen He said, <J>"A certain man had two sons.</J>‚)Ç„Kê
<J>In the same way, I tell you, there is joy in the presence of the angels of God over one sinner who repents <n>[that is, changes his inner selfâ€”his old way of thinking, regrets past sins, lives his life in a way that proves repentance; and seeks God's purpose for his life]</n>."</J>"Ç~‚=ê	<J>And when she has found it, she calls together her <n>[women]</n> friends and neighbors, saying, 'Rejoice with me, because I found the lost coin!'</J>LÇ}ƒê<J>"Or what woman, if she has ten silver coins <n>[each one equal to a day's wages]</n> and loses one coin, does not light a lamp and sweep the house and search carefully until she finds it?</J>0Ç|‚Yê<J>I tell you, in the same way there will be more joy in heaven over one sinner who repents than over ninety-nine righteous people who have no need of repentance.</J>Ç{‚7ê<J>And when he gets home, he calls together his friends and his neighbors, saying to them, 'Rejoice with me, because I have found my lost sheep!'</J>RÇzê<J>And when he has found it, he lays it on his shoulders, rejoicing.</J>QÇyƒê<J>"What man among you, if he has a hundred sheep and loses one of them, does not leave the ninety-nine in the wilderness and go after the one which is lost, <n>[searching]</n> until he finds it?</J>+ÇxQê<pb/>So He told them this parable:&Çw‚EêBoth the Pharisees and the scribes <i>began</i> muttering <i>and</i> complaining, saying, "This man accepts <i>and</i> welcomes sinners and eats with them."Çv	‚ê<pb/>Now all the tax collectors and sinners <n>[including non-observant Jews]</n> were coming near Jesus to listen to Him.Çu‚1ê#<J>It is fit neither for the soil nor for the manure pile; it is thrown away. He who has ears to hear, let him hear <i>and</i> heed My words."</J>pÇtYê"<pb/><J>"Therefore, salt is good; but if salt has become tasteless, with what will it be seasoned?</J>*Çs‚Mê!<J>So then, none of you can be My disciple who does not <n>[carefully consider the cost and then for My sake]</n><f>[4]</f> give up all his own possessions.</J>3Çr‚_ê <J>Or else <n>[if he feels he is not powerful enough]</n>, while the other <n>[king]</n> is still a far distance away, he sends an envoy and asks for terms of peace.</J>kÇqƒOê<J>Or what king, when he sets out to meet another king in battle, will not first sit down and consider whether he is strong enough with ten thousand men to encounter the one who is coming against him with twenty thousand?</J>NÇpê<J>saying, 'This man began to build and was not able to finish!'</J>Ço‚ê<J>Otherwise, when he has laid a foundation and is unable to finish <n>[the building]</n>, all who see it will begin to ridicule him,</J>9Çn‚kê<J>For which one of you, when he wants to build a watchtower <n>[for his guards]</n>, does not first sit down and calculate the cost, to see if he has enough to finish it?</J>‚Çm„5ê<J>Whoever does not carry his own cross <n>[expressing a willingness to endure whatever may come]</n> and follow after Me <n>[believing in Me, conforming to My example in living and, if need be, suffering or perhaps dying because of faith in Me]</n> cannot be My disciple.</J>‚?Çl„wê<J>"If anyone comes to Me, and does not<f>[3]</f> hate his own father and mother and wife and children and brothers and sisters, yes, and even his own life <n>[in the sense of indifference to or relative disregard for them in comparison with his attitude toward God]</n></J>â€”<J>he cannot be My disciple.</J>WÇk'êNow large crowds were going along with Jesus; and He turned and said to them,pÇjYê<J>For I tell you, not one of those who were invited <n>[and declined]</n> will taste my dinner.'"</J>3Çi‚_ê<J>Then the master told the servant, 'Go out into the highways and along the hedges, and compel them to come in, so that my house may be filled <n>[with guests]</n>.</J>Çh{ê<J>And the servant <n>[after returning]</n> said, 'Sir, what you commanded has been done, and still there is room.'</J>‚VÇg…%ê<J>So the servant came back and reported this to his master. Then <n>[his master,]</n> the head of the household, became angry <n>[at the rejections of his invitation]</n> and said to his servant, 'Go out quickly into the streets and the lanes of the city and bring in here the poor and the disabled and the blind and the lame.'</J>vÇfeê<J>And another said, 'I have <n>[recently]</n> married a wife, and for that reason I am unable to come.'</J>Çe‚ê<J>Another one said, 'I have purchased five yoke of oxen, and I am going to try them out; please consider me excused.'</J>5Çd‚cê<J>But they all alike began to make excuses. The first one said to him, 'I have purchased a piece of land and I have to go out and see it; please consider me excused.'</J>Çc‚	ê<J>and at the dinner hour he sent his servant to tell those who had been invited, 'Come, because everything is ready now.'</J>jÇbMêBut Jesus said to him, <J>"A man was giving a big dinner, and he invited many <i>guests</i>;</J>IÇaƒê<pb/>When one of those who were reclining <i>at the table</i> with Him heard this, he said to Him, "Blessed (happy, prosperous, to be admired) is he who will eat bread in the kingdom of God!"'Ç`‚Gê<J>and you will be blessed because they cannot repay you; for you will be repaid at the resurrection of the<f>[2]</f> righteous (the just, the upright)."</J>{Ç_oê
<J>But when you give a banquet <i>or</i> a reception, invite the poor, the disabled, the lame, and the blind,</J>‚Ç^„)ê<pb/>Jesus also went on to say to the one who had invited Him, <J>"When you give a luncheon or a dinner, do not invite your friends or your brothers or your relatives or wealthy neighbors, otherwise they may also invite you in return and that will be your repayment.</J>5Ç]‚cê<J>For everyone who exalts himself will be humbled <n>[before others]</n>, and he who <i>habitually</i> humbles himself (keeps a realistic self-view) will be exalted."</J>|Ç\ƒqê
