pb/>Then He said to them, <J>"How <i>is it that</i> people say that the Christ (the Messiah, the Anointed) is David's son?</J>É\}ê(And they did not dare to question Him further about anything <n>[because of the wisdom He displayed in His answers]</n>.sÉ[_ê'Some of the scribes replied, "Teacher, you have spoken well <n>[so that there is no room for blame]</n>."<ÉZ‚qê&<J>Now He is not the God of the dead, but of the living <n>[so these forefathers will be among the resurrected]</n>; for all live <n>[in a definite relationship]</n> to Him."</J>vÉYƒeê%<J>But <n>[as for the fact]</n> that the dead are raised <n>[from death]</n>, even Moses showed, in the <i>passage about the burning</i> bush, when he calls the Lord <e>the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob</e>.</J>PÉXƒê$<J>and they cannot die again, because they are <n>[immortal]</n> like the angels (equal to, angel-like). And they are<f>[4]</f> children of God, being<f>[5]</f> participants in the resurrection.</J>7ÉW‚gê#<J>but those who are considered worthy to gain that <n>[other world and that future]</n> age and the resurrection from the dead, neither marry nor are given in marriage;</J>ÉV‚ê"<pb/>Jesus said to them, <J>"The sons of this <n>[world and present]</n> age marry and <n>[the women]</n> are given in marriage;</J>qÉU[ê!So in the <n>[life after]</n> resurrection, whose wife does she become? For all seven had married her."%ÉTEê Finally the woman also died.]ÉS3êand the third married her, and in the same way all seven died, leaving no children.ÉR+êAnd the second,VÉQ%êNow there were seven brothers; and the first took a wife and died childless.xÉPƒiêand they questioned Him, saying, "Teacher, Moses wrote for us <n>[a law that]</n> <e>if a man's brother dies</e>, leaving a wife <e>and no children, his brother should</e><f>[3]</f><e>marry the wife and raise children for his brother</e>.WÉO'êNow some of the Sadducees (who say that there is no resurrection) came to HimÉN‚êThey were not able to seize on anything He said in the presence of the people; and being unnerved at His reply, they were silent.zÉMmêHe said to them, <J>"Then pay to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."</J>ÉL‚5ê<J>"Show Me a <n>[Roman]</n> denarius. Whose image and inscription does the coin have?"</J> They answered, "<n>[the Emperor Tiberius]</n> Caesar's."<ÉKsêBut He saw through their trickery and said to them,ÉJ‚êIs it lawful <n>[according to Jewish law and tradition]</n> for us to pay taxes (tribute money) to<f>[2]</f> Caesar or not?"ÉI‚7êThey asked Him, "Teacher, we know that You speak and teach correctly, and that You show no partiality to anyone, but teach the way of God truthfully.‚FÉH…êSo they watched <n>[for a chance to trap]</n> Him. They sent spies who pretended to be upright <i>and</i> sincere, in order that they might catch Him in some statement <n>[that they could distort and use against Him]</n>, so that they could turn Him over to the control and authority of <n>[Pilate]</n> the governor.NÉGƒêThe scribes and the chief priests tried to <n>[find a way to]</n> arrest Him at that very hour, but they were afraid of the people; because they understood that He spoke this parable against them.ÉF‚ê<pb/><J>Everyone who falls on that stone will be broken <i>and</i> shattered in pieces; and on whomever it falls, it will crush him."</J>‚ÉE„ê<t>But Jesus looked at them and said, <J>"What then is <n>[the meaning of]</n> this that is written:</J> </t><br/><t><J>'<e>The</e> <n>[very]</n> <e>Stone which the builders rejected</e>,</J> </t><t><J><e>this became the chief Cornerstone'</e>?</J></t>AÉD‚{ê<J>He will come and put these tenants to death and will give the vineyard to others."</J> When the chief priests, the scribes, and the elders heard this, they said, "May it never be!"ÉC{ê<J>So they threw the son out of the vineyard and killed him. What, then, will the owner of the vineyard do to them?</J>1ÉB‚[ê<J>But when the tenants saw him, they discussed it among themselves, saying, 'This <n>[man]</n> is the heir; let us kill him so that the inheritance will be ours.'</J>ÉA‚ê
<J>Then the owner of the vineyard said, 'What shall I do? I will send my beloved son; perhaps they will have respect for him.'</J>oÉ@Wê<J>And he sent yet a third; and this one too they wounded and threw out <n>[of the vineyard]</n>.</J>É?‚+ê<J>So he again sent another servant; they also beat him and dishonored <i>and</i> treated him disgracefully and sent him away empty-handed.</J>tÉ>ƒaê
<J>At <i>harvest</i> time he sent a servant <n>[as his representative]</n> to the tenants, so that they would give him <i>his share</i> of the fruit of the vineyard; but the tenants beat the servant and sent him away empty-handed.</J>=É=‚sê	Then He began to tell the people this parable: <J>"A man planted a vineyard and leased it to tenant farmers, and went on a journey for a long time <n>[to another country]</n>.</J>vÉ<eêThen Jesus said to them, <J>"Nor am I telling you by what <n>[kind of]</n> authority I do these things."</J>KÉ;êSo they replied that they did not know from where <i>it came</i>.
