So the chief priests planned to kill Lazarus also,8Ï^‚iô	<pb/>A large crowd of Jews learned that He was there <n>[at Bethany]</n>; and they came, not only because of Jesus, but also to see Lazarus, whom He had raised from the dead.SÏ]ô<J>You always have the poor with you, but you do not always have Me."</J>vÏ\eôSo Jesus said, <J>"Let her alone, so that she may keep <n>[the rest of]</n> it for the day of My burial.</J>‚Ï[„ôNow he said this, not because he cared about the poor <n>[for he had never cared about them]</n>, but because he was a thief; and since he had the money box <n>[serving as treasurer for the twelve disciples]</n>, he used to pilfer what was put into it.wÏZgô"Why was this perfume not sold for<f>[3]</f> three hundred denarii and <n>[the money]</n> given to the poor?"^ÏY5ôBut Judas Iscariot, one of His disciples, the one who was going to betray Him, said,[ÏXƒ/ôThen<f>[1]</f> Mary took a pound of very expensive perfume of pure<f>[2]</f> nard, and she poured it on Jesus' feet and wiped His feet with her hair; and the house was filled with the fragrance of the perfume.ÏW‚ôSo they gave a supper for Him there. Martha was serving, and Lazarus was one of those reclining <i>at the table</i> with Him.wÏV	iô<pb/>Six days before the Passover, Jesus went to Bethany, where Lazarus was, whom He had raised from the dead.ÏU‚ô9Now the chief priests and Pharisees had given orders that if anyone knew where He was, he was to report it so that they might arrest Him.+ÏT‚Oô8So they were looking for Jesus as they stood in the temple <n>[area]</n>, and saying among themselves, "What do you think? Will He not come to the feast at all?"_ÏSƒ7ô7<pb/>Now the Passover of the Jews was approaching, and many from the country went up to Jerusalem before Passover to purify themselves <n>[ceremonially, so that they would be able to participate in the feast]</n>.iÏRƒKô6<pb/>For that reason Jesus no longer walked openly among the Jews, but left there <i>and</i> went to the district that borders on the uninhabited wilderness, to a town called Ephraim; and He stayed there with the disciples.?ÏQyô5So from that day on they planned together to kill Him.ÏP‚'ô4and not only for the nation, but also for the purpose of gathering together into one body the children of God who have been scattered abroad.PÏOƒô3Now he did not say this <n>[simply]</n> on his own initiative; but being the high priest that year, he <n>[was unknowingly used by God and]</n> prophesied that Jesus was going to die for the nation,(ÏN‚Iô2Nor do you understand that it is expedient <i>and</i> politically advantageous for you that one man die for the people, and that the whole nation not perish."#ÏM‚?ô1But one of them,<f>[7]</f> Caiaphas, who was the high priest that year <n>[the year of Christ's crucifixion]</n>, said to them, "You know nothing at all!(ÏL‚Iô0If we let Him go on like this, everyone will believe in Him, and the Romans will come and take away both our <n>[holy]</n> place (the temple) and our nation."7ÏK‚gô/So the chief priests and Pharisees convened a council <n>[of the leaders in Israel]</n>, and said, "What are we doing? For this man performs many signs (attesting miracles).XÏJ)ô.But some of them went back to the Pharisees and told them what Jesus had done.ÏI‚ô-<pb/>So then, many of the Jews who had come to <n>[be with]</n> Mary and who were eyewitnesses to what Jesus had done, believed in Him.kÏHƒOô,Out came the man who had been dead, his hands and feet <i>tightly</i> wrapped in burial cloths (linen strips), and with a <n>[burial]</n> cloth wrapped around his face. Jesus said to them, <J>"Unwrap him and release him."</J>ZÏG-ô+When He had said this, He shouted with a loud voice, <J>"Lazarus, come out!"</J>lÏFƒQô*<J>I knew that You always hear Me <i>and</i> listen to Me; but I have said this because of the people standing around, so that they may believe that You have sent Me <n>[and that You have made Me Your representative]</n>."</J>ÏE‚#ô)So they took away the stone. And Jesus raised His eyes <n>[toward heaven]</n> and said, <J>"Father, I thank You that You have heard Me.</J>'ÏD‚Gô(Jesus said to her, <J>"Did I not say to you that if you believe <n>[in Me]</n>, you will see the glory of God <n>[the expression of His excellence]</n>?"</J>ZÏCƒ-ô'Jesus said, <J>"Take away the stone."</J> Martha, the sister of the dead man, said to Him, "Lord, by this time there will be an offensive odor, for he has been <i>dead</i> four days! <n>[It is hopeless!]</n>"9ÏB‚kô&<pb/>So Jesus, again deeply moved within <n>[to the point of anger]</n>, approached the tomb. It was a cave, and a boulder was lying against it <n>[to cover the entrance]</n>.vÏAeô%But some of them said, "Could not this Man, who opened the blind man's eyes, have kept this man from dying?"UÏ@#ô$So the Jews were saying, "See how He loved him <n>[as a close friend]</n>!"Ï?#ô#Jesus wept.VÏ>%ô"and said, <J>"Where have you laid him?"</J> They said, "Lord, come and see."MÏ=ƒô!When Jesus saw her sobbing, and the Jews who had come with her also sobbing, He was<f>[6]</f> deeply moved in spirit <n>[to the point of anger at the sorrow caused by death]</n> and was troubled,.Ï<‚Uô When Mary came <n>[to the place]</n> where Jesus was and saw Him, she fell at His feet, saying to Him, "Lord, if You had been here, my brother would not have died."9Ï;‚kôSo when the Jews who were with her in the house comforting her, saw how quickly Mary got up and left, they followed her, assuming that she was going to the tomb to weep there.nÏ:Uô<pb/>Now Jesus had not yet entered the village, but was still at the place where Martha had met Him.FÏ9ôAnd when she heard this, she got up quickly and went to Him.Ï8‚7ô<pb/>After she had said this, she left and called her sister Mary, privately whispering <n>[to her]</n>, "The Teacher is here and is asking for you."‚Ï7„1ôShe said to Him, "Yes, Lord; I have believed <i>and</i> continue to believe that You are the Christ (the Messiah, the Anointed), the Son of God,<f>[5]</f> He who was <n>[destined and promised]</n> to come into the world <n>[and it is for You that the world has waited]</n>."sÏ6_ô<J>and everyone who lives and believes in Me <n>[as Savior]</n> will never die. Do you believe this?"</J>;Ï5‚oôJesus said to her, <J>"<f>[4]</f> I am the Resurrection and the Life. Whoever believes in (adheres to, trusts in, relies on) Me <n>[as Savior]</n> will live even if he dies;</J>pÏ4YôMartha replied, "I know that he will rise <n>[from the dead]</n> in the resurrection on the last day."QÏ3ôJesus told her, <J>"Your brother will rise <n>[from the dead]</n>."</J>NÏ2ôEven now I know that whatever You ask of God, God will give to You."aÏ1;ôThen Martha said to Jesus, "Lord, if You had been here, my brother would not have died.uÏ0côSo when Martha heard that Jesus was coming, she went to meet Him, while Mary remained sitting in the house.~Ï/uôand many of the Jews had come to see Martha and Mary, to comfort them concerning <n>[the loss of]</n> their brother.:Ï.oôBethany was near Jerusalem, about two miles away;mÏ-Sô<pb/>So when Jesus arrived, He found that Lazarus had already been in the tomb<f>[3]</f> four days.Ï,}ôThen Thomas, who was called Didymus (the twin), said to his fellow disciples, "Let us go too, that we may die with Him."rÏ+]ô<J>And for your sake I am glad that I was not there, so that you may believe. But let us go to him."</J>BÏ*ôSo then Jesus told them plainly, <J>"Lazarus is dead.</J>jÏ)Mô
