[þAt this remark <e>Moses fled and became an exile in the land of Midian</e>, where he fathered two sons.PÔ	þ<e>Do you intend to kill me as you killed the Egyptian yesterday</e>?'Ô{þBut the man who was injuring his neighbor pushed Moses away, saying, '<e>Who appointed you ruler and judge over us</e>?8Ô‚iþThen on the next day he suddenly appeared to two of them as they were fighting, and he tried to reconcile them, saying, 'Men, you are brothers; why do you wrong one another?',Ô‚QþHe expected his countrymen to understand that God was granting them freedom through him <n>[assuming that they would accept him]</n>, but they did not understand.!Ô‚;þAnd when he saw one <n>[of them]</n> being treated unfairly, he defended the oppressed man and avenged him by striking <i>and</i> killing the Egyptian.qÔ[þBut when he reached the age of forty, it came into his heart to visit his brothers, the sons of Israel.Ô‚þSo Moses was educated in all the wisdom <i>and</i> culture of the Egyptians, and he was a man of power in words and deeds.Ô‚1þThen when he was set outside <n>[to die]</n>, Pharaoh's daughter rescued him <i>and</i> claimed him for herself, and cared for him as her own son.(Ô‚IþIt was at this <n>[critical]</n> time that Moses was born; and he was lovely in the sight of God, and for three months he was nourished in his father's house.Ô ‚þHe shrewdly exploited our race and mistreated our fathers, forcing them to expose their <n>[male]</n> babies so that they would die.)Ó‚Kþuntil <n>[the time when]</n> <e>there arose another king over egypt who did not know joseph</e> <n>[nor his history and the merit of his service to Egypt]</n>.8Ó~‚iþ<pb/>"But as the time <n>[for the fulfillment]</n> of the promise which God had made to Abraham was approaching, the <n>[Hebrew]</n> people increased and multiplied in Egypt,<Ó}‚qþand <n>[from Egypt]</n><