But they were not able to successfully withstand <i>and</i> cope with the wisdom <i>and</i> the intelligence <n>[and the power and inspiration]</n> of the Spirit by whom he was speaking.vÓgƒeþ	However, some men from what was called the Synagogue of the Freedmen (freed Jewish slaves), both Cyrenians and Alexandrians, and some from Cilicia and <n>[the province of]</n> Asia, rose up and questioned <i>and</i> argued with Stephen.Óf‚%þ<pb/>Now Stephen, full of grace (divine blessing, favor) and power, was doing great wonders and signs (attesting miracles) among the people.‚7Óe„gþ<pb/>And the message of God kept on growing <i>and</i> spreading, and the number of disciples continued to increase greatly in Jerusalem; and a large number of the priests were becoming obedient to the faith <n>[accepting Jesus as Messiah and acknowledging Him as the Source of eternal salvation]</n>.!Ód‚;þThey brought these men before the apostles; and after praying, they laid their hands on them <n>[to dedicate and commission them for this service]</n>.‚5Óc„cþThe suggestion pleased the whole congregation; and they selected<f>[4]</f> Stephen, a man full of faith <n>[in Christ Jesus]</n>, and <n>[filled with and led by]</n> the Holy Spirit, and Philip, Prochorus, Nicanor, Timon, Parmenas, and Nicolas (Nikolaos), a proselyte (Gentile convert) from Antioch.|ÓbqþBut we will <n>[continue to]</n> devote ourselves <n>[steadfastly]</n> to prayer and to the ministry of the word."SÓaƒþTherefore, brothers, choose from among you seven men with good reputations <n>[men of godly character and moral integrity]</n>, full of the Spirit and of wisdom, whom we may put in charge of this task.YÓ`ƒ+þSo the<f>[3]</f> Twelve called the disciples together and said, "It is not appropriate for us to neglect <n>[teaching]</n> the word of God in order to serve tables <i>and</i> manage the distribution of food.‚Ó_	„'þ<pb/>Now about this time, when the number of disciples was increasing, a complaint was made by the<f>[1]</f> Hellenists (Greek-speaking Jews) against the <n>[<f>[2]</f> native]</n> Hebrews, because their widows were being overlooked in the daily serving <i>of food</i>.2Ó^‚]þ*And every single day, in the temple <n>[area]</n> and in homes, they did not stop teaching and telling the good news of Jesus as the Christ (the Messiah, the Anointed).%Ó]‚Cþ)So they left the Council, rejoicing that they had been considered worthy <n>[dignified by indignity]</n> to suffer shame for <n>[the sake of]</n> His name.EÓ\ƒþ(<pb/>The Council (Sanhedrin, Jewish High Court) took his advice; and after summoning the apostles, they flogged them and ordered them not to speak in the name of Jesus, and released them.Ó[‚+þ'but if it is of God <n>[and it appears that it is]</n>, you will not be able to stop them; or else you may even be found fighting against God!"HÓZƒ	þ&So in the present case, I say to you, stay away from these men and let them alone, for if this plan or action is of men <n>[merely human in origin]</n>, it will fail <i>and</i> be destroyed;FÓYƒþ%After this man, Judas the Galilean rose up, <n>[and led an uprising]</n> during the time of the census, and drew people after him; he was also killed, and all his followers were scattered.nÓXƒUþ$For some time ago Theudas rose up, claiming to be somebody <n>[of importance]</n>, and a group of about four hundred men allied themselves with him. But he was killed, and all who followed him were scattered and came to nothing.sÓW_þ#Then he said to the Council, "Men of Israel, be careful in regard to what you propose to do to these men.rÓVƒ]þ"But a Pharisee named<f>[5]</f> Gamaliel, a teacher of the Law <n>[of Moses]</n>, highly esteemed by all the people, stood up in the Council (Sanhedrin, Jewish High Court) and ordered that the men be taken outside for a little while.`ÓU9þ!Now when they heard this, they were infuriated and they intended to kill the apostles.xÓTiþ And we are witnesses of these things; and so is the Holy Spirit, whom God has bestowed on those who obey Him.")ÓS‚KþGod exalted Him to His right hand as Prince and Savior <i>and</i> Deliverer, in order to grant repentance to Israel, and <n>[to grant]</n> forgiveness of sins.ÓR‚þThe God of our fathers raised up Jesus, whom you had put to death by hanging Him on a<f>[4]</f> cross <n>[and you are responsible]</n>.rÓQ]þThen Peter and the apostles replied, "We must obey God rather than men <n>[we have no other choice]</n>.lÓPƒQþsaying, "We gave you strict orders not to continue teaching in this name, and yet you have filled Jerusalem with your teaching and you intend to bring this<f>[3]</f> Man's blood on us <n>[by accusing us as His murderers]</n>."
