þ<pb/>So they brought them and presented them before the Council (Sanhedrin, Jewish High Court). The high priest questioned them,*ÓN‚MþThen the captain went with the officers and brought them back, without hurting them (because they were afraid of the people, worried that they might be stoned).ÓM‚3þBut someone came and told them, "The men whom you put in prison are standing <n>[right here]</n> in the temple <n>[area]</n>, teaching the people!"#ÓL‚?þNow when the captain of the temple <i>guard</i> and the chief priests heard these things, they were greatly perplexed, wondering what would come of this.ÓK‚!þ"We found the prison securely locked and the guards standing at the doors, but when we opened <n>[the doors]</n>, we found no one inside."oÓJWþBut when the officers arrived, they did not find them in the prison; and they came back and reported,ƒ ÓI…yþ<t>When they heard this, they went into the temple <n>[courtyards]</n> about daybreak and <i>began</i> teaching. </t><br/>Now when the high priest and his associates arrived, they called together the Council (Sanhedrin, Jewish High Court), even all the council of elders of the sons of Israel, and sent word to the prison for the apostles to be brought <n>[before them]</n>.JÓHƒ
