<pb/>Now in Damascus there was a disciple named Ananias; and the Lord said to him in a vision, <J>"Ananias."</J> And he answered, "Here I am, Lord."PÔZþ		And he was unable to see for three days, and he neither ate nor drank.ÔY‚%þ	Saul got up from the ground, but though his eyes were open, he could see nothing; so they led him by the hand and brought him into Damascus. ÔXyþ	The men who were traveling with him <n>[were terrified and]</n> stood speechless, hearing the voice but seeing no one.YÔW+þ	<J>now get up and go into the city, and you will be told what you must do."</J>rÔV]þ	And Saul said, "Who are You, Lord?" And He <i>answered</i>, <J>"I am Jesus whom you are persecuting,</J> ÔU‚9þ	and he fell to the ground and heard a voice <n>[from heaven]</n> saying to him, <J>"Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting <i>and</i> oppressing Me?"</J>ÔT‚+þ	As he traveled he approached Damascus, and suddenly a light from heaven flashed around him <n>[displaying the glory and majesty of Christ]</n>;‚GÔS…þ	and he asked for letters <n>[of authority]</n> from him to the synagogues at Damascus, so that if he found any men or women there belonging to<f>[3]</f> the Way <n>[believers, followers of Jesus the Messiah]</n>, men and women alike, he could arrest them <i>and</i> bring them bound <n>[with chains]</n> to Jerusalem.>ÔR	‚wþ	<pb/>Now<f>[1]</f> Saul, still breathing threats and murder against the disciples of the Lord <n>[and relentless in his search for believers]</n>, went to the<f>[2]</f> high priest,JÔQƒ
þ(But Philip found himself at<f>[6]</f> Azotus, and as he passed through he preached the good news <n>[of salvation]</n> to all the cities, until he came to<f>[7]</f> Caesarea <n>[Maritima]</n>.cÔPƒ?þ'When they came up out of the water, the Spirit of the Lord <n>[suddenly]</n> took Philip <n>[and carried him]</n> away <n>[to a different place]</n>; and the eunuch no longer saw him, but he went on his way rejoicing.ÔO‚þ&And he ordered that the chariot be stopped; and both Philip and the eunuch went down into the water, and Philip baptized him.#ÔN‚?þ%<f>[5]</f><n>[Philip said to him, "If you believe with all your heart, you may." And he replied, "I do believe that Jesus Christ is the Son of God."]</n>ÔM‚þ$As they continued along the road, they came to some water; and the eunuch exclaimed, "Look! Water! What forbids me from being baptized?"'ÔL‚Gþ#Then Philip spoke and beginning with this Scripture he preached Jesus to him <n>[explaining that He is the promised Messiah and the source of salvation]</n>.ÔK‚þ"<pb/>The eunuch replied to Philip, "Please <i>tell me</i>, about whom does the prophet say this? About himself or about someone else?"IÔJƒþ!<t>"<e>In humiliation His judgment was taken away</e> <n>[justice was denied Him]</n>. </t><t><e>Who will describe His generation</e>? </t><t><e>For His life is taken from the earth</e>."</t>vÔIƒeþ <t>Now this was the passage of Scripture which he was reading: </t><br/><t>"<e>Like a sheep He was led to the slaughter</e>; </t><t><e>And as a lamb before its shearer is silent</e>, </t><t><e>So He does not open His mouth</e>.</t><br/>ÔH‚5þAnd he said, "Well, how could I <n>[understand]</n> unless someone guides me <n>[correctly]</n>?" And he invited Philip to come up and sit with him.zÔGmþPhilip ran up and heard the man reading the prophet Isaiah, and asked, "Do you understand what you are reading?"VÔF%þThen the <n>[Holy]</n> Spirit said to Philip, "Go up and join this chariot."tÔEaþand he was returning, and sitting in his chariot he was reading <n>[the scroll of]</n> the prophet Isaiah.gÔDƒGþSo he got up and went; and there was an Ethiopian eunuch <n>[a man of great authority]</n>, a court official of Candace, queen of the Ethiopians, who was in charge of all her treasure. He had come to Jerusalem to worship,
