-	z£’¹ç9Ñ‰þ                                                                                                                                                Øh‚þThen Paul, calling in one of the centurions, said, "Take this young man to the commander, for he has something to tell him."~Øguþ<pb/>But the son of Paul's sister heard of their <n>[planned]</n> ambush, and he went to the barracks and told Paul.‚Øfƒ{þSo now you, along with the Council (Sanhedrin, Jewish High Court), notify the commander to bring Paul down to you, as if you were going to investigate his case more thoroughly. But we are ready to kill him before he comes near <n>[the place]</n>.";Øe‚oþThey went to the chief priests and elders and said, "We have bound ourselves under a solemn oath not to taste anything <n>[neither food nor drink]</n> until we have killed Paul.dØdAþ
There were more than forty <n>[men]</n> who formed this plot <n>[and swore this oath]</n>.)Øc‚KþNow when day came, the Jews formed a conspiracy and bound themselves under an oath (curse), saying that they would not eat or drink until they had killed Paul.MØbƒþ<pb/>On the following night the Lord stood near Paul and said, <J>"Be brave; for as you have solemnly <i>and</i> faithfully witnessed about Me at Jerusalem, so you must also testify at Rome."</J>TØaƒ!þ
And as the dissension became even greater, the commander, fearing that Paul would be torn to pieces by them, ordered the troops to go down and forcibly take him from them, and bring him to the barracks.‚Ø`„þ	Then a great uproar occurred, and some of the scribes of the Pharisees' party stood up and <i>began</i> to argue heatedly <n>[in Paul's favor]</n>, saying, "We find nothing wrong with this man; suppose a spirit or an angel has <n>[really]</n> spoken to him?"RØ_ƒþFor the Sadducees say that there is no <n>[such thing as a]</n> resurrection, nor an angel, nor a spirit, but the Pharisees <n>[speak out freely and]</n> acknowledge <n>[their belief in]</n> them all..Ø^‚UþWhen he said this, an angry dispute erupted between the Pharisees and the Sadducees, and the <n>[whole crowded]</n> assembly was divided <n>[into two factions]</n>.‚ Ø]ƒyþ<pb/>But recognizing that one group were Sadducees and the other Pharisees, Paul <i>began</i> affirming loudly in the Council <i>chamber</i>, "Kinsmen, I am a Pharisee, a son of Pharisees; I am on trial for the hope and resurrection of the dead!"$Ø\‚AþPaul said, "I was not aware, brothers, that he was<f>[3]</f> high priest; for it is written, '<e>You shall not speak evil of a ruler of your people</e>.'"YØ[+þBut those who stood near Paul said, "Are you insulting the high priest of God?"KØZƒþThen Paul said to him, "God is going to strike you, you<f>[2]</f> whitewashed wall! Do you actually sit to judge me according to the Law, and yet in violation of the Law order me to be struck?"|ØYqþ<n>[At this]</n> the high priest<f>[1]</f> Ananias ordered those who stood beside him to strike Paul on the mouth.?ØX	‚yþ<pb/>Then Paul, looking intently at the Council (Sanhedrin, Jewish High Court), said, "Kinsmen, I have lived my life before God with a perfectly good conscience until this very day."‚ØW„þ<pb/>But on the next day, wanting to know the real reason why Paul was being accused by the Jews, he released him and ordered the chief priests and the whole Council (Sanhedrin, Jewish High Court) to assemble; and brought Paul down and presented him before them.EØVƒþSo those who were about to interrogate him <i>by torture</i> immediately let him go; and the commander was also afraid when he realized that Paul was a Roman and he had put him in chains.RØUƒþThe commander replied, "I<f>[8]</f> purchased this citizenship <n>[of mine]</n> for a large sum of money <n>[so how did you acquire yours?]</n>." Paul said, "But I was actually born a <i>citizen</i>."_ØT7þSo the commander came and asked Paul, "Tell me, are you a Roman?" And he said, "Yes."ØS‚!þWhen the centurion heard <i>this</i>, he went to the commander and said to him, "What are you about to do? This man is a<f>[7]</f> Roman!"‚ØR„)þBut when they had stretched him out<f>[6]</f> with the <i>leather</i> straps <n>[in preparation for the whip]</n>, Paul said to the centurion who was standing by, "Is it legal for you to whip a man who is a Roman <i>citizen</i> and uncondemned <n>[without a trial]</n>?"FØQƒþthe commander ordered him to be brought into the barracks, stating that he was to be<f>[5]</f> interrogated with a whip in order to learn why the people were shouting against him that way.$ØP‚AþAnd as they were shouting and throwing off their coats <n>[getting ready to stone Paul]</n> and tossing dust into the air <n>[expressing their anger]</n>,EØOƒþ<pb/>They listened to Paul until <n>[he made]</n> this <n>[last]</n> statement, but now they raised their voices and shouted, "Away with such a man from the earth! He is not fit to live!"ZØN-þAnd the Lord said to me, <J>'Go, I will send you far away to the Gentiles.'"</J>MØMƒþAnd when the blood of Your witness Stephen was being shed, I also was standing nearby approving <i>and</i> consenting <n>[to his death]</n>, and guarding the coats of those who were killing him.'NØLƒþAnd I said, 'Lord, they themselves know <n>[without any doubt]</n> that in one synagogue after another I used to imprison and beat those who believed in You <n>[and Your message of salvation]</n>.ØK‚þand I saw Him saying to me, <J>'Hurry and get out of Jerusalem quickly, because they will not accept your testimony about Me.'</J>ØJ‚'þ<pb/>"Then it happened when I had returned to Jerusalem and was praying in the temple <n>[enclosure]</n>, that I fell into a trance (vision); ØIyþNow, why do you delay? Get up and be baptized, and wash away your sins by calling on His name <n>[for salvation]</n>.'cØH?þFor you will be His witness to all men <i>testifying</i> of what you have seen and heard.‚ØG„þAnd he said, 'The God of our fathers has appointed you to know His will, <n>[and to progressively understand His plan with clarity and power]</n> and to see the Righteous One <n>[Jesus Christ, the Messiah]</n> and to hear a message from His <n>[own]</n> mouth.4ØF‚aþ
came to <n>[see]</n> me, and standing near, he said to me, 'Brother Saul, receive your sight!' And at that very moment I <n>[recovered my sight and]</n> looked up at him.ØE‚þ<pb/>"And one Ananias, a devout man according to<f>[4]</f> the standard of the Law, and well spoken of by all the Jews who lived there,=ØD‚sþBut since I could not see because of the <n>[glorious intensity and dazzling]</n> brightness of that light, I was led by the hand by those who were with me and came into Damascus.?ØC‚wþ
And I asked, 'What shall I do, Lord?' And the Lord answered me, <J>'Get up and go into Damascus. There you will be told all that is appointed <i>and</i> destined for you to do.'</J>ØB{þ	Now those who were with me<f>[3]</f> saw the light, but did not understand the voice of the One who was speaking to me.|ØAqþAnd I replied, 'Who are You, Lord?' And He said to me, <J>'I am Jesus the Nazarene, whom you are persecuting.'</J>sØ@_þAnd I fell to the ground and heard a voice saying to me, <J>'Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting Me?'</J>Ø?‚1þ<pb/>"But as I was on my way, approaching Damascus about noontime, a great blaze of light suddenly flashed from heaven <i>and</i> shone around me.‚!Ø>„;þas the high priest and all the Council of the elders (Sanhedrin, Jewish High Court) can testify; because from them I received letters to the brothers, and I was on my way to Damascus in order to bring those <n>[believers]</n> who were there to Jerusalem in chains to be punished.=Ø=‚sþI persecuted <i>and</i> pursued the followers of this<f>[2]</f> Way to the death, binding them with chains and putting <n>[followers of Jesus]</n> both men and women into prisons,~Ø<ƒuþ<pb/>"I am a Jew, born in Tarsus of Cilicia, but brought up in this city, educated at the feet of<f>[1]</f> Gamaliel according to the strictness of the law of our fathers, being ardent <i>and</i> passionate for God just as all of you are today. Ø;yþ<pb/>When they heard that he was addressing them in the Hebrew dialect, they became even more quiet. And he continued,YØ:	-þ<pb/>"Brethren and fathers (kinsmen), hear my defense which I now offer to you."[Ø9ƒ/þ(When the commander had given him permission, Paul, standing on the steps, gestured with his hand to the people; and when there was a great hush, he spoke to them in the Hebrew dialect (Jewish Aramaic), saying,1Ø8‚[þ'Paul said, "I am a Jew from<f>[7]</f> Tarsus in Cilicia (Mersin Province, Turkey), a citizen of no insignificant city; and I beg you, allow me to speak to the people."3Ø7‚_þ&Then you are not <n>[as I assumed]</n> the Egyptian who<f>[6]</f> some time ago stirred up a rebellion and led those 4,000 men of the Assassins out into the wilderness?"#Ø6‚?þ%<pb/>Just as Paul was about to be taken into the barracks, he asked the commander, "May I say something to you?" And the man replied, "Do you know Greek?kØ5Oþ$for the majority of the people kept following them, shouting, "Away with him! <n>[Kill him!]</n>"hØ4Iþ#When Paul got to the steps, he was carried by the soldiers because of the violence of the mob;dØ3ƒAþ"But some in the crowd were shouting one thing and others something else; and since he could not determine the facts because of the uproar, he ordered that Paul be taken to the barracks <n>[in the tower of Antonia]</n>.Ø2‚!þ!Then the commander came up and arrested Paul, and ordered that he be bound with two chains. Then he asked who he was and what he had done. Ø1‚9þ So he immediately took soldiers and centurions and ran down among them. When the people saw the commander and the soldiers, they stopped beating Paul.Ø0‚-þNow while they were trying to kill him, word came to the commander of the <n>[Roman]</n> garrison that all Jerusalem was in a state of upheaval.:Ø/‚mþThen the whole city was provoked <i>and</i> confused, and the people rushed together. They seized Paul and dragged him out of the temple, and immediately the gates were closed.<Ø.‚qþFor they had previously seen Trophimus the Ephesian in the city with Paul, and they assumed that he had brought the man into the temple <n>[beyond the court of the Gentiles]</n>._Ø-ƒ7þshouting, "Men of Israel, help us! This is the man who teaches all men everywhere against our people and the Law and this place. And besides, he has brought Greeks into the temple and has defiled this holy place."}Ø,ƒsþWhen the seven days <n>[required to complete the ritual]</n> were almost over, <n>[some]</n> Jews from <n>[the province of]</n> Asia <n>[Minor]</n>, caught sight of him in the temple, and <i>began</i> to stir up the crowd, and they seized him,‚QØ+…þThen Paul took the <n>[four]</n> men, and the next day he purified himself along with them <n>[by submitting to the ritual]</n>. He went into the temple to give notice of <n>[the time]</n> when the days of purification <n>[ending each vow]</n> would be fulfilled and the usual offering could be presented on behalf of each one.‚(Ø*„IþBut with regard to the Gentiles who have believed <n>[in Christ]</n>, we have sent them a letter with our decision that they should abstain from meat sacrificed to idols and from <n>[consuming]</n> blood and from <n>[the meat of]</n> what has been strangled and from sexual immorality."‚)Ø)„Kþtake these men and purify yourself along with them, and pay their expenses <n>[for the temple offerings]</n> so that they may shave their heads. Then everyone will know that there is nothing to the things they have been told about you, but that you yourself also follow and keep the Law.TØ(!þTherefore do just what we tell you. We have four men who have taken a vow;SØ'þWhat then should be done? They will certainly hear that you have arrived.‚	Ø&„þNow they have been told about you, that you are teaching all the Jews who are <i>living</i> among the Gentiles to turn away from <n>[the Law of]</n> Moses, advising them not to circumcise their children or to live according to the <n>[Mosaic]</n> customs.‚Ø%ƒ{þAnd when they heard it, they <i>began</i> glorifying <i>and</i> praising God; and they said to him, "You see, brother, how many thousands of believers there are among the Jews, and they are all enthusiastic supporters of the Law <n>[of Moses]</n>.Ø$‚!þAfter greeting them, Paul <i>began</i> to give a detailed account of the things that God had done among the Gentiles through his ministry.Ø#}þOn the next day Paul went with us to <n>[see]</n><f>[5]</f> James, and all the elders <i>of the church</i> were present.cØ"?þ<pb/>When we arrived in Jerusalem, the<f>[4]</f> brothers and sisters welcomed us gladly.6Ø!‚eþSome of the disciples from Caesarea also came with us, taking us to <n>[the house of]</n> Mnason, a man from Cyprus, a disciple of long standing with whom we were to lodge.IØ þAfter this we got ready and started on our way up to Jerusalem.}ØsþAnd since he would not be dissuaded, we stopped pleading <i>and</i> fell silent, saying, "The Lord's will be done!"\Øƒ1þ
Then Paul replied, "What are you doing, weeping and breaking my heart <n>[like this]</n>? For I am ready not only to be bound <i>and</i> imprisoned, but even to die at Jerusalem for the name of the Lord Jesus."Ø‚'þNow when we had heard this, both we and the local residents <i>began</i> pleading with Paul trying to persuade him not to go up to Jerusalem.‚7Ø„gþAnd coming to <n>[see]</n> us, he took Paul's <n>[wide]</n><f>[3]</f> band (belt, sash) and bound his own feet and hands, and said, "This is what the Holy Spirit says: 'In this same way the Jews in Jerusalem will bind the man who owns this band, and they will hand him over to the Gentiles (pagans).'"^Ø5þ
As we were staying there for some time, a prophet named Agabus came down from Judea.DØþ	He had four virgin daughters who had the gift of prophecy.*Ø‚MþOn the next day we left and came to Caesarea, and we went to the house of Philip the evangelist, who was one of the seven <n>[deacons]</n>, and stayed with him.BØ‚}þ<pb/>When we had completed the voyage from Tyre, we landed <n>[twenty miles to the south]</n> at Ptolemais, and after greeting the<f>[2]</f> believers, we stayed with them for one day.EØþThen we boarded the ship, and they returned to their homes.‚Ø„'þWhen our days there came to an end, we left and proceeded on our journey, while all of the disciples, with their wives and children, escorted us on our way until <i>we were</i> outside the city. After kneeling down on the beach and praying, we told one another goodbye.MØƒþAfter looking up the disciples <n>[in Tyre]</n>, we stayed there <n>[with them]</n> seven days; and they kept telling Paul through the <n>[Holy]</n> Spirit<f>[1]</f> not to set foot in Jerusalem.%Ø‚CþAfter we came in sight of Cyprus, leaving it on our left (port side), we sailed on to Syria and put in at Tyre; for there the ship was to unload her cargo.]Ø3þand after finding a ship crossing over to Phoenicia, we went on board and set sail.(Ø	‚Kþ<pb/>When we had torn ourselves away from them and set sail, we ran a straight course and came to Cos, and on the next day to Rhodes, and from there to Patara;Ø‚7þ&grieving <i>and</i> distressed especially over the word which he had spoken, that they would not see him again. And they accompanied him to the ship.|Øqþ%And<f>[6]</f> they <i>began</i> to weep openly and threw their arms around Paul's neck, and repeatedly kissed him,UØ#þ$<pb/>When he had said these things, he knelt down and prayed with them all.‚Ø„þ#In everything I showed you <n>[by example]</n> that by working hard in this way you must help the weak and remember the words of the Lord Jesus, that He Himself said, <J>'It is more blessed <n>[and brings greater joy]</n> to give than to receive.'"</J> Ø
‚9þ"You know personally that these hands ministered to my own needs <n>[working in manual labor]</n> and to <n>[those of]</n> the people who were with me.TØ!þ!I had no desire for anyone's silver or gold or <n>[expensive]</n> clothes.ƒØ†þ And now I commend you to God <n>[placing you in His protective, loving care]</n> and <n>[I commend you]</n> to the word of His grace <n>[the counsel and promises of His unmerited favor]</n>. His grace is able to build you up and to give you the <n>[rightful]</n> inheritance among all those who are sanctified <n>[that is, among those who are set apart for God's purposeâ€”all believers]</n>.4Ø
‚aþTherefore be <i>continually</i> alert, remembering that for three years, night or day, I did not stop admonishing <i>and</i> advising each one <n>[of you]</n> with tears.1Ø	‚[þeven from among your own selves men will arise, speaking perverse <i>and</i> distorted things, to draw away the disciples after themselves <n>[as their followers]</n>.ØþI know that after I am gone, <n>[false teachers like]</n> ferocious wolves will come in among you, not sparing the flock;bØƒ=þTake care <i>and</i> be on guard for yourselves and for the whole flock over which the Holy Spirit has appointed you as overseers, to shepherd (tend, feed, guide) the church of God which He bought with His own blood.^Ø5þFor I did not shrink from declaring to you the whole purpose <i>and</i> plan of God.zØmþFor that reason I testify to you on this <n>[our parting]</n> day that I am innocent of the blood of all people.Ø‚þ<pb/>"And now, listen carefully: I know that none of you, among whom I went about preaching the kingdom,<f>[5]</f> will see me again.‚\Ø…1þBut I do not consider my life as something of value <i>or</i> dear to me, so that I may <n>[with joy]</n> finish my course and the ministry which I received from the Lord Jesus, to testify faithfully of the good news of God's <n>[precious, undeserved]</n> grace <n>[which makes us free of the guilt of sin and grants us eternal life]</n>.Ø‚þexcept that the Holy Spirit solemnly <n>[and emphatically]</n> affirms to me in city after city that imprisonment and suffering await me.Ø‚#þAnd now, compelled by the Spirit <i>and</i> obligated by my convictions, I am going to Jerusalem, not knowing what will happen to me there,GØ ƒþsolemnly <n>[and wholeheartedly]</n> testifying to both Jews and Greeks, urging them to turn in repentance to God and <n>[to have]</n> faith in our Lord Jesus Christ <n>[for salvation]</n>.4×‚aþ<n>[you know]</n> how I did not shrink back in fear from telling you anything that was for your benefit, or from teaching you in public meetings, and from house to house,
×~‚þserving the Lord with all humility and with tears and trials which came on me because of the plots of the Jews <n>[against me]</n>;4×}‚aþ<t>And when they arrived he said to them: </t><br/>"You know well how I <n>[lived when I]</n> was with you, from the first day that I set foot in Asia <n>[until now]</n>, ×|yþHowever, from Miletus he sent <i>word</i> to Ephesus and summoned the elders of the church <n>[to meet him there]</n>.×{ƒwþPaul had decided to sail on past Ephesus so that he would not end up spending time <n>[unnecessarily]</n> in <n>[the province of]</n> Asia (modern Turkey); for he was in a hurry to be in Jerusalem, if possible, on the day of<f>[4]</f> Pentecost.V×zƒ%þSailing from there, we arrived the next day <n>[at a point]</n> opposite Chios; the following day we crossed over to Samos, and the next day we arrived at Miletus <n>[about 30 miles south of Ephesus]</n>.U×y#þSo when he met us at Assos, we took him on board and sailed on to Mitylene.F×xƒþ
But we went on ahead to the ship and set sail for Assos, intending to take Paul on board there; for that was what he had arranged, intending himself to go <n>[a shorter route]</n> by land.k×wOþThey took the boy <n>[Eutychus]</n> home alive, and were greatly comforted <i>and</i> encouraged.O×vƒþWhen Paul had gone back upstairs and had broken the bread and eaten, he talked <n>[informally and confidentially]</n> with them for a long timeâ€”until daybreak <n>[in fact]</n>â€”and then he left.,×u‚Qþ
But Paul went down and threw himself on him and embraced him, and said <n>[to those standing around him]</n>, "Do not be troubled, because<f>[3]</f> he is alive."‚×t„%þ	and there was a young man named Eutychus ("Lucky") sitting on the window sill. He was sinking into a deep sleep, and as Paul<f>[2]</f> kept on talking longer and longer, he was completely overcome by sleep and fell down from the third story; and he was picked up dead.N×sþNow there were many lamps in the upper room where we were assembled,p×rƒYþ<pb/>Now on the first day of the week (Sunday), when we were gathered together to break bread (share communion), Paul <i>began</i> talking with them, intending to leave the next day; and he kept on with his message until midnight.$×q‚AþWe sailed from Philippi after the days of Unleavened Bread (Passover week), and within five days we reached them at Troas, where we stayed for seven days.T×p!þThese men went on ahead and were waiting for us (including Luke) at Troas.J×oƒ
þHe was accompanied by Sopater of Berea, <i>the son</i> of Pyrrhus, and by Aristarchus and Secundus of the Thessalonians, and by Gaius of Derbe, and Timothy, and Tychicus and Trophimus of Asia.9×n‚kþAnd he stayed three months, and when a plot was formed against him by the Jews as he was about to set sail for Syria, he decided to return through Macedonia (northern Greece).h×mIþAfter he had gone through those districts and had encouraged the believers, he came to Greece.&×l	‚Gþ<pb/>After the uproar had ended, Paul sent for the disciples, and when he had encouraged them he told them goodbye, and set off to go to<f>[1]</f> Macedonia.G×kþ)And when he had said these things, he dismissed the assembly.W×jƒ'þ(For we are running the risk of being accused of rioting in regard to today's events, and since there is no reason for it, we will be unable to give an account <i>and</i> justify this disorderly gathering."Z×i-þ'But if you want anything beyond this, it will be settled in the lawful assembly.T×hƒ!þ&So then, if Demetrius and the craftsmen who are with him have a complaint against anyone, the courts are in session and proconsuls are <i>available</i>; let them bring charges against one another there.l×gQþ%For you have brought these men here who are neither temple robbers nor blasphemers of our goddess.w×fgþ$So, since these things cannot be denied, you ought to be quiet <i>and</i> stay calm and not do anything rash.‚'×e„Gþ#After the town<f>[10]</f> clerk had quieted the crowd, he said, "Men of Ephesus, what person is there who does not know that the city of the Ephesians is the guardian of the temple of the great Artemis and of that <n>[<f>[11]</f> sacred stone image of her]</n> which fell from the sky?$×d‚Aþ"But when they realized that he was a Jew, a single outcry went up from the crowd as they shouted for about two hours, "Great is Artemis of the Ephesians!"Q×cƒþ!Some of the crowd advised Alexander <n>[to speak]</n>, since the Jews had pushed him forward; and Alexander motioned with his hand <n>[for attention]</n> and intended to make a defense to the people.#×b‚?þ Now some shouted one thing and some another, for the gathering was in confusion and most of the people did not know<f>[9]</f> why they had come together.×a‚1þEven some of the Asiarchs (officials) who were his friends sent <i>word</i> to him and repeatedly warned him not to venture into the amphitheater.b×`=þPaul wanted to go into the <n>[pagan]</n> assembly, but the disciples would not let him.Z×_ƒ-þThen the city was filled with confusion; and people rushed together <n>[as a group]</n> into the amphitheater, dragging along with them Gaius and Aristarchus, Macedonians who were Paul's traveling companions.×^‚þ<pb/>When they heard this, they were filled with rage, and they <i>began</i> shouting, "Great is Artemis of the Ephesians!"‚7×]„gþNot only is there danger that this trade of ours will be discredited, but also that the <n>[magnificent]</n><f>[7]</f> temple of the great goddess<f>[8]</f> Artemis will be discredited, and that she whom all Asia and the world worship will even be dethroned <i>and</i> lose her glorious magnificence."‚*×\„MþYou see and hear that not only at Ephesus, but almost all over <n>[the province of]</n> Asia, this Paul has persuaded <n>[people to believe his teaching]</n> and has misled a large number of people, claiming that gods made by <n>[human]</n> hands are not <i>really</i> gods <i>at all</i>.9×[‚kþThese <n>[craftsmen]</n> he called together, along with the workmen of similar <i>trades</i>, and said, "Men, you are well aware that we make a good living from this business.#×Z‚?þNow a man named Demetrius, a silversmith, who made silver shrines of <n>[the goddess]</n> Artemis (Diana), was bringing no small profit to the craftsmen.l×YQþ<pb/>About that time there occurred no small disturbance concerning the Way (Jesus, Christianity).J×Xƒ
þAnd after sending two of his assistants, Timothy and<f>[6]</f> Erastus, to Macedonia <n>[ahead of him]</n>, he stayed on in <n>[the west coast province of]</n> Asia <n>[Minor]</n> for a while.‚×W„/þ<pb/>Now after these events, Paul determined in the Spirit that he would travel through<f>[5]</f> Macedonia and Achaia (most of the Greek mainland), and go to Jerusalem, saying, "After I have been there, I must also see Rome <n>[and preach the good news of salvation]</n>."×VþSo the word of the Lord <n>[concerning eternal salvation through faith in Christ]</n> was growing greatly and prevailing.|×UƒqþAnd many of those who had practiced magical arts collected their books and <n>[throwing book after book on the pile]</n> began burning them in front of everyone. They calculated their value and found it to be<f>[4]</f>50,000 pieces of silver.×TwþMany of those who had become believers were coming, confessing and disclosing their <n>[former sinful]</n> practices.,×S‚QþThis became known to all who lived in Ephesus, both Jews and Greeks. And fear fell upon them all, and the name of the Lord Jesus was magnified <i>and</i> exalted.N×RƒþThen the man, in whom was the evil spirit, leaped on them and subdued<f>[3]</f> all of them and overpowered them, so that they ran out of that house <n>[in terror, stripped]</n> naked and wounded.×Q‚'þBut the evil spirit retorted, "I know <i>and</i> recognize <i>and</i> acknowledge Jesus, and I know about Paul, but as for you, who are you?"Y×P+þSeven sons of one <n>[named]</n> Sceva, a Jewish chief priest, were doing this.a×Oƒ;þ
Then some of the traveling Jewish exorcists also attempted to call the name of the Lord Jesus over those who had evil spirits, saying, "I implore you <i>and</i> solemnly command you by the Jesus whom Paul preaches!"E×Nƒþso that even handkerchiefs <i>or</i> face-towels or aprons that had touched his skin were brought to the sick, and their diseases left them and the evil spirits came out <n>[of them]</n>.W×M'þGod was doing extraordinary <i>and</i> unusual miracles by the hands of Paul,q×Lƒ[þ
This continued for two years, so that all the inhabitants of <n>[the west coast province of]</n> Asia <n>[Minor]</n>, Jews as well as Greeks, heard the word of the Lord <n>[concerning eternal salvation through faith in Christ]</n>.‚j×K…Mþ	But when some were becoming hardened and disobedient <n>[to the word of God]</n>, discrediting <i>and</i> speaking evil of<f>[1]</f> the Way (Jesus, Christianity) before the congregation, Paul left them, taking the disciples with him, and went on holding<f>[2]</f> daily discussions in the lecture hall of Tyrannus <n>[instead of in the synagogue]</n>."×J‚=þ<pb/>And he went into the synagogue and for three months spoke boldly, reasoning <i>and</i> arguing and persuading <i>them</i> about the kingdom of God.,×ISþThere were about twelve men in all.%×H‚CþAnd when Paul laid his hands on them, the Holy Spirit came on them, and they <i>began</i> speaking in <n>[unknown]</n> tongues (languages) and prophesying.i×GKþAfter hearing this, they were baptized <n>[again, this time]</n> in the name of the Lord Jesus.w×FƒgþPaul said, "John performed a baptism of repentance, <i>continually</i> telling the people to believe in Him who was coming after him, that is, <n>[to confidently accept and joyfully believe]</n> in Jesus <n>[the Messiah and Savior]</n>."]×E3þAnd he asked, "Into what then were you baptized?" They said, "Into John's baptism."6×D‚eþHe asked them, "Did you receive the Holy Spirit when you believed <n>[in Jesus as the Christ]</n>?" And they said, "No, we have not even heard that there is a Holy Spirit."&×C	‚Gþ<pb/>It happened that while Apollos was in Corinth, Paul went through the upper <n>[inland]</n> districts and came down to Ephesus, and found some disciples.×B‚-þfor he powerfully refuted the Jews in public <i>discussions</i>, proving by the Scriptures that Jesus is the Christ (the Messiah, the Anointed).‚0×A„YþAnd when Apollos wanted to go across to Achaia (southern Greece), the brothers encouraged him and wrote to the disciples, <n>[urging them]</n> to welcome him gladly. When he arrived, he was a great help to those who, through grace, had believed <i>and</i> had followed Jesus as Lord and Savior,r×@ƒ]þand he began to speak boldly <i>and</i> fearlessly in the synagogue. But when Priscilla and Aquila heard him, they took him aside and explained more accurately to him the way of God <n>[and the full story of the life of Christ]</n>.J×?ƒ
þThis man had been instructed in the way of the Lord, and being spiritually impassioned, he was speaking and teaching accurately the things about Jesus, though he knew only the baptism of John;/×>‚Wþ<pb/>Now a Jew named Apollos, a native of Alexandria, came to Ephesus. He was an eloquent <i>and</i> cultured man, and well versed in the <n>[Hebrew]</n> Scriptures.+×=‚OþAfter spending some time <i>there</i>, he left and traveled through the territory of Galatia and Phrygia, strengthening <i>and</i> encouraging all the disciples.×<‚þ<pb/>When he had landed at Caesarea, he went up and<f>[9]</f> greeted the church <n>[at Jerusalem]</n>, and then went down to Antioch.w×;gþbut after telling them goodbye and saying, "I will return again if God is willing," he set sail from Ephesus.D×:þWhen they asked him to stay for a longer time, he refused;×9‚þThen they arrived in Ephesus, and he left the others there; but he entered the synagogue and reasoned <i>and</i> debated with the Jews.‚?×8„wþ<pb/>Paul stayed for a while longer, and then told the<f>[8]</f> brothers and sisters goodbye and sailed for Syria; and he was accompanied by Priscilla and Aquila. At Cenchrea <n>[the southeastern port of Corinth]</n> he had his hair cut, because he was keeping a <n>[Nazirite]</n> vow <n>[of abstention]</n>.E×7ƒþThen the Greeks all seized<f>[7]</f> Sosthenes, the leader of the synagogue, and <i>began</i> beating him right in front of the judgment seat; but Gallio paid no attention to any of this.7×6iþAnd he drove them away from the judgment seat.
