For I testify about them that they have a certain enthusiasm for God, but not in accordance with <n>[correct and vital]</n> knowledge <n>[about Him and His purposes]</n>.mÜ	U
<pb/>Brothers and sisters, my heart's desire and my prayer to God for Israel is for their salvation.‚;Ü„o	!As it is written <i>and</i> forever remains written, </t><br/><t>"<e>Behold I am laying in Zion a Stone of stumbling and a Rock of offense</e>; </t><t><e>And he who believes in Him</e> <n>[whoever adheres to, trusts in, and relies on Him]</n> <e>will not be disappointed</e> <n>[in his expectations]</n>."sÜƒ_	 And why not? Because it was not by faith <n>[that they pursued it]</n>, but as though it were by works <n>[relying on the merit of their works instead of their faith]</n>. They stumbled over the stumbling Stone <n>[Jesus Christ]</n>.zÜm	whereas Israel, <n>[though always]</n> pursuing the law of righteousness, did not succeed in fulfilling the law.yÜƒk	<pb/>What shall we say then? That Gentiles, who did not pursue righteousness <n>[who did not seek salvation and a right relationship with God, nevertheless]</n> obtained righteousness, that is, the righteousness which is produced by faith;‚2Ü„]	<t>It is as Isaiah foretold, </t><br/><t>"<e>If the Lord of Hosts had not left us seed</e> <n>[future generations from which a believing remnant of Israelites came]</n>, </t><t><e>We would have become like Sodom, and would have resembled Gomorrah</e> <n>[totally rejected and destroyed]</n>!"</t>Ü‚1	<e>For the Lord will execute His word upon the earth</e> <n>[He will conclude His dealings with mankind]</n> <e>completely and without delay</e>."vÜƒe	<pb/>And Isaiah calls out concerning Israel: "<e>Though the number of the sons of Israel be like the sand of the sea, it is only the remnant</e> <n>[a small believing minority]</n> <e>that will be saved</e> <n>[from God's judgment]</n>;0Ü‚Y	<t>"<e>And it shall be that in the place where it was said to them, 'you are not My people</e>,' </t><t><e>There they shall be called sons of the living God</e>."</t>rÜƒ]	<t>Just as He says in <n>[the writings of the prophet]</n> Hosea: </t><br/><t>"<e>I will call those who were not My people, 'My people</e>,' </t><t><e>And</e> <n>[I will call]</n> <e>her who was not beloved, 'beloved</e>.'"</t><br/>sÜ_	<i>including</i> us, whom He also called, not only from among the Jews, but also from among the Gentiles?Ü‚	And what if He has done so to make known the riches of His glory to the objects of His mercy, which He has prepared beforehand for glory,PÜƒ	What if God, although willing to show His <n>[terrible]</n> wrath and to make His power known, has tolerated with great patience the objects of His wrath <n>[which are]</n> prepared for destruction?rÜƒ]	Does the potter not have the right over the clay, to make from the same lump <n>[of clay]</n> one object for honorable use <n>[something beautiful or distinctive]</n> and another for common use <n>[something ordinary or menial]</n>?SÜƒ	On the contrary, who are you, O man, who answers <n>[arrogantly]</n> back to God <i>and</i> dares to defy Him? Will the thing which is formed say to him who formed it, "Why have you made me like this?"4Ü‚a	<pb/>You will say to me then, "Why does He still blame me <n>[for sinning]</n>? For who <n>[including myself]</n> has <n>[ever]</n> resisted His will <i>and</i> purpose?"oÜW	So then, He has mercy on whom He wills (chooses), and He hardens <n>[the heart of]</n> whom He wills.OÜ
ƒ	For the Scripture says to Pharaoh, "<e>I raised you up for this very purpose, to display My power in</e> <n>[dealing with]</n> <e>you, and so that My name would be proclaimed in all the earth</e>."RÜƒ	So then God's choice is not dependent on human will, nor on human effort <n>[the totality of human striving]</n>, but on God who shows mercy <n>[to whomever He choosesâ€”it is His sovereign gift]</n>.Ü‚	For He says to Moses, "<e>I will have mercy on whomever I have mercy, and I will have compassion on whomever I have compassion</e>."RÜ
	<pb/>What shall we say then? Is there injustice with God? Certainly not!KÜ	ƒ	
As it is written <i>and</i> forever remains written, "<f>[4]</f><e>Jacob I loved</e> (chose, protected, blessed), <e>but</e><f>[5]</f><e>Esau I hated</e> (held in disregard compared to Jacob)."~Üu	it was said to her, "<e>The</e><f>[2]</f><e>older</e> (Esau) <e>will serve the</e><f>[3]</f><e>younger</e> (Jacob)."‚Ü„3	and though <i>the twins</i> were not yet born and had not done anything <i>either</i> good or bad, so that God's purpose <n>[His choice, His election]</n> would stand, not because of works <n>[done by either child]</n>, but because of <n>[the plan of]</n> Him who calls them,Ü‚!	
And not only that, but this too: Rebekah conceived <i>twin sons</i> by one man <n>[under the same circumstances]</n>, by our father Isaac;Ü‚			For this is what the promise said: "<e>About this time</e> <n>[next year]</n> <e>I will come, and Sarah shall have a son</e>."RÜƒ	That is, it is not the children of the body <n>[Abraham's natural descendants]</n> who are God's children, but it is the children of the promise who are counted as <n>[Abraham's true]</n> descendants.fÜƒE	and they are not all the children of Abraham because they are his descendants <n>[by blood]</n>, but <n>[the promise was]</n>: "<e>Your descendants will be named through Isaac</e>" <n>[though Abraham had other sons]</n>.-Ü‚S	<pb/>However, it is not as though God's word has failed <n>[coming to nothing]</n>. For not all who are descended from Israel (Jacob) are <n>[the true]</n> Israel;WÜƒ'	To them belong the patriarchs, and from them, according to His natural descent, <i>came</i> the Christ (the Messiah, the Anointed), He who is exalted <i>and</i> supreme over all, God blessed forever. Amen.‚Ü „	who are Israelites, to whom belongs the adoption as sons, the glory (<f>[1]</f> Shekinah), the <n>[special]</n> covenants <n>[with Abraham, Moses, and David]</n>, the giving of the Law, the <n>[system of temple]</n> worship, and the <n>[original]</n> promises.HÛƒ		For <n>[if it were possible]</n> I would wish that I myself were accursed, <n>[separated, banished]</n> from Christ for the sake <n>[of the salvation]</n> of my brothers, my natural kinsmen,EÛ~	that I have great sorrow and unceasing anguish in my heart.Û}	‚%	<pb/>I am telling the truth in Christ, I am not lying, my conscience testifies with me <n>[enlightened and prompted]</n> by the Holy Spirit,%Û|‚C'nor height, nor depth, nor any other created thing, will be able to separate us from the <n>[unlimited]</n> love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord.^Û{ƒ5&For I am convinced <n>[and continue to be convincedâ€”beyond any doubt]</n> that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor things present <i>and</i> threatening, nor things to come, nor powers,)Ûz‚K%<pb/>Yet in all these things we are more than conquerors <i>and</i> gain an overwhelming victory through Him who loved us <n>[so much that He died for us]</n>.IÛyƒ$<t>Just as it is written <i>and</i> forever remains written, </t><br/><t>"<e>For Your sake we are put to death all day long</e>; </t><t><e>We are regarded as sheep for the slaughter</e>."</t>$Ûx‚A#Who shall ever separate us from the love of<f>[4]</f> Christ? Will tribulation, or distress, or persecution, or famine, or nakedness, or danger, or sword?zÛwƒm"Who is the one who condemns us? Christ Jesus is the One who died <n>[to pay our penalty]</n>, and more <i>than that</i>, who was raised <n>[from the dead]</n>, and who is at the right hand of God interceding <n>[with the Father]</n> for us.:Ûv‚m!Who will bring any charge against God's elect (His chosen ones)? It is God who justifies us <n>[declaring us blameless and putting us in a right relationship with Himself]</n>.Ûu‚- He who did not spare <n>[even]</n> His own Son, but gave Him up for us all, how will He not also, along with Him, graciously give us all things?wÛtg<pb/>What then shall we say to all these things? If God is for us, who can be <n>[successful]</n> against us?lÛsƒQAnd those whom He predestined, He also called; and those whom He called, He also justified <n>[declared free of the guilt of sin]</n>; and those whom He justified, He also glorified <n>[raising them to a heavenly dignity]</n>.‚%Ûr„CFor those whom He foreknew <n>[and loved and chose beforehand]</n>, He also predestined to be conformed to the image of His Son <n>[and ultimately share in His complete sanctification]</n>, so that He would be the firstborn <n>[the most beloved and honored]</n> among many believers.‚Ûq„<pb/>And we know <n>[with great confidence]</n> that God <n>[who is deeply concerned about us]</n> causes all things to work together <n>[as a plan]</n> for good for those who love God, to those who are called according to His plan <i>and</i> purpose.CÛp‚And He who searches the hearts knows what the mind of the Spirit is, because the Spirit intercedes <n>[before God]</n> on behalf of<f>[3]</f> God's people in accordance with God's will.‚5Ûo„cIn the same way the Spirit <n>[comes to us and]</n> helps us in our weakness. We do not know what prayer to offer <i>or</i> how to offer it as we should, but the Spirit Himself <n>[knows our need and at the right time]</n> intercedes on our behalf with sighs <i>and</i> groanings too deep for words.kÛnOBut if we hope for what we do not see, we wait eagerly for it with patience <i>and</i> composure.Ûm‚5For in this hope we were saved <n>[by faith]</n>. But hope <n>[the object of]</n> which is seen is not hope. For who hopes for what he already sees?‚5Ûl„cAnd not only this, but we too, who have the first fruits of the Spirit <n>[a joyful indication of the blessings to come]</n>, even we groan inwardly, as we wait eagerly for <n>[the sign of]</n> our adoption as sonsâ€”the redemption <i>and</i> transformation of our body <n>[at the resurrection]</n>.pÛkYFor we know that the whole creation has been moaning together as in the pains of childbirth until now.Ûj‚1that the creation itself will also be freed from its bondage to decay <n>[and gain entrance]</n> into the glorious freedom of the children of God.CÛi‚For the creation was subjected to frustration <i>and</i> futility, not willingly <n>[because of some intentional fault on its part]</n>, but by the will of Him who subjected it, in hopexÛhiFor <n>[even the whole]</n> creation <n>[all nature]</n> waits eagerly for the children of God to be revealed.MÛgƒ<pb/>For I consider <n>[from the standpoint of faith]</n> that the sufferings of the present life are not worthy to be compared with the glory that is about to be revealed to us <i>and</i> in us!‚Ûfƒ}And if <n>[we are His]</n> children, <n>[then we are His]</n> heirs also: heirs of God and fellow heirs with Christ <n>[sharing His spiritual blessing and inheritance]</n>, if indeed we share in His suffering so that we may also share in His glory.Ûe‚)The Spirit Himself testifies <i>and</i> confirms together with our spirit <n>[assuring us]</n> that we <n>[believers]</n> are children of God.‚Ûdƒ}For you have not received a spirit of slavery leading again to fear <n>[of God's judgment]</n>, but you have received the Spirit of adoption as sons <n>[the Spirit producing sonship]</n> by which we <n>[joyfully]</n> cry, "<f>[2]</f> Abba! Father!"dÛcAFor all who are <i>allowing themselves to</i> be led by the Spirit of God are sons of God.‚,Ûb„Q
for if you are living according to the <n>[impulses of the]</n> flesh, you are going to die. But if <n>[you are living]</n> by the <n>[power of the Holy]</n> Spirit you are <i>habitually</i> putting to death the <i>sinful</i> deeds of the body, you will <n>[really]</n> live <i>forever</i>.‚Ûa„<pb/>So then,<f>[1]</f> brothers and sisters, we have an obligation, but not to our flesh <n>[our human nature, our worldliness, our sinful capacity]</n>, to live according to the <n>[impulses of the]</n> flesh <n>[our nature without the Holy Spirit]</n>â€”HÛ`ƒ	And if the Spirit of Him who raised Jesus from the dead lives in you, He who raised Christ Jesus from the dead will also give life to your mortal bodies through His Spirit, who lives in you.%Û_‚C
If Christ lives in you, though your <n>[natural]</n> body is dead because of sin, your spirit is alive because of righteousness <n>[which He provides]</n>.‚:Û^„m	<pb/>However, you are not <n>[living]</n> in the flesh <n>[controlled by the sinful nature]</n> but in the Spirit, if in fact the Spirit of God lives in you <n>[directing and guiding you]</n>. But if anyone does not have the Spirit of Christ, he does not belong to Him <n>[and is not a child of God]</n>.Û]wand those who are in the flesh <n>[living a life that caters to sinful appetites and impulses]</n> cannot please God.Û\‚%the mind of the flesh <n>[with its sinful pursuits]</n> is actively hostile to God. It does not submit itself to God's law, since it cannot,lÛ[ƒQNow the mind of the flesh is death <n>[both now and foreverâ€”because it pursues sin]</n>; but the mind of the Spirit is life and peace <n>[the spiritual well-being that comes from walking with Godâ€”both now and forever]</n>;‚ÛZ„+For those who are <i>living</i> according to the flesh set their minds on the things of the flesh <n>[which gratify the body]</n>, but those who are <i>living</i> according to the Spirit, <n>[set their minds on]</n> the things of the Spirit <n>[His will and purpose]</n>.‚ÛY„5so that the <n>[righteous and just]</n> requirement of the Law might be fulfilled in us who do not live our lives in the ways of the flesh <n>[guided by worldliness and our sinful nature]</n>, but <n>[live our lives]</n> in the ways of the Spirit <n>[guided by His power]</n>.‚jÛX…MFor what the Law could not do <n>[that is, overcome sin and remove its penalty, its power]</n> being weakened by the flesh <n>[man's nature without the Holy Spirit]</n>, God did: He sent His own Son in the likeness of sinful man as an offering for sin. And He condemned sin in the flesh <n>[subdued it and overcame it in the person of His own Son]</n>,"ÛW‚=For the law of the Spirit of life <n>[which is]</n> in Christ Jesus <n>[the law of our new being]</n> has set you free from the law of sin and of death.9ÛV	‚m<pb/>Therefore there is now no condemnation <n>[no guilty verdict, no punishment]</n> for those who are in Christ Jesus <n>[who believe in Him as personal Lord and Savior]</n>.‚ÛU„Thanks be to God <n>[for my deliverance]</n> through Jesus Christ our Lord! So then, on the one hand I myself with my mind serve the law of God, but on the other, with my flesh <n>[my human nature, my worldliness, my sinful capacityâ€”I serve]</n> the law of sin.#ÛT‚?Wretched <i>and</i> miserable man that I am! Who will <n>[rescue me and]</n> set me free from this body of death <n>[this corrupt, mortal existence]</n>?‚ÛSƒ}but I see a different law <i>and</i> rule of action in the members of my body <n>[in its appetites and desires]</n>, waging war against the law of my mind <i>and</i> subduing me and making me a prisoner of the law of sin which is within my members.`ÛR9For I joyfully delight in the law of God in my inner self <n>[with my new nature]</n>,ÛQ‚<pb/>So I find <i>it to be</i> the law <n>[of my inner self]</n>, that evil is present in me, the one who wants to do good.8ÛP‚iBut if I am doing the very thing I do not want to do, I am no longer the one doing it <n>[that is, it is not me that acts]</n>, but the sin <n>[nature]</n> which lives in me.gÛOGFor the good that I want to do, I do not do, but I practice the very evil that I do not want._ÛNƒ7For I know that nothing good lives in me, that is, in my flesh <n>[my human nature, my worldlinessâ€”my sinful capacity]</n>. For the willingness <n>[to do good]</n> is present in me, but the doing of good is not..ÛM‚USo now <n>[if that is the case, then]</n> it is no longer I who do it <n>[the disobedient thing which I despise]</n>, but the sin <n>[nature]</n> which lives in me."ÛL‚=Now if I <i>habitually</i> do what I do not want to do, <n>[that means]</n> I agree with the Law, <i>confessing</i> that it is good (morally excellent).~ÛKƒuFor I do not understand my own actions <n>[I am baffled and bewildered by them]</n>. I do not practice what I want <i>to do</i>, but I am doing the very thing I hate <n>[and yielding to my human nature, my worldlinessâ€”my sinful capacity]</n>.NÛJƒWe know that the Law is spiritual, but I am <i>a creature</i> of the flesh <n>[worldly, self-reliantâ€”carnal and unspiritual]</n>, sold into slavery to sin <n>[and serving under its control]</n>.‚*ÛI„M
<pb/>Did that which is good <n>[the Law]</n>, then become death to me? Certainly not! But sin, in order that it might be revealed as sin, was producing death in me by <n>[using]</n> this good thing <n>[as a weapon]</n>, so that through the commandment sin would become exceedingly sinful.WÛH'So then, the Law is holy, and the commandment is holy and righteous and good.=ÛG‚sFor sin, seizing its opportunity through the commandment, beguiled <i>and</i> completely deceived me, and<f>[4]</f> using it as a weapon killed me <n>[separating me from God]</n>.rÛF]
And the <i>very</i> commandment which was intended to bring life, actually proved to bring death for me.PÛEƒ	I was once alive without <n>[knowledge of]</n> the Law; but when the commandment came <n>[and I understood its meaning]</n>, sin became alive and I died <n>[since the Law sentenced me to death]</n>.jÛDƒMBut sin, finding an opportunity through the commandment <n>[to express itself]</n> produced in me every kind of coveting <i>and</i> selfish desire. For without the Law sin is dead <n>[the recognition of sin is inactive]</n>.‚hÛC…I<pb/>What shall we say then? Is the Law sin? Certainly not! On the contrary, if it had not been for the Law, I would not have recognized sin. For I would not have known <n>[for example]</n> about coveting <n>[what belongs to another, and would have had no sense of guilt]</n> if the Law had not <n>[repeatedly]</n> said, "<e>You shall not covet</e>."‚ÛB„But now we have been released from the Law <i>and</i> its penalty, having died <n>[through Christ]</n> to that by which we were held captive, so that we serve <n>[God]</n> in the newness of the Spirit and not in the oldness of the letter <n>[of the Law]</n>.‚+ÛA„OWhen we were <i>living</i> in the flesh <n>[trapped by sin]</n>, the sinful passions, which were awakened by <n>[that which]</n> the Law <n>[identifies as sin]</n>, were at work in our body to bear fruit for death <n>[since the willingness to sin led to death and separation from God]</n>.pÛ@ƒY<pb/>Therefore, my<f>[3]</f> fellow believers, you too died to the Law through the <n>[crucified]</n> body of Christ, so that you may belong to another, to Him who was raised from the dead, in order that we may bear fruit for God.‚Û?„#Accordingly, she will be designated as an adulteress if she<f>[2]</f> unites herself to another man while her husband is alive. But if her husband dies, she is free from the law <n>[regarding marriage]</n>, so that she is not an adulteress if she marries another man.^Û>ƒ5For the married woman <n>[as an example]</n> is bound <i>and</i> remains bound by law to her husband while he lives; but if her husband dies, she is released <i>and</i> exempt from the law concerning her husband.AÛ=	‚}<pb/>Or do you not know,<f>[1]</f> brothers and sisters (for I am speaking to those who know the Law), that the Law has jurisdiction <n>[to rule]</n> over a person as long as he lives?5Û<‚cFor the wages of sin is death, but the free gift of God <n>[that is, His remarkable, overwhelming gift of grace to believers]</n> is eternal life in Christ Jesus our Lord.‚Û;„But now since you have been set free from sin and have become <n>[willing]</n> slaves to God, you have your benefit, resulting in sanctification <n>[being made holy and set apart for God's purpose]</n>, and the outcome <n>[of this]</n> is eternal life.Û:‚+So what benefit did you get at that time from the things of which you are now ashamed? <n>[None!]</n> For the outcome of those things is death!	Û9‚<pb/>When you were slaves of sin, you were free in regard to righteousness <n>[you had no desire to conform to God's will]</n>.
