And all <n>[of them]</n> were baptized into Moses <n>[into his safekeeping as their leader]</n> in the cloud and in the sea;lß	ƒS
<pb/>For I do not want you to be unaware, believers, that our fathers were all under the cloud <n>[in which God's presence went before them]</n> and they all passed <n>[miraculously and safely]</n> through the <n>[Red]</n> Sea;^ßƒ5	But <n>[like a boxer]</n> I strictly discipline my body and make it my slave, so that, after I have preached <n>[the gospel]</n> to others, I myself will not somehow be disqualified <n>[as unfit for service]</n>.ß‚	Therefore I do not run without a definite goal; I do not flail around like one beating the air <n>[just shadow boxing]</n>.‚ß„/	Now every athlete who <n>[goes into training and]</n> competes in the games is disciplined <i>and</i> exercises self-control in all things. They do it to win a<f>[8]</f> crown that withers, but we <n>[do it to receive]</n> an imperishable <n>[crown that cannot wither]</n>.jßƒM	<pb/><f>[7]</f> Do you not know that in a race all the runners run <n>[their very best to win]</n>, but only one receives the prize? Run <n>[your race]</n> in such a way that you may seize the prize <i>and</i> make it yours!lßQ	And I do all this for the sake of the gospel, so that I may share in its blessings along with you.jßƒM	To the<f>[6]</f> weak I became <n>[as the]</n> weak, to win the weak. I have become all things to all men, so that I may by all means <n>[in any and every way]</n> save some <n>[by leading them to faith in Jesus Christ]</n>.\ßƒ1	To those who are without (outside) the Law, <n>[I became]</n> as one without the Law, though <n>[I am]</n> not without the law of God, but under the law of Christ, so that I might win those who are without law.vßƒe	To the Jews I became as a Jew, so that I might win Jews <n>[for Christ]</n>; to men under the Law, <n>[I became]</n> as one<f>[5]</f> under the Law, though not being under the Law myself, so that I might win those who are under the Law.ß‚	<pb/>For though I am free from all men, I have made myself a slave to everyone, so that I may win more <n>[for Christ]</n>.ßƒw	What then is my reward? <n>[Just this:]</n> that, when I preach the gospel, I may offer the gospel without charge <n>[to everyone]</n>, so as not to take advantage of my rights <n>[as a preacher and apostle]</n> in <n>[preaching]</n> the gospel.Jßƒ
	For if I do this work<f>[4]</f> of my own free will, then I have a reward; but if it is not of my will <n>[but by God's choosing]</n>, I have been entrusted with a <n>[sacred]</n> stewardship.Zß
ƒ-	For if I <n>[merely]</n> preach the gospel, I have nothing to boast about, for I am compelled <n>[that is, absolutely obligated to do it]</n>. Woe to me if I do not preach the good news <n>[of salvation]</n>!‚ß„%	<pb/>But I have used none of these privileges, nor am I writing this <n>[to suggest]</n> that any such provision be made for me now. For it would be better for me to die than to have anyone deprive me of my<f>[3]</f> boast <n>[in this matter of financial support]</n>.ß	So also <n>[on the same principle]</n> the Lord directed those who preach the gospel to get their living from the gospel.|ß
ƒq	
Do you not know that those who officiate in the sacred services of the temple eat from the temple <n>[offerings of meat and bread]</n> and those who regularly attend the altar have their share from the <n>[offerings brought to the]</n> altar?aß	ƒ;	If others share in this rightful claim over you, do not we even more? However, we did not exercise this right, but we put up with everything so that we will not hinder <n>[the spread of]</n> the good news of Christ. ßy	If we have sown <n>[the good seed of]</n> spiritual things in you, is it too much if we reap material things from you?;ß‚o	
Or does He speak entirely for our sake? Yes, it was written for our sake: The plowman ought to plow<f>[2]</f> in hope, and the thresher to thresh in hope of sharing the harvest.Oßƒ		For it is written in the Law of Moses, "<e>You shall not muzzle an ox while it is treading out the grain</e> <n>[to keep it from eating the grain]</n>." Is it <n>[only]</n> for oxen that God cares?qß[	<pb/>Do I say these things only from a man's perspective? Does the Law not endorse the same principles?Hßƒ		<n>[Consider this:]</n> Who at any time serves as a soldier at his own expense? Who plants a vineyard and does not eat its fruit? Or who tends a flock and does not use the milk of the flock?~ßu	Or is it only Barnabas and I who have no right to stop doing manual labor <n>[in order to support our ministry]</n>?ß‚	Have we not the right to take along with us a believing wife, as do the rest of the apostles and the Lord's brothers and Cephas (Peter)?^ß5	Have we not the right to our food and drink <n>[at the expense of the churches]</n>?ß ‚	<pb/>This is my defense to those who would put me on trial <i>and</i> interrogate me <n>[concerning my authority as an apostle]</n>:qÞƒ[	If I am not <n>[considered]</n> an apostle to others, at least I am one to you; for you are the seal <i>and</i> the certificate <i>and</i> the living evidence of my apostleship in the Lord <n>[confirming and authenticating it]</n>.tÞ~	ƒc	<pb/>Am I not free <n>[unrestrained and exempt from any obligation]</n>? Am I not an apostle? Have I not<f>[1]</f> seen Jesus our <n>[risen]</n> Lord <n>[in person]</n>? Are you not <n>[the result and proof of]</n> my work in the Lord?;Þ}‚o
Therefore, if <n>[my eating a certain]</n> food causes my brother to stumble (sin), I will not eat <n>[such]</n> meat ever again, so that I will not cause my brother to stumble.Þ|‚/And when you sin against the brothers and sisters in this way and wound their weak conscience <n>[by confusing them]</n>, you sin against Christ.'Þ{‚GFor through your knowledge (spiritual maturity) this weak man is ruined <n>[that is, he suffers in his spiritual life]</n>, the brother for whom Christ died.gÞzƒG
For if someone sees you, a person having<f>[3]</f> knowledge,<f>[4]</f> eating in an idol's temple, then if he is weak, will he not be encouraged to eat things sacrificed to idols <n>[and violate his own convictions]</n>?FÞyƒ	Only be careful that this liberty of yours <n>[this power to choose]</n> does not somehow become a stumbling block <n>[that is, a temptation to sin]</n> to the weak <n>[in conscience]</n>.Þx‚#Now food will not commend us to God <i>nor</i> bring us close to Him; we are no worse off if we do not eat, nor are we better if we do eat.‚OÞw…<pb/>However, not all <n>[believers]</n> have this knowledge. But some, being accustomed <n>[throughout their lives]</n> to <n>[thinking of]</n> the idol until now <n>[as real and living]</n>, still eat food<f>[2]</f> as if it were sacrificed to an idol; and because their conscience is weak, it is defiled (guilty, ashamed).‚$Þv„Ayet for us there is but one God, the Father,<f>[1]</f> who is the source of all things, and we <i>exist</i> for Him; and one Lord, Jesus Christ, by whom are all things <n>[that have been created]</n>, and we <n>[believers exist and have life and have been redeemed]</n> through Him.|ÞuqFor even if there are so-called gods, whether in heaven or on earth, as indeed there are many gods and many lords,7Þt‚g<pb/>In this matter, then, of eating food offered to idols, we know that an idol is nothing in the world <n>[it has no real existence]</n>, and that there is no God but one. Þs‚9But if anyone loves God <n>[with awe-filled reverence, obedience and gratitude]</n>, he is known by Him <n>[as His very own and is greatly loved]</n>.Þr‚5If anyone imagines that he knows <i>and</i> understands anything <n>[of divine matters, without love]</n>, he has not yet known as he ought to know.‚8Þq	„k<pb/>Now about food sacrificed to idols, we know that we all have knowledge <n>[concerning this]</n>. Knowledge <n>[alone]</n> makes <n>[people self-righteously]</n> arrogant, but love <n>[that unselfishly seeks the best for others]</n> builds up <i>and</i> encourages others to grow <n>[in wisdom]</n>.Þp‚(But in my opinion a widow is happier if she stays as she is. And I think that I also have the Spirit of God <n>[in this matter]</n>.HÞoƒ	'<pb/>A wife is bound <n>[to her husband by law]</n> as long as he lives. But if her husband dies, she is free to marry whomever she wishes, only <n>[provided that he too is]</n> in the Lord.Þn‚%&So then both the father who gives his virgin <i>daughter</i> in marriage does well, and he who does not give her in marriage will do better.‚Þm„#%But the man who stands firmly committed in his heart, having no compulsion <n>[to yield to his daughter's request]</n>, and has authority over his own will, and has decided in his own heart to keep his own virgin <n>[daughter from being married]</n>, he will do well.‚!Þl„;$<pb/>But if any man thinks that he is not acting properly <i>and</i> honorably toward his virgin <i>daughter</i>, <n>[<f>[14]</f> by not permitting her to marry]</n>, if she is<f>[15]</f> past her youth, and it must be so, let him do as he wishes, he does not sin; let her marry.Þk‚!#Now I say this for your own benefit; not to restrict you, but to promote what is appropriate and secure undistracted devotion to the Lord.‚Þj„!"and <i>his interests</i> are divided. The unmarried woman or the virgin is concerned about the matters of the Lord, how to be holy <i>and</i> set apart both in body and in spirit; but a married woman is concerned about worldly things, how she may please her husband.\Þi1!but the married man is concerned about worldly things, how he may please his wife,Þh‚ <pb/>But I want you to be free from concern. The unmarried man is concerned about the things of the Lord, how he may please the Lord;eÞgƒCand those who use the world <n>[taking advantage of its opportunities]</n>, as though they did not make full use of it. For the outward form of this world <n>[its present social and material nature]</n> is passing away.6Þf‚eand those who weep, as though they did not weep; and those who rejoice, as though they did not rejoice; and those who buy, as though they did not possess <n>[anything]</n>;Þe‚1But I say this, believers: the<f>[13]</f> time has been shortened, so that from now on even those who have wives should be as though they did not;‚ ÞdƒyBut if you do marry, you have not sinned <n>[in doing so]</n>; and if a virgin marries, she has not sinned <n>[in doing so]</n>. Yet those <n>[who marry]</n> will have troubles (special challenges) in this life, and I am trying to spare you that.pÞcYAre you bound to a wife? Do not seek to be released. Are you<f>[12]</f> unmarried? Do not seek a wife.Þb‚3I think then that because of the impending distress <n>[that is, the pressure of the current trouble]</n>, it is good for a man to remain as he is.*Þa‚M<pb/>Now concerning the virgins <n>[of marriageable age]</n> I have no command of the Lord, but I give my opinion as one who by the Lord's mercy is trustworthy.vÞ`eBrothers, let each one remain with God in that <i>condition</i> in which he was <n>[when he was]</n> called.Þ_‚You were bought with a price <n>[a precious price paid by Christ]</n>; do not become slaves to men <n>[but to Christ]</n>.Þ^‚1For he who was a slave when he was called in the Lord is a freedman of the Lord, likewise he who was free when he was called is a slave of Christ.hÞ]ƒI<pb/>Were you a slave when you were called? Do not worry about that <n>[since your status as a believer is<f>[10]</f> equal to that of a freeborn believer]</n>; but if you are able to gain your freedom,<f>[11]</f> do that.^Þ\5Each one should remain in the condition in which he was <n>[when he was]</n> called.}Þ[sCircumcision is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing, but <i>what matters is</i> keeping the commandments of God.VÞZƒ%Was anyone at the time of his calling <n>[from God already]</n> circumcised? He is not to<f>[8]</f> become uncircumcised. Has anyone been called while uncircumcised?<f>[9]</f> He is not to be circumcised.‚
ÞY„
<pb/>Only, let each one live the life which the Lord has assigned him, and to which God has called him <n>[for each person is unique and is accountable for his choices and conduct, let him walk in this way]</n>. This is the rule I make in all the churches.JÞXƒ
For how do you know, wife, whether you will save your husband <n>[by leading him to Christ]</n>? Or how do you know, husband, whether you will save your wife <n>[by leading her to Christ]</n>?NÞWƒBut if the unbelieving partner leaves, let him leave. In such cases the <n>[remaining]</n> brother or sister is not <n>[spiritually or morally]</n> bound. But God has called us to<f>[7]</f> peace.‚BÞV„}For the unbelieving husband is<f>[5]</f> sanctified <n>[that is, he receives the blessings granted]</n> through his <n>[Christian]</n> wife, and the unbelieving wife is sanctified through her believing husband. Otherwise your children would be <n>[ceremonially]</n><f>[6]</f> unclean, but as it is they are holy.ÞU
And if any <n>[believing]</n> woman has an unbelieving husband, and he consents to live with her, she must not leave him.‚ÞTƒ{<pb/>To the<f>[4]</f> rest I declareâ€”I, not the Lord <n>[since Jesus did not discuss this]</n>â€”that if any <n>[believing]</n> brother has a wife who does not believe <n>[in Christ]</n>, and she consents to live with him, he must not leave her. ÞS‚9(but even if she does leave him, let her remain single or else be<f>[3]</f> reconciled to her husband) and that the husband should not leave his wife.ÞR‚3
<pb/>But to the<f>[2]</f> married <n>[believers]</n> I give instructionsâ€”not I, but the Lordâ€”that the wife is not to separate from her husband,ÞQ‚	But if they do not have <n>[sufficient]</n> self-control, they should marry; for it is better to marry than to burn <i>with passion</i>.>ÞP‚u<pb/>But I say to the unmarried and to the widows, <n>[that as a practical matter]</n> it is good if they remain <n>[single and entirely devoted to the Lord]</n><f>[1]</f> as I am.ÞOwI wish that all the people were as I am; but each person has his own gift from God, one of this kind and one of that.@ÞN{But I am saying this as a concession, not as a command.‚ÞM„1Do not deprive each other <n>[of marital rights]</n>, except perhaps by mutual consent for a time, so that you may devote yourselves <n>[unhindered]</n> to prayer, but come together again so that Satan will not tempt you <n>[to sin]</n> because of your lack of self-control.pÞLƒYThe wife does not have <n>[exclusive]</n> authority over her own body, but the husband <i>shares with her</i>; and likewise the husband does not have <n>[exclusive]</n> authority over his body, but the wife <i>shares with him</i>.ÞK‚!The husband must fulfill his <n>[marital]</n> duty to his wife <n>[with good will and kindness]</n>, and likewise the wife to her husband.ÞJ‚5But because of <n>[the temptation to participate in]</n> sexual immorality, let each man have his own wife, and let each woman have her own husband.ÞI	‚)<pb/>Now as to the matters of which you wrote: It is good (beneficial, advantageous) for a man not to touch a woman <n>[outside marriage]</n>.4ÞH‚aYou were bought with a price <n>[you were actually purchased with the precious blood of Jesus and made His own]</n>. So then, honor <i>and</i> glorify God with your body.?ÞG‚wDo you not know that your body is a temple of the Holy Spirit who is within you, whom you have <n>[received as a gift]</n> from God, and that you are not your own <n>[property]</n>?uÞFƒcRun away from sexual immorality <n>[in any form, whether thought or behavior, whether visual or written]</n>. Every <i>other</i> sin that a man commits is outside the body, but the one who is sexually immoral sins against his own body.`ÞE9But the one who is united <i>and</i> joined to the Lord is one spirit <i>with Him</i>.ÞD‚'Do you not know that the one who joins himself to a prostitute is one body <i>with her</i>? For He says, "<e>The two shall be one flesh</e>."+ÞC‚ODo you not know that your bodies are members of Christ? Am I therefore to take the members of Christ and make them part of a<f>[6]</f> prostitute? Certainly not!hÞBIAnd God has not only raised the Lord <n>[to life]</n>, but will also raise us up by His power.‚ÞA„)
Food is for the stomach and the stomach for food, but God will do away with both of them. The body is not intended for sexual immorality, but for the Lord, and the Lord is for the body <n>[to save, sanctify, and raise it again because of the sacrifice of the cross]</n>.XÞ@ƒ)Everything is permissible for me, but not all things are beneficial. Everything is permissible for me, but I will not be enslaved by anything <n>[and brought under its power, allowing it to control me]</n>.ƒ	Þ?†And such were some of you <n>[before you believed]</n>. But you were washed <n>[by the atoning sacrifice of Christ]</n>, you were sanctified <n>[set apart for God, and made holy]</n>, you were justified <n>[declared free of guilt]</n> in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ and in the <n>[Holy]</n> Spirit of our God <n>[the source of the believer's new life and changed behavior]</n>.kÞ>ƒO
nor thieves, nor the greedy, nor drunkards, nor revilers <n>[whose words are used as weapons to abuse, insult, humiliate, intimidate, or slander]</n>, nor swindlers will inherit <i>or</i> have any share in the kingdom of God.‚,Þ=„Q	<pb/>Do you not know that the unrighteous will not inherit <i>or</i> have any share in the kingdom of God? Do not be deceived;<f>[4]</f> neither the sexually immoral, nor idolaters, nor adulterers, nor effeminate <n>[by perversion]</n>, nor<f>[5]</f> those who participate in homosexuality,nÞ<UOn the contrary, it is you who wrong and defraud, and you do this even to your brothers and sisters.Þ;‚%<pb/>Why, the very fact that you have lawsuits with one another is already a defeat. Why not rather be wronged? Why not rather be defrauded?wÞ:gbut <i>instead</i>, brother goes to law against brother, and that before <n>[judges who are]</n> unbelievers?dÞ9ƒAI say this to your shame. Can it be that there is not one wise man among you who <n>[is governed by integrity and]</n> will be able <i>and</i> competent to decide <n>[private disputes]</n> between his fellow believers,&Þ8‚ESo if you have lawsuits dealing with matters of this life, are you appointing those as judges <n>[to hear disputes]</n> who are of no account in the church?Þ7wDo you not know that we <n>[believers]</n> will judge angels? How much more then <n>[as to]</n> matters of this life?\Þ6ƒ1<f>[2]</f> Do you not know that the saints (God's people) will <n>[one day]</n> judge the world? If the world is to be judged by you, are you not competent<f>[3]</f> to try trivial (insignificant, petty) cases?uÞ5	ƒe<pb/>Does any one of you, when he has a<f>[1]</f> complaint (civil dispute) with another <n>[believer]</n>, dare to go to law before unrighteous men (non-believers) instead of <n>[placing the issue]</n> before the saints (God's people)?)Þ4‚K
God <i>alone</i> sits in judgment on those who are outside <n>[the faith]</n>. <e>Remove the wicked one from among you</e> <n>[expel him from your church]</n>.?Þ3‚wFor what business is it of mine to judge outsiders (non-believers)? Do you not judge those who are within <i>the church</i> <n>[to protect the church as the situation requires]</n>?ƒÞ2†But actually, I have written to you not to associate with any so-called <n>[Christian]</n> brother if he is sexually immoral or greedy, or is an idolater <n>[devoted to anything that takes the place of God]</n>, or is a reviler <n>[who insults or slanders or otherwise verbally abuses others]</n>, or is a drunkard or a swindlerâ€”you must not so much as<f>[5]</f> eat with such a person.<Þ1‚q
not meaning the immoral people of this world, or the greedy ones and swindlers, or idolaters, for then you would have to get out of the world <i>and</i> human society altogether!sÞ0_	<pb/>I wrote you in my <n>[previous]</n> letter not to associate with <n>[sexually]</n> immoral peopleâ€”HÞ/ƒ	Therefore, let us celebrate the feast, not with old leaven, nor with leaven of vice <i>and</i> malice and wickedness, but with the unleavened bread of sincerity and <n>[untainted]</n> truth."Þ.‚=<f>[4]</f> Clean out the old leaven so that you may be a new batch, just as you are, still unleavened. For Christ our Passover Lamb has been sacrificed.‚5Þ-„c<pb/>Your boasting <n>[over the supposed spirituality of your church]</n> is not good <n>[indeed, it is vulgar and inappropriate]</n>.<f>[3]</f> Do you not know that <n>[just]</n> a little leaven ferments the whole batch <n>[of dough, just as a little sin corrupts a person or an entire church]</n>?Þ,‚+you are to<f>[2]</f> hand over this man to Satan for the destruction of his body, so that his spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus.Þ+wIn the name of our Lord Jesus, when you are assembled, and I am with you in spirit, with the power of our Lord Jesus,3Þ*‚_<pb/>For I, though absent <n>[from you]</n> in body but present in spirit, have already passed judgment on him who has committed this <n>[act]</n>, as if I were present.5Þ)‚cAnd you are proud <i>and</i> arrogant! You should have mourned in shame so that the man who has done this <n>[disgraceful]</n> thing would be removed from your fellowship!‚Þ(	„<pb/>It is actually reported <n>[everywhere]</n> that there is sexual immorality among you, a kind of immorality that is condemned even among the <n>[unbelieving]</n> Gentiles: that someone<f>[1]</f> has <n>[an intimate relationship with]</n> his father's wife.Þ'‚Which do you prefer? Shall I come to you with a rod <n>[of discipline and correction]</n>, or with love and a gentle spirit?BÞ&For the kingdom of God is not based on talk but on power.fÞ%ƒEBut I will come to you soon, if the Lord is willing, and I will find out not just the talk of these arrogant people, but <n>[evaluate]</n> their <n>[spiritual]</n> power <n>[whether they live up to their own claims]</n>.nÞ$UNow some of you have become arrogant <i>and</i> pretentious, as though I were not coming to see you.|Þ#ƒqFor this reason I have sent Timothy to you, who is my beloved and faithful child in the Lord, and he will remind you of my way of life in Christ <n>[my conduct and my precepts for godly living]</n>, just as I teach everywhere in every church.YÞ"+So I urge you, be imitators of me <n>[just as a child imitates his father]</n>.‚Þ!„-For even if you were to have ten thousand teachers <n>[to guide you]</n> in Christ, yet you would not have many fathers <n>[who led you to Christ and assumed responsibility for you]</n>, for I became your father in Christ Jesus through the good news <n>[of salvation]</n>.rÞ ]<pb/>I do not write these things to shame you, but to warn <i>and</i> advise you as my beloved children./Þ‚W
When we are slandered, we <i>try to be</i> conciliatory <i>and</i> answer softly. We have become like the scum of the world, the dregs of all things, even until now.LÞƒWe work <n>[for our living]</n>, working <i>hard</i> with our own hands. When we are reviled <i>and</i> verbally abused, we bless. When we are persecuted, we take it patiently <i>and</i> endure.Þ‚+To this present hour we are both hungry and thirsty; we are <i>continually</i> poorly dressed, and we are roughly treated, and wander homeless.'Þ‚G
We are <n>[regarded as]</n> fools for Christ, but you are so wise in Christ; we are weak, but you are strong; you are highly esteemed, but we are dishonored.‚Þ„!	For, I think, God has exhibited us apostles at the end of the line, like men sentenced to death <n>[and paraded as prisoners in a procession]</n>, because we have become a spectacle to the world <n>[a show in the world's amphitheater]</n>, both to angels and to men.‚}Þ…s<pb/><n>[You behave as if]</n> you are already filled <n>[with spiritual wisdom and in need of nothing more]</n>. Already you have become rich <n>[in spiritual gifts]</n>! You <n>[in your conceit]</n> have ascended your thrones <i>and</i> become kings without us; and how I wish <n>[that it were true and]</n> that you did reign as kings, so that we might reign with you.‚-Þ„SFor who regards you as superior <i>or</i> what sets you apart as special? What do you have that you did not receive <n>[from another]</n>? And if in fact you received it <n>[from God or someone else]</n>, why do you boast as if you had not received it <n>[but had gained it by yourself]</n>?‚`Þ…9<pb/>Now I have applied these things <n>[that is, the analogies about factions]</n> to myself and Apollos for your benefit, believers, so that you may learn from us not to go beyond what is written <n>[in Scripture]</n>, so that none of you will become arrogant <i>and</i> boast in favor of one <n>[minister or teacher]</n> against the other.‚Þ„#So do not go on passing judgment before the appointed time, <i>but wait</i> until the Lord comes, for He will both bring to light the <n>[secret]</n> things that are hidden in darkness and disclose the motives of the hearts. Then each one's praise will come from God.Þ‚3I am aware of nothing against myself <i>and</i> I feel blameless, but I am not by this acquitted <n>[before God]</n>. It is the Lord who judges me.;Þ‚oBut <n>[as for me personally]</n> it matters very little to me that I may be judged by you or <i>any</i> human court <n>[on this point]</n>; in fact, I do not even judge myself.Þ‚In this case, moreover, it is required <n>[as essential and demanded]</n> of stewards that one be found faithful <i>and</i> trustworthy.9Þ	‚m<pb/>So then, let us <n>[who minister]</n> be regarded as servants of Christ and stewards (trustees, administrators) of the mysteries of God <n>[that He chooses to reveal]</n>.=Þuand you belong to Christ; and Christ belongs to God.Þ‚whether Paul or Apollos or Cephas (Peter) or the world or life or death or things present or things to come; all things are yours,Þ‚So let no one boast in men <n>[about their wisdom, or of having this or that one as a leader]</n>. For all things are yours,uÞcand again, "<e>The Lord knows the thoughts</e> of the <n>[humanly]</n> wise, <e>that they are useless</e>."dÞƒAFor the wisdom of this world is foolishness (absurdity, stupidity) before God; for it is written <n>[in Scripture]</n>, "<n>[He is]</n> <e>The one who catches the wise</e> <i>and</i> <e>clever in their craftiness</e>;"uÞ
ƒc<pb/>Let no one deceive himself. If anyone among you thinks that he is wise in this age, let him become a fool <n>[discarding his worldly pretensions and acknowledging his lack of wisdom]</n>, so that he may become <n>[truly]</n> wise.<Þ‚qIf anyone destroys the temple of God <n>[corrupting it with false doctrine]</n>, God will destroy the destroyer; for the temple of God is holy (sacred), and that is what you are.YÞƒ+<pb/><f>[4]</f> Do you not know <i>and</i> understand that you <n>[the church]</n> are the temple of God, and that the Spirit of God dwells <n>[permanently]</n> in you <n>[collectively and individually]</n>?QÞ
ƒBut if any person's work is burned up <n>[by the test]</n>, he will suffer the loss <n>[of his reward]</n>; yet he himself will be saved, but only as <n>[one who has barely escaped]</n> through fire.4Þ	‚aIf any person's work which he has built <n>[on this foundation, that is, any outcome of his effort]</n> remains <n>[and survives this test]</n>, he will receive a reward.‚Þƒ
each one's work will be clearly shown <n>[for what it is]</n>; for the day <n>[of judgment]</n> will disclose it, because it is to be revealed with fire, and the fire will test the quality <i>and</i> character <i>and</i> worth of each person's work.fÞEBut if anyone builds on the foundation with gold, silver, precious stones, wood, hay, straw,sÞ_for no one can lay a foundation other than the one which is <n>[already]</n> laid, which is Jesus Christ.‚Þƒ
<pb/>According to the <n>[remarkable]</n> grace of God which was given to me <n>[to prepare me for my task]</n>, like a skillful master builder I laid a foundation, and now another is building on it. But each one must be careful how he builds on it,#Þ‚?	For we are God's fellow workers <n>[His servants working together]</n>; you are God's cultivated field <n>[His garden, His vineyard]</n>, God's building.6Þ‚eHe who plants and he who waters are one <n>[in importance and esteem, working toward the same purpose]</n>; but each will receive his own reward according to his own labor.xÞiSo neither is the one who plants nor the one who waters anything, but <n>[only]</n> God who causes the growth.\Þ1I planted, Apollos watered, but God <n>[all the while]</n> was causing the growth.Þ ‚7<pb/>What then is Apollos? And what is Paul? Just servants through whom you believed <n>[in Christ]</n>, even as the Lord appointed to each his task.RÝƒFor when one <i>of you</i> says, "I am <n>[a disciple]</n> of Paul," and another, "I am <n>[a disciple]</n> of Apollos," are you not <n>[proving yourselves unchanged, just]</n> <i>ordinary</i> people?
