g	Ïê^ËDÃO•Œï‚                                      }áus<t>And I will be a Father to you, </t><t>And you will be My sons and daughters," </t><t>Says the Lord Almighty.</t>gátƒG<t>"So <e>come out from among unbelievers and be separate</e>," says the Lord, </t><t>"<e>And do not touch what is unclean</e>; </t><t>And I will graciously receive you <i>and</i> welcome you <n>[with favor]</n>,</t><br/>‚ás„<t>What agreement is there between the temple of God and idols? For we are the temple of the living God; just as God said: </t><br/><t>"<e>I will dwell among them and walk among them</e>; </t><t><e>And I will be their God, and they shall be My people</e>.</t><br/>ár{What harmony can there be between Christ and Belial (Satan)? Or what does a believer have in common with an unbeliever?‚áq„<pb/>Do not be unequally bound together with unbelievers <n>[do not make mismatched alliances with them, inconsistent with your faith]</n>. For what partnership can righteousness have with lawlessness? Or what fellowship can light have with darkness?5áp‚c
Now in the same way as a fair exchange <n>[for our love toward you]</n>â€”I am speaking as <n>[I would]</n> to childrenâ€”open wide <n>[your hearts]</n> <i>to us</i> also.páoYThere is no limit to our affection for you, but you are limited in your own affection <n>[for us]</n>.}áns<pb/>We are speaking freely to you, Corinthians <n>[we are keeping nothing back]</n>, and our heart is opened wide.ám}
as sorrowful, yet always rejoicing; as poor, yet bestowing riches on many; as having nothing, yet possessing all things.ál‚	as unknown <n>[to the world]</n>, yet well-known <n>[by God and His people]</n>; as dying, yet we live; as punished, yet not killed;ák‚amid glory and dishonor; by evil report and good report; <i>branded</i> as deceivers and yet <n>[vindicated as]</n> truthful;`ájƒ9in <n>[speaking]</n> the word of truth, in the power of God; by the weapons of righteousness for the right hand <n>[like holding the sword to attack]</n> and for the left <n>[like holding the shield to defend]</n>,ái‚in purity <i>and</i> sincerity, in knowledge <i>and</i> spiritual insight, in patience, in kindness, in the Holy Spirit, in genuine love,]áh3in beatings, in imprisonments, in riots, in labors, in sleepless nights, in hunger,ágbut we commend ourselves in every way as servants of God: in great endurance, in sufferings, in hardships, in distresses,dáfA<pb/>we put no obstruction in anyone's path, so that the ministry will not be discredited,‚áe„)<t>For He says, </t><br/><t>"<e>At the acceptable time</e> (the time of grace) <e>I listened to you</e>, </t><t><e>And I helped you on the day of salvation</e>." </t><t>Behold, now is "<e>the acceptable time</e>," behold, now is "<e>the day of salvation</e>"â€”</t><br/>/ád	‚Y<pb/>Working together <i>with Him</i>, we strongly urge you not to receive God's grace in vain <n>[by turning away from sound doctrine and His merciful kindness]</n>.‚ác„He made Christ who knew no sin to <n>[judicially]</n> be sin on our behalf, so that in Him we would become the righteousness of God <n>[that is, we would be made acceptable to Him and placed in a right relationship with Him by His gracious lovingkindness]</n>.Eábƒ<pb/>So we are ambassadors for Christ, as though God were making His appeal through us; we <n>[as Christ's representatives]</n> plead with you on behalf of Christ to be reconciled to God.záaƒmthat is, that God was in Christ reconciling the world to Himself, not counting people's sins against them <n>[but canceling them]</n>. And He has committed to us the message of reconciliation <n>[that is, restoration to favor with God]</n>.má`ƒSBut all <i>these</i> things are from God, who reconciled us to Himself through Christ <n>[making us acceptable to Him]</n> and gave us the ministry of reconciliation <n>[so that by our example we might bring others to Him]</n>,‚gá_…GTherefore if anyone is in Christ <n>[that is, grafted in, joined to Him by faith in Him as Savior]</n>, <i>he is</i> a new creature <n>[reborn and renewed by the Holy Spirit]</n>; the old things <n>[the previous moral and spiritual condition]</n> have passed away. Behold, new things have come <n>[because spiritual awakening brings a new life]</n>.cá^ƒ?<pb/>So from now on we regard no one from a human point of view <n>[according to worldly standards and values]</n>. Though we have known Christ from a human point of view, now we no longer know Him <i>in this way</i>.á]‚and He died for all, so that all those who live would no longer live for themselves, but for Him who died and was raised for their sake.á\‚For the love of Christ controls <i>and</i> compels us, because we have concluded this, that One died for all, therefore all died;á[‚1
If we are out of our mind <n>[just unstable fanatics as some critics say]</n>, it is for God; if we are in our right mind, it is for your benefit.‚7áZ„gWe are not commending ourselves to you again, but are giving you an occasion to be <n>[rightfully]</n> proud of us, so that you will have <i>an answer</i> for those who take pride in <n>[outward]</n> appearances <n>[the virtues they pretend to have]</n> rather than what is <n>[actually]</n> in heart.‚\áY…1<pb/>Therefore, since we know the fear of the Lord <n>[and understand the importance of obedience and worship]</n>, we persuade people <n>[to be reconciled to Him]</n>. But we are plainly known to God <n>[He knows everything about us]</n>; and I hope that we are plainly known also in your consciences <n>[your God-given discernment]</n>.‚xáX…i
For we <n>[believers will be called to account and]</n> must all appear before the<f>[1]</f> judgment seat of Christ, so that each one may be repaid for what has been done in the body, whether good or<f>[2]</f> bad <n>[that is, each will be held responsible for his actions, purposes, goals, motivesâ€”the use or misuse of his time, opportunities and abilities]</n>."áW‚=	Therefore, whether we are at home <n>[on earth]</n> or away from home <n>[and with Him]</n>, it is our <n>[constant]</n> ambition to be pleasing to Him."áV‚=we are <n>[as I was saying]</n> of good courage <i>and</i> confident hope, and prefer rather to be absent from the body and to be at home with the Lord.áU‚for we walk by faith, not by sight <n>[living our lives in a manner consistent with our confident belief in God's promises]</n>â€”)áT‚K<pb/>So then, being always filled with good courage <i>and</i> confident hope, and knowing that while we are at home in the body we are absent from the Lordâ€”NáSƒNow He who has made us <i>and</i> prepared us for this very purpose is God, who gave us the <n>[Holy]</n> Spirit as a pledge <n>[a guarantee, a down payment on the fulfillment of His promise]</n>.‚/áR„WFor while we are in this tent, we groan, being burdened <n>[often weighed down, oppressed]</n>, not that we want to be unclothed <n>[separated by death from the body]</n>, but to be clothed, so that what is mortal <n>[the body]</n> will be swallowed up by life <n>[after the resurrection]</n>.=áQuso that by putting it on we will not be found naked.áP{For indeed in this <i>house</i> we groan, longing to be clothed with our <n>[immortal, eternal]</n> celestial dwelling,WáO	ƒ)<pb/>For we know that if the earthly tent <n>[our physical body]</n> which is our house is torn down <n>[through death]</n>, we have a building from God, a house not made with hands, eternal in the heavens.váNƒeSo we look not at the things which are seen, but at the things which are unseen; for the things which are visible are temporal <n>[just brief and fleeting]</n>, but the things which are invisible are everlasting <i>and</i> imperishable.xáMƒiFor our momentary, light distress <n>[this passing trouble]</n> is producing for us an eternal weight of glory <n>[a fullness]</n> beyond all measure <n>[surpassing all comparisons, a transcendent splendor and an endless blessedness]</n>!oáLƒW<pb/>Therefore we do not become discouraged <n>[spiritless, disappointed, or afraid]</n>. Though our outer self is <n>[progressively]</n> wasting away, yet our inner <i>self</i> is being <n>[progressively]</n> renewed day by day.WáKƒ'For all <n>[these]</n> things are for your sake, so that as <n>[God's remarkable, undeserved]</n> grace reaches to more and more people it may increase thanksgiving, to the glory of <n>[our great]</n> God.áJ‚knowing that He who raised the Lord Jesus will also raise us with Jesus and will present us <n>[along]</n> with you in His presence./áI‚W
<pb/>Yet we have the same spirit of faith as he had, who wrote <i>in Scripture</i>, "<e>I believed, therefore I spoke</e>." We also believe, therefore we also speak,áH‚So <i>physical</i> death is <n>[actively]</n> at work in us, but <n>[spiritual]</n> life <n>[is actively at work]</n> in you.uáGƒcFor we who live are constantly <n>[experiencing the threat of]</n> being handed over to death for Jesus' sake, so that the <n>[resurrection]</n> life of Jesus also may be evidenced in our mortal body <n>[which is subject to death]</n>.áF‚
always carrying around in the body the dying of Jesus, so that the <n>[resurrection]</n> life of Jesus also may be shown in our body.xáEi	hunted down <i>and</i> persecuted, but not deserted <n>[to stand alone]</n>; struck down, but never destroyed;áD‚'We are pressured in every way <n>[hedged in]</n>, but not crushed; perplexed <n>[unsure of finding a way out]</n>, but not driven to despair;‚8áC„i<pb/>But we have this <i>precious</i> treasure <n>[the good news about salvation]</n> in <n>[unworthy]</n> earthen vessels <n>[of human frailty]</n>, so that the grandeur <i>and</i> surpassing greatness of the power will be <n>[shown to be]</n> from God <n>[His sufficiency]</n> and not from ourselves.dáBƒAFor God, who said, "Let light shine out of darkness," is the One who has shone in our hearts to give us the Light of the knowledge of the glory <i>and</i> majesty of God <n>[clearly revealed]</n> in the face of Christ.áA‚	For we do not preach ourselves, but Jesus Christ as Lord, and ourselves <n>[merely]</n> as your bond-servants for Jesus' sake.Tá@ƒ!among them the god of this world <n>[Satan]</n> has blinded the minds of the unbelieving to prevent them from seeing the illuminating light of the gospel of the glory of Christ, who is the image of God.á?‚But even if our gospel is <n>[in some sense]</n> hidden <n>[behind a veil]</n>, it is hidden <n>[only]</n> to those who are perishing;‚á>ƒ{But we have renounced the disgraceful things hidden because of shame; not walking in trickery or adulterating the word of God, but by stating the truth <n>[openly and plainly]</n>, we commend ourselves to everyone's conscience in the sight of God.Qá=	ƒ<pb/>Therefore, since we have this ministry, just as we received mercy <n>[from God, granting us salvation, opportunities, and blessings]</n>, we do not get discouraged <i>nor</i> lose our motivation.‚á<„+And we all, with unveiled face, <i>continually</i> seeing as in a mirror the glory of the Lord, are <i>progressively</i> being transformed into His image from <n>[one degree of]</n> glory to <n>[even more]</n> glory, which comes from the Lord, <n>[who is]</n> the Spirit.á;‚!Now the Lord is the Spirit, and where the Spirit of the Lord is, <i>there</i> is liberty <n>[emancipation from bondage, true freedom]</n>.ká:Obut whenever a person turns <n>[in repentance and faith]</n> to the Lord, the veil is taken away.eá9CBut to this day whenever Moses is read, a veil <n>[of blindness]</n> lies over their heart;xá8ƒiBut <n>[in fact]</n> their minds were hardened <n>[for they had lost the ability to understand]</n>; for until this very day at the reading of the old covenant the same veil remains unlifted, because it is removed <n>[only]</n> in Christ.%á7‚C
and we are not like Moses, <i>who</i> used to put a veil over his face so that the Israelites would not gaze at the end of the glory which was fading away.yá6k<pb/>Since we have such a <n>[glorious]</n> hope <i>and</i> confident expectation, we speak with great courage,Ká5ƒFor if that <n>[Law]</n> which fades away <i>came</i> with glory, <i>how</i> much more <i>must</i> that <n>[gospel]</n> which remains <i>and</i> is permanent abide in glory <i>and</i> splendor!á4‚
Indeed, what had glory <n>[the Law]</n>, in this case no longer has glory because of the glory that surpasses it <n>[the gospel]</n>.‚ á3„9	For if the ministry that brings condemnation <n>[the old covenant, the Law]</n> has glory, how much more does glory overflow in the ministry that brings righteousness <n>[the new covenant which declares believers free of guilt and sets them apart for God's special purpose]</n>!á2‚5how will the ministry of the Spirit <n>[the new covenant which allows us to be Spirit-filled]</n> fail to be even more glorious <i>and</i> splendid?‚<á1„q<pb/>Now if the ministry of death, engraved in letters on stones <n>[the covenant of the Law which led to death because of sin]</n>, came with such glory <i>and</i> splendor that the Israelites were not able to look steadily at the face of Moses because of its glory, <n>[a brilliance]</n> that was fading,‚7á0„gHe has qualified us <n>[making us sufficient]</n> as ministers of a new covenant <n>[of salvation through Christ]</n>, not of the letter <n>[of a written code]</n> but of the Spirit; for the letter <n>[of the Law]</n> kills <n>[by revealing sin and demanding obedience]</n>, but the Spirit gives life.&á/‚ENot that we are sufficiently qualified in ourselves to claim anything as <i>coming</i> from us, but our sufficiency <i>and</i> qualifications come from God.á.‚<pb/>Such is the confidence <i>and</i> steadfast reliance <i>and</i> absolute trust that we have through Christ toward God.:á-‚mYou show that you are a letter from Christ, delivered by us, written not with ink but with the Spirit of the living God, not on tablets of stone but on tablets of human hearts.pá,YYou are our letter <n>[of recommendation]</n>, written in our hearts, recognized and read by everyone.&á+	‚G<pb/>Are we starting to commend ourselves again? Or do we need, like some <n>[false teachers]</n>, letters of recommendation to you or from you? <n>[No!]</n>‚$á*„AFor we are not like many, <n>[acting like merchants]</n> peddling God's word <n>[shortchanging and adulterating God's message]</n>; but from pure <n>[uncompromised]</n> motives, as <n>[commissioned and sent]</n> from God, we speak <n>[His message]</n> in Christ in the sight of God.‚á)ƒto the <i>latter</i> one an aroma from death to death <n>[a fatal, offensive odor]</n>, but to the other an aroma from life to life <n>[a vital fragrance, living and fresh]</n>. And who is adequate <i>and</i> sufficiently qualified for these things?5á(‚cFor we are the <i>sweet</i> fragrance of Christ <n>[which ascends]</n> to God, <n>[discernible both]</n> among those who are being saved and among those who are perishing;6á'‚e<pb/>But thanks be to God, who always leads us in triumph in Christ, and through us spreads <i>and</i> makes evident everywhere the sweet fragrance of the knowledge of Him.	á&‚
my spirit could not rest because I did not find my brother Titus <i>there</i>; so saying goodbye to them, I left for Macedonia.á%‚-<pb/>Now when I arrived at Troas to <i>preach</i> the good news of Christ, even though a door <n>[of opportunity]</n> opened for me in the Lord,\á$1to keep Satan from taking advantage of us; for we are not ignorant of his schemes.Uá#ƒ#
If you forgive anyone anything, I too forgive <n>[that one]</n>; and what I have forgiven, if I have forgiven anything, has been for your sake in the presence of <n>[and with the approval of]</n> Christ,*á"‚M	For this was my purpose in writing, to see if you would stand the test, whether you are obedient <i>and</i> committed to following my instruction in all things.sá!_Therefore I urge you to reinstate him in your affections <i>and</i> reaffirm your<f>[3]</f> love for him.@á ‚yso instead <n>[of further rebuke, now]</n> you should rather <n>[graciously]</n> forgive and comfort <i>and</i> encourage him, to keep him from being overwhelmed by excessive sorrow.GáFor such a one this punishment by the majority is sufficient,Máƒ<pb/>But if<f>[2]</f> someone has caused <n>[all this]</n> sorrow, he has caused it not to me, but in some degreeâ€”not to put it too severelyâ€”<n>[he has distressed and grieved]</n> all of you.SáƒFor I wrote to you out of great distress and with an anguished heart, and with many tears, not to cause you sorrow but to make you realize the <n>[overflowing]</n> love which I have especially for you.láƒQAnd I<f>[1]</f> wrote this same thing to you, so that when I came, I would not be filled with sorrow by those who ought to make me glad, for I trusted in you <i>and</i> felt confident that my joy would be shared by all of you.á‚For if I cause you grief <n>[by a well-deserved rebuke]</n>, who then provides me enjoyment but the very one whom I have made sad?Qá	<pb/>But I made up my mind not to grieve you with another painful visit.‚4á	„cnot that we rule <n>[like dictators]</n> over your faith, but <i>rather</i> we work with you for <n>[the increase of]</n> your joy; for in your faith you stand firm <n>[in your strong conviction that Jesus of Nazarethâ€”the Messiahâ€”is the Son of God, through whom we obtain eternal salvation]</n>.á	‚)<pb/>But I call on God as my soul's witness, that it was to spare you <n>[pain and discouragement]</n> that I did not come again to Corinthâ€”‚á	„#it is He who has also put His seal on us <n>[that is, He has appropriated us and certified us as His]</n> and has given us the <n>[Holy]</n> Spirit in our hearts as a pledge <n>[like a security deposit to guarantee the fulfillment of His promise of eternal life]</n>.;á	‚qNow it is God who establishes <i>and</i> confirms us <n>[in joint fellowship]</n> with you in Christ, and who has anointed us <n>[empowering us with the gifts of the Spirit]</n>;á	‚'For as many as are the promises of God, in Christ they are <n>[all answered]</n> "Yes." So through Him we say our "Amen" to the glory of God.ká	ƒQFor the Son of God, Jesus Christ, who was preached among you by us, by me, Silvanus, and Timothy, was not "Yes" and "No," but has proved to be "Yes" in Him <n>[true and faithful, the divine "Yes" affirming God's promises]</n>.á	‚%But <n>[as surely as]</n> God is faithful <i>and</i> means what He says, our message to you is not "Yes" and "No" <n>[at the same time]</n>.zá	ƒoSo then, was I indecisive <i>or</i> capricious when I was <n>[originally]</n> planning this? Or the things I plan, do I plan<f>[5]</f> in a self-serving way like a worldly man, ready to say, "Yes, yes" and "No, no" <n>[at the same time]</n>?=á	‚uthat is, <n>[I wanted]</n> to visit you on my way to Macedonia, and <n>[then]</n> to come back to you <n>[on my return]</n> from Macedonia, and have you send me on my way to Judea.á	}<pb/>It was with this confidence that I planned at first to visit you, so that you might receive twice a token of grace;8á	‚kjust as you have <n>[already]</n> partially understood us, <n>[and one day will recognize]</n> that you can be proud of us just as we are of you, in the day of our Lord Jesus.Vá	ƒ'
For we write you nothing other than what you read and understand <n>[there is no double meaning in what we say]</n>. And I hope you will <n>[accurately]</n> understand <n>[divine things]</n> until the end;‚gá
	…IThis is our <n>[reason for]</n> proud confidence: our conscience testifies that we have conducted ourselves in the world <n>[in general]</n>, and especially toward you, with pure motives and godly sincerity, not in human wisdom, but in the grace of God <n>[that is, His gracious lovingkindness that leads people to Christ and spiritual maturity]</n>.aá	ƒ=while you join in helping us by your prayers. Then thanks will be given by many persons on our behalf for the gracious gift <n>[of deliverance]</n> granted to us through <i>the prayers of</i> many <n>[believers]</n>.Eá	ƒ
He rescued us from so great a <i>threat of</i> death, and will <i>continue to</i> rescue us. On Him we have set our hope. And He will again rescue us <n>[from danger and draw us near]</n>,^á
	ƒ7	Indeed, we felt within ourselves that we had received the sentence of death <n>[and were convinced that we would die, but this happened]</n> so that we would not trust in ourselves, but in God who raises the dead.‚á		„<pb/>For we do not want you to be uninformed, brothers and sisters, about<f>[4]</f> our trouble in <n>[the west coast province of]</n> Asia <n>[Minor]</n>, how we were utterly weighed down, beyond our strength, so that we despaired even of life <n>[itself]</n>.‚á	„And our<f>[3]</f> hope for you <n>[our confident expectation of good for you]</n> is firmly grounded <n>[assured and unshaken]</n>, since we know that just as you share <i>as partners</i> in our sufferings, so also you share <i>as partners</i> in our comfort.‚á	„But if we are troubled <i>and</i> distressed, it is for your comfort and salvation; or if we are comforted <i>and</i> encouraged, it is for your comfort, which works <n>[in you]</n> when you patiently endure the same sufferings which we<f>[2]</f> experience.‚á	„#For just as Christ's sufferings are ours in abundance <n>[as they overflow to His followers]</n>, so also our comfort <n>[our reassurance, our encouragement, our consolation]</n> is abundant through Christ <n>[it is truly more than enough to endure what we must]</n>.\á	ƒ3who comforts <i>and</i> encourages us in every trouble so that we will be able to comfort <i>and</i> encourage those who are in any kind of trouble, with the comfort with which we ourselves are comforted by God.á	‚7<pb/>Blessed <n>[gratefully praised and adored]</n> be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of mercies and the God of all comfort, á	{<pb/>Grace to you and peace <n>[inner calm and spiritual well-being]</n> from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ.‚/á		„[<pb/><t>Paul, an apostle (special messenger, personally chosen representative) of Christ Jesus (the Messiah) by the will of God, and Timothy <i>our</i> brother, </t><br/>To the church of God which is at Corinth, and to all the<f>[1]</f> saints (God's people) throughout Achaia (southern Greece)::áoMy love be with all of you in Christ Jesus. Amen.{á oThe grace of our Lord Jesus <n>[His unmerited favor, His spiritual blessing, His profound mercy]</n> be with you..à‚UIf anyone does not love the Lord <n>[does not obey and respect and believe in Jesus Christ and His message]</n>, he is to be accursed. Maranatha (O our Lord, come)!5à~e<pb/>This greeting is in my own handâ€”Paul.Jà}
All the believers greet you. Greet one another with a holy kiss.8à|‚i<pb/>The churches of Asia send you their greetings. Aquila and Prisca, together with the church <n>[that meets]</n> in their house, send you their warm greetings in the Lord.zà{mThey have refreshed my spirit as well as yours. So fully acknowledge such men <i>and</i> deeply appreciate them.wàzgI rejoice because Stephanas and Fortunatus and Achaicus have arrived, for they have made up for your absence.Aày‚{to be subject to such leaders <n>[treating them with courtesy and respect]</n>, and to everyone who helps in the work and labors <n>[for the benefit of yourselves and the church]</n>.^àxƒ5<pb/>Brothers and sisters, you know that <i>those of</i> the household of Stephanas were the first converts in Achaia, and that they have devoted themselves for ministry to<f>[2]</f> God's peopleâ€”now I urge youeàwCLet everything you do be done in love <n>[motivated and inspired by God's love for us]</n>.@àv‚y
<pb/>Be on guard; stand firm in your faith <n>[in God, respecting His precepts and keeping your doctrine sound]</n>. Act like <n>[mature]</n> men <i>and</i> be courageous; be strong.Gàuƒ<pb/>As for our brother Apollos, I have strongly encouraged him to visit you with the other brothers. It was not at all his desire to come now, but he will come when he has the opportunity.‚àt„So allow no one to treat him with disdain <n>[as if he were inconsequential]</n>. But send him off <n>[cordially, and speed him on his way]</n> in peace, so that he may come to me, for I am expecting him <n>[to come along]</n> with the <i>other</i> brothers.@às‚y
<pb/>If Timothy comes, see to it that <n>[you put him at ease, so that]</n> he has nothing to fear in regard to you, for he is <n>[devotedly]</n> doing the Lord's work, just as I am.àr‚'	because a wide door for effective service has opened to me <n>[in Ephesus, a very promising opportunity]</n>, and there are many adversaries.>àqwBut I will stay in Ephesus until<f>[1]</f> Pentecost,àp‚1For I do not wish to see you right now <i>just</i> in passing, but I hope to remain with you for some time <n>[later on]</n>, if the Lord permits.*ào‚Mbut it may be that I will stay with you <n>[for a while]</n>, or even spend the winter, so that you may send me on my way to wherever I may go <i>afterward</i>.fànE<pb/>I will visit you after I go through Macedonia, for I am only passing through Macedonia;Hàm	and if it is fitting for me to go too, they will accompany me.àl‚3When I arrive, I will send whomever you approve with letters <n>[of authorization]</n> to take your gift <n>[of charity and love]</n> to Jerusalem;Bàk‚}On the first day of every week each one of you is to put something aside, in proportion to his prosperity, and save it so that no collections <n>[will need to]</n> be made when I come.8àj	‚k<pb/>Now concerning the money collected for <n>[the relief of]</n> the saints <n>[in Jerusalem]</n>, you are to do the same as I directed the churches of Galatia <i>to do</i>.‚eài…C:<pb/>Therefore, my beloved brothers and sisters, be steadfast, immovable, always excelling in the work of the Lord <n>[always doing your best and doing more than is needed]</n>, being <i>continually</i> aware that your labor <n>[even to the point of exhaustion]</n> in the Lord is not futile <i>nor</i> wasted <n>[it is never without purpose]</n>.nàhU9but thanks be to God, who gives us the victory <n>[as conquerors]</n> through our Lord Jesus Christ.gàgG8The sting of death is sin, and the power of sin <n>[by which it brings death]</n> is the law;Vàf%7<e>O death, where is your victory</e>? <e>O death, where is your sting</e>?"Pàeƒ6And when this perishable puts on the imperishable, and this mortal puts on immortality, then the Scripture will be fulfilled that says, "<e>Death is swallowed up</e> in victory (vanquished forever).Zàdƒ-5For this perishable <n>[part of us]</n> must put on the imperishable <n>[nature]</n>, and this mortal <n>[part of us that is capable of dying]</n> must put on immortality <n>[which is freedom from death]</n>.‚àc„4in a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at <n>[the sound of]</n> the last trumpet call. For a trumpet will sound, and the dead <n>[who believed in Christ]</n> will be raised imperishable, and we will be <n>[completely]</n> changed <n>[wondrously transformed]</n>.~àbƒu3Listen very carefully, I tell you a mystery <n>[a secret truth decreed by God and previously hidden, but now revealed]</n>; we will not all sleep <n>[in death]</n>, but we will all be <n>[completely]</n> changed <n>[wondrously transformed]</n>,8àa‚i2Now I say this, believers, that flesh and blood cannot inherit <i>nor</i> be part of the kingdom of God; nor does the perishable (mortal) inherit the imperishable (immortal).%à`‚C1Just as we have borne the image of the earthly <n>[the man of dust]</n>,<f>[10]</f> we will also bear the image of the heavenly <n>[the Man of heaven]</n>.à_‚30As is the earthly man <n>[the man of dust]</n>, so are those who are of earth; and as is the heavenly <n>[Man]</n>, so are those who are of heaven.à^‚/The first man <n>[Adam]</n> is from the earth, earthy <n>[made of dust]</n>; the second Man <n>[Christ, the Lord]</n> is from heaven.à]‚	.However, the spiritual <n>[the immortal life]</n> is not first, but the physical <n>[the mortal life]</n>; then the spiritual.[à\ƒ/-So it is written <n>[in Scripture]</n>, "The first <e>man</e>, Adam, <e>became a living soul</e> (an individual);" the last Adam (Christ) <i>became</i> a life-giving spirit <n>[restoring the dead to life]</n>.Và[ƒ%,it is sown a natural body <n>[mortal, suited to earth]</n>, it is raised a spiritual body <n>[immortal, suited to heaven]</n>. As surely as there is a physical body, there is also a spiritual <i>body</i>.jàZM+It is sown in dishonor, it is raised in glory; it is sown in weakness, it is raised in strength;/àY‚W*<pb/>So it is with the resurrection of the dead. The <n>[human]</n> body that is sown is perishable <i>and</i> mortal, it is raised imperishable <i>and</i> immortal.NàXƒ)There is a glory <i>and</i> beauty of the sun, another glory of the moon, and yet another <n>[distinctive]</n> glory of the stars; and one star differs from another in glory <i>and</i> brilliance.kàWƒO(There are also heavenly bodies <n>[sun, moon and stars]</n> and earthly bodies <n>[humans, animals, and plants]</n>, but the glory <i>and</i> beauty of the heavenly is one kind, and the <i>glory</i> of the earthly is another. àVy'All flesh is not the same. There is one kind for humans, another for animals, another for birds, and another for fish.sàU_&But God gives it a body just as He planned, and to each kind of seed a body of its own <n>[is given]</n>.àT‚%The seed you sow is not the body (the plant) which it is going to become, but it is a bare seed, perhaps of wheat or some other grain.-àS‚S$You fool! Every time you plant <i>seed</i> you sow something that does not come to life <n>[germinating, springing up and growing]</n> unless it <i>first</i> dies.jàRM#<pb/>But someone will say, "How are the dead raised? And with what kind of body will they come?"‚àQ„"Be sober-minded <n>[be sensible, wake up from your spiritual stupor]</n> as you ought, and stop sinning; for some <n>[of you]</n> have no knowledge of God <n>[you are disgracefully ignorant of Him, and ignore His truths]</n>. I say this to your shame.JàP
!Do not be deceived:<f>[9]</f>"Bad company corrupts good morals."‚àO„	 What good has it done me if, <n>[merely]</n> from a human point of view, I<f>[8]</f> fought with wild animals at Ephesus? If the dead are not raised <n>[at all]</n>, <e>let us eat and drink</e> <n>[enjoying ourselves now]</n>, <e>for tomorrow we die</e>.)àN‚KI assure you, believers, by the pride which I have in you in <n>[your union with]</n> Christ Jesus our Lord, I die daily <n>[I face death and die to self]</n>.4àM‚a<n>[For that matter]</n> why are we <n>[running such risks and putting ourselves]</n><f>[7]</f> in danger <n>[nearly]</n> every hour <n>[if there is no resurrection]</n>?%àL‚C<pb/>Otherwise, what will those do who are being<f>[6]</f> baptized for the dead? If the dead are not raised at all, why are people even baptized for them?
