úér¦
 	E±å_të™                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             ‚Mìq	…”<pb/>Not many <n>[of you]</n> should become teachers <n>[serving in an official teaching capacity]</n>, my brothers and sisters, for you know that we <n>[who are teachers]</n> will<f>[1]</f> be judged by a higher standard <n>[because we have assumed greater accountability and more condemnation if we teach incorrectly]</n>.ìp‚”For just as the <n>[human]</n> body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works <n>[of obedience]</n> is also dead.fìoƒE”In the same way, was Rahab the prostitute not justified by works too, when she received the <n>[Hebrew]</n><f>[8]</f> spies as guests <i>and</i> protected them, and sent them away <n>[to escape]</n> by a different route?$ìn‚A”You see that a man (believer) is justified by works and not by faith alone <n>[that is, by acts of obedience a born-again believer reveals his faith]</n>.‚ìmƒ{”And the Scripture was fulfilled which says, "<e>Abraham believed God, and this</e> <n>[faith]</n> <e>was credited to him</e> <n>[by God]</n> <e>as righteousness</e> <i>and</i> <e>as conformity to His will</e>," and he was called the friend of God.Rìlƒ”You see that <n>[his]</n> faith was working together with his works, and as a result of the works, his faith was completed <n>[reaching its maturity when he expressed his faith through obedience]</n>.Gìkƒ”Was our father Abraham not <n>[shown to be]</n> justified by works <n>[of obedience which expressed his faith]</n> when he offered Isaac his son on the altar <n>[as a sacrifice to God]</n>?ìj‚”But are you willing to recognize, you foolish <n>[spiritually shallow]</n> person, that faith without <n>[good]</n> works is useless?Vìiƒ%”You believe that<f>[7]</f> God is one; you do well <n>[to believe that]</n>. The demons also believe <n>[that]</n>, and shudder <i>and</i> bristle <n>[in awe-filled terrorâ€”they have seen His wrath]</n>!zìhƒm”<pb/>But someone may say, "You <n>[claim to]</n> have faith and I have <n>[good]</n> works; show me your <n>[alleged]</n> faith without the works <n>[if you can]</n>, and I will show you my faith by my works <n>[that is, by what I do]</n>."ìg}”So too, faith, if it does not have works <n>[to back it up]</n>, is by itself dead <n>[inoperative and ineffective]</n>.Gìfƒ”and one of you says to them, "Go in peace <n>[with my blessing]</n>, <n>[keep]</n> warm and feed yourselves," but he does not give them the necessities for the body, what good does that do?sìe_”If a brother or sister is without <n>[adequate]</n> clothing and lacks <n>[enough]</n> food for each day,‚ìd„”What is the benefit, my fellow believers, if someone claims to have faith but has no <n>[good]</n> works <n>[as evidence]</n>? Can that <n>[kind of]</n> faith save him? <n>[No, a mere claim of faith is not sufficientâ€”genuine faith produces good works.]</n>.ìc‚U”
For judgment <i>will be</i> merciless to one who has shown no mercy; but <n>[to the one who has shown mercy]</n> mercy triumphs <n>[victoriously]</n> over judgment.8ìb‚i”Speak and act <n>[consistently]</n> as people who are going to be judged by the law of liberty <n>[that moral law that frees obedient Christians from the bondage of sin]</n>.\ìaƒ1”For He who said, "<e>Do not commit adultery</e>," also said, "<e>Do not murder</e>." Now if you do not commit adultery, but you murder, you have become<f>[6]</f> guilty of transgressing the <n>[entire]</n> Law. ì`y”
For whoever keeps the whole Law but stumbles in one <i>point</i>, he has become guilty of <n>[breaking]</n> all of it.ì_‚”	But if you show partiality <n>[prejudice, favoritism]</n>, you are committing sin and are convicted by the Law as offenders.‚ì^„”<pb/>If, however, you are <n>[really]</n> fulfilling the royal law according to the Scripture, "<e>You shall love your neighbor as yourself</e> <n>[that is, if you have an unselfish concern for others and do things for their benefit]</n>" you are doing well.gì]G”Do they not blaspheme the<f>[5]</f> precious name <n>[of Christ]</n> by which you are called?+ì\‚O”But you <n>[in contrast]</n> have dishonored the poor man. Is it not the rich who oppress <i>and</i> exploit you, and personally drag you into the courts of law?Nì[ƒ”Listen, my beloved brothers and sisters: has not God chosen the poor of this world to be rich in faith and <n>[as believers to be]</n> heirs of the kingdom which He promised to those who love Him?\ìZ1”have you not discriminated among yourselves, and become judges with wrong motives?pìYƒY”and you pay special attention to the one who wears the<f>[4]</f> fine clothes, and say to him, "You sit here in this good seat," and you tell the poor man, "You stand over there, or sit down <n>[on the floor]</n> by my footstool,"'ìX‚G”For if a man comes into your<f>[1]</f> meeting place<f>[2]</f> wearing a gold ring and<f>[3]</f> fine clothes, and a poor man in dirty clothes also comes in,MìW	ƒ”<pb/>My fellow believers, do not practice your faith in our glorious Lord Jesus Christ with <i>an attitude</i> of partiality <n>[toward peopleâ€”show no favoritism, no prejudice, no snobbery]</n>.‚ìV	„”Pure and unblemished religion <n>[as it is expressed in outward acts]</n> in the sight of our God and Father is this: to visit <i>and</i> look after the fatherless and the widows in their distress, and to keep oneself uncontaminated by the <n>[secular]</n> world.kìU	ƒQ”<pb/>If anyone thinks himself to be religious <n>[scrupulously observant of the rituals of his faith]</n>, and does not control his tongue but deludes his <i>own</i> heart, this person's religion is worthless (futile, barren).‚=ìT	„u”But he who looks carefully into the perfect law, the <i>law</i> of liberty, and faithfully abides by it, not having become a <n>[careless]</n> listener who forgets but<f>[8]</f> an active doer <n>[who obeys]</n>, he will be blessed <i>and</i> favored by God in what he does <n>[in his life of obedience]</n>.vìS	g”for <i>once</i> he has looked at himself and gone away, he immediately forgets<f>[7]</f> what he looked like.ìR	‚+”For if anyone only listens to the word<f>[6]</f> without obeying it, he is like a man who looks very carefully at his natural face in a mirror;‚ìQ	„”But prove yourselves doers of the word <n>[actively and continually obeying God's precepts]</n>, and not merely listeners <n>[who hear the word but fail to internalize its meaning]</n>, deluding yourselves <n>[by unsound reasoning contrary to the truth]</n>.kìP	ƒQ”So get rid of all uncleanness and<f>[5]</f> all that remains of wickedness, and with a humble spirit receive the word <n>[of God]</n> which is implanted <n>[actually rooted in your heart]</n>, which is able to save your souls.%ìO	‚E”for the <n>[resentful, deep-seated]</n> anger of man does not produce the righteousness of God <n>[that standard of behavior which He requires from us]</n>.‚ ìN	ƒ{”<pb/>Understand this, my beloved brothers and sisters. Let everyone be quick to hear <n>[be a careful, thoughtful listener]</n>, slow to speak <n>[a speaker of carefully chosen words and]</n>, slow to anger <n>[patient, reflective, forgiving]</n>;‚ìM	„+”It was of His own will that He gave us birth <n>[as His children]</n> by the word of truth, so that we would be a kind of first fruits of His creatures <n>[a prime example of what He created to be set apart to Himselfâ€”sanctified, made holy for His divine purposes]</n>.‚2ìL	„_”Every good thing given and every perfect gift is from above; it comes down from the Father of lights <n>[the Creator and Sustainer of the heavens]</n>, in whom there is no variation <n>[no rising or setting]</n> or shadow<f>[4]</f> cast by His turning <n>[for He is perfect and never changes]</n>.:ìK	q”Do not be misled, my beloved brothers and sisters.ìJ	‚”Then when the illicit desire has conceived, it gives birth to sin; and when sin has run its course, it gives birth to death.ìI	‚7”But each one is tempted when he is dragged away, enticed <i>and</i> baited <n>[to commit sin]</n> by his own <n>[worldly]</n> desire (lust, passion).fìH	ƒG”
Let no one say when he is tempted, "I am being tempted by God" <n>[for temptation does not originate from God, but from our own flaws]</n>; for God cannot be tempted by <n>[what is]</n> evil, and He Himself tempts no one.‚@ìG	„{”<pb/>Blessed <n>[happy, spiritually prosperous, favored by God]</n> is the man who is steadfast under trial <i>and</i> perseveres when tempted; for when he has passed the test <i>and</i> been approved, he will receive the <n>[victor's]</n> crown of life which <i>the Lord</i> has promised to those who love Him.DìF	ƒ”For the sun rises with a scorching wind and withers the grass; its flower falls off and<f>[3]</f> its beauty fades away; so too will the rich man, in the midst of his pursuits, fade away.PìE	ƒ”
and the rich man <i>is to glory</i> in being humbled <n>[by trials revealing human frailty, knowing true riches are found in the grace of God]</n>, for like the flower of the grass he will pass away.'ìD	‚I”	<pb/>Let the brother in humble circumstances glory in his high position <n>[as a born-again believer, called to the true riches and to be an heir of God]</n>;ìC	‚”<i>being</i> a double-minded man, unstable <i>and</i> restless in all his ways <n>[in everything he thinks, feels, or decides]</n>.{ìB	q”For such a person ought not to think <i>or</i> expect that he will receive anything <n>[at all]</n> from the Lord,SìA	ƒ!”But he must ask <n>[for wisdom]</n> in faith, without doubting <n>[God's willingness to help]</n>, for the one who doubts is like a billowing surge of the sea that is blown about and tossed by the wind.qì@	ƒ]”<pb/>If any of you lacks wisdom <n>[to guide him through a decision or circumstance]</n>, he is to ask of <n>[our benevolent]</n> God, who gives to everyone generously and without rebuke <i>or</i> blame, and it will be given to him.1ì?	‚]”And let endurance have its perfect result <i>and</i> do a thorough work, so that you may be perfect and completely developed <n>[in your faith]</n>, lacking in nothing.ì>	‚/”Be assured that the testing of your faith <n>[through experience]</n> produces endurance <n>[leading to spiritual maturity, and inner peace]</n>.tì=	c”<pb/>Consider it nothing but joy, my<f>[2]</f> brothers and sisters, whenever you fall into various trials.Xì<		ƒ-”<pb/><t><f>[1]</f> James, a bond-servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, </t><br/>To the twelve <n>[Hebrew]</n> tribes <n>[scattered abroad among the Gentiles]</n> in the dispersion: Greetings (rejoice)!$ì;CŠ
