and, "<e>If even a wild animal touches the mountain, it will be stoned</e> <n>[to death]</n>."ì‚Šand to the blast of a trumpet and a sound of words <n>[such that]</n> those who heard it begged that nothing more be said to them.Bì‚}ŠFor you have not come <n>[as did the Israelites in the wilderness]</n> to <i>a mountain</i> that can be touched and to a blazing fire, and to gloom and darkness and a raging windstorm,‚Hì…	ŠFor you know that later on, when he wanted <n>[to regain title to]</n> his inheritance of the blessing, he was rejected, for he found no opportunity for repentance <n>[there was no way to repair what he had done, no chance to recall the choice he had made]</n>, even though he sought for it with <n>[bitter]</n> tears.ìŠand <n>[see to it]</n> that no one is immoral or godless like Esau, who sold his own birthright for a <i>single</i> meal.ì‚ŠSee to it that no one falls short of God's grace; that no root of resentment springs up and causes trouble, and by it many be defiled;
